    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "2",
        "question": "What did you learn about the life and writings of Domhnall Mac Amhlaigh and/or the contribution of Maureen O'Hara to the creation of Irish images through film?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Domhnall Mac Amhlaigh (1926–1989) was a significant Irish-language writer and migrant worker whose life and writings illuminate the Irish diaspora experience, particularly labour migration to Britain. His autobiographical works, *Swabhairne* (1960) and *Imeall an Bhéil* (1976), documented the everyday lived experience of Irish migrants—their work, hardship, cultural dislocation, and resilience—offering a ground-level perspective on diaspora rarely captured in official narratives.\n\nMac Amhlaigh's significance lies in his authentic portrayal of migrant labour. As a shearer, farm worker, and labourer in Scotland and England during the 1950s, he wrote from direct experience. His writings recorded the exploitation of Irish workers—low wages, dangerous conditions, poor housing—and the cultural alienation of being away from Ireland. His work in Irish-language newspapers and journals preserved migrant voices and experiences that might otherwise have been lost to historical record. Mac Amhlaigh's contribution was to validate the experiences of ordinary working-class migrants, documenting their agency and adaptation rather than portraying them as passive victims. His writings became a cultural archive of Irish–British labour relations and migrant resilience during the post-war period when tens of thousands of Irish crossed the Irish Sea seeking employment.\n\nMaureen O'Hara (1920–2015) was an Irish-American actress who profoundly shaped global perceptions of Irish identity and womanhood through cinema. Born in Dublin, O'Hara became Hollywood's most iconic Irish actress, appearing in over 60 films. Her contribution to creating Irish images was substantial and complex, operating on both positive and problematic levels.\n\nO'Hara's most celebrated films—notably *The Quiet Man* (1952) directed by John Ford and *The Black Stallion* (1979)—constructed a romanticised, visually striking image of Ireland and Irish femininity. In *The Quiet Man*, filmed partly on location in Ireland, O'Hara portrayed Mary Kate Danaher opposite John Wayne. The film presented Ireland through a distinctly American lens—emphasizing landscape beauty, Irish temperament stereotypes, and a narrative that centred male authority and romantic resolution. O'Hara's red-haired, spirited characterization became emblematic of 'Irish womanhood' in American popular culture, influencing how millions of diaspora Irish and non-Irish audiences understood Irish identity.\n\nO'Hara's screen persona was notably strong and assertive compared to many female contemporaries. Her characters often displayed independence and wit; in *The Quiet Man*, despite the film's conventional resolution, Mary Kate asserts herself physically and verbally, demanding respect from her male partner. This represented a departure from passive feminine stereotypes, though it remained contained within patriarchal narrative frameworks. O'Hara herself was acutely aware of her role in shaping Irish representation and deliberately cultivated an image of Irish pride and dignity in Hollywood, resisting demeaning stereotypes when possible.\n\nHowever, O'Hara's contribution to Irish image-creation was mediated through American commercial interests and reflected American, not Irish, perspectives. *The Quiet Man*, though filmed in Ireland with Irish extras, was fundamentally a Hollywood product shaped by Ford's directorial vision and American audience expectations. The film perpetuated certain stereotypes—Irish rurality, male dominance, Catholic wholesomeness—that, while not entirely false, were selective and romanticised. O'Hara's Ireland existed primarily in American imagination rather than reflecting Irish self-representation.\n\nO'Hara's broader filmography included roles in adventure and costume dramas where her Irish identity was incidental. Yet across all her films, she embodied a particular Irishness—articulate, attractive, dignified—that challenged negative stereotypes prevalent in earlier cinema and in American society. Her prominence in Hollywood during an era of significant Irish-American demographic influence meant her image circulated widely within diaspora communities, reinforcing particular notions of Irish identity for second and third-generation Irish-Americans.\n\nComparatively, Mac Amhlaigh and O'Hara operated in entirely different registers of cultural production. Mac Amhlaigh created Irish-language literature for Irish and diaspora audiences, grounded in material reality and lived experience. O'Hara produced images for mass commercial cinema, reaching global audiences but filtered through entertainment industry priorities. Mac Amhlaigh documented the unglamorous reality of migrant labour; O'Hara presented aestheticised, narrative-driven representations of Irishness.\n\nYet both shaped diaspora identity and Irish self-perception. Mac Amhlaigh's writings validated migrant experiences and created a literary archive that later scholars and diaspora members could recognize as authentic to their own or their ancestors' lives. O'Hara's films, despite their artificiality, provided visual and narrative templates through which diaspora and non-diaspora populations understood Ireland and Irishness. Both, in different ways, responded to and shaped the Irish diaspora experience during the mid-twentieth century—a period of massive Irish emigration and significant cultural negotiation over Irish identity in diaspora contexts.\n\nMac Amhlaigh's contribution was one of testimony and documentation; O'Hara's was one of representation and imagination. Together, they illustrate the multiple, sometimes contradictory ways Irish identity was constructed and transmitted through diaspora during a crucial period of Irish emigration and cultural change.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
