        "question": "During the period 1871–1920, what were key developments in science, technology and medicine?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "During 1871–1920, science, technology and medicine underwent transformative developments that reshaped society, industry and daily life. These advances were interconnected, each building upon the other to accelerate progress across multiple fields.\n\nIn physics and chemistry, the period witnessed foundational breakthroughs. In 1905, Albert Einstein published his theory of special relativity, fundamentally challenging Newtonian concepts of space and time. His mass-energy equivalence equation (E=mc²) provided theoretical foundations for future nuclear physics. In chemistry, the periodic table, systematised by Dmitri Mendeleev in the 1870s, enabled chemists to predict element properties and organise chemical knowledge systematically. These theoretical advances elevated understanding of matter and energy at the most fundamental level.\n\nElectrical technology revolutionised industrial production and domestic life. Thomas Edison's practical incandescent light bulb (1879) and his development of direct current systems made electric lighting commercially viable, transforming factories, streets and homes. Nikola Tesla's alternating current (AC) system proved superior for long-distance power transmission, enabling centralised power generation and distribution networks. By 1920, electricity had become the dominant power source in industrialised nations, replacing gas and oil lighting and driving factory machinery with unprecedented efficiency and safety.\n\nTransportation technology experienced dramatic transformation. The internal combustion engine, developed through the 1870s–1890s, powered Karl Benz's automobile (1885) and Gottlieb Daimler's innovations. Henry Ford revolutionised manufacturing through assembly-line production (introduced 1913), making automobiles affordable for middle-class consumers. The Model T exemplified mass production efficiency. Concurrently, the aviation industry emerged: the Wright brothers achieved powered flight (1903), and by 1920, aircraft were used commercially and militarily. Railway technology also advanced, with faster engines, safer brakes and more comfortable passenger services.\n\nIn medicine and public health, germ theory transformed medical practice. Louis Pasteur's work on microbiology and vaccination (rabies vaccine, 1885) established that microorganisms caused disease. Robert Koch identified the tuberculosis bacillus (1882), validating germ theory and enabling targeted disease control. Joseph Lister's antiseptic surgery methods, building on germ theory, dramatically reduced post-operative infections and mortality. Vaccination programmes, particularly against smallpox, reduced disease prevalence significantly across Europe and North America.\n\nSurgical and anaesthetic advances made complex operations safer and more tolerable. Anaesthetics developed during the 1870s–1880s—including chloroform and ether—allowed surgeons to perform longer, more intricate procedures without patient consciousness. Antiseptic and aseptic surgical techniques reduced infection rates catastrophically. X-ray technology, discovered by Wilhelm Röntgen (1895), provided physicians with unprecedented diagnostic capability, allowing visualisation of internal injuries and diseases without invasive exploration. By 1920, X-rays were standard in hospitals.\n\nPublic health infrastructure improvements paralleled medical advances. Urban sanitation systems—including clean water supply and sewage treatment—became standard in industrialised cities. Pasteurisation of milk (pioneered by Pasteur) eliminated disease transmission through contaminated dairy. These sanitary improvements prevented cholera, typhoid and other water-borne diseases. Maternal and infant mortality declined as germ theory influenced childbirth practices. Public health campaigns promoted hygiene education, vaccination and disease prevention.\n\nCommunication technology compressed distances. Alexander Graham Bell's telephone (1876) and later improvements enabled real-time voice communication across vast distances. Guglielmo Marconi's wireless telegraphy (1895 onwards) transmitted signals without cables, revolutionising maritime communication and eventually broadcasting. These technologies accelerated business transactions, emergency responses and information dissemination.\n\nMaterials science advanced through steel production innovations. The Bessemer process and later open-hearth methods made high-quality steel affordable and abundant, enabling construction of bridges, ships, railways and buildings of unprecedented scale and durability. Reinforced concrete, combining steel and concrete, offered new architectural possibilities and structural strength.\n\nThe interconnectedness of these developments was crucial. Electrical power enabled new manufacturing processes; transportation innovations required materials science advances; medical breakthroughs depended on chemical and optical technologies; communications technology supported industrial coordination. Scientific theory (relativity, germ theory) informed practical applications. Industrial demand incentivised innovation, creating a virtuous cycle.\n\nThese developments produced profound social consequences. Factory electrification increased productivity and shifted manufacturing towards urban centres, accelerating urbanisation. Automobile production created new industries and employment while transforming urban planning and social mobility. Medical advances extended life expectancy, reduced child mortality and improved quality of life, particularly in industrialised nations. Improved communications integrated business, accelerated news dissemination and facilitated international coordination.\n\nHowever, technological developments were unevenly distributed geographically and socially. Industrialised Western nations benefited disproportionately, widening gaps between developed and colonised regions. Working-class populations often endured dangerous factory conditions despite technological safety improvements. Medical advances reached wealthier populations first.\n\nBy 1920, science, technology and medicine had fundamentally restructured industrial societies. Electricity, automobiles, aviation, and medical advances represented irreversible transformations. The theoretical breakthroughs in physics and chemistry established foundations for twentieth-century developments including nuclear physics, antibiotics and electronics. The period 1871–1920 thus represents a watershed in human technological and scientific history, establishing patterns of innovation and application that would accelerate throughout the twentieth century.",
