    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "3",
        "question": "What internal and external problems faced Sukarno's Indonesia and how effective were his attempts to resolve them?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Sukarno's Indonesia faced profound internal and external problems that severely strained his attempts at nation-building. Internally, the newly independent state struggled with religious and ideological divisions, ethnic fragmentation, and economic instability. Externally, Cold War pressures, territorial disputes, and the legacy of colonialism posed acute challenges.\n\nInternally, Indonesia's most significant problem was managing religious and ideological divisions. With a Muslim majority but substantial Hindu, Christian, and Buddhist minorities, and with secular nationalists competing against Islamic parties, Sukarno had to navigate conflicting visions for the state's character. The Darul Islam rebellion (1948-1962) in West Java and Aceh represented armed Islamic opposition to secular nationalism, challenging state authority and consuming military resources. Sukarno's response—guided democracy (1957 onwards)—attempted to balance these forces by incorporating all major ideological streams (nationalist, religious, communist) into a unified national framework under his personal leadership. However, this created dangerous instability by empowering the Communist Party of Indonesia (PKI), which grew to over three million members by 1965, threatening both Islamic and military interests.\n\nEthnic and regional fragmentation posed another severe internal challenge. Indonesia comprised over 300 ethnic groups and more than 700 languages spread across thousands of islands. Regional rebellions erupted, particularly the Permesta rebellion (1958-1961) in Sumatra and Sulawesi, where military officers and regional elites resisted Jakarta's centralising authority and opposed communist influence. These rebellions demonstrated that binding the archipelago into a cohesive nation-state was extraordinarily difficult. Sukarno's response involved strengthening central authority and the military, particularly under General Suharto. He also promoted a unifying national ideology (Pancasila) and national language (Bahasa Indonesia), but these cultural measures alone could not resolve deep regional grievances about Jakarta's dominance and resource distribution. By concentrating power, Sukarno inadvertently weakened institutional checks that might have prevented the catastrophic violence of 1965-1966.\n\nEconomic crisis severely undermined Sukarno's effectiveness. The nationalist drive to expel Dutch economic interests and nationalise foreign enterprises disrupted productive capacity and alienated international investors. Ambitious development projects (the Bandung Conference in 1955 promoted non-alignment and anti-imperialism but generated costly commitments) combined with military spending to drain state finances. Inflation accelerated dramatically: the rupiah collapsed from approximately 4 per dollar in 1950 to over 600 per dollar by 1965. This impoverished ordinary Indonesians and eroded support for the government. Sukarno's \"Confrontation\" policy against Malaysia (1963-1966)—a nationalist gambit to prevent the formation of the Malaysian Federation—further strained resources without achieving its objective. His economic policies, including heavy state intervention and trade restrictions, failed to deliver development, and his reliance on loans from the Soviet Union (despite official non-alignment) mortgaged Indonesia's future without solving fundamental problems.\n\nExternally, the Cold War posed the most dangerous challenge. Despite Indonesia's leadership of the Non-Aligned Movement from 1955 onwards (hosting the Bandung Conference of Asian and African nations), Sukarno tilted increasingly towards the Soviet Union and, critically, the Chinese Communist Party. This alarmed the United States and Western powers, who feared Indonesia—with its 100 million population and strategic position controlling sea lanes—might fall into the communist bloc. The CIA covertly supported the Permesta rebellion and regional opponents of Sukarno. Sukarno's response—deepening ties with communist powers and particularly with the PKI—increased Western hostility and investment withdrawal, worsening economic crisis. His anti-Western rhetoric and anti-American posturing (withdrawing from the UN in 1965 over the admission of Malaysia) isolated Indonesia diplomatically and economically.\n\nTerritorial disputes also challenged Sukarno. The Confrontation against Malaysia (1963-1966) was rooted in Sukarno's vision of a Greater Indonesia including Malaya, but it failed to prevent Malaysian independence and federation. This humiliating strategic defeat weakened his authority. The unresolved West Irian question (though eventually resolved through UN-supervised integration in 1963) had similarly consumed nationalist energy without clear victory. These external policy failures, combined with economic deterioration, undermined the charisma and nationalist credibility on which Sukarno's personal rule depended.\n\nSukarno's attempts to resolve these problems ultimately proved ineffective or counterproductive. Guided democracy concentrated power in his hands but empowered the PKI, creating the conditions for the catastrophic 1965-1966 crisis. His nationalist foreign policy and Confrontation policy enhanced his revolutionary credentials but damaged the economy. His non-alignment rhetoric masked deepening dependence on communist powers. His cultural nationalism (Pancasila, Bahasa Indonesia) was genuinely unifying but insufficient to overcome structural problems. By 1965, Sukarno's authority rested increasingly on balancing incompatible forces—communists, Islamic movements, regionalists, the military—rather than on institutional strength or economic success. When the attempted coup of 30 September 1965 occurred (whether communist-led or engineered by anti-communist forces remains debated), these accumulated tensions exploded into mass violence that killed between 500,000 and one million people. Sukarno's inability to manage internal and external pressures effectively contributed directly to this tragedy. His regime collapsed by 1966, replaced by Suharto's authoritarian military order, indicating that Sukarno's methods—personal charisma, ideological balancing, nationalist mobilisation—could not resolve the fundamental structural problems facing the new Indonesian nation-state in a Cold War context.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
