        "question": "What were the fortunes of the Sinn Féin movement during the period 1912-1922?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "The fortunes of Sinn Féin between 1912 and 1922 underwent a dramatic transformation, from a marginalised political force to the dominant nationalist movement, fundamentally reshaping Irish politics and leading directly to the creation of the Irish Free State.\n\nIn 1912, Sinn Féin occupied a peripheral position in Irish nationalism. Founded by Arthur Griffith in 1905, it advocated a policy of abstentionism and self-reliance, rejecting participation in Westminster politics. However, it remained a fringe movement overshadowed by the Irish Parliamentary Party, which dominated nationalist representation and pursued Home Rule through constitutional means. The IPP's apparent success in securing the Third Home Rule Bill (1914) appeared to validate constitutional nationalism, leaving Sinn Féin with minimal electoral support or public influence. The party's membership was small and its electoral performance negligible; it won no seats in the 1911 general election.\n\nThe Easter Rising of 1916 proved the pivotal turning point. Although Sinn Féin members participated—notably Michael Collins and later Éamon de Valera—the rising was primarily an IRB initiative. However, the British response fundamentally altered Sinn Féin's fortunes. The execution of the rebel leaders, particularly the execution of de Valera (later reprieved), and the heavy-handed military reprisals generated massive public sympathy for the rebels. Crucially, nationalist public opinion, which had initially been hostile to the rising, swung decisively towards support for the executed rebels and for Irish independence. Sinn Féin's association with the rebels—though not entirely accurate—allowed the party to capitalise on this shift in sentiment. By 1917, Sinn Féin had transformed from political irrelevance to the primary vehicle for channelling anti-British and pro-independence feeling.\n\nThe party's revival accelerated through the 1917 by-elections. Sinn Féin won four by-elections in 1917, most notably when de Valera was elected for East Clare. These victories, against IPP candidates, demonstrated the electorate's rejection of constitutional nationalism and embrace of Sinn Féin's independence platform. The party's success reflected broader disillusionment with the IPP's failure to achieve Home Rule (suspended due to World War I) and wider revulsion against British policy, particularly conscription, which the British government attempted to impose on Ireland in 1918. Sinn Féin's vocal opposition to conscription strengthened its position as the authentic voice of Irish nationalism.\n\nThe general election of December 1918 represented the apex of Sinn Féin's political ascendancy during this period. Sinn Féin won a crushing victory, securing 73 of 105 Irish seats, while the IPP—which had dominated Irish politics for decades—was reduced to just six seats. This represented an unprecedented collapse of constitutional nationalism and an equally unprecedented endorsement of Sinn Féin's independence programme. The election result offered clear evidence that the Irish electorate had voted for a republic and for Sinn Féin as the expression of that demand.\n\nFollowing the election victory, Sinn Féin sought to consolidate its political mandate into actual independence. In January 1919, elected Sinn Féin MPs who had not been imprisoned or killed established Dáil Éireann (the Irish parliament), with de Valera as president. This represented Sinn Féin's transformation from opposition party to government-in-being. Simultaneously, Sinn Féin politicians, particularly Michael Collins, oversaw the Irish War of Independence (1919-1921), during which the Irish Republican Army prosecuted a guerrilla campaign against British forces. Sinn Féin thereby became the political wing of nationalist Ireland's struggle for independence, working in tandem with military action.\n\nHowever, Sinn Féin's fortunes became complicated and contested after the Anglo-Irish Treaty of December 1921. The treaty, negotiated by Collins and others, fell short of the republic that Sinn Féin had promised. It established the Irish Free State, a self-governing dominion within the British Commonwealth, not a fully independent republic. The treaty split the Sinn Féin movement irrevocably. De Valera and a faction of Sinn Féin opposed the treaty, viewing it as a betrayal of the republic. Collins and a majority faction supported it as a stepping stone to full independence. This schism reflected deeper tensions between pragmatism and ideological purity that had existed within Sinn Féin throughout its existence.\n\nThe split culminated in civil war (1922-1923). In the context of the civil war, Sinn Féin effectively ceased to exist as a unified force. The pro-treaty faction evolved into Cumann na nGaedheal, the government party of the Free State, while the anti-treaty faction remained as Sinn Féin but was marginalised and proscribed. By 1922, therefore, Sinn Féin's dramatic rise had ended in fragmentation and political supersession. The movement that had achieved the 1918 election triumph and provided the political framework for independence had divided irreparably over the nature and extent of that independence.\n\nIn summary, Sinn Féin's fortunes from 1912 to 1922 followed a trajectory of dramatic reversal and rise, followed by equally dramatic fragmentation. From political insignificance in 1912, it became the dominant force in Irish nationalism by 1918-1919, directly enabling the emergence of the Irish Free State. However, the movement's inability to bridge ideological and pragmatic divisions over the treaty ensured that its unity was brief and its ultimate political dominance shortlived. Its legacy was the Free State and, indirectly, the subsequent history of Irish independence, but Sinn Féin itself did not survive the period intact as a coherent political force.",
