        "question": "How did France change from being a monarchy to being a republic to being an empire during the period, 1815-1871?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "France underwent radical political transformation between 1815 and 1871, oscillating between monarchy, republic, and empire—a turbulent period reflecting deeper tensions between revolutionary ideals, monarchical legitimacy, and nationalist aspirations.\n\nThe Bourbon Restoration (1815-1830) initially reasserted monarchy after Napoleon's defeat. Louis XVIII accepted the Constitutional Charter (1814), establishing a limited monarchy with a bicameral legislature. However, this compromise satisfied neither ultraroyal forces nor liberals. The ultras, seeking to restore absolute monarchy and clerical power, gained strength after 1820 under Charles X. Their policies—including indemnification of émigrés and sacrilege laws—alienated the propertied middle classes and intellectuals. Economic distress and Charles X's attempt to bypass the Charter through the July Ordinances (1830) provoked armed revolt. The July Revolution of 1830 demonstrated popular rejection of pure monarchy and ultraconservative reaction.\n\nThe July Monarchy (1830-1848) under Louis-Philippe promised constitutional government and national sovereignty. The reformed Charter of 1830 expanded the electoral franchise and curtailed royal prerogative. Initially, this satisfied the bourgeoisie and propertied classes who dominated politics. However, Louis-Philippe's conservative foreign policy—avoiding intervention in Italian and Polish uprisings—disappointed nationalists who sought French leadership of European liberation movements. Domestically, rapid industrialisation created an urban working class excluded from political participation through high property qualifications for voting. By the 1840s, economic recession, food shortages, and pervasive corruption undermined regime legitimacy. The refusal to extend suffrage and address social misery—despite campaigns for reform—revealed the fundamental inadequacy of a narrow oligarchic monarchy.\n\nThe Revolution of 1848 abolished the July Monarchy and established the Second Republic. Universal male suffrage was introduced, reflecting the revolutionary principle that legitimate government rested on popular consent, not dynastic right. However, the Republic faced internal contradictions. Conservative rural voters, fearful of socialist agitation in Paris, elected a National Assembly dominated by monarchists and moderate republicans. Social tensions erupted in the June Days Uprising (1848), when working-class insurgents were brutally suppressed. The presidential election of December 1848 saw overwhelming victory for Louis-Napoleon Bonaparte, nephew of the Emperor, indicating public nostalgia for strong executive leadership and national glory. His election marked the Republic's weakness: ordinary citizens preferred a powerful individual promising order and greatness over republican parliamentary government.\n\nLouis-Napoleon transformed the Republic into an empire through constitutional manipulation and plebiscites—instruments of popular endorsement masking authoritarianism. The Constitution of 1852 created an elected presidency with extensive powers; Louis-Napoleon then orchestrated a coup on 2 December 1851, consolidating personal authority. A plebiscite overwhelmingly endorsed this seizure. In November 1852, another plebiscite approved his proclamation as Emperor Napoleon III, restoring the imperial title but with a different character: the Second Empire claimed to represent national will through democratic ratification, not hereditary right alone.\n\nNapoleon III's regime (1852-1870) combined authoritarian governance with nationalist foreign policy and economic modernisation. Domestically, opposition was suppressed; the legislature was subordinated to executive authority. However, Napoleon pursued projects enhancing national prestige: the Crimean War (1853-1856) reasserted French great-power status; intervention in Italian unification (1859-1860) supporting Cavour advanced nationalist causes, though French acquisition of Nice and Savoy revealed imperial self-interest. Railway expansion, urban renovation (particularly in Paris under Haussmann), and industrial development modernised France and benefited elites. By the 1860s, economic liberalisation and cautious political concessions (including press freedoms from 1860) suggested the regime might evolve toward constitutional empire.\n\nHowever, contradictions remained unresolved. The Mexican adventure (1861-1867), attempting to install an imperial puppet, ended in humiliation and troop withdrawal. Prussian expansion under Bismarck increasingly threatened French continental dominance. The Liberal Empire reforms of the 1860s, granting greater parliamentary rights and free debate, inadvertently strengthened republican and radical critics. The emperor's health declined; his son, the Prince Imperial, offered no guarantee of succession stability. Elections in 1869 showed significant republican gains despite official restrictions on campaigning.\n\nFrance's final transformation in this period came through military defeat. The Franco-Prussian War (1870-1871) exposed the empire's weaknesses: Prussian military superiority, reflected in defeats at Sedan (September 1870) and the capture of Napoleon III, shattered the regime's legitimacy. The Emperor's surrender discredited the entire system built on his authority and military reputation. A provisional government proclaimed the Third Republic on 4 September 1870, ending imperial rule.\n\nThe Paris Commune (1871) and its suppression revealed the intensity of social conflict underlying regime changes. The Third Republic, emerging from catastrophic war, faced the task of reconciling monarchists, republicans, Catholics, and emerging socialist movements—a challenge that would dominate French politics for decades.\n\nIn summary, France's evolution from monarchy (1815-1830) through constitutional monarchy (1830-1848) to republic to empire to republic reflected the incompatibility between traditional legitimacy and modern popular sovereignty. Each regime's collapse stemmed from fundamental contradictions: between liberal aspirations and authoritarian practice, between nationalist ambitions and conservative foreign policy, or between claims to represent popular will and suppression of opposition. The Franco-Prussian War ultimately resolved the cycle by destroying the empire's material and moral foundations, forcing France toward republican government as the system most compatible with national recovery and modern political legitimacy.",
