        "question": "What were the causes of World War I?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "World War I resulted from a combination of long-term structural tensions and short-term trigger events within the international system. The principal causes were militarism, alliance structures, imperial competition, and nationalist rivalries that destabilised the European balance of power between 1871 and 1914.\n\nMilitarism significantly elevated war as a feasible policy instrument. Following Germany's military success in the Franco-Prussian War (1870-71), European powers engaged in competitive arms buildups. Germany's military expenditure doubled between 1900 and 1914, while Britain maintained naval supremacy through the Dreadnought programme (launched 1906), designed to counter German naval expansion. The German Schlieffen Plan epitomised military thinking: its rigid timetable assumed war must come and dictated that Germany attack France through Belgium before Russia mobilised. These military preparations and planning created momentum toward conflict—once mobilisation began, political solutions became difficult to implement. Military leaders exercised disproportionate influence over civilian decision-makers, particularly in Germany and Austria-Hungary.\n\nThe alliance system created a framework that transformed a regional dispute into continental war. The Triple Alliance (Germany, Austria-Hungary, Italy, formalised 1882) and Triple Entente (France, Russia, Britain, consolidated by 1907) divided Europe into two competing blocs. France and Russia formed their alliance (1894) partly to contain German expansion and secure mutual support. Britain, initially detached, moved closer to France after the Entente Cordiale (1904), which resolved colonial disputes in Morocco and Egypt, and to Russia after the Anglo-Russian Convention (1907), settling Persian and Central Asian rivalries. These alliances created a logic whereby conflict between any two powers threatened to engulf the continent. When Austria-Hungary moved against Serbia in July 1914, Russia's treaty obligation to Serbia and France's commitment to Russia meant German involvement automatically triggered French entry, and German war plans required attacking France through neutral Belgium, bringing Britain in.\n\nImperial competition intensified rivalries, particularly over Morocco. The First Moroccan Crisis (1905) arose when Germany challenged French influence in Morocco; this was resolved at the Algeciras Conference (1906) but demonstrated German resentment at being excluded from colonial gains. The Second Moroccan Crisis (1911) saw Germany send a gunboat to Agadir; France responded by consolidating control. These confrontations revealed that colonial issues could destabilise the European balance. Competition for African and Asian territories divided the powers and created mutual suspicions—Britain feared German naval and commercial expansion; France resented German industrial growth; Germany felt encircled and denied its \"place in the sun.\"\n\nNationalism fuelled mutual hostility. Revanchism in France—desire to recover Alsace-Lorraine lost in 1871—made Franco-German reconciliation impossible. Pan-Slavism in Russia encouraged support for Serbia and other Slavic peoples against Austria-Hungary, complicating the Eastern Question. Austro-Serbian tensions escalated after Serbia's victories in the Balkan Wars (1912-13), which expanded Serbian territory and influence, threatening Austria-Hungary's position. Within Austria-Hungary, ethnic nationalism among Serbs, Czechs, and Poles destabilised the multinational state. German nationalism, intensified by industrial growth and military strength, generated ambitions for continental hegemony and resentment at being contained by the Entente.\n\nBalkan instability provided the immediate regional context. Ottoman decline created a power vacuum. The Balkan Wars (1912-13) saw Serbia, Bulgaria, Greece, and Romania attack Ottoman Europe; Serbia emerged strengthened and more nationalist, while Austria-Hungary felt threatened by Serbian expansion and Russian support for Serbia. These wars revealed that great powers could not control regional conflicts—Russia supported Serbia despite the risk to Austria-Hungary; Austria-Hungary could not eliminate the Serbian threat without great-power intervention. The Balkan peninsula became the flashpoint where structural tensions collided with regional volatility.\n\nThe July Crisis (1914) transformed these underlying causes into active war. Archduke Franz Ferdinand's assassination by Serbian nationalist Gavrilo Princip on 28 June 1914 gave Austria-Hungary a pretext to crush Serbian nationalism. The July Crisis unfolded as follows: Austria-Hungary issued a harsh ultimatum to Serbia (23 July) designed to be unacceptable; Serbia's response, though conciliatory on most points, was judged insufficient; Austria-Hungary declared war (28 July). Russia mobilised to support Serbia (30 July), invoking Pan-Slavic solidarity and refusing to allow Austria-Hungary to dominate the Balkans. Germany, committed by the Dual Alliance and fearing encirclement, declared war on Russia (1 August) and France (3 August), implementing the Schlieffen Plan by invading Belgium (4 August). Britain, bound by treaty to defend Belgian neutrality and alarmed by German hegemony in Europe, declared war (4 August). Within days, the alliance system had converted a regional crisis into continental war.\n\nThe causes of World War I were thus deeply rooted in European power politics. Militarism created institutional momentum toward war; alliances meant that regional conflicts could trigger general conflagration; imperialism and nationalism generated mutual resentments and fears; Balkan instability provided a volatile arena. The July Crisis revealed that these structural factors had created a system so rigid and so laden with tension that political leaders could not manage the confrontation. Once mobilisation began, the Schlieffen Plan's logic overrode diplomatic possibilities. The war resulted not from anyone's deliberate plan to conquer Europe but from a system of competitive military buildups, rigid alliances, and nationalist rivalries that left no room for the kind of compromise and restraint that had contained previous crises. The conjunction of structural causes and the Sarajevo trigger created conditions in which even cautious statesmen felt compelled to support their allies, leading inexorably to general war.",
