        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Isabella Tod was a leading figure in the women's suffrage movement in Ireland during the late nineteenth century. Born in 1836, she founded the Ulster Women's Liberal Association and became a prominent advocate for women's rights, particularly focusing on education and voting rights. Tod believed that women's suffrage was essential for broader social reform and worked tirelessly to campaign for this cause in Ulster and beyond. She was instrumental in establishing the Irish Women's Suffrage and Local Government Association, which sought to secure the franchise for women at both local and national levels. Through her writing, public speaking, and organisational work, Tod challenged the prevailing attitudes towards women's political participation. Although she did not live to see women obtain the vote in Ireland, her pioneering work laid crucial groundwork for the suffrage movement. Tod's legacy demonstrates how individual activists challenged existing social hierarchies and advanced progressive causes in the pre-1914 period. Her efforts represented the broader movement for social reform during this era, alongside Home Rule agitation and economic movements like the Co-operative Movement. Tod exemplified the diverse range of reforming initiatives that characterised the period between 1870 and 1914, showing that reform movements extended beyond purely political nationalism to encompass fundamental questions of social justice and democratic participation.",
        "diagram": "",
