        "label": "B",
        "question": "Write a short paragraph on one of the following:\n1. Irish emigrants at Grosse Isle.\n2. The Orange Order in Canada and Australia.\n3. John Devoy.\n4. Maureen O'Hara.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Grosse Isle in the St. Lawrence River became a quarantine station for Irish emigrants fleeing the Great Famine after 1845. Thousands of Irish arrived in cramped, disease-ridden ships; in 1847 alone, over 5,000 died there from typhus and cholera before reaching North America. The island was overwhelmed: makeshift hospitals, mass graves, and insufficient provisions characterised conditions. Many survivors who landed were already weakened and continued to sicken in Canadian cities. Grosse Isle symbolised the desperate circumstances driving emigration—Irish families risked the Atlantic crossing knowing quarantine stations awaited them, yet perceived even this as preferable to starvation at home. The experience was traumatic: families were separated, the sick isolated, and the dead buried in unmarked graves. Despite horror, Grosse Isle was a necessary medical measure to protect North American populations. Those who survived passed through and settled in Canada, the United States, and beyond, forming diaspora communities. Grosse Isle thus represents both the catastrophe of the Famine and the Irish determination to escape it, marking a pivotal point in the Irish diaspora story where millions left Ireland permanently.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
