        "label": "C",
        "question": "Answer one of the following:\n1. What did de Valera's visit to America (June, 1919‐December, 1921) achieve?\n2. Was the Holy Ghost Mission to Nigeria a success or a failure? Argue your case.\n3. In what ways did the career of one of the following illustrate Irish‐American experience: Boss Croker; Joe Kennedy; John F. Kennedy?\n4. How did one or more of the following promote the interests of the Catholic Church among Irish emigrants: Archbishop Daniel Mannix; Bishop Edward Galvin; Mother Mary Martin?",
        "marks": 40,
        "model": "De Valera's visit to America from June 1919 to December 1921 achieved limited but significant diplomatic and financial objectives for the Irish independence cause. He secured recognition of the Irish Republic from some American states and raised substantial funds—approximately $5 million—through bond drives and public donations, which provided crucial financial support for the War of Independence. However, his political achievements fell short of his ambitions. Despite lobbying Congress and meeting political figures, he failed to secure official US government recognition of the Irish Republic, as President Wilson prioritised maintaining US relations with Britain. De Valera's efforts to gain American diplomatic backing proved ineffective against American strategic interests. His visit did achieve important propaganda victories, generating sympathy for Irish independence among Irish-American communities and the broader American public through lectures and media engagement, which indirectly supported the Irish cause internationally. The funds raised were tangible successes that financed republican activities at a critical moment. However, the absence of formal American government support meant that his diplomatic mission ultimately disappointed expectations. His visit demonstrated both the potential and limits of Irish-American influence on US foreign policy, as emotional and financial support could be mobilised but official state recognition could not. By December 1921, when he returned to Ireland following the Anglo-Irish Treaty, he had secured the Irish diaspora's financial commitment but not the political intervention he had sought from Washington. De Valera's American visit thus achieved moderate success in fundraising and public relations but failed in its primary objective of securing American diplomatic recognition for the Irish Republic.",
