        "question": "Write a short paragraph on one of the following:\n1. Key developments in science, technology and medicine.\n2. Industrialisation and economic crisis in Tsarist Russia.\n3. Women in the workforce during World War I.\n4. Rosa Luxemburg.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Industrialisation in Tsarist Russia transformed the economy but created severe social and economic crises that destabilised the state. Russia's rapid industrial growth from the 1890s, driven by Sergei Witte's policies of foreign investment and protective tariffs, concentrated factories in St Petersburg and Moscow. However, this created a vast, impoverished industrial workforce living in overcrowded conditions with low wages and long hours—breeding grounds for revolutionary sentiment. The Trans-Siberian Railway (begun 1891) exemplified both technological ambition and the strain on state finances. Crucially, industrialisation was uneven: while heavy industry expanded, agriculture remained backward, and Russia lacked a strong middle class to stabilise society politically. The 1900-1903 industrial depression caused mass unemployment and strikes, radicalising workers. The Russo-Japanese War (1904-1905) exposed military weakness and economic fragility, triggering the 1905 Revolution. After 1905, Nicholas II's limited reforms and continued reliance on heavy industry and war spending failed to resolve underlying tensions. By 1917, the strain of World War I on an insufficiently industrialised economy—food shortages, military defeats, inflation—proved catastrophic. Thus industrialisation, pursued without corresponding political reform or social investment, deepened class conflict and economic instability rather than modernising the state, making Russia vulnerable to revolution.",
