        "question": "Write a short paragraph on one of the following:\n1. Why the US economy prospered, 1945‐1968.\n2. Urban poverty, drugs and crime.\n3. Muhammad Ali.\n4. Multiculturalism in the US.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Why the US economy prospered, 1945–1968\n\nThe US economy flourished in the postwar period due to several interconnected factors. America emerged from World War II as the sole major industrial power with its infrastructure intact, while European and Asian competitors lay in ruins. This gave US manufacturers a global monopoly in manufactured goods, generating enormous export markets and foreign investment opportunities. Domestically, the GI Bill (1944) created an educated workforce, while massive government spending on defence during the Cold War—particularly the Korean War (1950–1953) and the nuclear arms race—sustained demand for industrial production and technological innovation. The Interstate Highway System, authorised under Eisenhower in 1956, stimulated construction, automobile manufacturing, and suburban development, generating employment across multiple sectors. Consumer culture expanded dramatically as real wages rose and credit availability increased; automobile ownership, home ownership, and household appliance purchases drove sustained demand. The oil industry boomed as cheap Middle Eastern oil fuelled this consumption. Corporate profits remained high due to relatively low taxation and weak trade union competition. Technological advancement—in aviation, electronics, chemicals, and pharmaceuticals—created new industries and productivity gains. Additionally, the 'baby boom' (1945–1964) expanded the domestic market for housing, education, and consumer goods. By 1968, however, rising defence spending on the Vietnam War, inflation, and competition from recovering European and Japanese economies began to strain this prosperity. Overall, geopolitical advantage, government investment, consumer demand, technological innovation, and demographic expansion combined to create unprecedented economic growth, making the period the 'Golden Age' of American capitalism.",
