        "label": "1",
        "question": "What was the importance of one or more of the following: land agitation and land reform; the co-operative movement; industrial development in Belfast?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "The land question, co-operative movement, and Belfast's industrial development were interconnected forces that reshaped Irish society between 1870 and 1914, each addressing different but overlapping aspects of economic and social reform.\n\nLand agitation and reform proved transformative. The Great Famine had left Irish peasants deeply insecure; most were tenants without ownership rights. The Irish Land League, founded by Michael Davitt in 1879, mobilised mass resistance through the \"Plan of Campaign\" and boycott tactics (notably against Captain Boycott in 1880), linking agrarian grievances to nationalist politics. This agitation forced legislative action. Gladstone's Land Acts (1881, 1891) established fair rent courts, fixity of tenure, and free sale—the \"Three Fs\"—fundamentally altering landlord-tenant relations. The 1903 Wyndham Act accelerated this by enabling tenant purchase through state-aided loans, gradually transferring land ownership from Protestant landlords to Catholic peasant proprietors. By 1914, the landscape had shifted decisively: owner-occupancy rose from negligible levels in 1870 to substantial proportions. This was crucial not merely economically but politically: it undermined the old ascendancy's power base, strengthened Catholic farmer prosperity and confidence, and created a new rural middle class invested in constitutional nationalism. Land reform thus stabilised rural society and enabled agricultural modernisation.\n\nThe co-operative movement, pioneered by Horace Plunkett, complemented land reform by addressing production and marketing. Plunkett founded the Irish Agricultural Organisation Society (1894), promoting co-operative creameries, dairies, and supply societies. By 1914, hundreds of co-operatives existed, with the Dairy Industry standing out as a success: co-operative creameries increased butter exports substantially and brought economies of scale to small farmers. This was vital because smallholdings alone—the outcome of land reform—could lack viability; co-operation allowed fragmented producers to compete in wider markets. The movement also embodied Plunkett's vision of rural development without emigration, promoting education, credit facilities, and organised marketing. The Irish Co-operative Wholesale Society (ICWS) coordinated these efforts. However, the movement's scope remained limited—it relied on voluntary participation and faced resistance from traditional merchants and some clergy suspicious of Plunkett's non-sectarian approach. Yet it demonstrated that Irish agriculture could modernise and that cooperation, not conflict, could drive rural prosperity, influencing policymakers and offering an alternative to purely political agitation.\n\nBelfast's industrial development represented a contrasting reform pathway—not agrarian but urban and industrial. Belfast transformed dramatically after 1870, becoming the industrial heartland of Ireland, driven by shipbuilding, engineering, and textiles. Harland and Wolff shipyard, founded 1861, expanded massively; by 1914 it employed over 35,000 workers and was the world's largest shipyard, building the Titanic (1912). Linen manufacturing also thrived: Belfast became the global linen centre, with hundreds of mills. This created employment at unprecedented scale and attracted workers, swelling Belfast's population from c. 120,000 (1870) to c. 387,000 (1911). However, this industrialisation embodied significant tensions. It was concentrated in Protestant east Belfast; Catholic workers were largely excluded from skilled positions in shipbuilding, confined to labouring roles. This economic segregation reinforced sectarian divisions. Moreover, while wages rose, conditions were often brutal; the 1907 strike among carters and dock workers, brutally suppressed, revealed labour exploitation. Industrial development thus brought prosperity but also deepened communal divides and labour grievances.\n\nThe importance of these movements lay partly in their interconnection. Land reform created a stable, prosperous rural base; co-operation modernised production; industrial growth absorbed rural surplus population and generated urban wealth. Together they represented Ireland's economic maturation—moving beyond subsistence to market integration. Yet they operated unevenly: land reform benefited Catholics disproportionately, solidifying their economic position; industrialisation enriched mainly Protestants in Ulster. This asymmetry mattered profoundly by 1914, when these economic transformations interacted with political nationalism. A prosperous Catholic farming class, empowered by land ownership and co-operative success, increasingly demanded political voice, fuelling Home Rule support. Conversely, Protestant industrial workers and employers in Belfast feared Catholic-majority Home Rule would damage their economic interests. Thus these reform movements, while modernising Ireland, paradoxically sharpened sectarian economic interests and contributed to the political polarisation that would erupt in 1914-1921.\n\nIn conclusion, the land question proved most immediately transformative: it directly addressed the core source of rural grievance and misery, secured constitutional reform, and created a new propertied class. The co-operative movement, though more limited, demonstrated sustainable agricultural modernisation. Belfast's industrialisation created wealth but deepened sectional divisions. Together, these movements reflected Irish society's capacity for reform and modernisation between 1870 and 1914, yet also revealed the fragility of that progress when economic transformation intersected with unresolved questions of nationality and political power.",
