        "label": "2",
        "question": "Would you agree that Charles Stewart Parnell's achievements and failures were both significant? Explain your answer.",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Charles Stewart Parnell's career between 1877 and 1891 was defined by both substantial achievements and consequential failures, making him a figure whose impact cannot be understood without acknowledging both dimensions.\n\nParnell's achievements were genuinely significant. He transformed Irish nationalism from a fragmented movement into a disciplined, cohesive force capable of exercising real power at Westminster. On entering Parliament in 1877, he inherited a tradition of constitutional nationalism that had achieved little; through his leadership of the Irish Parliamentary Party from 1880, he made Irish Home Rule impossible to ignore. His most tangible achievement was forcing the British government to engage seriously with land reform. The Land League, which he championed, mobilised rural Ireland around the \"three Fs\"—fair rent, fixity of tenure, and free sale. This agitation, combined with Parnell's parliamentary strategy, pressured Gladstone to introduce the Land Act of 1881, which granted most of these demands. This was a concrete victory: tenant farmers gained statutory protection, marking a real shift in their relationship with landlords. The Act did not resolve the land question entirely, but it represented the most substantial legislative achievement for Irish interests in a generation.\n\nParnell's organisational achievement was equally significant. He imposed unprecedented discipline on the Irish Parliamentary Party, reducing it from a loose coalition of 60 independent MPs to a tightly controlled bloc of 86 members by 1885. This \"party within a party\" at Westminster became a formidable force; the Irish vote could no longer be taken for granted by either Liberals or Conservatives. In 1885-86, Parnell's leverage forced Gladstone towards Home Rule, achieving the first serious commitment from a major British party to Irish self-government. The First Home Rule Bill of 1886, though defeated, was a watershed: the question of Irish independence was now on the agenda of British politics as a mainstream issue. This was Parnell's strategic victory—not the passage of Home Rule (he failed to deliver that), but the establishment of it as a real political objective.\n\nParnell also demonstrated astute political flexibility. His support for the Kilmainham Treaty of 1882, whereby he was released from prison in exchange for moderating land agitation, showed pragmatism rather than rigidity. His evolution from Land League agitation towards constitutionalism and parliamentary strategy proved that he could read political conditions and shift tactics accordingly. By the mid-1880s, he had become a statesman figure, received in respectable society and treated as a serious negotiating partner by Gladstone himself.\n\nHowever, Parnell's failures were equally significant. Most critically, he failed to deliver Home Rule. This was not merely a missed opportunity but a fundamental failure of his life's work. The Home Rule Bill of 1886 collapsed, and subsequent Bills of 1893 and beyond remained stalled. Parnell died in 1891 without achieving the central objective around which he had organised Irish nationalism. Given that the entire point of his political strategy was to extract Home Rule from Westminster, this failure cannot be minimised. He had raised expectations to an unprecedented pitch; their disappointment was deeply demoralising to Irish nationalists.\n\nMore fatally, Parnell's personal failures destroyed his political achievement. The revelation of his affair with Katharine O'Shea, cited in the 1890 divorce proceedings, devastated his standing. The Irish Catholic hierarchy turned against him, denouncing him from pulpits. The Irish Parliamentary Party itself split, with 45 MPs following him and 71 rejecting him. Within months of the scandal becoming public, Parnell's authority collapsed. His attempt to maintain leadership during the North Kilkenny and Carlow by-elections proved disastrous; his preferred candidates lost badly, demonstrating that his personal appeal no longer translated into political power. His death in October 1891, just a year after the scandal broke, left Irish nationalism traumatised and fractured. The split lasted until 1900, gravely weakening the nationalist position and delaying Home Rule for another generation.\n\nThis personal failure was not incidental to his political legacy—it was catastrophic for it. Parnell had built everything on his own authority and charisma; when that was undermined, the structure collapsed. He had no institutional succession plan. His departure from politics was therefore not merely a personal tragedy but a strategic disaster: the momentum towards Home Rule dissipated, and the reunified party of later years, while eventually successful, operated in a different political context entirely.\n\nParnell's failures extended beyond personal scandal. His handling of the divorce crisis itself was strategically inept. Rather than stepping back temporarily to allow the movement to weather the storm, he fought on, insisting his leadership was essential. This intransigence, while displaying courage, was politically catastrophic. A more flexible approach—accepting temporary retirement—might have preserved his movement's coherence. His failure to anticipate or manage the clerical opposition was also significant; he appeared surprised and unable to navigate the Church's power within Irish society.\n\nTherefore, agreement with the proposition must be qualified: both Parnell's achievements and failures were significant, but they were of different kinds. His achievements were structural and tangible—land reform, party discipline, elevation of Home Rule to mainstream British politics. His failures were both strategic (failure to secure Home Rule) and catastrophic (personal and political disintegration). The significance lies not in weighing one against the other, but in recognising that Parnell's career demonstrates how dramatically circumstances can reverse even a powerful political achievement. He transformed Irish nationalism, but he also shattered it. Both aspects define his historical significance.",
