        "label": "3",
        "question": "What was the contribution of James Connolly and/or James Larkin to the Irish labour movement?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "James Connolly and James Larkin were the two most significant figures in transforming the Irish labour movement from a marginalised force into a powerful organised movement capable of challenging both employers and the British state. Their distinct contributions—Connolly's ideological vision and Larkin's militant organising genius—combined to reshape Irish labour between 1896 and 1916.\n\nJames Larkin arrived in Ireland in 1907 as a union organiser and immediately revolutionised labour tactics through mass mobilisation and confrontational industrial action. In Belfast, he organised the port workers across sectarian lines, achieving a significant strike in 1907 that forced wage increases. Crucially, Larkin recognised that Irish labour's weakness lay in fragmentation and craft exclusivity. His approach was to organise all workers—skilled and unskilled—into one powerful general union, the Irish Transport and General Workers' Union (ITGWU), which he founded in 1909. This represented a fundamental break with the prevailing British model where craft unions remained separate. By 1913, the ITGWU had grown to 10,000 members, making it the strongest organisation in Ireland.\n\nLarkin's most significant contribution was demonstrating labour's capacity for sustained mass action. The Dublin lockout of 1913 was the defining moment. When William Murphy, a major employer, locked out his workers who refused to leave the ITGWU, Larkin organised a sympathetic response that paralysed Dublin's transport sector and spread to general workers across the city. Over 25,000 workers were locked out; Larkin's rallies attracted tens of thousands of supporters. Though the lockout ultimately ended in defeat in early 1914 through worker exhaustion and lack of funds, it permanently changed the political landscape. Larkin demonstrated that Irish workers possessed class consciousness and would suffer significant hardship rather than submit to employers' demands. The lockout established labour as a political force that could not be ignored. Larkin's fiery oratory and fearless confrontation with authority—he was imprisoned multiple times—made him a legendary figure who inspired working-class mobilisation across Ireland.\n\nJames Connolly's contribution was primarily ideological and organisational, though less immediately visible than Larkin's dramatic strikes. Connolly arrived in Ireland in 1896 and immediately began establishing socialist organisations. He founded the Irish Socialist Republican Party in 1896, which remained small but significant as the first explicitly socialist party in Ireland. Unlike Larkin, who focused on immediate industrial gains, Connolly linked labour struggle to broader political and national questions. His writings and speeches articulated a vision of labour as the vanguard of social revolution. Crucially, Connolly argued that Irish independence and workers' emancipation were inseparable—that an Irish republic liberated by British imperialism would be meaningless unless it was also a workers' republic.\n\nConnolly's organisational impact came through his work with the Socialist Party of Ireland and his collaboration with the ITGWU. During the 1913 lockout, Connolly was Larkin's second-in-command and helped sustain the workers' resistance through public speaking and by establishing relief networks. More significantly, Connolly articulated the lockout's political meaning: this was not merely an industrial dispute but a clash between labour and capital that revealed the necessity of fundamental social transformation.\n\nConnolly's most enduring contribution was linking labour activism to the independence struggle. When Larkin departed Ireland for America in 1914, Connolly became the dominant labour leader. He transformed the Irish Citizen Army—initially a workers' defence force established during the lockout—into a disciplined paramilitary organisation. Connolly's decision to involve the Citizen Army in the 1916 Easter Rising (he was a signatory of the Proclamation and military commander of the rising's contingent) demonstrated his conviction that labour struggle and national struggle must be unified. Though controversial among some labour figures, this decisively shaped Irish labour's political identity: the labour movement became inseparable from republicanism.\n\nTheir combined legacy redefined Irish labour's position. Before 1907, Irish labour was fragmented, subordinate to British unions, and without distinct political voice. Trade union density was low, and workers lacked organisational capacity for major industrial action. By 1913-1916, Irish labour had demonstrated capacity for mass strikes, established itself as a political force, articulated a distinctive ideology linking labour to national liberation, and created permanent institutions (the ITGWU and trade union councils) that would dominate Irish labour for generations.\n\nThe contributions differed but complemented each other. Larkin provided the energy, tactical brilliance, and mass mobilisation that created a real labour movement. His genius lay in recognising that Irish workers possessed enormous latent power if properly organised, and he proved it through the 1913 lockout. Connolly provided the ideological framework that transformed labour from a purely economic interest into a political force with a vision of social transformation. He ensured labour remained connected to the independence struggle.\n\nBy 1916, Irish labour had been transformed. The ITGWU was a powerful force representing thousands of workers. The labour movement had demonstrated its capacity to challenge major employers and survive sustained conflict. Most importantly, labour had established itself as a distinct political force with its own ideology, its own armed force (the Citizen Army), and its own vision for Ireland's future. Without Larkin's organisational genius and mass mobilisation, labour would have remained marginal. Without Connolly's ideological vision and political linking of labour to national liberation, the movement would have lacked direction beyond immediate wage claims. Together, they created the modern Irish labour movement.",
