        "label": "1",
        "question": "How did the Sinn Féin movement develop during the period 1912–1923?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Sinn Féin's development between 1912 and 1923 underwent three distinct and interconnected phases: early political formation, revolutionary transformation following 1916, and consolidation as a nationalist political force.\n\nOriginally founded in 1905 by Arthur Griffith, Sinn Féin initially pursued a dual-monarchy policy and cultural nationalist agenda centred on promoting Irish language, industry and self-sufficiency. By 1912, the movement remained relatively marginal in Irish politics, overshadowed by the Irish Parliamentary Party's dominance and the constitutional Home Rule campaigns. Griffith's original Sinn Féin emphasised non-violent resistance and economic independence through a policy of withdrawing from Westminster and establishing an alternative Irish parliament.\n\nThe 1916 Easter Rising marked a fundamental turning point. Although Sinn Féin as an organisation did not directly orchestrate the rebellion—it was led by the Irish Republican Brotherhood and Irish Citizen Army—the aftermath transformed Sinn Féin's political fortunes. British executions of the Rising's leaders, including Pearse, MacDonagh and Connolly, generated massive public sympathy. Sinn Féin, as the most visible nationalist organisation not tainted by Parliamentary Party compromise, benefited enormously from this nationalist surge. The movement absorbed survivors and relatives of executed rebels, including Michael Collins, Éamon de Valera and Constance Markievicz.\n\nBetween 1917 and 1918, Sinn Féin underwent radical reorganisation and ideological shift. De Valera became president in October 1917, replacing Griffith. The party's 1917 Ard Fheis adopted a more explicitly republican platform, moving beyond Griffith's dual-monarchy concept towards a commitment to a fully independent republic. Crucially, Sinn Féin now championed armed struggle as legitimate, absorbing IRB volunteers and incorporating military republicans into its structures. The party's 1918 election manifesto explicitly endorsed the Easter Rising and promised to establish an independent Irish republic.\n\nThe December 1918 general election proved decisive. Sinn Féin won 73 of 105 Irish seats, delivering a stunning repudiation of the Parliamentary Party, which won only 6 seats. This electoral mandate—secured despite British wartime restrictions and violence—legitimised Sinn Féin as the authentic voice of Irish nationalism. Sinn Féin TDs (elected members) refused to recognise Westminster and instead constituted themselves as Dáil Éireann (the Irish parliament) in January 1919, with de Valera as president.\n\nDuring the War of Independence (1919–1921), Sinn Féin functioned as both political movement and organisational umbrella for armed resistance. The IRA, nominally subordinate to Dáil authority, conducted guerrilla warfare under Michael Collins's direction. Sinn Féin provided political legitimacy for the military campaign and administered rudimentary civilian governance through Dáil courts and local administration in areas where British authority had collapsed. The movement's dual function—political legitimacy and military coordination—proved essential to sustaining the struggle.\n\nThe Anglo-Irish Treaty of December 1921 fractured Sinn Féin irrevocably. The party split over whether the treaty's dominion status satisfied nationalist demands. De Valera opposed the treaty, favouring continued resistance to achieve a republic; Collins and others accepted it as the best achievable settlement. The January 1922 Dáil vote narrowly approved the treaty (64–57), but this formal political decision masked deeper organisational rupture. Sinn Féin effectively ceased functioning as a unified movement.\n\nDuring the Irish Civil War (1922–1923), the term 'Sinn Féin' became ideologically contested. Pro-treaty forces controlled the new Irish Free State government and claimed governmental legitimacy. Anti-treaty republicans, led by de Valera and Liam Lynch, rejected both the treaty and Free State legitimacy, insisting they represented the true Sinn Féin tradition. However, anti-treaty forces lacked the political-military cohesion Sinn Féin had previously embodied. By the Civil War's end in May 1923, anti-treaty forces were militarily defeated, and de Valera briefly imprisoned.\n\nBy 1923, Sinn Féin had transformed from a minor early-century political movement into a mass nationalist force capable of unseating the Parliamentary Party, securing electoral endorsement for independence, and sustaining armed struggle. Yet this very success contained the seeds of fragmentation. The movement's internal tensions—between constitutional and revolutionary approaches, between Griffith's economic nationalism and republican maximalism, between political and military authority—could not survive the treaty decision. The unified Sinn Féin of 1918–1921 splintered into competing factions claiming its legacy: the Free State government, de Valera's republican opposition (which eventually reconstituted as Fianna Fáil in 1926), and various republican splinter groups. Sinn Féin's development thus culminates not in stable institutional consolidation but in ideological and organisational dissolution, though its brief period of dominance (1917–1921) fundamentally redirected Irish politics toward independence and republicanism.",
