        "label": "2",
        "question": "Why were both the negotiations and the terms of the Anglo-Irish Treaty, 1921, controversial?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "The Anglo-Irish Treaty of 1921 proved controversial in both its negotiation and terms because it represented a compromise settlement that satisfied neither Irish republicanism nor British imperial interests, while triggering a bitter civil war that fractured the Irish independence movement.\n\nNEGOTIATIONS: The treaty was negotiated under conditions that inflamed nationalist opinion. Michael Collins and Arthur Griffith led the Irish delegation with ambiguous instructions from the Dáil: they were mandated to achieve a republic but told they could exercise discretion. British Prime Minister Lloyd George issued an ultimatum on 5 December 1921, threatening \"immediate and terrible war\" if Ireland rejected dominion status. This ultimatum became widely publicized and created the impression that Irish negotiators had capitulated under duress. Collins accepted dominion status rather than a republic—a fundamental compromise on the national question. The secrecy surrounding negotiations and the limited consultation with the Dáil before the treaty was signed fuelled suspicion that Collins and Griffith had exceeded their mandate. The Irish delegation negotiated as representatives of a de facto state with limited leverage against a major imperial power, yet they were accountable to a revolutionary parliament that had committed itself to a republic. De Valera's absence from the delegation was controversial; his decision to remain in Dublin while Collins and Griffith negotiated meant the president was not directly involved in what became the defining agreement of the independence struggle. The composition of the delegation—pragmatists like Collins rather than uncompromising republicans—suggested a predetermined acceptance of dominion status, which alienated those who believed any compromise on the republic was betrayal.\n\nTERMS: The treaty's substantive provisions were deeply contentious. Dominion status, not a republic, was the core of Irish independence—a fundamental breach with the declared objective of the 1916 Rising and the Dáil's 1919 Declaration of Independence. While dominion status granted Ireland control of domestic affairs and the right to develop its own foreign policy, it implicitly retained the British Crown as head of state, a symbol many republicans regarded as incompatible with genuine independence. Irish independence was conditional on accepting the monarchy; this compromise undermined the ideological legitimacy of the settlement for republicans. The Oath of Allegiance to the Crown was particularly inflammatory. All members of the Irish parliament and public officials had to swear an oath to the King, a requirement that seemed to negate the all-Ireland republic for which thousands had fought. For de Valera and his supporters, the oath was an unacceptable humiliation that perpetuated British dominance. The treaty partitioned Ireland: it recognized the existing Home Rule parliament in Northern Ireland and granted it the right to opt out of the new Irish Free State. Article 12 allowed the Northern Irish parliament six months to choose to remain in the UK, which it did. This formalized partition—ratifying the 1920 Government of Ireland Act—and handed over six northeastern counties to continued British rule. Nationalists felt betrayed; they had expected a unified independent state, not formal partition. The loss of the six counties meant that Irish independence was territorially incomplete and left a substantial nationalist population under British jurisdiction. The financial settlement was also controversial. Ireland was obliged to assume a share of the British national debt, estimated at around £17 million, a burden many saw as unjust reparation for centuries of colonial exploitation. This debt was widely perceived as punitive and constrained the new state's resources for development. The treaty granted Britain significant strategic concessions: British forces could maintain ports and naval facilities in Irish territory (the Treaty Ports), giving Britain the ability to project military power from Irish soil. This formal retention of British military presence on Irish territory seemed inconsistent with a genuinely independent nation and gave Britain potential leverage over Irish foreign policy. These ports became symbols of incomplete sovereignty and remained a source of friction until the late 1930s.\n\nThe terms also raised questions about legitimacy and procedure. The treaty was presented to the Dáil in December 1921 with strong pressure to accept it; the two-day debate was intense. De Valera argued that accepting dominion status betrayed the republic and the sacrifice of those who had died for it. The Dáil approved the treaty by a narrow margin (64 to 57), splitting the chamber almost evenly and revealing deep division within Irish republicanism. This narrow majority made the treaty constitutionally contentious in the eyes of its opponents, who argued that such a fundamental departure from the republic required overwhelming consensus. The treaty was ratified as the Constitution of the Irish Free State in 1922, but anti-treaty forces, led by de Valera, rejected its legitimacy. Within months, the Irish Civil War erupted, pitting pro-treaty forces (who accepted the treaty as a step toward full independence) against anti-treaty republicans (who saw it as betrayal). The civil war lasted until 1923 and killed more people than the War of Independence itself, demonstrating how deeply controversial the treaty was within Irish nationalism. The treaty fractured the IRA and Sinn Féin, turning former allies into bitter enemies and causing lasting divisions in Irish politics that persisted for decades. Pro-treaty supporters argued the treaty was the best achievable under the circumstances and could be used as a stepping stone to fuller independence (a position vindicated when the Free State evolved toward a republic in the 1930s-1949). Anti-treaty republicans insisted that principle could not be compromised and that accepting dominion status and partition betrayed the entire independence struggle. The controversy was ultimately resolved only in 1949, when Ireland declared itself a republic outside the Commonwealth, removing the crown symbolism that had made the treaty unacceptable to republicans but requiring a further 28 years of political division to achieve.\n\nIn summary, the treaty was controversial because it represented a compromise settlement that violated the core nationalist objective of a united republic, was negotiated under duress and with limited consultation, and partitioned the island while retaining British military and institutional presence. It satisfied neither British strategic interests nor Irish nationalist aspirations, triggering immediate civil war and poisoning Irish politics for a generation.",
