        "label": "2",
        "question": "What problems faced France in the years 1920–1945, and to what extent were these problems overcome?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "France faced profound economic, political and security challenges between 1920 and 1945, stemming from World War I and exacerbated by global crisis. The extent to which these problems were overcome remains limited, with fundamental instability persisting throughout the period.\n\nEconomic instability was a defining problem. France emerged from World War I with massive war debts and physical devastation in the north. Reconstruction was costly and slow. The franc depreciated dramatically in the early 1920s, creating inflation that eroded purchasing power and savings. This instability was partly addressed by the Dawes Plan (1924) and French occupation of the Ruhr (1923), which temporarily stabilised finances, but France remained economically vulnerable. The Wall Street Crash (1929) and subsequent global depression exposed these underlying weaknesses. Unemployment rose sharply after 1930. Agricultural prices collapsed, devastating rural France. Industrial production fell significantly. Deflationary policies under successive governments actually worsened the depression rather than alleviating it. The franc's final devaluation came only in 1936 under the Popular Front, very late compared to other nations. Economic recovery was therefore only partial and came too late; France remained economically weaker than Germany by 1939.\n\nPolitical instability represented a second major problem. The Third Republic was characterised by chronic ministerial instability—there were seventeen governments between 1920 and 1940, making coherent long-term policy impossible. No government commanded stable majority support. The Chamber of Deputies remained deeply fractionalised between left and right. This instability was partly overcome during Poincaré's administration (1926–1929), which achieved relative political consensus and fiscal stabilisation through orthodox economic measures and restored confidence in the franc. However, the depression shattered this consensus. By the 1930s, political polarisation intensified dangerously. The rise of fascist leagues (Croix de Feu, Action Française) threatened republican institutions. The Stavisky scandal (1934) and subsequent riots demonstrated how fragile political legitimacy had become. The Popular Front victory (1936) under Léon Blum offered radical solutions—the forty-hour week, collective bargaining, nationalisation—but provoked fierce conservative opposition and ultimately deepened rather than resolved divisions. By 1940, the political system had collapsed entirely, with the National Assembly voting full powers to Pétain. Political problems thus were not overcome; they intensified catastrophically.\n\nSecondary problems included social tension and class conflict. The working class faced unemployment and hardship, particularly after 1930. The Popular Front's reforms (paid holidays, wage increases) were significant but short-lived and provoked a capital flight crisis. Strikes and industrial unrest destabilised production. Agricultural depression created rural discontent, pushing farmers toward extremist politics. These tensions were never adequately resolved; they festered and contributed to the collapse of social consensus by 1940.\n\nSecurity and defence presented an existential problem, intimately connected to Germany. France needed security against German resurgence but faced a strategic dilemma: the Treaty of Versailles (1919) had weakened Germany temporarily but created lasting resentment. During the 1920s, the Locarno Treaties (1925) and Kellogg-Briand Pact (1928) created an illusion of security through international cooperation. However, this security framework collapsed with German rearmament under Hitler (1935 onwards). France's military doctrine remained defensive (exemplified by the Maginot Line, begun 1930), while Germany pursued aggressive rearmament and expansion. The Remilitarisation of the Rhineland (1936) violated Versailles, yet France took no military action. The Anschluss (1938) and Munich crisis (1938) further exposed French weakness. By September 1939, France was forced into war unprepared psychologically and strategically. The subsequent defeat in 1940 proved catastrophic. France's security problems were not overcome; they culminated in military collapse and occupation.\n\nCultural and intellectual problems also afflicted France. There was a pervasive sense of national decline and loss of confidence in republican values. Intellectuals and artists debated France's future anxiously. The memory of 1914–1918 created pacifist sentiment in parts of society, making rearmament politically difficult. Anti-parliamentary sentiment grew among intellectuals and the right. This cultural-political malaise was never adequately addressed; instead, it deepened into defeatism by 1940.\n\nIn evaluating the extent to which problems were overcome, the evidence is predominantly negative. Some specific problems were partially addressed: economic stabilisation occurred briefly (1926–1929), and important social reforms were enacted (Popular Front). However, these improvements were temporary and fragile. More fundamentally, the core problems—economic structural weakness, political fragmentation, and German military threat—were not overcome. Instead, they accumulated and intersected catastrophically. By 1940, France faced simultaneous military defeat, political collapse, and economic crisis. The Vichy régime that followed represented not a solution but an admission of comprehensive failure.\n\nThe fundamental issue was that France's problems were interconnected and international in character. Economic stability required political consensus and peaceful international conditions, neither of which materialised. Political stability required economic success and security; neither was assured. Security required sustained rearmament and strong alliances; France lacked the economic base and political will for sustained rearmament, and British alliance proved insufficient. The problems of 1920 were never truly overcome because they were rooted in France's diminished relative position in European affairs after 1918. German resurgence, whether through economic recovery or rearmament, was always likely to destabilise the European order that Versailles had imposed. France could not solve these structural problems through internal reform alone. The defeat of 1940 represented the ultimate failure to overcome the challenges of the 1920–1945 period.",
