        "question": "Why was there an uprising in Hungary in 1956 and why did the Soviet Union take steps to crush it?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "The Hungarian Uprising of 1956 emerged from a combination of nationalist resentment, economic grievance, and political reform within the Soviet bloc, while Soviet intervention reflected Moscow's determination to maintain ideological and strategic control over Eastern Europe.\n\nThe roots of Hungarian discontent lay in the Soviet domination established after World War II. Hungary, despite being liberated from Nazi occupation, found itself firmly within the Soviet sphere of influence under the Warsaw Pact (signed 1955). The Stalinist policies imposed on Hungary under Mátyás Rákosi created widespread resentment. Rákosi's regime pursued forced collectivization of agriculture, heavy industrialization, and strict political control, mirroring Soviet communism. These policies caused economic hardship, food shortages, and disrupted traditional Hungarian life. The secret police (ÁVO) enforced compliance through terror, imprisoning and executing political opponents and intellectuals. By the mid-1950s, Hungary's economy was strained, and living standards had declined significantly compared to pre-war conditions.\n\nThe death of Stalin in 1953 and Nikita Khrushchev's rise prompted a thaw in Soviet policy. Khrushchev's 1956 'Secret Speech' at the Twentieth Party Congress denounced Stalin's crimes and suggested a more liberal approach within communist states. This speech, though intended to consolidate Khrushchev's power, inadvertently encouraged Eastern European communists to believe limited reform was possible. In Hungary, these signals emboldened intellectuals, students, and reform-minded communists to push for genuine change. The Hungarian Writers' Union became a forum for nationalist and anti-Soviet sentiment, publishing criticism of Rákosi's regime and Soviet domination. Rákosi was removed as First Secretary in July 1956, replaced by Ernő Gerő, but Gerő maintained hardline policies, disappointing reformers.\n\nNationalism was a powerful force driving the uprising. Hungary had a strong historical identity and tradition of resistance to foreign domination, particularly against Austrian Habsburg rule in the nineteenth century. Many Hungarians viewed Soviet control as a new form of imperial oppression. The memory of 1848-49, when Hungarian patriots under Lajos Kossuth had rebelled against Habsburg rule, resonated deeply. The presence of Soviet troops, the Cyrillic characters in Budapest streets, and the requirement to learn Russian in schools all symbolized foreign domination. Hungarians sought genuine independence and the right to determine their own political system, not merely reforms within Soviet communism.\n\nThe immediate trigger came on 23 October 1956. A peaceful student demonstration in Budapest, initially supporting Polish reformer Władysław Gomułka's stand against Soviet pressure, rapidly escalated. Protesters demanded the withdrawal of Soviet troops, free elections, and the return of Imre Nagy, a reform communist, to power. The crowd, numbering tens of thousands, stormed the radio station and toppled the Stalin statue, powerful symbolic acts of defiance. When Soviet tanks moved in, the protests transformed into armed uprising. Workers' councils formed, providing organization and legitimacy to the revolt beyond mere student activism.\n\nImre Nagy's appointment as Prime Minister on 24 October 1956 initially appeared to address grievances. Nagy, a moderate communist who believed reform could occur within socialism, moved to liberalize Hungary's system. He promised free elections, allowed non-communist parties to form, and promised withdrawal of Soviet troops. For a brief moment (28 October - 3 November), it seemed the Soviet Union might tolerate Hungarian independence. Soviet tanks temporarily withdrew from Budapest. However, Nagy went further than Moscow would accept. By late October, he announced Hungary's withdrawal from the Warsaw Pact and declared Hungary neutral, requesting United Nations intervention. This crossed the critical line for Soviet leadership.\n\nKhrushchev and the Soviet leadership could not permit Hungary's defection from the Warsaw Pact. Strategic considerations were paramount. Hungary was not a peripheral satellite state but lay in the heart of Eastern Europe, bordering Austria and in proximity to Soviet borders. If Hungary successfully left the Soviet bloc, the example would be catastrophic for Soviet control elsewhere. East Germany, Poland, Czechoslovakia, and other satellites might follow, collapsing the entire Soviet buffer zone established after World War II and essential to Soviet security doctrine. The Warsaw Pact, created in 1955 to bind Eastern Europe militarily to the Soviet Union, would be rendered meaningless. Furthermore, Hungary's exit threatened to align it with Western Europe and NATO, directly strengthening Soviet adversaries on the USSR's western border.\n\nIdeologically, the Kremlin could not tolerate a non-communist Hungary. Despite Khrushchev's denunciation of Stalin's methods, Soviet leadership remained committed to communism's global spread and to maintaining communist hegemony in the sphere they controlled. A democratic, multi-party Hungary would constitute a fundamental rejection of communist ideology and Soviet leadership of the communist world, with implications for communist parties everywhere.\n\nSoviet decision-making crystallized in early November 1956. The Suez Crisis, which erupted simultaneously, creating a distraction for Western powers, may have emboldened Soviet action. On 4 November, the Soviet Union launched a massive military intervention. Over 200,000 Soviet troops, equipped with tanks and artillery, invaded Hungary. The uprising, poorly armed with small weapons and Molotov cocktails, was crushed within days. Hundreds died, thousands were wounded, and some 200,000 Hungarians fled westward, creating a refugee crisis. Nagy was captured, tried, and executed in 1958, becoming a martyr for Hungarian nationalism.\n\nIn conclusion, the 1956 Hungarian Uprising reflected the incompatibility between Hungarian nationalism and Soviet imperial control, exacerbated by economic hardship and encouraged by Khrushchev's reformist signals. The Soviet Union crushed the uprising because Hungary's departure from the Warsaw Pact and communism threatened the strategic integrity of the Soviet bloc and Soviet ideological authority. The uprising demonstrated that consent for Soviet domination could not be assumed, and that Eastern European nationalism remained a potent force that Moscow could suppress only through military force.",
