        "label": "2",
        "question": "What problems were caused by the withdrawal from Africa of one or more of the following: Britain; Belgium; Portugal?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "The withdrawal of European powers from Africa between 1945 and 1990 created profound problems that destabilized the continent and reverberated internationally.\n\nBritain's decolonization in Africa, while generally orderly compared to other powers, nonetheless created significant difficulties. In Kenya, the Mau Mau Uprising (1952-1960) preceded independence in 1963, leaving deep ethnic and political divisions. Britain granted independence to many territories with inadequate preparation for self-governance: Ghana (1957), Nigeria (1960), Tanganyika (1961), Uganda (1962), and Zambia (1964). The rapid transfer of power created institutional vacuums. Nigeria exemplified these problems acutely. Upon independence in 1960, Nigeria inherited weak democratic institutions and tribal divisions—the Igbo, Yoruba, and Hausa-Fulani competed for dominance. The Western Region's political crisis in 1962-1963 destabilized the federation. These tensions culminated in the military coup of January 1966, followed by the Biafran War (1967-1970), in which approximately one million people died from combat and starvation. Britain's withdrawal had left a state structurally fragile and ethnically fractious. Similarly, in Kenya, independence left unresolved land disputes and inequalities between the Kikuyu-dominated government and other ethnic groups, creating long-term grievances. Uganda's independence in 1962 under Milton Obote preceded years of instability, military rule under Idi Amin (1971-1979), and the deaths of approximately 300,000 people. Britain's hasty withdrawal without addressing underlying ethnic and institutional weaknesses thus contributed directly to post-independence violence and authoritarianism.\n\nBelgium's withdrawal from the Congo was catastrophically abrupt and ill-prepared, creating one of Africa's gravest post-colonial crises. Belgium had governed the Congo with minimal preparation of Africans for independence, and independence was granted on 30 June 1960 with almost no transition planning. The Congo immediately descended into chaos. On 5 July 1960, the Force Publique (the colonial army) mutinied, committing atrocities against Belgian settlers and destabilizing state authority. Prime Minister Patrice Lumumba appealed to the United Nations, and Belgium intervened militarily, allegedly to protect its citizens but effectively to reassert control. The secession of Katanga province under Moise Tshombe on 11 July 1960 threatened the Congo's territorial integrity and economic viability—Katanga contained the copper wealth upon which the Congo's economy depended. The resulting Congo Crisis (1960-1965) saw repeated interventions by the UN, including the controversial death of Lumumba in January 1961 while in captivity, likely with Belgian and American involvement. Approximately 100,000 people died in the Congo Crisis. The state apparatus collapsed; there was no functioning civil administration, education system, or healthcare infrastructure. The Katanga secession and subsequent conflicts destroyed economic infrastructure. The crisis demonstrated that Belgium's refusal to prepare the Congo for independence—maintaining a rigid colonial hierarchy and limiting African access to higher education and senior administrative positions—made the transition catastrophic. The resulting state weakness and institutional collapse persisted for decades, contributing to the broader instability and civil wars that afflicted the Democratic Republic of Congo (as it became known) through subsequent decades.\n\nPortugal's withdrawal from Africa was the most violent and protracted, creating immense humanitarian suffering. Portugal, under the authoritarian Estado Novo regime, refused to grant independence and instead fought prolonged wars against liberation movements. The independence wars in Angola (1961-1974), Mozambique (1964-1974), and Guinea-Bissau (1963-1974) were brutal, radicalized, and destabilizing. These wars cost approximately 500,000 lives and displaced hundreds of thousands. Portugal's intransigence meant that unlike British or Belgian territories, African nationalist movements radicalized through armed struggle, often adopting Marxist-Leninist ideologies. The MPLA (Movimento Popular de Libertação de Angola) in Angola, FRELIMO in Mozambique, and PAIGC in Guinea-Bissau became communist-oriented through the experience of anti-colonial warfare. When Portugal finally withdrew after the 1974 Carnation Revolution in Lisbon—which itself occurred because the colonial wars were economically and socially unsustainable—independence came suddenly. Angola erupted into immediate civil war (1975-2002), as the MPLA, UNITA, and FNLA factions fought for control. This conflict killed over 500,000 people, drew in Cold War superpowers (the Soviet Union and Cuba supporting the MPLA; the United States and South Africa supporting UNITA), and destroyed the economy and infrastructure. Mozambique, under FRELIMO's one-party rule from 1975, faced the Rhodesian and South African-backed RENAMO insurgency from 1976 onward, in a war lasting until 1992 that killed approximately 900,000 people. The prolonged wars of liberation and immediate post-independence conflicts meant that Angola and Mozambique emerged from colonialism devastated, with destroyed infrastructure, displaced populations, and militarized societies. Economic development was set back by decades. Guinea-Bissau similarly faced post-colonial instability and military coups. Portugal's late and violent withdrawal thus created warfare patterns that persisted throughout the Cold War era.\n\nIn synthesis, these withdrawals caused multiple interconnected problems: ethnic and political instability where colonial rule had suppressed national consciousness; institutional weakness where inadequate preparation left no functioning state apparatus; economic disruption and resource conflicts, particularly over mineral wealth; civil wars and state collapse in the most severe cases; and the drawing of Africa into Cold War competition. Britain's withdrawal, though generally orderly, nonetheless precipitated coups and wars through inadequate institutional preparation. Belgium's abrupt abandonment of the Congo caused immediate state collapse and massive loss of life. Portugal's violent resistance created wars that radicalized African movements and extended conflict far into the post-independence period. All three cases demonstrated that decolonization, whether rapid or delayed, created profound instability when colonial powers had failed to build indigenous institutions, address ethnic divisions, or prepare populations for self-governance. The problems extended beyond the initial transition, creating long-term patterns of authoritarianism, war, and underdevelopment that persisted throughout the late twentieth century.",
