        "label": "a",
        "question": "To what extent did the Montgomery bus boycott bring about change?",
        "marks": 40,
        "model": "The Montgomery bus boycott (1955–56) brought about significant change, though its impact was both immediate and long-term, and remained contested even after victory.\n\nThe most direct outcome was the successful desegregation of Montgomery's buses. On 20 December 1956, following the Supreme Court's Browder v. Gayle decision (November 1956) that declared segregation on buses unconstitutional, the city's bus system was formally desegregated. African Americans could now sit wherever they chose, ending the humiliating system where they had been forced to the back. This represented a concrete, measurable victory achieved through collective action.\n\nHowever, the extent of change must be qualified. Desegregation on buses did not translate into broader social transformation. Montgomery remained deeply segregated in housing, education, employment, and public facilities. The White Citizens Council and other segregationists continued to resist, and white hostility persisted. Indeed, some white citizens responded by using private transportation rather than share buses with African Americans.\n\nYet the boycott catalysed wider change. It demonstrated the power of non-violent protest and civil disobedience, providing a template for subsequent campaigns. The boycott elevated Martin Luther King Jr. to national prominence as a leader and theorist of non-violence, influencing the broader Civil Rights Movement. It energised Black activism across the South, contributing to sit-ins, Freedom Rides, and voter registration drives in the following decade.\n\nInternational dimensions matter too. The boycott generated significant media coverage, damaging America's Cold War image at a time when Soviet propaganda emphasised racial injustice in the US. This foreign pressure, combined with moral argument, influenced federal intervention—eventually leading to the Civil Rights Act (1964) and Voting Rights Act (1965).\n\nThe boycott thus achieved change at multiple levels: immediate desegregation of one city's buses; establishment of non-violent protest as a viable strategy; emergence of leadership that shaped the Civil Rights Movement; and contribution to federal legislation that outlawed segregation nationwide. Yet it did not end racism or segregation comprehensively. Change was real, but incomplete and hard-won. The boycott's significance lies not in solving segregation but in proving it could be challenged successfully, thereby opening a path toward broader transformation.",
