    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "a",
        "question": "What was the Sunningdale Agreement and why did the power-sharing executive, 1973-1974, collapse?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "The Sunningdale Agreement was a power-sharing arrangement negotiated in December 1973 at Sunningdale in England, designed to establish a devolved executive in Northern Ireland that would include both unionist and nationalist politicians. It followed the suspension of Stormont in 1972 and represented the first serious attempt to create a government that reflected the community balance in Northern Ireland.\n\nThe Agreement created an eleven-member executive comprising six Ulster Unionists (led by Brian Faulkner), four SDLP members (the moderate nationalist party), and one independent. This reflected the balance of seats in the 78-seat assembly elected in June 1973. Crucially, it was underpinned by the principle of 'weighted majority voting', requiring support from both communities for major decisions. The Agreement also established a Council of Ireland with 30 members (15 from North, 15 from South) to coordinate matters of mutual interest and provide a cross-border dimension, a concession to nationalists seeking an all-Ireland dimension to any settlement.\n\nThe Agreement gained cross-border legitimacy when both the Irish government and Westminster endorsed it. The Irish government agreed to recognize Northern Ireland's status as part of the UK as long as Irish unity was pursued by peaceful means, while London committed to the principle of consent: no change to Northern Ireland's constitutional status without the consent of its people.\n\nHowever, the power-sharing executive collapsed in May 1974 after just five months, destroyed by a combination of internal and external pressures. Internally, the executive was undermined from the outset by unionist opposition. Although Faulkner had won assembly backing for Sunningdale, he faced fierce resistance from hardline unionists both within and outside the UUP. The Ulster Unionist Council, meeting in January 1974, rejected Sunningdale by 427 to 374 votes, with opponents arguing that power-sharing represented surrender to republicanism and that the Council of Ireland was an unacceptable step towards a united Ireland. Faulkner's position as leader became untenable, weakening the executive's unionist component.\n\nThe critical external blow came with the British general election of February 1974. Anti-Sunningdale unionists, reorganized as the United Ulster Unionist Coalition (UUUC), won 11 of Northern Ireland's 12 Westminster seats, demonstrating overwhelming public opposition to the Agreement among unionists. This electoral rejection emboldened unionist opponents and demoralized Faulkner. The UUUC's victory signaled that a significant section of unionism saw Sunningdale as a betrayal.\n\nThe immediate catalyst for collapse came in May 1974 with the Ulster Workers' Council (UWC) strike. This general strike, organized by loyalist workers and tacitly supported by hardline unionists including the DUP, brought Northern Ireland to a standstill over two weeks. The strike was ostensibly called in protest at the Council of Ireland, which loyalists viewed as the thin end of a wedge towards Irish reunification. The strike paralyzed industry, transport, and utilities, creating severe shortages of fuel, water, and food. The Executive proved unable to maintain essential services, and the security forces (controlled by Westminster) were reluctant to break the strike, fearing a descent into large-scale violence.\n\nBritish Secretary of State Merlyn Rees, concluding that the executive could not function in the face of such determined opposition, effectively ended it by allowing it to collapse. The government did not use troops to break the strike or seriously attempt to sustain the executive. Faulkner resigned from his position on 28 May 1974, and the executive formally ended.\n\nThe collapse reflected the depth of unionist anxiety about power-sharing and the all-Ireland dimension. For many unionists, Sunningdale represented a fundamental threat to the Union. The executive had lacked sufficient unionist support and faced implacable opposition from organized loyalism. Nationalists, conversely, felt that the Agreement offered too little, particularly regarding the Council of Ireland's limited powers. The SDLP participated constructively, but their ability to deliver sustained nationalist support was limited by more radical republican groups.\n\nSunningdale's failure had profound consequences. It discredited the possibility of devolved government in Northern Ireland for many years, leading directly to the reimposition of direct rule from Westminster. It demonstrated that power-sharing, though theoretically sound, could not survive without active consent from the largest community—unionists—and that constitutional matters affecting Ireland's status could not be resolved by negotiation alone when one side harbored deep fears about its implications. The Agreement's collapse also strengthened the DUP, which emerged as the voice of unionist opposition, ultimately reshaping unionist politics for decades. Despite its short life, Sunningdale established principles—power-sharing, cross-border cooperation, consent—that would resurface and eventually succeed in the Good Friday Agreement of 1998.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
