    "id": "history-pp-2021-HI-Q2",
    "subject": "history",
    "chapterId": "hist-ire6",
    "sectionId": "hist-section2",
    "source": "LC History Higher 2021 — Q2",
    "year": 2021,
    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "a",
        "question": "What problems faced Seán Lemass and/or Jack Lynch as Taoiseach?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Seán Lemass (1959–1966) and Jack Lynch (1966–1973) inherited and confronted severe structural and cyclical economic problems that threatened the viability of independent Irish statehood.\n\nLemass's primary challenge was economic stagnation following decades of protectionist policy. In 1958, when he took office, Ireland faced emigration exceeding 40,000 annually, unemployment above 8%, and negligible industrial growth. The 1950s had witnessed economic contraction, with GDP growth negative in several years. Agricultural productivity remained low, and the farming population—still over 40% of the workforce—was underemployed and impoverished. The exhaustion of protectionism was evident: native industries, sheltered behind tariff walls since 1932, had become inefficient, uncompetitive internationally, and unable to generate employment. Foreign exchange reserves were dangerously depleted. Lemass inherited a closed economy incapable of sustaining population and employment.\n\nHis response—opening Ireland to foreign investment and trade—created transition difficulties. Dismantling protectionist barriers exposed indigenous manufacturers to competition they could not meet. Redundancies in traditional sectors preceded job creation in new industries. Industrial restructuring caused social dislocation, particularly in small towns dependent on protected manufacturing. Trade union resistance to productivity demands and wage restraint complicated economic modernisation. Additionally, the transition to free trade through the Anglo-Irish Free Trade Agreement (1965) and moves toward GATT membership exposed the economy to external shocks without immediate compensatory gains.\n\nLynch assumed office during apparent economic success: growth rates of 4-5% annually (1965–1968), rising living standards, and reduced emigration. However, this expansion proved unsustainable and masked deeper structural weaknesses. International recession from 1969 onwards exposed Ireland's dependence on foreign investment and export markets. Unemployment began rising again after 1970, reaching 6% by 1973. Emigration resumed as job creation slowed. Lynch faced the problem of maintaining growth rates that had created political expectations among an electorate increasingly accustomed to rising prosperity. The \"dynamic decade\" model proved illusory.\n\nInflation emerged as a critical challenge for Lynch. Irish inflation exceeded British and European rates, eroding competitiveness and wage-earners' purchasing power. By 1973, annual inflation approached 13%. Wage demands intensified as workers sought to protect living standards. The public sector workforce, expanded during the growth years, demanded parity with private sector earnings and better conditions. Trade union militancy increased, with significant strikes in transport (1968) and other sectors. Lynch attempted wage restraint through national wage agreements, but these collapsed under pressure from militant unions and inter-union competition.\n\nBoth Taoiseach faced agrarian problems. Despite modernisation initiatives, farming remained economically marginal for most practitioners. Smallholders could not achieve viable incomes from agricultural production alone. Rural depopulation accelerated as young people migrated to cities or emigrated. Agricultural income grew slower than non-agricultural income, creating relative deprivation among farmers. The 1960s Common Agricultural Policy negotiations and EEC membership (achieved in 1973 under Lynch) created anxiety among farmers regarding price supports and competition. Lynch had to manage farmer expectations regarding EEC benefits while implementing controversial agricultural modernisation.\n\nRegional inequality intensified during this period. Economic growth concentrated in Dublin and the East, while the West and Northwest experienced relative decline and accelerated emigration. Lemass and Lynch both attempted regional development through industrial estates and incentives, but these proved insufficient to counteract market-driven centralisation. The problem of sustaining viable rural communities remained largely unresolved.\n\nHousing presented an acute urban problem. Rapid rural-urban migration created accommodation shortages, particularly in Dublin. Slum housing persisted, and local authority housing construction could not match demand. Both Taoiseach committed resources to housing programmes, but urban expansion outpaced provision. Housing became politically contentious, with tenant organisations and left-wing activists agitating for improved conditions and rent controls.\n\nEducation expansion created its own difficulties. Secondary education expanded dramatically in the 1960s, raising expectations for third-level places. University capacity proved inadequate, creating frustration among school-leavers. Third-level education remained expensive and inaccessible to working-class children despite expansion. The education system's ability to prepare students for available employment was questioned, and graduate unemployment emerged as a problem by the early 1970s.\n\nFor Lynch particularly, Northern Ireland became an increasingly urgent problem after 1968. Civil rights agitation and sectarian violence in the North created pressure on the Dublin government to respond. Lynch faced demands from nationalist opinion to \"do something\" about Northern Ireland, yet any intervention risked military confrontation with Britain and destabilisation of the Republic's economy and political system. The Arms Trial (1970) and Lynch's dismissal of ministers Haughey and Blaney demonstrated how Northern Ireland policy could fracture the government. This problem dominated Lynch's later tenure and constrained his freedom of action on economic and social issues.\n\nFinancial sector constraints affected both. The Central Bank's limited regulatory scope and the banking system's conservatism restricted credit availability for productive investment. The fiscal system's reliance on agriculture-based taxation provided unstable revenue. Both Taoiseach grappled with balancing public spending demands against revenue constraints and inflationary pressures.\n\nIn summary, Lemass faced the fundamental problem of economic transformation from a protectionist, agrarian, emigration-prone society toward an open, industrial, employment-generating economy. Transition costs were severe, and success uncertain. Lynch inherited a modernised but fragile economy vulnerable to international recession, with inflation, unemployment, and sectarian crisis combining to constrain his options. Both confronted the intractable problem of sustaining employment and living standards in a small, open economy dependent on international conditions beyond Irish control.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
