    "id": "history-pp-2021-HI-Q4",
    "subject": "history",
    "chapterId": "hist-ire6",
    "sectionId": "hist-section2",
    "source": "LC History Higher 2021 — Q4",
    "year": 2021,
    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "a",
        "question": "What was the contribution to Irish life of Sylvia Meehan and/or Garret FitzGerald?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Sylvia Meehan and Garret FitzGerald made substantial and distinct contributions to Irish life during the period 1949–1989, each reshaping different spheres of Irish society.\n\n**Sylvia Meehan: Women's Rights and Industrial Relations**\n\nSylvia Meehan (1912–1991) was a pioneering trade unionist and feminist activist whose work fundamentally advanced women's economic and social rights in Ireland. As a trade union organizer with the Irish Women Workers' Union (later amalgamated into the ITGWU), Meehan fought for equal pay and workplace rights at a time when Irish law and social convention severely restricted women's employment. She was instrumental in the campaign for equal pay legislation, which culminated in the Equal Pay Act, 1974—transforming the legal framework for women workers. This legislation obliged employers to provide equal remuneration for equal work, directly addressing the systematic wage discrimination women faced across industries.\n\nMeehan's contribution extended beyond legislative victory to cultural change. As a delegate to the International Labour Organization and representative at international women's conferences, she raised Ireland's profile on gender equality issues globally. Domestically, she advocated for women's participation in trade unions at a leadership level, challenging the male-dominated structures of Irish industrial relations. Her activism helped shift Irish society away from the assumption that women's primary role was domestic, legitimizing women's participation in the formal economy as workers with rights.\n\nHer work occurred within a conservative Catholic social context where official ideology emphasized women's maternal and familial roles. Yet Meehan persisted, building alliances with male trade unionists and progressive politicians, demonstrating the possibility of advancing women's interests within Irish institutions rather than against them. Her legacy includes not only statutory protection against wage discrimination but also the normalization of women as permanent members of the industrial workforce—a profound cultural shift in a society where women's employment had been transient and undervalued.\n\n**Garret FitzGerald: Political Leadership and Constitutional Change**\n\nGarret FitzGerald (1926–2011) served as Taoiseach in two administrations (1981–1982 and 1982–1987) and shaped Irish governance, economic policy, and the nation's constitutional trajectory during a critical period. His most enduring contribution was his vision of a modern, inclusive Ireland and his efforts to remove constitutional and legal barriers that divided the island and alienated unionists.\n\nFitzGerald's first major initiative was the New Ireland Forum (1983–1984), a constitutional inquiry that produced a published report examining possible constitutional and political structures for a united Ireland. Although politically divisive—the British government under Margaret Thatcher immediately rejected all options presented—the forum broadened public discourse on Irish identity and unity beyond traditional republican rhetoric, introducing federal and confederal models as alternatives to simple reintegration. This intellectual contribution helped reframe Irish unity as a complex political question requiring negotiation rather than an ideological certainty.\n\nFitzGerald's most concrete achievement was constitutional reform. He initiated the removal of the constitutional prohibition on divorce through a 1986 referendum, though this initially failed. More significantly, he removed Article 41.2 from the Constitution, which had enshrined the ideal of women in the home—a symbolic and legal recognition that women's role in Irish society extended beyond domesticity. This reform reflected Meehan's earlier activism and demonstrated FitzGerald's commitment to modernizing Irish law and social attitudes.\n\nEconomically, FitzGerald inherited and managed the debt crisis of the early 1980s, implementing austere fiscal policies to stabilize the Irish economy. While painful—unemployment reached 17% and emigration resumed—these measures laid groundwork for later economic recovery. His governments also negotiated Ireland's integration into European structures, facilitating Ireland's fuller participation in the Single European Act (1986), which deepened economic and political ties with Europe.\n\nFitzGerald's political philosophy was explicitly secular and pluralist. He advocated for Irish Protestantism's place in the Irish polity, for women's full equality, and for constitutional structures that could accommodate unionists within an all-island framework. His rhetoric emphasized an Ireland of \"pluralism\" and \"diversity\" rather than Catholic nationalism. This ideological repositioning—though not immediately realized in constitutional or political terms—represented an important intellectual shift, offering an alternative to majoritarian Catholic nationalism that had dominated Irish politics since partition.\n\n**Comparative Impact**\n\nBoth Meehan and FitzGerald worked to extend rights and remake Irish identity, but through different mechanisms. Meehan operated through trade union organization and direct advocacy, achieving concrete legislative change (equal pay) that directly improved women's material conditions. FitzGerald operated through electoral politics and constitutional vision, initiating symbolic reforms and intellectual reframing that set agendas for future change.\n\nMeehan's contribution was more socially rooted—addressing the everyday economic exploitation of working women. FitzGerald's was more elite and institutional—reshaping the constitutional and political frameworks within which Irish society operated. Yet both challenged the conservative settlement that had characterized much of independent Ireland, particularly its treatment of women and its defensive Catholic nationalism.\n\nTogether, they exemplify how Irish life between 1949 and 1989 became progressively more open, rights-conscious, and pluralist—though these changes faced substantial resistance and remained incomplete by 1989. Meehan's equal pay victory and FitzGerald's constitutional revisionism represented genuine, if contested, departures from traditional Ireland.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
