    "year": 2021,
    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "a",
        "question": "What did you learn about the role of women in society, 1871-1920, from your study of one or more of the following: Marie Curie; women in the workforce during World War I; Rosa Luxemburg?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "The period 1871–1920 witnessed significant contradictions in women's roles and opportunities, illuminated starkly by examining Marie Curie, women in the workforce during World War I, and Rosa Luxemburg together.\n\nMarie Curie's scientific career demonstrates both exceptional individual achievement and structural barriers limiting women in society. Curie conducted groundbreaking research in radioactivity, discovering polonium and radium (1898), and became the first woman to win a Nobel Prize (1903) and the first person to win two Nobel Prizes (1911). Yet her trajectory reveals how exceptional talent alone was insufficient: she required her husband Pierre's collaboration to gain scientific credibility, and after his death in 1906, despite her eminence, the University of Paris initially refused her a full professorial position—granting it only under public pressure. This illustrates that even brilliant women faced institutional discrimination; scientific authority was gendered male. Curie worked in male-dominated academia where women were largely absent from universities and professional positions. Her international recognition paradoxically underscored women's marginalisation: she remained exceptional precisely because so few women could access scientific training or secure research positions.\n\nWomen's experiences in the workforce during World War I (1914–1918) reveal how economic necessity temporarily opened occupational doors while reinforcing traditional gender hierarchies. As millions of men enlisted or were conscripted, women entered factories, transport, agriculture, and munitions work in unprecedented numbers. In Britain, women comprised over 65 per cent of the munitions workforce by 1917. Women worked as tram conductors, railway workers, and in heavy industry. This demonstrated women's capacity for skilled, physically demanding labour previously reserved for men. However, this expansion was explicitly temporary and contingent. Employers and governments framed women's war work as patriotic duty, not as permanent entitlement to employment. Crucially, women were paid significantly less than men for identical work—a systematic wage differential justified by the argument that women's earnings merely supplemented family income, while men's wages supported dependents. When war ended, women were rapidly dismissed from these positions, with returning soldiers prioritised for employment. By 1920, many women had been pushed back into domestic service, sweated trades, or domestic unpaid labour. This cyclical pattern—mobilisation in crisis, dismissal in peace—reveals that women's economic participation was conditional, not structural. Women's workforce entry did not fundamentally challenge the assumption that their primary role remained domestic.\n\nRosa Luxemburg's political activism and theoretical writings expose women's marginalisation within socialist and radical movements, even among those claiming to challenge exploitation. Luxemburg was a prominent Marxist revolutionary, co-founder of the German Communist League (Spartacist League) during World War I, and author of influential political writings critiquing imperialism and capitalism. She participated actively in the German Revolution (1918–1919) and was murdered by right-wing militia in January 1919. Luxemburg's significance lies in her intellectual and political prominence: she was treated as a serious political theorist and revolutionary leader. Yet her position was exceptional and precarious. Within socialist movements, despite theoretical commitment to women's equality, women occupied subordinate roles. Luxemburg herself was frequently marginalised or criticised for her gender; contemporaries focused on her appearance and personality as much as her ideas. Her murder, and the relative indifference to it compared to male revolutionary leaders' deaths, illustrates women's vulnerability in political movements. Moreover, Luxemburg's commitment to class struggle meant she sometimes subordinated gender-specific demands to broader revolutionary aims—a position reflecting how even radical women internalised hierarchies within male-dominated political structures.\n\nCollectively, these figures and developments reveal that between 1871 and 1920, women's societal roles remained fundamentally constrained despite visible changes. Women's entry into science, industry, and politics was possible but exceptional and conditional. Each domain reveals the same pattern: structural barriers prevented women from full participation; when opportunities arose (through individual brilliance, wartime necessity, or political revolution), women succeeded but remained marginalised; gains were temporary or precarious, vulnerable to reversal. Scientific institutions, labour markets, and political movements all operated on the assumption that women's primary identity was domestic—as wives, mothers, or supporters of male endeavour. Even women who transcended these limitations, like Curie or Luxemburg, could not fundamentally alter the systems that marginalised them.\n\nThe significance of 1871–1920 is thus that it was a period of simultaneous possibility and confinement. Industrialisation, world war, and ideological challenges to tradition created openings. Yet the period closed without fundamental transformation of women's social position. Women had proven their capability in every domain, yet institutional structures, legal systems, and ideological assumptions about gender remained largely intact. The post-1920 period would continue these tensions, as women's suffrage (achieved in several nations during this period) did not automatically translate into economic equality or social transformation. The study of Curie, wartime women workers, and Luxemburg thus demonstrates that women's emancipation was not simply impeded by lack of ability or opportunity, but by systematic, institutionalised exclusion that persisted even when individual women or temporary circumstances challenged it.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
