        "question": "From your study of Great Britain, 1920-1945, what did you learn about two of the following: J.M. Keynes; the success or failure of the Jarrow March, October 1936; life on the Home Front during World War II?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "J.M. Keynes was an economist whose ideas transformed economic policy during the interwar period and beyond. During the 1920s, Keynes emerged as a major intellectual figure, publishing *The Economic Consequences of the Peace* (1919), which predicted that the punitive Treaty of Versailles would destabilize Europe economically. He argued that Germany could not pay the reparations demanded and that the treaty would harm European recovery and international trade. His analysis proved prescient. In the mid-1920s, Keynes supported the return to the gold standard under Winston Churchill in 1925, though he later regretted this decision as it overvalued sterling and damaged British exports. His most influential work, *The General Theory of Employment, Interest and Money* (1936), revolutionized economic thinking. Keynes rejected the classical economic orthodoxy that dominated British policy. He argued that in a depression, markets would not automatically return to full employment; government intervention was necessary. He advocated increased government spending and public works to stimulate demand, create jobs, and end mass unemployment. This represented a radical departure from the balanced-budget policies pursued by the British government during the Great Depression. Though Keynes' ideas were not fully adopted in Britain during the 1930s, they became increasingly influential during World War II, when the government necessarily engaged in massive deficit spending for war production. His theories later became the foundation of postwar economic management. Keynes embodied the importance of intellectual challenge to established economic orthodoxy during Britain's interwar crisis.\n\nThe Jarrow March of October 1936 was a protest march by unemployed workers from the depressed town of Jarrow in northeast England to London. The march aimed to draw attention to mass unemployment and industrial decline in the region and to petition Parliament for assistance. Approximately 200 marchers, led by local MP Ellen Wilkinson, walked 291 miles over several weeks. The march was a dignified protest: marchers were well-organized, wore uniforms, and maintained order throughout. The march gained substantial public sympathy and media coverage. However, in terms of achieving its immediate objectives, the Jarrow March was ultimately unsuccessful. The government made no significant policy response to address the town's unemployment crisis. Parliament received the marchers' petition but took no concrete action to provide special assistance to Jarrow or the northeast. Industrial recovery in the region remained limited until World War II began in 1939, when rearmament and war production finally created jobs. The march did, however, represent an important moment of working-class political protest and highlighted the desperation of communities devastated by deindustrialization. It demonstrated the failure of government policy to address regional economic collapse during the 1930s. The march became a symbol of the struggles of ordinary British people during the Depression and illustrated the limitations of peaceful protest in the face of government indifference to unemployment.",
