        "label": "B",
        "question": "Write a short paragraph on one of the following:\n1. The GAA and the Irish diaspora.\n2. Archbishop Daniel Mannix.\n3. The Holy Ghost mission to Nigeria, 1945–1966.\n4. John F. Kennedy.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "John F. Kennedy represented the culmination of Irish-American integration and political influence in the United States. Born in 1917 to a wealthy Irish-American family, Kennedy's election as president in 1960 marked a watershed moment for Irish diaspora identity. His presidency symbolised how far Irish emigrants and their descendants had progressed from nineteenth-century discrimination and marginalisation. Kennedy actively cultivated his Irish heritage, visiting Ireland in 1963 and receiving a hero's welcome that reinforced the emotional and cultural bonds between Irish Americans and their ancestral homeland. His administration benefited from Irish-American political networks and voting blocs that had been consolidated over generations. Kennedy's Catholicism, once a barrier to high office, became irrelevant—demonstrating the successful assimilation of Irish Americans into mainstream American society and politics. His presidency generated widespread pride within Irish communities both in America and Ireland itself. However, Kennedy's Irish identity, while symbolically important, was secondary to his identity as an American president. His policies prioritised American national interests rather than explicitly Irish-American concerns. His assassination in 1963 created an outpouring of grief in Ireland, underlining the persistent emotional connections between the diaspora and the homeland. Kennedy's rise illustrated how by the mid-twentieth century, Irish Americans had achieved economic prosperity and political power, moving beyond the survival struggles that had characterised earlier waves of emigration.",
