        "question": "Write a short paragraph on one of the following:\n1. Terence O'Neill.\n2. The welfare state in Northern Ireland.\n3. Bernadette Devlin.\n4. The Downing Street Declaration, 1993.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "The Downing Street Declaration, 1993, represented a major diplomatic breakthrough in addressing the conflict in Northern Ireland. Issued jointly by the British Prime Minister John Major and Irish Taoiseach Albert Reynolds on 15 December 1993, the Declaration set out principles for future peace negotiations. Crucially, it affirmed that the people of Ireland, north and south, had the right to self-determination, while acknowledging that this could only be exercised with the consent of a majority in Northern Ireland. This balanced approach was significant because it satisfied Irish nationalist aspirations for unity while respecting unionist concerns about consent. The Declaration also stated that Britain had no selfish strategic or economic interest in Northern Ireland, which reassured nationalists about British intentions. It established the framework for inclusive dialogue, committing all parties to democratic and peaceful methods, and paved the way for Sinn Féin's participation in peace talks. The Declaration's emphasis on consent and self-determination became cornerstones of the 1998 Good Friday Agreement. By providing a clear statement of principle acceptable to both the Irish and British governments, the Declaration restored momentum to the peace process after years of violence and stalemate. It demonstrated that negotiated settlement was possible and created the political conditions necessary for the comprehensive agreement that would eventually end the conflict.",
