        "label": "C",
        "question": "Answer one of the following:\n1. How did President Harry Truman respond to problems at home and abroad?\n2. How did the war in Vietnam develop under the presidency of Lyndon Johnson, 1963–1968?\n3. By what methods did Billy Graham promote religion in modern America?\n4. In what ways did Norman Mailer and/or Betty Friedan criticise aspects of American life?",
        "marks": 40,
        "model": "Under Lyndon Johnson, the Vietnam War escalated dramatically from a limited advisory conflict into a major military engagement. Following the Gulf of Tonkin incident in August 1964—disputed reports of attacks on US destroyers—Congress passed the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution, granting Johnson broad war powers without a formal declaration of war. This enabled Operation Rolling Thunder, the sustained bombing campaign against North Vietnam launched in March 1965, which lasted until 1968 and dropped more tonnage than the atomic bombs on Japan.\n\nJohnson committed ground forces massively. Troop levels rose from 900 advisers in 1963 to over 540,000 by 1968. The strategy relied on attrition: killing enough Viet Cong and North Vietnamese forces to break enemy morale. However, despite overwhelming American firepower and technological superiority, the war proved unwinnable. The enemy adapted through tunnel systems, guerrilla tactics, and supply lines via the Ho Chi Minh Trail.\n\nThe turning point came with the Tet Offensive (January 1968), when North Vietnamese and Viet Cong forces launched coordinated attacks across South Vietnam. Although militarily defeated, the offensive shocked Americans watching televised combat footage and shattered confidence in official optimistic statements. Walter Cronkite's declaration that the war was unwinnable reflected growing media scepticism.\n\nDomestically, the war fractured American society. Student protests, most dramatically at the Democratic Convention in Chicago (August 1968), intensified anti-war sentiment. The draft sparked resistance, and the credibility gap—gap between government claims and reality—deepened. By 1968, Johnson's approval ratings collapsed. He announced on March 31, 1968, that he would not seek re-election, effectively a political casualty of Vietnam.\n\nThe war ultimately killed 58,000 Americans and over 2 million Vietnamese. Johnson's vision of a Great Society domestically was overshadowed by the mounting costs and casualties of a war that reshaped American politics and society, delegitimising government institutions and marking a watershed in American confidence.",
