        "question": "How effective were Parnell's attempts to achieve land reform and Home Rule?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Parnell's attempts to achieve land reform and Home Rule were substantially effective, though his success was uneven and ultimately incomplete. His strategy of parliamentary leverage, combined with organised grassroots agitation, delivered significant land reform but fell short of securing Home Rule before his political collapse.\n\nOn land reform, Parnell achieved considerable success. He championed the Land League from 1879, which mobilised tenant farmers against landlord exploitation through campaigns of non-cooperation. This pressure, combined with Parnell's parliamentary tactics of obstruction and delegation, forced Gladstone's Liberal government to introduce the Land Act of 1881. This Act granted tenants the \"Three Fs\"—fair rent, fixity of tenure, and free sale—fundamentally protecting tenant interests. While imperfect, this represented a major concession that had eluded Irish politicians for decades. Parnell's ability to sustain the Land League's agitation while maintaining his parliamentary position demonstrated tactical skill. His imprisonment in Kilmainham Jail in 1882, followed by the Kilmainham Treaty negotiated with the government, showed his capacity to extract concessions. The 1887 Land Act, which extended relief to those in arrears and provided financial assistance, further testified to the pressure Parnell's movement exerted on Westminster. By the 1890s, successive Land Acts had substantially transformed the position of Irish tenant farmers, moving towards peasant proprietorship—a transformation Parnell initiated.\n\nOn Home Rule, however, Parnell's effectiveness was considerably more limited. He built the Irish Parliamentary Party into a disciplined force of 86 MPs by 1885, holding the balance of power after the 1885 election. This leverage proved valuable: Gladstone's conversion to Home Rule in 1886 appeared to validate Parnell's constitutional approach. Yet the First Home Rule Bill failed in the Commons, defeated partly by Liberal defections. Parnell's subsequent tactics during the second Home Rule crisis (1892-93) were less decisive. Although the Second Home Rule Bill passed the Commons in 1893, it was decisively rejected by the Lords. More damaging, Parnell's personal life—particularly his relationship with Katharine O'Shea and subsequent divorce scandal (1890)—fractured his party and destroyed his political credibility. The revelation that he had been in a long-term affair, combined with Catholic Church opposition to divorce, saw him deposed as party leader in December 1890. This collapse was not primarily a failure of strategy but of circumstance and social convention, yet it utterly undermined his capacity to deliver Home Rule.\n\nParnell's legacy reveals a paradox: he was more effective in delivering tangible, incremental land reform than in securing the constitutional independence he rhetorically championed. The Land Acts fundamentally altered Irish rural society and created an emerging class of smallholding proprietors. This was genuine, lasting reform. Home Rule remained elusive during his lifetime, though it would eventually be conceded in 1914—after his death. His constitutional methods proved adequate for extracting land reform from a Liberal government under pressure, but insufficient for forcing Home Rule through a Conservative-dominated Upper House and a deeply divided United Kingdom. His personal disgrace in 1890 removed him at the precise moment when his political skills might have been essential to navigate the complexities of the second Home Rule campaign.\n\nIn conclusion, Parnell's effectiveness must be measured against realistic objectives and the constraints of the constitutional system. He succeeded in making the Irish question unavoidable in Westminster politics and in securing substantive land reform that restructured Irish agrarian society. In Home Rule, he failed to secure implementation but did extract parliamentary acceptance of the principle, shifting the question from whether Home Rule would be granted to when and in what form. His methods—parliamentary obstruction combined with extra-parliamentary mobilisation—proved effective for land reform but insufficient for Home Rule, which ultimately required circumstances (the weakness of the Lords after 1911 and wartime politics) beyond his lifetime to achieve.",
