    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "3",
        "question": "What were the successes and failures of Éamon de Valera as a political leader?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Éamon de Valera's career as political leader from 1926 to 1948 reveals both significant achievements and substantial failures, shaped by the constraints of Irish independence and partition.\n\nDe Valera's greatest successes lay in consolidating Irish sovereignty and constructing a distinct national identity. His formation of Fianna Fáil in 1926, after rejecting the Treaty, gave voice to republican dissent and provided an alternative to Cumann na nGaedheal. His election victory in 1932 marked a watershed: he dismantled the oath of allegiance to the Crown, removed the governor-general's powers, and ceased land annuity payments to Britain—acts that asserted Irish independence. The Anglo-Irish Trade Agreement of 1938 returned the Treaty ports, enhancing Irish sovereignty and reducing British military control. His 1937 Constitution replaced the Free State with Éire, enshrining Catholic social teaching and the aspiration toward a unified 32-county republic, symbolizing cultural and political independence. These achievements resonated deeply: de Valera's vision of Irish self-determination resonated with the electorate, keeping Fianna Fáil in power through 1948, bar one interlude (1948–1951).\n\nHis economic policies, however, proved largely unsuccessful. De Valera's protectionist strategy of self-sufficiency, articulated through tariffs and state intervention, aimed to reduce dependence on Britain and develop domestic industry. In practice, it stifled economic growth: Irish exports declined, unemployment persisted, and emigration accelerated from the 1930s onward. The 1930s saw 166,000 emigrate; by the 1940s, the figure exceeded 80,000 per annum. Living standards remained low, industrial development lagged, and Ireland's economy became increasingly isolated and impoverished relative to international standards. This economic failure contradicted de Valera's vision of a prosperous, self-reliant nation.\n\nDe Valera's handling of partition constituted both aspiration and failure. The 1937 Constitution claimed sovereignty over the entire 32 counties, and de Valera never abandoned the goal of reunification. Yet he failed to achieve it. His strategy of non-coercive methods—constitutional claims, diplomatic appeals, and appeals to Irish-American opinion—lacked the force to move Britain or convince Northern Ireland's majority to join a united republic. The partition remained firm, undermining his fundamental nationalist objective. In this central ambition, de Valera achieved little beyond rhetorical assertion.\n\nHis management of World War II revealed both pragmatism and limitations. De Valera's neutrality policy (1939–1945) protected Irish lives and preserved independence from wartime entanglement; it was widely popular. Yet neutrality also isolated Ireland economically and diplomatically, forgoing the opportunity to negotiate with the Allies for a united Ireland—a calculation that proved ultimately fruitless. His refusal to allow Ireland to become a belligerent cost opportunities for postwar bargaining.\n\nDe Valera's political dominance masked institutional and democratic limitations. His long tenure (1932–1948, 1951–1954, 1957–1959) depended partly on his personal authority and republican mystique rather than on strong institutional safeguards. His 1937 Constitution, while aspirational, concentrated executive power and offered limited democratic accountability. His use of the Offences Against the State Act (1939) to suppress IRA activity—imprisoning republicans—showed authoritarian tendencies conflicting with his republican rhetoric. This contradiction between republican ideology and repressive practice undermined the moral authority his position claimed.\n\nCulturally, de Valera pursued the de-anglicization and Catholicization of Irish life with determination. His support for Irish language restoration, though ultimately incomplete, represented genuine commitment to cultural sovereignty. Yet his influence on education and social policy reinforced clerical dominance and narrow definitions of Irish identity, particularly regarding women and sexual morality. This cultural vision, while internally coherent, excluded pluralism and modernization—failures apparent only with historical hindsight.\n\nConclusion: Éamon de Valera succeeded in asserting Irish political independence, dismantling Treaty constraints, and consolidating a republican political identity through the Fianna Fáil party and 1937 Constitution. His sovereignty achievements were genuine and enduring. Yet he failed decisively in economic development, reunification, and modernization. His protectionist economic strategy impoverished Ireland; partition remained unresolved; and emigration testified to the inadequacy of his vision. De Valera was an effective nationalist leader within the constraints of a 26-county state, but an unsuccessful builder of prosperity or unity. His legacy reflects the tension between republican aspiration and the limited capacity of a small, economically peripheral nation to reshape its circumstances.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
