        "label": "3",
        "question": "What did you learn about the literature of World War I and/or the expression of national identity through literature and the arts, 1871-1914?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "The literature and arts of 1871–1914 reveal profound anxieties about national identity and foreshadow the catastrophe of World War I. Across Europe, writers and artists grappled with competing visions of the nation, oscillating between romantic idealism and modernist disillusionment.\n\nIn France, nationalist literature reinforced revanchist sentiment following the 1870–71 Franco-Prussian War. Writers like Maurice Barrès championed integral nationalism, emphasizing soil, blood, and ancestral tradition as the basis of French identity. His novels, particularly *Les Déracinés* (1897), promoted a mystical, organic view of the nation that rejected rationalism and cosmopolitanism. This literary nationalism was deliberately cultivated to sustain French resolve and prepare public opinion for eventual conflict with Germany. The loss of Alsace-Lorraine became a central motif in French cultural production, keeping national grievance alive across generations.\n\nIn Germany, literature expressed a swelling sense of national power and cultural superiority. German Romantic traditions, already established, were appropriated to justify aggressive expansion. Writers emphasized German genius, racial superiority, and historical destiny. This cultural nationalism created an ideological framework that made military conflict seem not merely permissible but historically necessary. The cult of the hero, derived from Romantic literature, glorified martial values and sacrifice for the nation-state.\n\nBritish literature of this period presents a more complex picture. While imperial literature celebrated British dominance—works like those of Rudyard Kipling promoted duty, sacrifice, and national greatness through service—there emerged countervailing voices expressing unease about modernity, industrialization, and social fragmentation. However, the dominant strain reinforced imperial nationalism and the moral superiority of British civilization. When war came, this cultural preparation made recruitment easier and resistance to militarism weaker than it might otherwise have been.\n\nIn Austria-Hungary, the multinational empire faced acute tensions reflected in its literature. Writers grappled with questions of identity in a fragmenting polity. Czech, Polish, and Hungarian literatures increasingly emphasized distinct national identities rather than supranational imperial loyalty, destabilizing the Austro-Hungarian cultural consensus. This literary expression of competing nationalisms mirrored and reinforced political tensions that would ultimately shatter the empire.\n\nRussia witnessed a rich literary tradition that expressed deep uncertainty about national identity and direction. The question of whether Russia was fundamentally European or Asian, Western or Slavic, animated major works. Dostoevsky's novels explored Russian spiritual superiority alongside apocalyptic anxieties. This cultural introspection, while less overtly militaristic than German nationalism, nonetheless created a sense of national uniqueness and destiny that made mobilization possible in 1914.\n\nThe relationship between literature and visual arts was significant. Artistic movements—Expressionism, Futurism, and others—both reflected and reinforced cultural anxieties and nationalist impulses. Italian Futurism, for instance, explicitly celebrated violence, war, and national renewal, creating aesthetic frameworks that glorified the coming conflict. Artists and writers were often at the forefront of nationalist agitation, using their cultural authority to shape public consciousness.\n\nKey observation: pre-1914 literature across Europe reveals a cultural ecosystem primed for war. Rather than serving as a moderating force, literature and the arts largely amplified nationalist sentiment, glorified sacrifice, marginalized pacifist voices, and created mythologies of national greatness and historical destiny that made mass mobilization psychologically and morally plausible. The romanticization of war, the cult of national superiority, and the treatment of conflict as an expression of national identity were deeply embedded in the cultural production of this period. When war came, these cultural narratives did not prepare people for its actual character—mechanized, anonymous, and catastrophically destructive—but they had successfully prepared populations to accept it as inevitable and even desirable. This disconnect between literary glorification and battlefield reality became one of the defining traumas of the war itself, producing the disillusioned modernism of the 1920s and 1930s.",
