        "label": "1",
        "question": "Which had the greater social and economic problems during the inter-war period, Britain or Germany?\nArgue your case, referring to both.",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Germany faced greater social and economic problems during the inter-war period than Britain, though both nations endured severe difficulties.\n\nGermany's economic crisis was catastrophic and unprecedented. The Treaty of Versailles imposed reparations of 132 billion gold marks, devastating the economy. Hyperinflation in 1923 destroyed savings and middle-class stability when the mark collapsed to 4.2 trillion to the dollar. The Dawes Plan (1924) temporarily stabilised the economy, but recovery was fragile and dependent on American loans. The Wall Street Crash of 1929 triggered immediate collapse: by 1932, unemployment reached 6 million (over 30% of the workforce). Industrial production fell 40% between 1929 and 1932. The crisis destroyed confidence in the Weimar Republic, creating mass desperation that fuelled extremism. The Nazi Party exploited this chaos, winning 37% of votes by July 1932.\n\nBritain's economic problems, while serious, were less acute and more manageable. Unemployment peaked at 2.8 million (around 12%) in 1932, significantly lower than Germany's rate. British industry contracted less severely; the economy was more diversified. Coal mining and textiles suffered severe decline, but financial services and newer industries like electrical manufacturing provided some stability. The General Strike of 1926 indicated industrial unrest, but Britain avoided the revolutionary ferment that gripped Germany. Recovery was slower but more steady; by the late 1930s, unemployment fell below 10%, and rearmament stimulated growth.\n\nThe crucial difference lay in social consequences. In Germany, economic collapse destroyed the entire middle class and created a vast unemployed underclass with no safety net. Weimar's welfare system, though advanced, could not cope with 6 million jobless. Social cohesion fractured; street violence between Nazi and Communist paramilitaries became endemic. In Britain, the dole system, established since 1911, provided some cushion against destitution. Although means-testing from 1931 created hardship, particularly in depressed regions like the North East and South Wales, mass starvation was avoided. Trade unions remained strong and organised, limiting revolutionary appeal.\n\nGeographically, Germany's problems were more evenly distributed across all regions, creating nationwide crisis. Britain's unemployment was regionally concentrated—the South and Midlands recovered faster while traditional industries in the North, Scotland, and Wales remained devastated. This regional unevenness, though painful for afflicted communities, prevented the total social collapse that Germany experienced.\n\nPolitically, the consequences diverged sharply. Britain's democratic institutions, though strained, survived intact. Ramsay MacDonald's National Government (1931) maintained parliamentary authority and avoided both fascism and communism gaining mass support. Germany's crisis destroyed democracy itself: the Reichstag Fire (1933) and Enabling Act gave Hitler dictatorial power. This was not merely a consequence of economic hardship but reflected how Germany's problems were qualitatively worse and more destabilising to the existing system.\n\nThe reparations burden was uniquely German. Britain faced no equivalent external debt obligation; rather, it was a creditor nation. This structural difference meant Germany's recovery was perpetually vulnerable to international conditions, whereas Britain, despite relative economic decline, had greater autonomy.\n\nIn conclusion, Germany's inter-war problems were greater in scale, intensity, and destructive social impact. Unemployment was higher, hyperinflation had no British parallel, and economic collapse was total and immediate after 1929. Most significantly, these conditions destroyed democratic governance and enabled Nazi totalitarianism. Britain faced serious unemployment and regional decay, but maintained social stability and democratic continuity. Germany's crisis was existential; Britain's was severe but manageable within existing structures.",
