        "question": "To what extent did Charles Stewart Parnell achieve success in his political career, 1875-1891?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Charles Stewart Parnell achieved substantial but ultimately incomplete political success between 1875 and 1891. His career demonstrated significant parliamentary influence and nationalist leadership, yet ended in personal and political catastrophe.\n\nParnell's early success lay in parliamentary tactics. From 1875, as newly elected MP for Meath, he pioneered 'obstruction'—deliberately prolonging parliamentary debate to highlight Irish grievances and force attention to Irish issues. This strategy proved effective; it elevated him within nationalist ranks and won recognition from older Home Rule leaders. By 1880, he was elected President of the Irish National Land League, positioning him at the intersection of parliamentary politics and agrarian activism. This dual authority was crucial: he commanded both the Irish Parliamentary Party and the rural mass movement during the Land War (1879–1882), giving him unprecedented leverage over British governments.\n\nHis greatest parliamentary achievement was winning the Third Home Rule Bill. Following the 1885 general election, Parnell held the balance of power between Liberal and Conservative parties. He allied with Liberal leader Gladstone, and in 1886 the First Home Rule Bill passed its second reading—a watershed moment. Although it failed in the Commons, Parnell had secured a British Prime Minister's commitment to Irish self-government. This represented vindication of his constitutional strategy and proof that parliamentary leverage could force Westminster to acknowledge Irish nationalist demands.\n\nParnell also achieved tangible agrarian reform. His leadership during the Land War, despite his imprisonment in Kilmainham Jail (1881–1882), resulted in the Kilmainham Treaty (May 1882). This secured the Land Act of 1881, which granted Irish tenants the 'Three Fs'—fair rent, fixity of tenure, and free sale. While incomplete land reform, this was a genuine concession extracted through Parnell's orchestration of agrarian pressure combined with parliamentary negotiation. Subsequent Land Acts (1887, 1891) moved further toward tenant purchase, a goal Parnell championed.\n\nHowever, significant limitations constrained his success. Home Rule remained unachieved. Despite the Second Home Rule Bill's passage in 1892, it was rejected by the House of Lords—a defeat beyond Parnell's control but which revealed the structural limits of Irish parliamentary power. The constitutional path he pursued could not overcome the institutional barriers of the Westminster system. Moreover, his domination of Irish politics was contested. While he united the Irish Parliamentary Party under strict discipline ('the uncrowned King of Ireland'), this unity depended on his personal authority and would fracture after his death.\n\nMost significantly, Parnell's career ended in catastrophic personal and political failure. The O'Shea divorce scandal of 1890 exposed his affair with Katharine O'Shea, wife of fellow nationalist MP William Henry O'Shea. This destroyed his public reputation in an era of strict moral expectations. The Catholic Church, crucial to Irish nationalism, condemned him; Gladstone declared Parnell unacceptable as a Home Rule leader. The Irish Parliamentary Party split in December 1890, with the majority abandoning him. Parnell's attempt to retain leadership through the Boulogne conferences and his brief return to Ireland in 1891 failed. He died in October 1891, politically isolated and personally broken.\n\nThis collapse revealed the fragility of his achievements. His success had rested partly on his unique personal authority and political skill, but also on contingent factors—the land question's urgency, the balance of parliamentary forces, Liberal receptiveness to Home Rule. Once these conditions shifted, and once his moral authority was shattered, the entire structure collapsed. The Irish Parliamentary Party reunited only after his death, under Edward Blake and John Redmond, suggesting his personal dominance had papered over real divisions.\n\nIn assessment, Parnell achieved considerable success in forcing British recognition of Irish nationalism and securing agrarian concessions. His Home Rule bills, though ultimately rejected, represented unprecedented parliamentary progress. His Land War leadership proved that extra-parliamentary pressure, harnessed through constitutional channels, could win reforms. Yet these achievements remained partial. Home Rule was not achieved; land reform was incomplete; and his movement did not survive his personal collapse. His career demonstrates both the possibilities and the severe limitations of late-nineteenth-century constitutional nationalism working within Westminster's constraints. By 1891, his political project lay in ruins, suggesting that whatever success he achieved between 1875 and 1890 was contingent and, without him, unsustainable. The extent of his success must therefore be judged as real but fundamentally limited: he advanced Irish nationalism significantly but failed to achieve his ultimate objectives, and his methods could not survive the personal scandal that destroyed him.",
