        "question": "Why did the 1913 strike and lockout take place, how was it carried out and what was its outcome?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "The 1913 Dublin lock-out was precipitated by fundamental conflict between capital and labour over workers' rights, wages, and union recognition. William Martin Murphy, owner of the Irish Independent newspaper and tramway company, sought to break the power of Jim Larkin's Irish Transport and General Workers' Union (ITGWU). When tramway workers were dismissed for union membership in August 1913, Larkin called a strike. Murphy retaliated by locking out 20,000 workers and blacklisting union members, compelling employers across Dublin to follow suit. The conflict reflected deeper tensions: workers endured poverty wages (often 15-20 shillings weekly), hazardous conditions, and overcrowded tenements; employers resisted union demands and the challenge to their absolute authority.\n\nThe strike itself was conducted through mass mobilisation and direct action. Larkin, a charismatic organiser, held massive public meetings at Liberty Hall and on O'Connell Street, inspiring workers and raising public sympathy. The ITGWU organised picketing, prevented blackleg labour, and maintained strike discipline across multiple industries—carters, dockers, coal workers, and general labourers joined, making this a general strike affecting Dublin's entire commercial life. Food kitchens provided relief; the Irish Worker newspaper sustained morale and spread propaganda. However, the lockout's scale overwhelmed union resources. British trade unions offered financial support, and sympathetic actions occurred in British ports. The Catholic Church, particularly Archbishop Walsh, issued ambiguous statements that ultimately offered little practical support, while the Lord Mayor and Dublin Corporation provided limited relief.\n\nThe outcome appeared a defeat for workers. By February 1914, after six months of hardship, strikers returned without secured recognition of the union, though the ITGWU survived and actually gained members. Employers refused formal agreements guaranteeing union rights. Many workers faced victimisation and blacklisting, returning to worse conditions than before. Murphy and the employers successfully prevented the closed shop and binding negotiations. Union funds were exhausted. However, the lockout's legacy proved more complex. It demonstrated working-class solidarity and power, inspiring future labour organisation. The brutal response to the strike—including police violence and deportations of suspected organisers—generated international publicity and sympathy. The lockout radicalized Dublin workers and contributed to broader labour consciousness across Ireland. Larkin emerged as a towering figure in labour history, and the ITGWU, though battered, continued as a significant force. The 1913 conflict ultimately catalysed the Irish labour movement's growth in the 1914-1920 period, despite its immediate tactical failure. It revealed the depth of class division in Irish society and the determination of workers to claim dignity and rights against determined employer resistance.",
