        "question": "During the period 1870-1914, which was the more successful movement, the GAA or the Gaelic League?\nArgue your case, referring to both.",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "The Gaelic League proved the more successful movement during 1870–1914, though both achieved significant cultural revival. Success requires assessment against their stated aims and measurable impact on Irish society.\n\nThe Gaelic League, founded 1893 by Douglas Hyde and Eoin MacNeill, explicitly aimed to revive the Irish language as a living tongue and restore Gaelic culture. By 1914, membership exceeded 100,000 across 600 branches. Crucially, the League secured official recognition: Irish became a compulsory subject for the Intermediate Certificate (1900) and for entry to the National University of Ireland (1908). These institutional victories meant the League's cultural agenda became embedded in the Irish educational system, ensuring long-term transmission of the language to successive generations. The League penetrated urban middle-class society and even attracted Protestant and unionist members, broadening its appeal beyond nationalist circles. Its cultural nationalism—non-sectarian and apolitical—allowed it to operate without state repression, enabling sustained expansion throughout the period.\n\nThe GAA, founded 1884 by Michael Cusack, aimed to preserve and promote Irish games (hurling, Gaelic football, handball) against the encroachment of English sports. Measurably, the GAA succeeded: by 1914, it operated in nearly every parish, claimed approximately 600,000 members, and had transformed Irish games from near-extinction into mass participation sports. The 1913 All-Ireland hurling final at Croke Park drew 80,000 spectators. However, the GAA's success was largely *sporting* rather than political or cultural in the broader sense. While it created national identity through sport and preserved Irish games, it did not achieve institutional educational recognition comparable to the League. Furthermore, the GAA became increasingly politicized, especially after 1905, when it banned members from playing English sports; this narrowed rather than broadened its appeal, alienating potential supporters.\n\nCritically, the League's achievement in securing language recognition proved transformative. By 1914, Irish was taught in schools nationwide; the League had shifted the cultural landscape such that revival of the language became a national priority. This institutional embedding of Irish language would have profound consequences after 1916. The GAA, while spectacularly successful in sport, remained essentially a sporting organization—important culturally but narrower in scope.\n\nThe League also avoided serious internal conflict, whereas the GAA experienced divisions (particularly over the Aonach Tailtean controversy, 1900–1906). The League's trajectory was steadier, more unified, and more deeply integrated into educational structures.\n\nConversely, the GAA achieved more *immediate* and *visible* mass participation than the League. Its impact on daily life in parishes across Ireland was tangible: every parish had teams, dances, and matches. The League's membership, while substantial, represented a smaller proportion of the total population and was more concentrated in urban and educated circles.\n\nHowever, 'success' must be measured against objectives. The League's explicit goal was language revival, and securing its place in schools and the university was a decisive strategic victory. The GAA's goal was to preserve Irish games, which it triumphantly achieved, but without comparable institutional transformation in the broader cultural sphere.\n\nIn conclusion, the Gaelic League was the more successful movement because it achieved its central objective—making Irish language revival a matter of national policy—and embedded this culturally in Irish institutions in a way that proved permanent and transformative. The GAA succeeded spectacularly in sport but did not alter the institutional or educational frameworks of Irish society as fundamentally. Both movements strengthened Irish cultural identity; the League alone reshaped official Irish educational and cultural policy.",
