    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "Q3",
        "question": "During the period 1923-1945, how skilfully did W.T. Cosgrave and Éamon de Valera handle Anglo-Irish relations?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Both W.T. Cosgrave (1923–1932) and Éamon de Valera (1932–1945) demonstrated considerable skill in navigating Anglo-Irish relations during a period of profound constitutional and political transition, though their approaches and achievements differed markedly.\n\nCosgrave's strategy centred on consolidating the Irish Free State's position within the Commonwealth framework established by the 1921 Treaty. Recognising Ireland's economic vulnerability and Britain's residual power, he adopted a gradualist approach that prioritised stability and de facto independence over dramatic constitutional rupture. His key achievement was the effective assertion of dominion autonomy through participation in the Balfour Declaration (1926), which established the principle that dominions possessed equality of status with Britain. This was a skilled diplomatic manoeuvre, secured through cooperation with other dominions, that subtly undermined the hierarchical structure of the Empire without provoking British retaliation. Cosgrave also handled the military dimension astutely: he avoided the militarism that had characterised the revolutionary period, instead building professional security forces loyal to the Free State. His handling of the Boundary Commission (1925), though disappointing to Irish nationalists, demonstrated pragmatism—accepting the status quo with Northern Ireland rather than pursuing costly or destabilising claims that Britain would never concede. This was politically unpopular but strategically sound. However, Cosgrave's deference to British interests—accepting the oath of allegiance to the Crown, retaining the Governor-General, and maintaining constitutional links to Westminster—meant that genuine independence remained incomplete and symbolic rather than substantive. His skill lay in working within constraints, not transcending them.\n\nDe Valera inherited a fundamentally different political mandate and possessed greater room for manoeuvre. His 1932–1945 tenure demonstrated exceptional diplomatic and constitutional skill in dismantling the Treaty settlement while maintaining reasonable Anglo-Irish relations and avoiding military conflict. His strategy was systematically incremental. The abolition of the oath of allegiance (1933), removal of the Governor-General (1936), and the adoption of a new constitution (1937) were all accomplished through legal and constitutional means rather than confrontation. The 1937 Constitution represented de Valera's masterwork: it redefined the state as Éire (not the Free State), asserted national sovereignty, and established an elected president as head of state, fundamentally altering Ireland's constitutional relationship with Britain. Yet de Valera skilfully avoided declaring a republic outright during this period, which might have provoked British intervention or economic retaliation he could ill afford. His Anglo-Irish Trade Agreement negotiations (1938) demonstrated sophisticated economic diplomacy—securing the return of the Treaty ports and resolving the land annuity dispute that had poisoned relations under Cosgrave, while making limited concessions to British interests. The recovery of the ports was particularly significant, removing a major symbol of retained British sovereignty and strengthening Irish strategic independence. De Valera's handling of neutrality during the Second World War (1939–1945) showed strategic acumen: he maintained Ireland's neutrality despite enormous British pressure, thereby consolidating sovereignty and consolidating domestic support, while carefully avoiding actions that would provoke a British military response. His decision to remain neutral rather than join the Allies was controversial but skilfully managed—he avoided providing active assistance to Britain while preventing German bases in Ireland, navigating an extremely delicate diplomatic position.\n\nHowever, de Valera's approach also had limitations. His economic self-sufficiency policies (protection and reduced trade with Britain) created hardship and economic stagnation, suggesting that skilled constitutional manoeuvre did not extend to economic policy. His handling of the IRA and republican militants was sometimes heavy-handed, and his continuation of certain British legal structures and the Commonwealth connection (Éire remained within the British Commonwealth until 1949) meant that the independence he achieved, while genuine and substantial, remained incomplete in republican eyes. Additionally, his failure to secure Irish unity—the Constitution's claim to the 26 counties effectively abandoned the prospect of immediate re-unification—represented a strategic limitation, though this reflected Irish weakness rather than diplomatic failure.\n\nComparison reveals that Cosgrave demonstrated skill within severe constraints, accepting limiting realities and working pragmatically to consolidate the Free State. De Valera possessed greater political capital and deployed it with remarkable effectiveness to transform Ireland's constitutional status and practical independence. De Valera's skill lay in recognising that the Treaty's provisions could be dismantled through legal and constitutional means without provoking British military response—a more ambitious and ultimately more successful project than Cosgrave's consolidation. Where Cosgrave maintained stability, de Valera advanced sovereignty.\n\nIn conclusion, both leaders handled Anglo-Irish relations skilfully within their respective contexts. Cosgrave's skill was defensive and cautious—preserving what had been won while working gradually within dominion frameworks. De Valera's skill was transformative and strategic—systematically dismantling the Treaty settlement while maintaining the pragmatic relationship with Britain necessary to avoid intervention. De Valera ultimately achieved substantially greater progress toward genuine Irish independence, despite working initially from a weaker political position (a minority government in 1932). His record between 1932 and 1945 represents the more impressive diplomatic achievement: Ireland moved from a constrained dominion status to an effectively independent republic in all but name, without military conflict or economic catastrophe. Cosgrave's contribution was essential but foundational; de Valera's was transformative.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
