        "question": "During the period 1949-1989, what changes took place in one or more of the following:\nattitudes towards Irish language and culture; the status of women; housing, schools and amenities?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Between 1949 and 1989, significant changes occurred in attitudes towards Irish language and culture, the status of women, and the provision of housing, schools and amenities in the Republic of Ireland.\n\nRegarding Irish language and culture, the early period saw strong state promotion of Irish as a unifying national symbol. The 1947 Education Act had emphasised Irish in schools, and in 1949 compulsory Irish remained a core subject. However, by the 1960s-1970s, attitudes shifted markedly. The Programme for Economic Expansion (1958) and the Second Programme (1963) prioritised economic development over cultural nationalism. Investment in Irish-language education diminished in real terms as schools focused on English and technical subjects needed for industrialisation. By the late 1970s and 1980s, the Irish language faced declining use outside educational contexts. Gaeltacht regions experienced emigration and economic decline, weakening the language's living base. Conversely, cultural attitudes became more pluralistic—the language was no longer seen as essential to Irish identity by many, and state pressure to learn Irish lessened. By 1989, Irish was still compulsory but treated more pragmatically than as an ideological cornerstone.\n\nThe status of women underwent transformative change. In 1949, Irish women faced significant legal and social restrictions. The Constitution (Bunreacht na hÉireann) enshrined a domestic ideal; married women were largely excluded from public employment. The Civil Service and teaching prohibited married women from remaining employed. Social norms heavily restricted women's economic participation and autonomy. By the 1970s, this began to shift rapidly. The Employment Equality Act (1975) prohibited discrimination in employment and established the Employment Equality Agency. Significantly, the Anti-Discrimination (Pay) Act (1974) established the right to equal pay. Educational access expanded—the number of women in third-level education increased substantially. Legal reforms removed restrictions on married women in employment, particularly after the 1973 Public Service reforms. The Status of Women Committee (1972) examined inequality comprehensively. By 1980, the Juries Act removed the requirement for women jurors to be specially summoned, moving towards genuine equality of civic participation. By 1989, while structural inequality persisted, women had secured legal equality in employment and education, though workplace segregation and pay gaps remained significant practical challenges.\n\nHousing, schools and amenities underwent massive expansion and modernisation. In 1949, Ireland faced serious housing shortage—many families lived in overcrowded, inadequate conditions, particularly in urban areas like Dublin. Rural areas lacked basic amenities; sanitation and running water were not universal. The early 1950s saw slow progress, but from the late 1950s onwards, state-driven development accelerated. Local authorities' housing programmes expanded dramatically: thousands of new local authority houses were built in Dublin and other urban centres during the 1960s and 1970s. Suburban housing estates emerged, transforming urban geography. By the 1980s, home ownership increased, aided by government mortgage schemes and tax incentives. School provision expanded significantly: the Investment in Education report (1965) identified deficiencies, and subsequent state investment modernised school buildings and infrastructure. Educational participation rates rose—the School Leaving Age remained at 14 in 1949 but by 1972 had been raised to 15, and educational access broadened. Basic amenities became near-universal: rural electrification completed; piped water and sewerage extended to previously unserved communities. By 1989, housing standards had improved dramatically, though urban sprawl and social problems in new estates became emerging concerns. Schools benefited from technological upgrades and improved teacher training. Community amenities—health centres, recreational facilities, shopping centres—expanded substantially, reflecting rising living standards and consumer culture.\n\nThese three areas of change interconnected. Economic expansion required educational modernisation and female participation in the workforce, driving legal equality. Urbanisation driven by housing and employment growth created new social structures that challenged traditional attitudes to language and culture. By 1989, the Republic presented a starkly different society from 1949: more economically developed, more legally egalitarian, more materially comfortable, and less defined by cultural nationalism—though older ideals persisted in residual form.",
