    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "Q3",
        "question": "What were the successes and failures of Mikhail Gorbachev as leader of the USSR?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Mikhail Gorbachev's leadership of the USSR (1985–1991) was marked by significant achievements in foreign policy and attempted domestic reform, yet ultimately ended in the Soviet Union's collapse, presenting a complex legacy of success and failure.\n\nGorbachev's most notable success was his transformation of Cold War relations. He pursued 'new thinking' in foreign policy, abandoning the ideological confrontation that had defined Soviet–American relations since 1945. He withdrew Soviet troops from Afghanistan (completed by 1989), ending a costly nine-year conflict that had damaged Soviet prestige and morale. Through arms control negotiations with President Reagan, he signed the Intermediate-Range Nuclear Forces Treaty (1987), which eliminated an entire class of nuclear weapons and marked a decisive shift toward détente. His summit meetings with Reagan and later George H. W. Bush demonstrated genuine commitment to reducing international tensions. By allowing Eastern European states to pursue independent paths—refusing military intervention when Poland, Hungary, and Czechoslovakia abandoned communism in 1989—he inadvertently facilitated the end of Soviet domination in Eastern Europe, though this was not his primary intention. These foreign policy achievements earned him international respect and contributed to the peaceful end of the Cold War.\n\nDomestically, Gorbachev initiated reforms intended to revitalize the Soviet system. His policy of *glasnost* (openness) lifted censorship, permitted public criticism of the government, and allowed open discussion of Soviet history and current problems. This enabled genuine democratic debate for the first time in decades and exposed widespread corruption and inefficiency. *Perestroika* (restructuring) aimed to modernize the economy and improve efficiency by introducing limited market mechanisms and greater enterprise autonomy. He also introduced *demokratizatsiya* (democratization), including multi-candidate elections for the Congress of People's Deputies in 1989, creating the first genuinely contested elections in Soviet history. These reforms demonstrated his sincere desire to transform the USSR into a more open and efficient system.\n\nHowever, Gorbachev's reforms ultimately proved to be failures that destroyed the very system he sought to save. *Glasnost* unleashed forces he could not control. By allowing public criticism, he exposed the Communist Party's crimes, weaknesses, and failures, fatally undermining its authority and legitimacy. Intellectuals, nationalists, and ordinary citizens began questioning not just specific policies but communism itself. The policy inadvertently encouraged separatism: as censorship lifted, national minorities in the Baltic republics (Lithuania, Latvia, Estonia) and the Caucasus demanded independence, and these movements gained irreversible momentum.\n\n*Perestroika* failed to improve the economy. The introduction of half-measures—neither fully planned nor fully market-based—created confusion and disruption. Production fell, shortages worsened, and inflation rose. Consumer goods became increasingly scarce by the late 1980s. Rather than revitalizing the economy, the reform created economic chaos that eroded public confidence in both Gorbachev and the Communist system. By 1990–1991, the Soviet economy was in crisis.\n\nThe attempted democratization, while theoretically progressive, destabilized the system. The Congress of People's Deputies became a forum where radical critics, including Boris Yeltsin, challenged Gorbachev's authority and pushed for faster, more radical change. Yeltsin's election as chairman of the Russian Republic's Supreme Soviet in 1990 created a rival power base. The 1991 Russian presidential election, which Yeltsin won by direct popular vote, gave him greater legitimacy than Gorbachev, who never stood for direct election as Soviet leader. This political fragmentation prevented coherent governance and empowered figures more radical than Gorbachev.\n\nGorbachev's position became increasingly untenable. Hardline communists attempted a coup in August 1991, viewing his reforms as betraying socialism. Though the coup failed, it demonstrated the depth of opposition within the establishment. Simultaneously, Yeltsin and other republican leaders accelerated moves toward independence. The failed coup fatally weakened Gorbachev's authority. By December 1991, the leaders of Russia, Ukraine, and Belarus declared the Soviet Union dissolved and replaced it with a loose Commonwealth of Independent States. Gorbachev resigned on December 25, 1991.\n\nGorbachev's fundamental failure was his contradiction: he attempted to preserve the Soviet system while introducing reforms that made preservation impossible. He could not reconcile communist ideology with genuine openness, nor could he manage the competing demands of hardliners, reformers, and nationalists. Unlike Yeltsin, who embraced radical change, or the hardliners, who opposed all reform, Gorbachev remained trapped in the middle, satisfying neither side. His reforms released centrifugal forces—nationalism, separatism, economic crisis—that tore the USSR apart.\n\nIn conclusion, Gorbachev succeeded in ending the Cold War and transforming Soviet foreign policy, earning international respect and achieving lasting nuclear arms reductions. His attempts at domestic reform were well-intentioned but catastrophically mismanaged. By introducing *glasnost* without controlling its consequences, attempting economic restructuring without coherent strategy, and democratizing without consolidating power, he inadvertently destroyed the Soviet state. His reforms, intended to save communism, instead ensured its collapse. Gorbachev was a transformative but ultimately tragic figure: his successes in foreign policy were overwhelmed by his failures in managing the consequences of his own domestic reforms.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
