        "label": "6",
        "question": "Write a short paragraph on one of the following:\n1. The Tithe War.\n2. Thomas Davis.\n3. Impact of the railways.\n4. James Stephens.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "**The Tithe War**\n\nThe Tithe War (1830–1838) emerged from widespread Catholic resentment at paying tithes to support the Protestant Church of Ireland, a deeply unpopular tax that struck at both religious identity and economic hardship. Catholic tenants, already burdened by rent and existing taxes, were forced to contribute to a church they did not attend, intensifying sectarian grievance. Resistance began in Leinster and Munster, escalating into organised campaigns where communities refused payment and obstructed collection. Confrontations between tithe proctors, police and local populations became increasingly violent; soldiers were deployed to enforce collection, leading to casualties and deepening alienation among Catholics from British rule.\n\nThe movement was significant in several ways. It demonstrated Catholic political consciousness and capacity for collective action against unpopular policies. Leaders like Fr Mathew supported resistance, showing clerical involvement in agrarian unrest. The campaigns caused considerable disruption to landlords' and the church's income, forcing the government to address the grievance. The conflict also revealed the weakness of English authority in rural Ireland when faced with determined popular opposition.\n\nThe Tithe War was ultimately resolved through the Tithe Commutation Act (1838), which converted tithes into a land charge, transferring the burden to landlords rather than tenants. This represented a significant concession to Catholic pressure, though not complete satisfaction. The War highlighted fundamental divisions within Irish society—religious, economic and political—that would remain unresolved into the later nineteenth century. It demonstrated that Irish Catholics would resist policies perceived as unjust, and that constitutional or parliamentary methods alone could not address their grievances. The Tithe War thus contributed to the broader context of Irish discontent that fuelled later movements for reform and Irish independence.",
