        "label": "7",
        "question": "Answer one of the following:\n1. In what ways did private charities respond to the Famine, 1845-1849?\n2. What did William Carleton and/or Charles Kickham contribute to Irish affairs?\n3. In what ways did Mother Mary Aikenhead contribute to Irish society?\n4. What decisions were made at the Synod of Thurles, 1850?",
        "marks": 40,
        "model": "In what ways did private charities respond to the Famine, 1845-1849?\n\nPrivate charities played a vital but limited role in alleviating suffering during the Great Famine. The Society of Friends (Quakers) were the most effective, establishing a Central Relief Committee in Dublin in 1846 that distributed aid directly to stricken areas. They provided food, clothing and organised employment schemes, demonstrating a systematic and compassionate response. Their work was particularly valued because they operated without religious discrimination, assisting both Catholic and Protestant families.\n\nThe British Association for the Relief of Distress in Ireland, established by the Lord Mayor of London, collected substantial donations from wealthy individuals and institutions. These funds were distributed through local committees to purchase food and provide temporary employment. However, the scale of their operation could never match the magnitude of need—millions were starving and the Association's resources were finite.\n\nThe Catholic Church, through individual priests and bishops, provided moral support and some material aid, though the Church as an institution lacked significant financial resources. Various local benevolent societies and landlord-sponsored relief efforts also emerged, though these were inconsistent and often inadequate. Some landlords funded soup kitchens and employment schemes, motivated by both humanitarian concern and self-interest in maintaining their estates.\n\nAmerican charities, particularly Irish-American organisations in Boston and New York, raised funds and sent aid across the Atlantic, demonstrating the growing importance of the Irish diaspora. These donations arrived in irregular shipments and could not prevent mass mortality.\n\nHowever, private charities faced fundamental limitations. The Famine's scale was unprecedented—over one million died and millions more emigrated. Charitable resources, however generous, could not substitute for adequate government relief. Charities often focused on temporary measures rather than addressing structural problems of poverty and land access. Additionally, some charity was conditional or humiliating, requiring religious conversion or workhouse entry.\n\nPrivate charitable response thus revealed both Irish and British compassion but ultimately highlighted the inadequacy of voluntary action in responding to catastrophic national disaster. The Famine demonstrated that large-scale social crises required state intervention on a scale that private charity, no matter how well-intentioned, could never provide.",
