        "label": "21",
        "question": "Write a short paragraph on one of the following:\n1. Ireland and the United Nations.\n2. Anglo-Irish relations, 1949-1989.\n3. Changes in education.\n4. Breandán Ó hEithir.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Ireland's relationship with the United Nations from 1949 to 1989 reflected the state's assertion of independence and active engagement in international affairs. Ireland applied for UN membership in 1946 but faced Soviet vetoes until 1955, when Cold War tensions eased and the Soviet Union finally permitted Ireland's admission alongside sixteen other nations. Once admitted, Ireland became a vocal advocate for decolonization and small-nation interests. Irish representatives, including diplomats like Conor Cruise O'Brien, participated prominently in General Assembly debates, particularly regarding anti-colonialism and human rights. Ireland's UN involvement demonstrated that the Republic had achieved genuine sovereignty—it could now participate as an equal in global forums and articulate independent foreign policy positions rather than remaining merely in Britain's orbit. The UN provided a platform for Ireland to champion causes beyond British influence, including support for newly independent African and Asian states. This international engagement strengthened Ireland's status as a modern nation-state. By the 1980s, Irish UN participation had become institutionalized, with permanent representation and active committees. However, Cold War constraints limited Ireland's ability to take independent stances, as the nation remained non-aligned but Western-oriented. The UN membership nonetheless represented a crucial development: Ireland had graduated from a post-colonial state seeking recognition to an established player in the international system, able to shape debates on matters of principle and contribute to global governance. This UN engagement complemented the Republic's broader assertion of sovereignty during the period.",
