    "year": 2009,
    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "10B",
        "question": "Write a short paragraph on one of the following:\n1. Changes in Irish education, 1949-1989.\n2. Irish involvement in the United Nations (UN).\n3. Archbishop John Charles McQuaid.\n4. Sylvia Meehan.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Irish involvement in the United Nations (UN) represented a significant dimension of Ireland's evolving international role during 1949–1989. Following independence and the declaration of the Republic in 1949, Ireland sought UN membership to assert its sovereignty and participate in global affairs. The country gained membership in 1955, ending a period of exclusion due to Cold War vetoes. From this point, Ireland pursued an active and independent foreign policy within the UN framework. Irish delegates championed decolonisation efforts, supporting emerging nations in Africa and Asia seeking independence from colonial powers. This reflected Ireland's own postcolonial experience and resonated with developing nations. Ireland also maintained a policy of military neutrality, refusing to join NATO while emphasising its UN commitment. The Irish government, particularly under Foreign Minister Frank Aiken, advocated for nuclear disarmament and humanitarian principles. Ireland contributed to UN peacekeeping operations, deploying troops to Cyprus and the Congo, demonstrating practical commitment beyond rhetoric. Within the General Assembly, Irish representatives consistently advocated for smaller nations' rights and the peaceful resolution of international disputes. By the 1980s, Ireland's UN presence had established it as a respected voice for developing and non-aligned nations. This UN involvement allowed Ireland to exercise diplomatic influence disproportionate to its economic or military size, enhancing its international prestige and independence. Through the UN, Ireland shaped its identity as a neutral, humanitarian-focused actor in global politics.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
