    "year": 2009,
    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "10C",
        "question": "Answer one of the following:\n1. What did Seán Lemass achieve as Taoiseach?\n2. What was the impact of RTÉ and Gay Byrne on Irish life?\n3. What was the impact of the EEC on Irish fisheries and/or other aspects of Irish life?\n4. What did Charles Haughey contribute to Irish life?",
        "marks": 40,
        "model": "Seán Lemass achieved fundamental economic transformation as Taoiseach (1959–1966). His Programme for Economic Expansion (1958) represented a decisive break from protectionist inward-looking policies that had caused stagnation and emigration. By opening the economy to foreign investment and export-oriented industry, Lemass attracted multinational corporations that created employment; industrial output grew significantly and emigration reversed. He modernised agriculture through improved techniques and mechanisation, increasing productivity. Lemass prioritised infrastructure development—transport, electricity, communications—essential for industrial growth. He reformed education, recognising that skilled workers were vital to economic development. His government expanded social welfare provision, improving living standards for working-class families. Lemass established the Central Bank and pursued economic planning through successive programmes, demonstrating a scientific approach to development. Diplomatically, he initiated the policy of seeking EEC membership, recognising that integration into European markets was essential for long-term growth; this laid groundwork for Ireland's 1973 entry. He met Seán Ó Bradaigh in 1962, a symbolic gesture towards reconciliation with Northern Ireland. Lemass also promoted tourism as an economic sector. His achievement was transforming Irish self-perception: from a nation accepting economic decline and emigration as inevitable to one actively pursuing modernisation and prosperity. By 1966, Ireland's economy was demonstrably growing; unemployment had fallen; emigration had ceased being a crisis; industrial investment was secured. Living standards rose measurably. Lemass's economic programme did not solve all problems—agriculture remained vulnerable; regional inequality persisted; dependence on multinational corporations created future vulnerabilities—but he fundamentally altered Ireland's economic trajectory and demonstrated that development was possible through strategic state intervention and openness to the world economy. His legacy was an Ireland confident in its capacity for progress.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
