        "label": "B",
        "question": "Write a short paragraph on one of the following:\n1. Countess Markievicz\n2. Evie Hone\n3. The economy of Northern Ireland, 1920-1939\n4. Developments in education, 1922-1949",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Countess Constance Markievicz (1868–1927) was a transformative figure in Irish nationalism and women's political activism. Born into the Anglo-Irish aristocratic Gore-Booth family, she embraced Irish republicanism and became a founding member of Sinn Féin in 1905. During the 1916 Easter Rising, she served as second-in-command of the St Stephen's Green garrison and was among those arrested and court-martialed; her death sentence was commuted owing to her sex, but her imprisonment strengthened her iconic status. After the Rising, Markievicz emerged as a leading voice in Sinn Féin's reorganization and was elected to the First Dáil in 1919, becoming the first woman elected to the British House of Commons (though she refused to take her seat). However, she opposed the Anglo-Irish Treaty of 1921, aligning with the anti-treaty faction in the Civil War, and was imprisoned again. Despite her radicalism, Markievicz remained committed to Irish labour and women's causes throughout her life, serving as Minister for Labour in the republican government-in-exile. Her legacy reflects the complexity of early Irish independence: a woman of privilege who became a revolutionary symbol, yet whose opposition to the Treaty meant she was sidelined by the Free State government that ultimately consolidated Irish sovereignty. She died in relative poverty in 1927, overshadowed by the new state's consolidation, yet her pioneering role in combining feminism with Irish republicanism secured her historical significance.",
