        "label": "B",
        "question": "Write a short paragraph on one of the following:\n1. Anti-Semitism in France\n2. Economic crisis in Tsarist Russia\n3. Why European countries wanted to expand their overseas empires\n4. Woodrow Wilson and Europe",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Anti-Semitism in France was deeply rooted in both religious prejudice and modern racial ideology. The Dreyfus Affair (1894–1906) exposed the extent of institutionalised anti-Semitism within French military and political establishments. Captain Alfred Dreyfus, a Jewish officer, was falsely accused of selling military secrets to Germany and convicted on fabricated evidence. Despite his innocence being eventually proven, the case revealed virulent anti-Semitic sentiment among French elites and the public, with prominent figures like General Esterhazy and others within the army and judiciary conspiring to suppress evidence that would clear Dreyfus. Anti-Semitic newspapers, notably Édouard Drumont's *La Libre Parole*, exploited the affair to stir up hatred against Jews, portraying them as a threat to French national security and identity. The scandal divided French society between Dreyfusards (supporters of Dreyfus) and anti-Dreyfusards (opponents), demonstrating how anti-Semitism was weaponised for nationalist and political purposes. The affair strengthened the belief among many French citizens that Jews were not truly French and harboured foreign loyalties. Although Dreyfus was eventually exonerated and reinstated, the episode solidified anti-Semitic attitudes in French culture and highlighted how prejudice could permeate even institutions supposedly bound by justice and rational procedure. The Dreyfus Affair remains a watershed moment illustrating the fragility of Jewish integration in late nineteenth-century France and the persistence of anti-Semitism as a mobilising force in European politics.",
