        "question": "Write a short paragraph on one of the following:\n1. Vladimir Ilyich Lenin\n2. Fascism in Italy\n3. Hitler's foreign policy, 1933-1939\n4. Winston Churchill",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "**Fascism in Italy**\n\nFascism emerged in Italy after World War I amid economic crisis, national humiliation, and fear of socialist revolution. Benito Mussolini, a former socialist journalist, founded the Fascist movement in 1919, establishing the Fasci Italiani di Combattimento. Fascism appealed to discontented war veterans, middle-class business owners, and landowners alarmed by socialist strikes and factory occupations. The movement offered ultra-nationalism, strong leadership, and action against perceived enemies—communists, socialists, and the weak liberal state. Mussolini's blackshirts brutally attacked socialists and communists in street violence, particularly during 1920–1922, weakening parliamentary democracy. The liberal government, unable to maintain order, invited Mussolini to form a government in 1922. Once in power, Mussolini gradually dismantled democratic institutions. By 1925–1926, he transformed Italy into a one-party dictatorship, eliminating parliamentary opposition and establishing totalitarian control through propaganda, secret police (OVRA), and corporatist organizations. The state controlled industry, labour, education, and culture. Mussolini pursued imperial expansion in Abyssinia (1935–1936), defying the League of Nations and drawing Italy closer to Nazi Germany. The Rome-Berlin Axis (1936) aligned Italian and German fascism. Italian fascism combined extreme nationalism with hierarchical state organization, cult of the leader, militarism, and aggressive foreign policy. Unlike Nazism, Italian fascism was not primarily racial in ideology, though Mussolini later introduced racial laws under German influence. Fascism in Italy demonstrated how economic instability, national resentment, and fear of radical change could enable an authoritarian movement to destroy liberal democracy and establish totalitarian rule in a modern European nation.",
