    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "3",
        "question": "During the period 1932-1945, which did Éamon de Valera manage better, the economy or Anglo-Irish relations?\nArgue your case, referring to both.",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "During 1932–1945, Éamon de Valera managed Anglo-Irish relations more effectively than the economy, though both domains presented severe difficulties.\n\nEconomically, de Valera's record was mixed to poor. The Economic War (1932–1938) with Britain, initiated over land annuities, severely damaged Irish agriculture and trade. Tariffs imposed by both nations reduced exports: Irish cattle exports fell dramatically, hurting farmers who formed de Valera's political base. Unemployment remained high throughout the 1930s, with emigration continuing. While de Valera pursued protectionist industrialisation through Seán Lemass's policies, encouraging native manufacturing and reducing reliance on imports, results were limited. Self-sufficiency proved elusive; Irish industry remained small-scale and uncompetitive. The 1938 Anglo-Irish Trade Agreement ended the Economic War but only partially restored trade. World War II (1939–1945) further damaged the economy: neutrality prevented access to British markets and materials; rationing and shortages caused hardship; emigration accelerated. By 1945, living standards had not recovered to 1932 levels. De Valera's economic strategy, though theoretically sound in pursuing independence, delivered disappointing material results.\n\nHowever, de Valera achieved significantly more in Anglo-Irish relations. The Economic War, though economically costly, was politically purposeful: it asserted Irish sovereignty over the land annuities issue, refusing British financial claims. The 1938 Trade Agreement, negotiated after six years of tension, represented a diplomatic success—de Valera recovered the Treaty ports (Berehaven, Queenstown, Lough Swilly), removing the last major British military presence from Irish soil. This strengthened Ireland's strategic independence and was a genuine foreign-policy triumph. De Valera's 1937 Constitution (Bunreacht na hÉireann) fundamentally restructured Anglo-Irish relations by repudiating the Free State's dominion status: it declared Ireland a sovereign republic in all but name (the formal Republic came only in 1949). Though Britain protested, it acquiesced. De Valera also successfully managed Irish neutrality during World War II, resisting enormous British and American pressure to abandon neutrality. Despite this isolation, he maintained diplomatic relations with Britain and avoided military conflict. The fact that Ireland remained formally at peace with Britain while refusing to join the war demonstrated skillful political navigation. De Valera's foreign policy, whether through negotiation (1938 Agreement) or assertion of sovereignty (1937 Constitution), consistently advanced Irish independence without provoking British military intervention—a genuine achievement given Ireland's geographic proximity and economic dependence on Britain.\n\nThe contrast is clear: Anglo-Irish relations improved measurably between 1932 and 1945 in Ireland's favour, moving from conflict (Economic War) to codified independence (Constitution, recovered ports); the economy stagnated, never recovering from depression-era collapse and wartime hardship. De Valera's political priority—sovereignty—was realised; his economic programme, subordinated to that priority, failed to deliver prosperity. In this sense, he managed what mattered most to him and his constituency very well, while accepting economic costs as the price of independence.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
