    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "3",
        "question": "What were the main social and economic challenges facing Britain, 1920-1945?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Britain faced profound social and economic challenges in the interwar period and during World War II, which fundamentally threatened its political stability and great power status.\n\nThe most immediate postwar economic problem was industrial decline and unemployment. Britain's staple industries—coal, textiles, iron and steel, and shipbuilding—contracted severely as international competition intensified and demand fell. The coal industry, employing over 1 million workers, faced a structural crisis: German and Polish coal flooded world markets while British pits suffered from outdated equipment and high production costs. Unemployment rose dramatically from 2% in 1920 to 11.3% by 1921, remaining stubbornly high throughout the 1920s, averaging 10-12%. By 1932, during the Great Depression, unemployment reached 22%—over 3 million people. Regional disparities were acute: industrial regions of South Wales, the North East, and Lancashire experienced unemployment rates of 30-40%, creating concentrated zones of social despair. The cotton industry declined from 8 million spindles in 1912 to 4 million by 1939, destroying communities dependent on textile production.\n\nThe return to the gold standard in 1925 at prewar parity—Churchill's decision as Chancellor—severely hampered recovery. Sterling was overvalued, making British exports uncompetitive and intensifying industrial decline. The policy particularly damaged already-struggling staple industries and contributed directly to the 1926 General Strike, when mine owners demanded wage cuts. This nine-day strike involved 1.7 million workers and paralyzed the economy, revealing deep class tensions and the vulnerability of social cohesion.\n\nAgricultural decline compounded economic difficulties. British farming contracted as cheap imported food flooded the market, particularly after American and Commonwealth producers increased output. Agricultural employment fell from 900,000 in 1920 to 600,000 by 1939. Rural communities experienced economic marginalization, and agricultural workers endured poor wages and working conditions.\n\nThe Great Depression from 1929 onwards intensified all these problems catastrophically. International trade collapsed; British exports fell 50% between 1929 and 1932. Manufacturing output declined sharply, and the financial crisis of 1931 forced Britain off the gold standard, creating currency instability. This global crisis exposed the structural weaknesses of the interwar British economy, revealing that prosperity had never been firmly established during the 1920s.\n\nSocial consequences of economic failure were severe. Poverty remained endemic: studies by Rowntree and Orwell documented how millions lived below subsistence level. Overcrowded, insanitary housing characterized working-class areas, particularly in industrial cities. Malnutrition was widespread, especially among children in depressed regions. Infant mortality and mortality rates from tuberculosis and other poverty-related diseases remained high. The \"Hungry Thirties\" saw hunger marches, including the 1936 Jarrow March by 200 unemployed workers, highlighting desperation in devastated communities.\n\nLabour relations were fractured. The General Strike's failure strengthened middle-class fears of working-class militancy while embittering workers. Trade union membership declined in the 1920s as unemployment weakened bargaining power. Wage cuts were imposed across industries; in mining, wages fell 20-30% between 1920 and 1926. The resulting alienation made workers receptive to radical politics, with the Communist Party gaining support, though the Labour Party remained the main alternative to Conservative dominance.\n\nFamily and social structures came under strain. High unemployment meant delayed marriages and reduced family size: the birth rate fell from 19.8 per 1,000 in 1920 to 14.3 by 1939. Male unemployment created psychological trauma, emasculation, and family breakdown. Crime rates increased in depressed areas. Alcoholism, particularly among unemployed men, increased. Suicide rates rose in regions of high unemployment. Children in poor families suffered educational disadvantage; school attendance in depressed areas was erratic, and many left school at the minimum age with minimal literacy.\n\nThe housing crisis was acute. Slums dominated working-class cities. Between 1920 and 1939, approximately 1 million council houses were built, but this failed to meet demand. Homelessness increased during the Depression. Poor housing standards contributed to disease transmission, particularly tuberculosis, which remained the leading infectious cause of death.\n\nWorld War II initially worsened some social problems through disruption but ultimately transformed others. The Blitz created vast homelessness—over 1 million people were bombed out of homes in 1940-41. However, full employment returned as rearmament and then war production demanded all available labour. By 1940, unemployment had fallen to 1.2%. This dramatic reversal proved that the mass unemployment of the 1920s and 1930s was not inevitable but resulted from policy choices and structural problems.\n\nWar production brought social dislocation: evacuation separated millions of mothers from children, disrupting family life. Rationing, introduced in 1940, was strictly enforced, creating equality of hardship across social classes. The Blitz demonstrated both community solidarity and the inadequacy of civil defence planning. Mortality from bombing reached approximately 40,000 civilians by 1945.\n\nYet war also generated social cohesion and reformist expectations. Shared sacrifice under bombing, full employment, and evacuation experiences generated demands for social reconstruction. The 1942 Beveridge Report's proposals for comprehensive social insurance resonated because war had made social problems collectively visible and solutions seemed achievable.\n\nBritain's interwar social and economic challenges reflected deeper structural transformations: the decline of nineteenth-century staple industries, loss of competitive advantage in global trade, and the burden of maintaining great power status with diminishing economic resources. Policy failures—particularly regarding the gold standard and the insufficient management of industrial decline—exacerbated these underlying problems. The result was regional economic collapse, persistent mass unemployment, endemic poverty, fractured social cohesion, and heightened class tension. These challenges were never resolved during the 1930s; only war's demands for total mobilization finally eliminated unemployment and generated conditions for the postwar social democratic consensus.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
