        "marks": 20,
        "model": "Arguments in favour of Sunningdale stressed that power-sharing between nationalist and unionist communities offered a genuine path to political stability and reduced sectarian conflict. Proponents argued that the Council of Ireland would provide a cross-border institutional framework facilitating cooperation on practical matters, easing unionist fears of Irish unity while reassuring nationalists of protection for their interests. The agreement was presented as a democratic compromise, reflecting the moderate SDLP and unionist politicians' commitment to consensual governance rather than majoritarian dominance.\n\nOpponents rejected Sunningdale fundamentally. Hard-line unionists, particularly Ian Paisley and militant loyalists, viewed power-sharing as a threat to unionist supremacy and interpreted the Council of Ireland as a stepping stone toward Irish unity, undermining their constitutional position. Republican critics and Sinn Féin dismissed the agreement as insufficient—it fell short of a united Ireland and merely offered token representation within a partitioned state. Loyalist paramilitaries and hardline unionists mobilized intense resistance, with the Ulster Workers' Council strike of May 1974 demonstrating that street-level unionist opposition could override the agreement politically. The speed of Sunningdale's collapse, after just five months, revealed that neither extreme could accept genuine power-sharing, fundamentally questioning whether institutional reform alone could overcome the deep communal divisions that defined Northern Ireland's politics.",
