        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Young Ireland was a nationalist movement founded in 1842 by Thomas Davis that profoundly shaped Irish politics in the 1840s. Operating through the Nation newspaper, Young Ireland combined cultural nationalism with political agitation, promoting Irish language, literature, and history to foster national identity and self-respect among Catholics. The movement attracted thousands of educated middle-class members and initially cooperated with Daniel O'Connell's Repeal Association, though tensions emerged when Young Ireland opposed O'Connell's non-violent constitutionalism. The Famine of 1845-1852 radicalized Young Ireland; leaders like John Mitchel argued that armed rebellion was justified against a government failing to protect the Irish people. The movement's significance lay in shifting Irish nationalism from constitutional methods toward revolutionary ideology. Though their 1848 rebellion failed, Young Ireland's intellectual legacy proved enduring. They demonstrated that Irish nationalism could appeal to an educated constituency beyond the peasantry, and their emphasis on cultural revival influenced later nationalist movements. Young Ireland thus marked a critical transition in nineteenth-century Irish history, establishing the template for revolutionary nationalism that would dominate Irish politics until independence.",
