        "label": "1C",
        "question": "Answer one of the following:\n1. How did Daniel O'Connell conduct the campaign for Catholic Emancipation and what was its outcome?\n2. What decisions were made at the Synod of Thurles, 1850?\n3. What industrial development took place in Belfast, 1815-1870, and how did it affect the people of the area?\n4. What did William Carleton and/or Charles Kickham contribute to Irish affairs?",
        "marks": 40,
        "model": "Daniel O'Connell conducted the Catholic Emancipation campaign through mass mobilization and constitutional methods. He founded the Catholic Association in 1823, which organized rent-paying Catholics into a disciplined political force. The Association collected the 'Catholic Rent'—a penny-a-month subscription from ordinary Catholics—generating unprecedented funds and demonstrating Catholic organizational power. O'Connell employed peaceful agitation, holding mass meetings and monster meetings, particularly in Ireland, to build pressure for change without provoking violent confrontation.\n\nO'Connell's most dramatic act was winning the Clare election in 1828. Though elected to Parliament as MP for County Clare, he could not take his seat because Catholics were barred from Parliament. This created a constitutional crisis: the government faced either backing down or coercing a Catholic majority in an Irish county. Prime Minister Wellington and Home Secretary Peel concluded that Catholic Emancipation was necessary to prevent potential rebellion.\n\nThe outcome was the Catholic Emancipation Act of 1829, which removed the major disabilities affecting Catholics. Catholics gained the right to sit in Parliament, hold judicial office, and access the professions—achievements O'Connell had consistently demanded. However, the Act simultaneously raised the property qualification for voting (the 40-shilling freeholder franchise), disenfranchising most Catholic voters and partially offsetting Catholic political gains.\n\nO'Connell's campaign succeeded through exploiting constitutional methods and demonstrating that organized Catholic political pressure could force Westminster's hand. His approach proved that change could be achieved without violent rebellion, establishing a model of constitutional nationalism. Yet Emancipation remained incomplete: the franchise restriction and continued Irish grievances over land and governance meant that Catholic political discontent persisted and eventually drove the Repeal movement that O'Connell led in the 1840s.",
