        "marks": 30,
        "model": "The Marshall Plan, officially the European Recovery Program announced by US Secretary of State George Marshall in June 1948, provided crucial economic aid to rebuild Western Europe after World War II. The United States allocated approximately $13 billion (roughly $150 billion in modern currency) to assist sixteen Western European nations over four years. Marshall Aid aimed to prevent economic collapse, which American policymakers feared could drive Western Europe towards communism, particularly given the appeal of Soviet expansion during the early Cold War. Recipients including Britain, France, West Germany, and Italy used funds to reconstruct devastated infrastructure, factories, and agricultural systems, facilitating rapid economic recovery. The aid proved highly effective: by 1952, Western European production exceeded pre-war levels, and living standards improved significantly. Marshall Aid strengthened Western European ties to the United States and reinforced the division of Europe, as Stalin prohibited Soviet-dominated Eastern European states from accepting assistance, deepening the East-West divide. The plan demonstrated American commitment to containment strategy while simultaneously creating economic interdependence between the US and Western Europe. Marshall Aid remains historically significant as it established the principle of large-scale international development assistance and contributed substantially to Western European economic integration and political stability during the critical post-war period.",
