        "label": "5B",
        "question": "Write a short paragraph on one of the following:\n1. Immigration patterns and policies in Britain, 1945-1990.\n2. The Suez Crisis, 1956.\n3. The 'winds of change' in Africa.\n4. Achmad Sukarno.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "The Suez Crisis of 1956 marked a decisive turning point in Britain's imperial decline and demonstrated the limits of British power in the post-war world. When Egypt's President Gamal Abdel Nasser nationalized the Suez Canal in July 1956, British Prime Minister Anthony Eden viewed this as a threat to vital British interests and a challenge to British prestige. Britain and France, without UN approval, coordinated with Israel to invade Egypt in October 1956. The invasion initially achieved military objectives, but international pressure—particularly from the United States and the Soviet Union—forced a humiliating British withdrawal by December 1956. The crisis exposed Britain's financial weakness; the pound sterling came under pressure and American support was withheld, compelling Eden to abandon the operation. The Suez Crisis had profound consequences for British imperialism. It demonstrated that Britain could no longer act unilaterally in world affairs and signalled to colonial territories that British military power was no longer invincible. The crisis accelerated decolonization throughout Africa and Asia, as nationalist movements gained confidence that independence could be achieved. It also marked the end of Britain's role as a great power acting independently; henceforth, Britain would operate within the framework of American Cold War strategy. For Eden personally, the crisis ended his political career. Suez thus represented not merely a military setback but a watershed moment in the decline of the British Empire, convincing both Britain's political leadership and colonial populations that the age of British imperial dominance had definitively ended.",
