        "label": "5C",
        "question": "Answer one of the following:\n1. What difficulties were associated with British withdrawal from India, 1945-1947?\n2. How did Charles de Gaulle respond to the demand for Algerian independence?\n3. Why did problems arise in race relations in France in the 1980s and how did French governments respond?\n4. What did you learn about the life and writings of Nadine Gordimer and/or English as a world language?",
        "marks": 40,
        "model": "Charles de Gaulle responded to Algerian independence demands through a strategy that evolved from military suppression to eventual acceptance. Initially, de Gaulle opposed decolonization. When he returned to power in 1958, he faced the Algerian War (1954–1962), where nationalist FLN insurgents demanded independence from French rule. De Gaulle first attempted to maintain French control, deploying significant military forces and proposing integration policies to keep Algeria within France. However, he gradually recognized that military victory was unattainable and that continued war drained French resources and damaged France's international standing.\n\nBy 1961–1962, de Gaulle shifted towards negotiation. He proposed the concept of \"Algérie française\" initially, but ultimately accepted Algerian self-determination. The Evian Accords (March 1962) granted Algeria independence following a referendum. This decision reflected de Gaulle's pragmatic reassessment: maintaining colonial control contradicted his vision of a strong, modern France focused on European grandeur and international influence. Decolonization, though costly, allowed France to redirect resources toward economic development and European leadership, particularly within the emerging European Economic Community.\n\nDe Gaulle's shift provoked significant domestic opposition. Right-wing groups and French settlers in Algeria (pieds-noirs) vehemently opposed independence, fearing loss of economic interests and security. The OAS (Organisation de l'Armée Secrète), an extremist group, launched terrorist attacks in France and Algeria to prevent withdrawal. De Gaulle survived multiple assassination attempts, demonstrating the intensity of resistance to his policy reversal.\n\nYet de Gaulle maintained political control through appeals to national unity and French grandeur. He framed decolonization not as defeat but as liberation from a costly burden, positioning France to lead a new European order. By granting independence while negotiating favorable terms protecting French economic interests and language rights, de Gaulle preserved French influence in former colonies through the Francophone community.\n\nDe Gaulle's response thus balanced recognition of colonial nationalism with preservation of French power. His pragmatic acceptance of Algerian independence, despite fierce internal opposition, ultimately enabled France to modernize while maintaining cultural and economic influence in North Africa and beyond.",
