        "marks": 30,
        "model": "The US economic boom, 1945–1968, transformed America into the world's dominant economic power. Following World War II, US manufacturing capacity expanded dramatically while European and Asian competitors rebuilt from destruction. The GNP rose from $213 billion in 1945 to over $860 billion by 1968, driven by defence spending, industrial production, and consumer demand. The Interstate Highway System, authorised in 1956, facilitated commerce and suburbanisation. The 'organisation man' culture of the 1950s emphasised corporate employment and conformity, with major corporations like General Motors and Ford expanding operations. Consumer goods—automobiles, televisions, refrigerators—became accessible to the expanding middle class. However, this prosperity was unequally distributed. While white suburban families benefited from FHA-backed mortgages and rising wages, African Americans and rural poor remained excluded from economic gains. By the mid-1960s, structural unemployment and poverty persisted despite overall growth. The Vietnam War's escalation after 1965 diverted resources and fuelled inflation, beginning the boom's decline. Technological advancement in computing and aerospace supported growth, but the boom's foundation relied on Cold War military expenditure and the competitive advantage of undamaged US industry—advantages that diminished as competitors recovered and social tensions mounted.",
