        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Isabella Tod was a prominent Ulster-born reformer who championed multiple progressive causes in late nineteenth-century Ireland. Born in 1823 into a prosperous merchant family in Belfast, Tod advocated fiercely for women's education, temperance, and crucially, women's suffrage. In 1872 she founded the Ulster Women's Liberal Association, giving women a direct voice in political activism during an era when female political participation was severely restricted. Her work extended beyond Ulster; she was instrumental in establishing the Irish Women's Suffrage and Local Government Association in 1876, which coordinated campaigns across the island for women to obtain voting rights and control over local government. Tod's significance lay in her bridging of feminist and nationalist concerns—she argued that Irish women's emancipation was integral to Ireland's broader struggle for self-determination. She engaged with moderate nationalists like Isaac Butt and Charles Stewart Parnell, seeking their support for women's causes. Though she died in 1896, before women's suffrage was achieved, Tod's pioneering organizational work created a template for twentieth-century feminist activism in Ireland. Her legacy demonstrates how reform movements of 1870-1914 were not monolithic but encompassed diverse struggles for social justice, with women reformers like Tod pushing against both imperial constraints and patriarchal structures within Irish society itself. Her career exemplifies the interconnection between Irish political reform and women's rights during this transformative period.",
