        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Anti-Semitism in France was a significant social and political phenomenon in the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, rooted in religious prejudice, economic resentment, and nationalist ideology. The Dreyfus Affair (1894–1906) crystallized these tensions and revealed the depth of anti-Semitic feeling within French society. Alfred Dreyfus, a Jewish officer in the French army, was falsely convicted of espionage and treason, based on fabricated evidence. The case became a defining national controversy, polarizing French society between those who defended Dreyfus (including Émile Zola, whose open letter 'J'accuse!' challenged the military establishment) and his opponents, who exploited and amplified anti-Jewish sentiment. The Dreyfus Affair exposed how anti-Semitism operated across institutions—the military, judiciary, press, and Church—and how it intersected with concerns about national security and French honour. The affair demonstrated that anti-Semitism was not merely private prejudice but was embedded in state structures and exploited for political purposes. Although Dreyfus was eventually exonerated and reinstated, the affair exposed that anti-Semitism retained powerful institutional and ideological support in France. The case became a watershed moment in European history, illustrating how prejudice against Jews could mobilize political movements and divide nations, and serving as a warning of the dangers of unchecked anti-Semitism in democratic societies during a period of mounting international tensions.",
