        "marks": 30,
        "model": "The multinational corporation became a defining feature of post-war American economic expansion and global influence. In the years 1945–1968, US corporations rapidly extended operations across Europe, Latin America, and Asia, driven by capital availability, technological superiority, and Cold War geopolitical strategy. Companies like Ford, General Motors, and Standard Oil established subsidiaries and manufacturing plants in host nations, transferring capital, expertise, and management practices. This expansion generated substantial profits for American shareholders and created employment in recipient countries, contributing to economic growth in Western Europe during reconstruction. However, multinationals exercised considerable power over local economies and governments. They extracted raw materials and profits, often with minimal taxation or regulation in host countries. In Latin America particularly, US corporations backed by CIA intervention secured favourable concessions; the United Fruit Company's dominance in Guatemala exemplified corporate influence in overthrowing elected governments perceived as communist threats. Multinationals also displaced local industries and created economic dependency. By 1968, American corporations controlled significant sectors of global commerce, establishing a pattern of economic imperialism that critics argued perpetuated underdevelopment in poorer nations. While multinationals represented American efficiency and innovation, their operations reflected Cold War priorities and prioritized profit over equitable development, embedding structural inequalities that persisted beyond the period.",
