        "question": "What did Charles Stewart Parnell contribute to land reform and to the development of the Home Rule movement?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Charles Stewart Parnell was the preeminent figure in Irish nationalism between 1880 and his death in 1891, transforming both land reform and Home Rule into disciplined, achievable political objectives. His contributions to each were substantial and interconnected.\n\nOn land reform, Parnell grasped that agrarian unrest was the engine of nationalist momentum. The Land War of 1879-1882 erupted during severe agricultural depression when tenant farmers faced eviction for non-payment of rents. Parnell, as President of the Irish Land League founded in 1879, mobilised this grievance into systematic political pressure. He articulated the \"Three Fs\"—Fair Rent, Fixity of Tenure, and Free Sale—as the reformist platform, demands that reconciled nationalist sentiment with practical economics. Crucially, Parnell endorsed the tactic of boycotting landlords and their agents; when Captain Boycott was targeted in County Mayo in 1880, the very term entered political vocabulary, demonstrating the effectiveness of Parnell's strategy. This was not mere agitation—it was calculated leverage. When arrested in October 1881 under Coercion Act measures, Parnell issued the \"No Rent Manifesto\" from Kilmainham Jail, instructing tenants to withhold rent entirely, a dramatic escalation that forced the government to negotiate.\n\nThe result was the Kilmainham Treaty (May 1882), brokered between Parnell and Chief Secretary Lord Frederick Cavendish. Parnell secured government agreement to address rent arrears for tenants in distress, effectively validating the Land League's core demands. This culminated in the Ashbourne Act (1885), which Parnell supported, introducing state-backed loans allowing tenants to purchase holdings. Parnell thus converted agrarian disorder into legislative reform, fundamentally altering the relationship between landlord and tenant in Ireland.\n\nOn Home Rule, Parnell's achievement was even more transformative. Elected MP for Cork in 1875, he entered Parliament as a relatively unknown figure but rapidly emerged as leader of the Irish Parliamentary Party by 1880. His genius lay in unifying fractious Irish nationalism under strict party discipline. The \"obstructionist\" tactics he initially employed—filibustering in Parliament to obstruct business until Irish grievances received attention—brought him notoriety but also demonstrated that Irish MPs could force Westminster's hand through procedural leverage. More significantly, Parnell reoriented Irish politics away from sporadic violence toward constitutional agitation.\n\nHe committed the Irish Parliamentary Party to Home Rule as the non-negotiable objective: Irish legislative independence within the union. Under his leadership, the party grew from a scattered grouping into a disciplined bloc of 86 MPs by 1885, commanding decisive influence in a hung Parliament. Parnell recognised that Home Rule required English Liberal support. His meeting with Liberal leader William Ewart Gladstone in 1889 symbolised this strategic realignment. When Gladstone introduced the First Home Rule Bill in 1886, Parnell's party provided essential backing, though the bill failed in the Commons. Parnell had achieved the historic breakthrough: Home Rule was now Liberal policy, supported by the organised force of Irish nationalism and backed by strategic parliamentary arithmetic.\n\nParnell's method was to render Home Rule inevitable through two mechanisms: first, making Irish constituencies ungovernable without concession, and second, making Irish parliamentary votes indispensable to any Liberal government commanding a Commons majority. His 1885 \"election manifesto\" to Irish-Americans—urging them to support only candidates pledging Home Rule—demonstrated his grasp of transatlantic leverage. By 1890, Parnell had positioned Home Rule not as an aspiration but as a practical political necessity.\n\nHowever, Parnell's personal downfall in 1890-1891 following the O'Shea divorce scandal fractured his achievement. His affair with Katharine O'Shea, revealed when her estranged husband sued for divorce naming Parnell, destroyed his moral standing among the Catholic hierarchy and sections of the party. The party split, with 45 MPs following John Redmond against Parnell's 27 loyalists. Gladstone, responsive to Irish Catholic opinion, announced he could not work with Parnell. By February 1891, Parnell was rejected even by his own Cork constituency. He died in October 1891, only forty-five years old.\n\nYet Parnell's legacy endured. Land reform continued under his successors: the Wyndham Act (1903) substantially completed the transfer of Irish land to peasant proprietorship, fulfilling the vision Parnell had articulated. On Home Rule, despite the party's post-Parnell fragmentation under Redmond's reunified leadership from 1900, the commitment remained constant. The Third Home Rule Bill passed in 1914, only suspended by the First World War. Parnell had shifted Irish nationalism from revolutionary fantasy to constitutional reality; subsequent leaders merely inherited and consolidated his achievement.\n\nParnell's dual contribution—reforming the land question through organised agitation and constructing Home Rule as inevitable Liberal policy through disciplined parliamentary power—made him arguably the most consequential Irish political figure of the nineteenth century. He proved that constitutional methods, when ruthlessly disciplined and strategically deployed, could extract substantial concessions from Westminster. His tragedy was personal rather than political: his death interrupted but did not reverse the momentum toward Irish self-government that his genius had set in motion.",
