        "label": "2",
        "question": "Who handled Anglo-Irish relations better, W. T. Cosgrave or Éamon de Valera? Argue your case, referring to both.",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "W.T. Cosgrave and Éamon de Valera pursued fundamentally different strategies in Anglo-Irish relations, each with distinct outcomes. De Valera ultimately handled them more effectively, though Cosgrave established crucial foundations.\n\nCosgrave's approach was pragmatic but constrained. As head of the Free State government (1922–1932), he accepted the Treaty settlement and worked within the dominion framework. His key achievements were limited but solid: he stabilized the new state's finances through Kevin O'Higgins's rigorous fiscal policies, established the Civil Service, and gradually increased Irish autonomy through the Balfour Declaration (1926), which affirmed dominion independence in foreign policy. However, Cosgrave's acceptance of the oath of allegiance to the Crown symbolized his fundamental accommodation with British sovereignty. The oath remained a flashpoint in Irish politics and undermined his credibility among nationalists. His policy of gradual constitutional evolution was cautious—he did not challenge the Governor-General's constitutional role meaningfully, nor did he advance Irish control over defence, currency, or external affairs. When the Great War struck in 1939, the Free State's limited independence became evident: Cosgrave's settlement left Britain with treaty ports in Berehaven, Queenstown, and Lough Swilly, which remained British strategic assets. Though Cosgrave maintained relative stability, his approach locked Ireland into a subordinate dominion status that fell short of true sovereignty.\n\nDe Valera's strategy was transformative and assertive. Upon taking office in 1932, he systematically dismantled the Treaty's restrictions. First, he removed the oath of allegiance (1933), a symbolic but powerful gesture that restored morale to anti-Treaty republicans. Second, he eliminated the Governor-General's political role through the 1936 Constitution, reducing that office to a figurehead. Third, he negotiated the Anglo-Irish Trade Agreement (1938), which resolved the economic war (1932–1938) that had crippled Irish agriculture and exports but demonstrated his willingness to challenge British interests directly. Most significantly, de Valera secured the return of the treaty ports through the 1938 agreements, a major diplomatic victory that enhanced Irish strategic independence. When World War II began, de Valera's constitutional position allowed Ireland to declare neutrality—a defining assertion of sovereignty that would have been impossible under Cosgrave's framework. The 1937 Constitution, de Valera's flagship achievement, formally reasserted Irish independence, explicitly claimed the whole island as Irish national territory, and positioned the president as head of state rather than a British-appointed Governor-General.\n\nDe Valera's handling was superior in outcomes. He recovered territory (the ports), reasserted symbols of sovereignty (the oath, the constitution), and created genuine strategic autonomy (neutrality in 1939). While his economic war caused hardship and his protectionist policies had mixed results, these were domestic costs of pursuing independence. Cosgrave's settlement, by contrast, left Ireland dependent on British goodwill and constrained by treaty obligations that ranked as fundamental constitutional law. The contrast was starkest in 1939: Cosgrave's framework would likely have obligated Irish participation in the British war effort; de Valera's independence allowed him to resist it.\n\nHowever, Cosgrave deserves credit for what he prevented. He maintained democratic institutions during a dangerous period when the Free State faced IRA insurgency and civil instability. His financial orthodoxy, though unpopular, ensured the state's economic credibility. His cautious approach avoided provoking a British military response that might have reversed Irish independence altogether. He also accepted the Balfour Declaration, which provided the constitutional argument de Valera later exploited: the idea that dominions were equal to Britain, not subordinate.\n\nYet Cosgrave's limitations were structural. He believed evolution within the dominion system was possible; it was not. The Statute of Westminster (1931) did not genuinely empower dominions—Britain retained control over dominion foreign policy in practice. De Valera recognized this impasse and chose confrontation, which proved more effective. His removal of the oath, his 1937 Constitution, and his recovery of the ports were not concessions granted by Britain but achievements extracted through negotiation backed by nationalist political strength and Britain's reduced power after World War I.\n\nIn conclusion, de Valera handled Anglo-Irish relations better. His outcomes—genuine sovereignty, constitutional independence, recovery of territory, and the freedom to declare neutrality—represent substantive gains for Irish independence. Cosgrave's achievements, though important for stabilizing the state, left Ireland in a subordinate position within the British Commonwealth. De Valera transformed that relationship, moving Ireland from dominion status to near-republican independence, even before the republic was formally declared in 1949. He demonstrated that assertiveness, backed by popular support and strategic timing, advanced Irish interests more effectively than Cosgrave's gradualism. Both men served their country, but de Valera's legacy—a genuinely independent Irish state—vindicated his approach to Anglo-Irish relations.",
