    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "1",
        "question": "What was the system of Metternich and how was it undermined, 1815-1848?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "The system of Metternich was the conservative international order established after the Napoleonic Wars, designed to prevent the recurrence of revolutionary upheaval that had destabilised Europe. At its core lay the Concert of Europe—regular meetings of the great powers (Austria, Russia, Prussia, Britain, France)—which maintained a balance of power and collective security. Metternich himself, Austria's Foreign Minister, articulated this system through the principle of legitimacy: the restoration of pre-revolutionary dynasties and territorial arrangements as outlined in the 1815 Treaty of Vienna. The Holy Alliance (1815) bound Christian monarchs to act as guardians of conservative principles, whilst the Quadruple Alliance provided military enforcement mechanisms. Domestically, the system relied on strict censorship, surveillance networks, and the suppression of nationalist and liberal movements; Metternich viewed these ideologies as existential threats to the multinational Austrian Empire and the stability of Europe.\n\nThe system functioned effectively during the 1820s. The Concert intervened militarily in Spain (1823) and Italy (1821) to crush liberal revolts, demonstrating the powers' commitment to collective repression. However, structural weaknesses became evident. Britain increasingly diverged from the continental consensus, particularly under George Canning from 1822, prioritising commercial interests and opposing French intervention in Spain. This fracture reflected deeper ideological tensions: liberalism and nationalism were not merely elite concerns but gaining grassroots support in industrialising societies.\n\nThe Greek War of Independence (1821-1830) fundamentally weakened the system. Initially, the powers attempted to enforce the anti-nationalist consensus, but European public opinion mobilised in support of Greek Orthodox Christians against Ottoman Muslims. Britain ultimately abandoned the Concert, supporting Greek independence in 1827. This demonstrated that Metternich could not indefinitely suppress nationalist sentiment, particularly when it aligned with religious sympathy and commercial interest.\n\nThe July Revolution of 1830 in France marked the system's decisive undermining. The overthrow of Charles X and installation of the constitutional monarchy under Louis-Philippe shattered the restoration principle—the legitimacy of absolute monarchy could no longer be maintained even in the heart of Europe. Revolutionary tremors spread: Belgian nationalists achieved independence from the Dutch (1830-1831), Polish nationalists rose against Russian rule (though brutally suppressed), and Italian liberals mobilised in several states. Metternich despaired at France's evolution, but could not restore monarchy without a general European war, which British opposition rendered impossible.\n\nFundamental economic and social forces undermined the system from within. Industrial capitalism created new middle classes demanding political representation and national economies organized around tariffs and trade, not conservative legitimacy. The railway and telegraph accelerated communication of liberal and nationalist ideas. Population growth strained land-based aristocratic economies. These transformations made the Congress system increasingly anachronistic.\n\nBy the 1840s, the system had become hollow. Metternich could still orchestrate conservative responses—as in his intervention against liberal movements in Italy in 1848—but his power was illusory. The Revolutions of 1848-1849, erupting across Europe from France to Hungary, demonstrated that the Concert had failed to eliminate the nationalist and liberal aspirations it sought to suppress. Significantly, the revolutions of 1848-1849 revealed nationalism's new face: not merely elite liberal nationalism, but mass popular movements seeking national unification and democratic reform. Metternich himself was forced to resign and flee Austria in March 1848.\n\nThe system collapsed because Metternich misdiagnosed the forces opposing it. He treated nationalism and liberalism as temporary disruptions to be managed through diplomacy and force. Instead, they represented structural transformations of European society rooted in economic development, intellectual ferment, and social composition. The Concert's fundamental assumption—that conservative monarchs could indefinitely prevent change through coordinated repression—proved impossible to sustain once Britain defected, public opinion mobilised, and revolutionary crises revealed the bankruptcy of pure restoration. The ultimate irony is that nationalism itself would reshape Europe not through revolution but through state-led unification movements (Italy, Germany) that would emerge immediately after Metternich's system finally expired. The system's rigidity and refusal to accommodate any change ensured that when it finally fractured, the result was not gradual reform but violent upheaval.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
