    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "3",
        "question": "What was the impact of World War I on economy and society in Europe?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "World War I fundamentally transformed European economy and society between 1914 and 1918, reshaping industrial production, labour structures, gender roles, and civilian welfare in ways that persisted long after the armistice.\n\nThe war necessitated a total mobilization of economic resources unprecedented in European history. Governments abandoned free-market principles and implemented centralized state control over production. Germany pioneered this through the Hindenburg Programme (1916), which placed all industry under military direction to maximize weapons output. Britain established the Ministry of Munitions (1915) under Lloyd George to coordinate arms manufacturing, while France centralized control through the War Ministry. Economies shifted entirely toward military production: by 1918, Allied and Central Powers allocated 50-60% of GDP to war effort. This demanded massive expansion of heavy industry, chemicals, steel, and explosives manufacturing. Factories that had produced consumer goods were converted to munitions plants. Industrial capacity grew dramatically in some sectors—German steel production, though constrained by British naval blockade, still supplied unprecedented quantities to the front.\n\nFinancing this colossal expenditure created severe economic strain. European governments borrowed heavily: Britain accumulated £2,000 million in debt, France £7,000 million. The British Treasury depleted gold reserves and depended increasingly on American loans. Germany, cut off by blockade from external credit, financed war through internal loans and hyperinflation of the money supply, which laid foundations for post-war currency collapse. Government bonds were sold to populations as patriotic duty. Taxation increased sharply—Britain's income tax rose from 9% to 30% by war's end. National debts ballooned, with consequences extending far beyond 1918.\n\nThe agricultural sector experienced severe disruption. Millions of farmers and agricultural labourers were conscripted, creating chronic labour shortages. In Germany and Austria-Hungary, the British blockade prevented grain imports, causing widespread food shortages. Bread rationing was introduced across Europe: in Germany, the \"turnip winter\" of 1916-17 saw civilians subsist on poor-quality foods as real food supplies collapsed. Potato blight and poor harvests compounded the crisis. By contrast, agricultural regions less exposed to fighting—parts of Russia until 1917, some Scandinavian areas—could export foodstuffs. Food prices rose dramatically everywhere, creating severe hardship for urban working-class populations dependent on wage labour.\n\nLabour structures transformed radically. With men conscripted in millions—France mobilized 8 million, Germany 11 million, Austria-Hungary 9 million—women entered industrial workforces in unprecedented numbers. British munitions factories employed over 900,000 women by 1917, French factories similarly expanded female workforces. Women worked in engineering, chemicals, ordnance production, and transport. They also sustained agriculture, nursing, and administration. Although temporary, this shift demonstrated women's industrial capability, lending weight to post-war suffrage campaigns. Trade unions negotiated \"dilution agreements\" permitting unskilled women to perform skilled work at lower wages, creating both opportunity and exploitation. Working conditions were harsh: long hours, dangerous machinery, toxic chemicals (TNT poisoning caused serious illness). Yet improved wages and economic independence, however temporary, altered social expectations.\n\nCivilian society experienced unprecedented hardship. Blockades—Britain's naval blockade of Germany being the most comprehensive—created acute shortages of food, fuel, and raw materials. German civilians faced caloric intake declining to near-subsistence levels by 1918. Influenza pandemic of 1918-19 killed approximately 50 million people globally, spreading through malnourished, war-exhausted populations. Urban areas suffered more acutely than rural regions. Housing deteriorated as construction labour was diverted to war production. Inflation eroded wages, and rationing systems—introduced first in Germany (1915), then Britain (1918), and France—became ubiquitous. Rationing, while preventing complete collapse, created resentment and black markets.\n\nInflation and currency instability became critical issues. Germany's money supply increased fourfold during the war; by 1918, the mark had lost roughly half its 1914 value. Austria-Hungary experienced similar collapse. This inflation redistributed wealth from savers and fixed-income earners to debtors and those with tangible assets, creating social tension. Neutral countries like Sweden and Switzerland initially profited from trade with both sides, but inflation affected them too. Post-war hyperinflation in Germany (1923) represented continuation of wartime monetary instability.\n\nThe war's impact on international trade patterns proved transformative. With European industrial capacity focused on warfare, overseas markets were lost. American manufacturers seized European markets, particularly in Latin America and Asia. When peace came, European nations could not easily recapture lost trade. The war thus accelerated America's emergence as dominant economic power—by 1918, the United States had shifted from net debtor to net creditor nation. European economic dependence on American credit and markets became structural feature of 1920s economy.\n\nSocial structures fractured under war's strain. Traditional hierarchies weakened as conscription drew all classes into military service. Working-class soldiers, having proved their value and endured horrors, returned with heightened political consciousness. Revolutionary ferment erupted across Europe—Russian Revolution (1917), German Revolution (1918-19), Hungarian Soviet Republic (1919)—reflecting working-class radicalization. Food riots, strikes, and mutinies became increasingly common, particularly in 1917-18. Governments' wartime propaganda, emphasizing national unity, rang hollow when peace brought unemployment and economic dislocation rather than prosperity.\n\nIn conclusion, World War I's economic and social impact was catastrophic and transformative. It demonstrated state capacity for total mobilization and economic direction, established patterns of female industrial participation, created massive international debts and American economic dominance, and generated social instability that produced revolutions and prolonged economic crisis. European economies emerged physically damaged (France and Belgium bore worst physical destruction), financially exhausted, and structurally altered. The war's economic consequences—inflation, debt, trade disruption—persisted throughout the 1920s, contributing substantially to the Great Depression and global instability of the 1930s.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
