        "label": "1",
        "question": "What was the importance for US foreign policy of one or more of the following: Berlin; Korea; Cuba?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "The Berlin crisis, Korean War, and Cuban Missile Crisis were pivotal episodes that fundamentally shaped US Cold War strategy and defined American global power projection between 1945 and 1989.\n\nThe Berlin crisis (1948–49) established the template for US Cold War confrontation. After Soviet blockade of West Berlin, President Truman authorised the Berlin Airlift, flying supplies continuously for nearly a year to sustain 2.2 million West Berliners. This demonstrated American commitment to containment and resolve against Soviet expansion. The crisis validated NSC-68 thinking—that US military strength must match Soviet aggression. Berlin became symbolically critical: a divided city embodying ideological division, with the Western sectors prospering while East Berlin stagnated under Soviet control. The successful airlift vindicated non-military response and showed the Western alliance could outlast Soviet coercion. Berlin's importance lay in establishing that the US would defend Western interests even at great cost, and that credibility with allies depended on visible commitment. The 1961 Berlin Wall construction further entrenched division and became iconic proof of communism's failure—people fleeing East to West—reinforcing American moral narrative in the Cold War.\n\nThe Korean War (1950–53) transformed US Cold War strategy from containment to confrontation. When North Korea invaded South Korea in June 1950, Truman interpreted this as Soviet-directed communist expansion requiring military response. The US secured UN condemnation and military intervention, framing Korea as defending the free world against aggression. General MacArthur's Inchon landing (September 1950) appeared to vindicate aggressive anti-communist strategy. However, Chinese entry (October 1950) exposed overreach and the war stalled near the 38th parallel. Korea's crucial importance was establishing that the US would fight communist expansion militarily, not merely contain it diplomatically. It justified massive rearmament: defence spending tripled from $13 billion (1950) to $50 billion (1953), creating the military-industrial complex President Eisenhower later warned against. Korea legitimised American bases globally—in Japan, South Korea, and Europe—creating a permanent forward military presence. The stalemate outcome left 30,000 American dead and a divided peninsula, but it cemented US determination to prevent communist unification of Korea and demonstrated American willingness to sustain military commitment indefinitely. Korea showed containment required military credibility, not merely economic aid as initially conceived.\n\nThe Cuban Missile Crisis (October 1962) represented the closest point to nuclear war and tested whether US nuclear superiority translated to geopolitical advantage. When reconnaissance revealed Soviet missile installations in Cuba, President Kennedy faced crisis management unprecedented in scale. His response—naval blockade ('quarantine') and demand for Soviet withdrawal—combined firmness with calculated restraint, avoiding immediate military strike that might trigger Soviet retaliation. The thirteen-day standoff with Soviet Premier Khrushchev ended when Soviets agreed to withdraw missiles in exchange for secret US pledge to remove Turkish missiles and public commitment not to invade Cuba. Cuba's importance lay in demonstrating that despite overall nuclear superiority, direct Soviet-American confrontation risked mutual annihilation. Both superpowers recognised nuclear weapons' paralysing effect. The crisis vindicated Kennedy's measured response and showed that credible threat of force, backed by willingness to negotiate, achieved American objectives without war. It established protocols for crisis management—the hot-line telephone between Moscow and Washington (1963) institutionalised careful communication. Cuba proved that in nuclear age, complete victory was impossible; Cold War would be managed, not decisively won. It reinforced that US strategy must balance containment with coexistence, deterrence with diplomacy.\n\nCollectively, these three crises shaped fundamental US Cold War strategy: Berlin established that America would defend allies and honour commitments; Korea demonstrated military credibility was essential to containment; Cuba revealed that nuclear weapons imposed limits on confrontation. Each episode reinforced American global presence and alliance leadership. The crises justified permanent rearmament, forward military bases, and interventionist presidency. They defined how the US understood its Cold War mission: containing communism through combinations of military strength, alliance solidarity, and carefully calibrated confrontation. These crises were not aberrations but the recurring pattern through which US-Soviet competition played out between 1945 and 1989.",
