        "label": "B",
        "question": "Write a short paragraph on one of the following:\n1. Church/State tensions in Germany.\n2. Colonial rivalries.\n3. The Paris Peace Settlement.\n4. Rosa Luxemburg.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Colonial rivalries were a major source of tension between European powers in the late 19th and early 20th centuries, contributing directly to the outbreak of World War I. Between 1871 and 1920, nations competed aggressively for colonies and spheres of influence in Africa, Asia, and the Pacific. Britain, France, Germany, and other powers clashed repeatedly over territorial acquisition and commercial dominance. The Scramble for Africa intensified rivalries, particularly between Britain and France (Fashoda Crisis, 1898), and between Germany and other powers seeking to establish an overseas empire. Germany's colonial ambitions under Kaiser Wilhelm II, combined with its naval expansion, threatened British naval supremacy and created suspicion among rival powers. France resented German colonial growth and remembered the loss of Alsace-Lorraine. These colonial tensions fed into alliance systems and arms races, heightening nationalist sentiment and mutual distrust. The Moroccan Crises (1905, 1911) demonstrated how colonial disputes could bring Europe to the brink of war. Although colonial rivalries alone did not cause World War I, they created an atmosphere of imperial competition and nationalist rivalry that made diplomacy increasingly fragile. Nations viewed colonial possessions as sources of prestige, raw materials, and strategic advantage, making compromise difficult. The interconnection between colonial competition and European great-power rivalries meant that disputes in distant colonies could escalate into continental conflicts, ultimately undermining peace and contributing to the outbreak of general war in 1914.",
        "diagram": "",
