        "label": "B",
        "question": "Write a short paragraph on one of the following:\n1. A development worker in Africa.\n2. The Lomé conventions.\n3. English as a world language.\n4. Achmad Sukarno of Indonesia.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "The Lomé conventions represented a significant attempt by the European Economic Community (EEC) to reshape post-colonial relationships with former colonies. Signed in 1975 in Togo, the first convention established a framework of economic and development cooperation between the EEC and 46 African, Caribbean and Pacific (ACP) states. The conventions moved beyond traditional colonial trade patterns by offering preferential market access and development aid without requiring ACP nations to grant reciprocal preferences to Europe—a principle of non-reciprocity that was revolutionary for its time. The agreements included the STABEX scheme, which stabilised export earnings for ACP countries dependent on commodity sales, addressing the vulnerability of developing economies to price fluctuations. Successive Lomé conventions (1980, 1985, 1990) expanded participation and refined cooperation mechanisms. However, the conventions also reflected continuing Western economic dominance: ACP states remained dependent on primary commodity exports while Europe maintained industrial superiority. The conventions did provide genuine benefits—substantial aid flows, technology transfer, and technical cooperation—yet critics argued they perpetuated rather than genuinely transformed the colonial economic relationship. The conventions ultimately symbolised European recognition that formal empire was obsolete, but demonstrated the difficulty of achieving truly equal partnership between developed and developing nations. They remained central to EU-ACP relations until replaced by Economic Partnership Agreements in 2000, representing both the best intentions and structural limitations of post-colonial development cooperation.",
