        "label": "a",
        "question": "To what extent would you agree that the political careers of Butt and Parnell ended in failure?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "The political careers of Butt and Parnell ended in qualified failure, though their legacies differed significantly.\n\nIsaac Butt's career concluded in clear failure. He founded the Home Rule League in 1870, articulating a moderate constitutional demand for Irish self-government within the Union. However, his approach—respectful parliamentary representation and reasoned argument—proved ineffective. Despite securing 59 Home Rule MPs in 1874, Butt could not translate this into legislative success. The Home Rule Bill of 1886, which embodied his vision, was defeated; crucially, Butt died in 1879 before witnessing even that attempt. His \"devolution\" strategy failed because it lacked the pressure mechanism to move Westminster. By the 1880 election, younger nationalists, particularly Parnell, eclipsed him, recognising that constitutional politeness alone could not extract concessions from an unwilling Parliament. Butt's \"independent opposition\" strategy collapsed as Irish MPs increasingly adopted obstructionist tactics. His inability to control or shape his movement's evolution—superseded by more aggressive leadership—marked his personal failure, though his foundational work on Home Rule proved durable.\n\nParnell's trajectory is more complex. He dominated Irish nationalism from 1880 to 1890 with unprecedented effectiveness. His \"New Departure\" synthesised constitutional methods with agrarian agitation, appealing to both political and rural constituencies. He commanded 86 MPs by 1885 and wielded real parliamentary leverage: he forced Gladstone into alliance, delivered the Irish vote in English constituencies, and extracted concrete concessions—notably the Land Act of 1881, which significantly reformed tenant conditions, though imperfectly. His ability to negotiate with Gladstone and secure Home Rule as a genuine parliamentary prospect (1886, 1893) represented genuine political achievement beyond Butt's reach.\n\nYet Parnell's career ended in catastrophic failure. His affair with Katharine O'Shea, revealed in the divorce proceedings of 1890, triggered moral condemnation from the Catholic hierarchy and English allies. Gladstone, dependent on Nonconformist Liberal support, demanded his resignation. The Irish parliamentary party split: 45 MPs remained loyal, but 71 repudiated him. Parnell died in October 1891, politically destroyed, his party fractured, and the Home Rule momentum he had built effectively halted. The Second Home Rule Bill (1893) passed the Commons but failed in the Lords. Parnell's personal downfall was total.\n\nHowever, distinguishing between personal and objective failure is essential. Parnell's legislative achievements persisted: the land reforms remained; the organisation of Irish nationalism endured; and his demonstration that Irish MPs could exercise power elevated their status permanently. The Home Rule cause, though defeated in 1886 and 1893, remained on the political agenda—a position Butt had never secured so firmly. When Home Rule eventually passed in 1914, it built on structures and expectations Parnell had created.\n\nMoreover, both men's failures must be contextualised within structural constraints. Home Rule faced determined opposition from Conservatives, Unionists, and an entrenched House of Lords. Irish nationalism's internal divisions—between constitutionalists and republicans, agrarian and political interests—made unified success difficult. Neither Butt nor Parnell controlled these external forces.\n\nIn conclusion, Butt's career was a personal and political failure: his methods proved inadequate, his leadership was superseded, and he achieved no tangible legislative progress. Parnell's career ended in personal catastrophe and immediate political collapse, yet his methods worked sufficiently to constitute partial success—he secured substantive concessions, elevated Irish parliamentary power, and made Home Rule a credible prospect. His failure was spectacular and tragic, but his political legacy proved more transformative than Butt's. Neither achieved their ultimate aim of Irish self-government during their lifetime, but Parnell's trajectory demonstrates that partial, contested success is distinguishable from straightforward failure. The question of degree matters: Butt failed more completely; Parnell's failure was more dramatic but less total in its negation of achievement.",
