    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "a",
        "question": "What changes took place in agriculture and industry, 1815-1871?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Between 1815 and 1871, agriculture and industry underwent transformative changes that reshaped European economies and societies. These shifts reflected technological innovation, capital investment, and evolving market structures, though their pace and extent varied significantly across regions.\n\nAGRICULTURAL CHANGE\n\nThe period saw substantial improvements in farming techniques and productivity. The Norfolk four-course crop rotation system, already established in Britain, spread gradually to continental Europe, replacing the medieval three-field system and allowing more intensive land use without soil depletion. This enabled farmers to maintain larger livestock herds year-round, improving meat and dairy production and providing better fertiliser for crops. Agricultural societies and publications disseminated these innovations, particularly in western Europe.\n\nMechanisation advanced significantly, though unevenly. The seed drill, improved ploughs, and mechanical threshers appeared first in Britain and spread to France and parts of Germany. By mid-century, mechanical reapers and other labour-saving devices were becoming established in prosperous grain-growing regions, particularly in Britain and northern France. This mechanisation reduced labour requirements, pushing rural populations towards towns and creating a mobile workforce for industrial development.\n\nCrop yields and production expanded substantially. Potato cultivation spread widely following its proven value, becoming a staple food for the poor across northern and central Europe. Grain production increased through both intensification and the opening of new lands. In eastern Europe, large estates expanded grain production for export to western markets. However, agriculture remained vulnerable to poor harvests; famines still occurred, most notably the Irish potato famine of 1845-1851, which killed approximately one million people and forced mass emigration.\n\nLand ownership patterns evolved. In Britain, the Enclosure Movement accelerated, consolidating fragmented holdings into larger farms worked by paid labour. This created a rural proletariat and released capital for investment elsewhere. In continental Europe, the Napoleonic Wars and subsequent reforms had broken up large ecclesiastical and noble estates in France, Germany, and Italy, creating peasant proprietorships. However, eastern European serfdom persisted until the 1860s, limiting agricultural modernisation there.\n\nINDUSTRIAL CHANGE\n\nThe Industrial Revolution, already underway in Britain before 1815, spread gradually to continental Europe. Britain maintained industrial dominance through 1871, but Belgium, France, and western Germany began serious industrialisation from the 1830s onward.\n\nTextile manufacture remained the leading industrial sector. Cotton production expanded enormously, driven by mechanised spinning and weaving. The power loom, water frame, and spinning jenny transformed production from cottage industries to factory-based systems. British cotton textiles dominated European markets, though some protection occurred; France and German states imposed tariffs to shelter domestic producers. Wool and silk industries similarly mechanised, though more slowly. By 1871, textiles remained the largest employer in European industry, but their share of economic output was beginning to decline.\n\nIron and steel production became increasingly central. James Watt's steam engine, perfected by 1800, was adapted to power bellows in iron furnaces, enabling the use of coke rather than charcoal and vastly increasing output. Henry Bessemer's steel-making process, patented in 1856, dramatically reduced steel production costs and allowed mass production of quality steel. Britain's iron output rose from approximately 400,000 tons annually in 1830 to over 4 million tons by 1871. France and Germany followed later but rapidly; Germany's Ruhr Valley developed into a major iron and steel centre. This growth in iron and steel underpinned railway construction and machinery manufacture.\n\nThe steam engine's application beyond textiles revolutionised production. Steam-powered machinery spread to paper manufacturing, printing, brewing, and other sectors. Steam power enabled factories to locate away from water sources, allowing urban concentration of industry and labour. The stationary steam engine became complemented by the railway locomotive, driving massive infrastructure investment from the 1830s onward.\n\nRailway development was revolutionary. The first steam-powered public railway opened between Liverpool and Manchester in 1830. By 1871, railways crisscrossed Britain, Belgium, France, Germany, and Italy, totalling tens of thousands of miles. Railways required massive capital investment, stimulating iron, coal, and engineering industries. They integrated regional markets, reduced transport costs, and enabled the rapid movement of goods and people. Railway construction required new financial instruments and techniques, advancing capital markets.\n\nCoal mining expanded dramatically as the fuel source for steam engines. Britain's coal output tripled between 1815 and 1871. Coal mining created new industrial regions and large concentrations of working-class populations. Dangerous working conditions and long hours characterised mining, making miners prominent in emerging trade union movements.\n\nNew industries emerged. Gas lighting for streets and homes created a utility industry. Chemical manufacturing developed, particularly in Germany, producing dyes, fertilisers, and pharmaceuticals. Engineering works multiplied, manufacturing machinery for textile factories, railways, and other purposes. By mid-century, new manufacturing centres had developed in Lancashire, the Midlands, the Ruhr Valley, and the Nord department in France.\n\nFACTORY ORGANISATION AND LABOUR\n\nThe factory system became dominant in leading industries. Workers, increasingly separated from ownership of means of production, laboured for wages under factory discipline. Hours were long—14-16 hours daily was common—and conditions often dangerous. Children and women comprised significant portions of the workforce, paid less than men. Factory towns grew rapidly; Manchester, for example, grew from 25,000 inhabitants in 1772 to over 300,000 by 1850. Urban overcrowding, poor sanitation, and disease accompanied industrial growth.\n\nLabour organisation began emerging. Early trade unions faced legal restrictions; Britain's Combination Acts (prohibiting labour combinations) lasted until 1824-1825. From the 1830s onward, trade unions grew, particularly in Britain. The Chartist movement (1838-1848) combined labour demands with political reform. By 1871, trade unions were becoming established institutions in industrial countries.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nBetween 1815 and 1871, both agriculture and industry transformed fundamentally. Agricultural improvements increased productivity and released labour for industry. Industrial mechanisation, steam power, and factory organisation created new wealth but also new social problems: urban squalor, labour exploitation, and class conflict. Regional disparities widened; industrial Britain far outpaced agricultural eastern Europe. These changes created the modern industrial economy and the working class, generating social movements and political conflicts that would define the remainder of the nineteenth century.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
