    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "a",
        "question": "During the period 1815-1871, how did city planning develop, with special reference to Haussmann's Paris?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "City planning in the period 1815–1871 underwent radical transformation, especially in Paris under Georges-Eugène Haussmann (1853–1870), who fundamentally reshaped European urban development through systematic modernization.\n\nBefore Haussmann, European cities were medieval in structure: congested, unhygienic, and socially volatile. Paris epitomized this: narrow winding streets, open sewers, dense tenements housing poor workers alongside bourgeois quarters. Urban problems bred cholera epidemics (Paris lost 20,000 to cholera in 1832 and 1849), crime, and revolutionary ferment—the barricades of 1830, 1848, and 1871 exposed how cramped urban geography facilitated insurrection. City planning was not merely aesthetic but political and sanitary necessity.\n\nNapoleon III (Emperor 1852–1870) appointed Haussmann prefect of the Seine department in 1853 with explicit mandate to transform Paris. Haussmann's vision was systematic and ruthless. He demolished medieval quarters, displacing around 500,000 people, to create a new Paris of broad, tree-lined boulevards (les grands boulevards), geometric precision, and modern infrastructure.\n\nThe physical transformation was extraordinary. Haussmann cut wide avenues through the dense city—the rue de Rivoli, boulevards de Sébastopol and de Strasbourg—facilitating traffic flow, police surveillance, and military movement (making barricades harder to construct). He created open squares and parks (Bois de Boulogne, Bois de Vincennes, Parc des Buttes-Chaumont), bringing green space to a suffocating city. Underground sewers and water mains replaced medieval systems; gas lighting appeared on streets; apartment buildings with standardized Haussmannian facades (six storeys, stone facing, mansard roofs) arose along new streets.\n\nThe ideology was threefold: modernization, control, and bourgeois order. Straight streets allowed rapid deployment of troops to suppress riots. The expense of reconstruction forced working-class populations eastward to outer arrondissements like Belleville, segregating poor from wealthy western districts. Parks were spaces of bourgeois leisure and moral regulation. Broad, uniform streetscapes embodied rational order, progress, and rational state authority.\n\nHaussmann's methods were novel: he utilized public credit, issuing loans (over 2 billion francs spent by 1870), and compulsory purchase of private land. While he beautified Paris, he also enriched speculators, financiers, and property developers—making Paris physically magnificent but economically benefiting capital, not workers.\n\nParis's reconstruction influenced European city planning. Vienna underwent systematic modernization: Emperor Franz Joseph initiated the Ringstrasse (1857–1888), a grand avenue circling the inner city, replacing medieval fortifications with parks, public buildings, and elegant streets. Like Haussmann's Paris, Vienna's Ringstrasse was about projecting imperial order and bourgeois refinement, segregating social classes through real estate prices.\n\nBrussels, Barcelona, and other European cities adopted similar principles: widened streets, regularized building standards, modern utilities. London, already less constrained by medieval geography, developed through suburban expansion rather than radical central reconstruction, but the principle of planned urban growth gained ground.\n\nHaussmann's approach had critics even then. Victor Baudelaire complained of the destruction of picturesque Paris. Socialists recognized class displacement. Yet Haussmann's methods became the template for modern city planning: rational grid-based layout, mixed-use development, public monuments and parks, and modern infrastructure.\n\nHaussmann's tenure ended in 1870 amid financial crisis and the Franco-Prussian War. Though incomplete, his reconstruction had fundamentally altered Paris: from medieval labyrinth to modern metropolis. The Paris of broad boulevards, standardized architecture, and planned green space persists today, testimony to his systematic vision.\n\nCity planning 1815–1871 thus evolved from ad hoc improvement to deliberate state-directed modernization. Before Haussmann, improvements were sporadic. After him, urban development became systematic, capitalized through public debt, and ideologically freighted with meanings of progress, order, and state authority. Paris became the model; other European cities adapted variants. By 1871, the modern planned city had become the aspiration of European capitals, representing both genuine modernization and the consolidation of bourgeois, state-directed social order.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
