    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "a",
        "question": "What were the social and economic problems facing Germany, 1920-1939, and how were they dealt with?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Germany faced severe social and economic crises during the 1920s and 1930s that shaped the Weimar Republic's trajectory and contributed to the rise of Nazism.\n\n**Economic Problems: 1920-1924**\n\nPost-WWI Germany inherited catastrophic economic conditions. The Treaty of Versailles imposed reparations of 132 billion gold marks, an enormous burden on a defeated economy. War debt, loss of industrial territory (Alsace-Lorraine, Polish corridors), and the need to support massive unemployment created fiscal chaos. Hyperinflation devastated the economy between 1921 and 1923. By November 1923, the mark had collapsed entirely—one US dollar equalled 4.2 trillion marks. Savings were obliterated, middle-class confidence destroyed, and civil disorder erupted. The occupation of the Ruhr by French and Belgian troops (January 1923) further crippled industrial production and exacerbated inflation through the government's policy of financing passive resistance through currency printing.\n\n**The Dawes Plan Response (1924)**\n\nThe Dawes Plan (August 1924) provided crucial relief. It restructured reparations payments into manageable annual instalments, arranged American loans to Germany (approximately 800 million marks initially), and established a new currency (the Rentenmark, later the Reichsmark). This stabilised the economy and restored confidence. Foreign investment, particularly from the United States, flooded into German industry, enabling modernisation and expansion.\n\n**The Golden Years (1924-1929)**\n\nFrom 1924 to 1929, Germany experienced economic recovery. Industrial production rose by 40%, reaching pre-war levels by 1927. Unemployment fell significantly. Wages increased and consumer goods became more widely available. This period saw cultural flourishing and apparent political stabilisation—the Locarno Treaties (1925) signalled Germany's acceptance into the international community. However, this recovery was artificially dependent on continued American lending, disguising underlying structural weaknesses.\n\n**Social Problems: 1920-1929**\n\nUnemployment remained persistently high, particularly among young males and returning war veterans. By 1928, approximately 1.4 million were jobless. Homelessness affected thousands, with inadequate housing stock in major cities. War widows and disabled veterans faced limited state support and social stigma. Class tensions were severe: the working class experienced radical politicisation, with the Communist Party (KPD) gaining membership after 1924, while middle-class resentment toward the Versailles settlement and perceived weakness of the republic intensified.\n\nAgriculture suffered chronic depression. Small farmers faced severe debt, as commodity prices remained depressed throughout the decade. Rural communities experienced economic decline and social dislocation, creating receptiveness to political extremism.\n\n**The Wall Street Crash and its Aftermath (1929-1933)**\n\nThe American stock market crash in October 1929 triggered economic catastrophe for Germany. American loans were withdrawn abruptly. German banks collapsed, industrial production fell sharply, and unemployment exploded. By January 1933, approximately 6 million were unemployed—roughly one-third of the workforce. Wage levels dropped, and poverty became widespread, particularly in industrial cities and rural areas.\n\nThe government's deflationary response, embodied in Chancellor Brüning's austerity policies (1930-1932), worsened conditions. Public spending was cut, wages reduced, and welfare benefits slashed—policies intended to balance the budget but which deepened depression and social misery. This approach destroyed faith in parliamentary democracy's ability to address the crisis.\n\n**Social Crisis: 1929-1933**\n\nSocial cohesion fractured. Homelessness increased dramatically. Malnutrition became visible in urban areas. Youth unemployment created a generation without prospects or employment experience. Labour unrest erupted, with strikes and confrontations between communist and right-wing paramilitaries becoming commonplace. Suicide rates rose notably. Political radicalisation accelerated: support for both the KPD and NSDAP surged, while centrist and moderate parties collapsed in electoral support. By 1933, the Nazi Party had become the largest single party, exploiting desperation and resentment.\n\n**Nazi Solutions (1933-1939)**\n\nAfter Hitler took power in January 1933, the Nazi regime implemented systematic policies to address unemployment and economic problems. Rearmament became central policy, with massive military spending stimulating industrial production and creating jobs. The Four-Year Plan (announced 1936) directed economic policy toward war preparation. Unemployment fell dramatically—from 6 million in January 1933 to approximately 1.5 million by 1937, through rearmament, public works (including the Autobahn project), conscription, and the removal of women and Jews from the labour market.\n\nThe regime pursued autarky (economic self-sufficiency) to reduce dependence on imported raw materials and foreign currency. Synthetic materials were developed; agricultural production was prioritised through price supports. Trade agreements, particularly with Southeastern European states, secured raw material supplies.\n\nSocial policy emphasised the Volksgemeinschaft (people's community). The Nazi regime provided welfare programmes targeting 'racially valuable' Germans, including the Winter Relief scheme. However, Jews and political opponents were excluded from benefits and employment. Rearmament spending prioritised military needs over consumer goods, keeping living standards modest despite falling unemployment.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nGermany's economic and social problems—reparations, hyperinflation, structural unemployment, agricultural depression, and dependency on foreign loans—were partially addressed by the Dawes Plan and American investment (1924-1929) but fundamentally unsolved. The 1929 depression exposed these vulnerabilities catastrophically. Conventional democratic responses proved inadequate, destroying faith in the Weimar system. The Nazi regime offered dramatic solutions through rearmament and autarky, reducing unemployment but ultimately subordinating the entire economy to war preparation, thereby perpetuating and intensifying crisis in human and material terms.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
