        "question": "Why did the Montgomery bus boycott (1956) take place, how was it carried out, and to what extent was it successful?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "The Montgomery bus boycott of 1956 was a pivotal act of civil disobedience that directly challenged racial segregation in public transport. It occurred because African Americans in Montgomery, Alabama faced systematic discrimination under Jim Crow laws that mandated racial segregation on city buses. Black passengers were required to sit at the back, surrender seats to white passengers without protest, and endure humiliating treatment. The immediate trigger came on 1 December 1955 when Rosa Parks, a 42-year-old seamstress and NAACP member, refused to give up her seat to a white passenger and was arrested. Her dignified protest galvanised the African American community, who saw in her act a symbol of resistance to unjust laws.\n\nThe boycott was organised by the Montgomery Improvement Association (MIA), led by the young minister Dr Martin Luther King Jr., who emerged as a key spokesperson. The campaign was meticulously planned and executed. African Americans, who comprised approximately 75% of the bus system's passengers, simply stopped using public buses. Instead, they organised an extensive car-pooling network, arranged private transportation, and many walked considerable distances. The boycott demonstrated remarkable discipline and unity across the black community, cutting bus company revenues significantly. Churches provided organisational infrastructure and moral support. The MIA made clear demands: respectful treatment of all passengers, first-come seating with blacks filling from back and whites from front (not desegregation, initially), and the employment of black drivers on predominantly black routes. King's leadership emphasised non-violent protest, drawing on Gandhian philosophy. He and other boycott leaders faced arrest, intimidation, and threats, including bombing of homes, yet the community held firm for 381 days.\n\nThe boycott achieved substantial success, though contested. The Supreme Court ruling in Browder v. Gayle (November 1956) declared segregation on public buses unconstitutional, effectively ending legal segregation in Montgomery's transport system. This represented a major legal victory that went beyond the MIA's initial demands. The boycott demonstrated the power of economic pressure and non-violent collective action, showing that African Americans could force change through unified resistance. It brought national and international attention to civil rights issues, establishing King as a national figure and the Montgomery campaign as a model for future protests. The boycott inspired similar actions elsewhere and contributed to the emerging Civil Rights Movement of the late 1950s and 1960s.\n\nHowever, success was limited in scope. While Montgomery buses were desegregated, segregation persisted in many other aspects of southern life. White citizens established alternative transportation systems to avoid using integrated buses, and violent resistance continued. The broader Jim Crow system remained largely intact across the South. Additionally, the Supreme Court victory, while legally decisive, required continued federal enforcement as local resistance was substantial. The boycott's true significance lay not in complete transformation of southern society but in proving that organised, non-violent African American protest could achieve legal change and in establishing King's philosophy and methods as central to the Civil Rights Movement. It was a crucial step toward eventual dismantling of segregation, though that process would require much further struggle through the 1960s.",
