    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "a",
        "question": "What was the American Dream and to what extent was it reflected in life in the US, 1945-1989?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "The American Dream was the ideological belief that through hard work, talent, and determination, individuals could achieve material success, upward social mobility, and personal fulfilment regardless of their social origins. It embodied optimism about capitalist democracy, homeownership, consumer prosperity, and the assumption that each generation would be better off than the last.\n\n**Extent reflected in life, 1945-1989: CONSIDERABLE BUT UNEQUALLY DISTRIBUTED**\n\n**Significant realisation for many:**\n\nThe immediate postwar period saw unprecedented economic growth. Real GDP doubled between 1945 and 1960, and median family income rose substantially. Suburban expansion enabled millions to achieve the dream's central symbol: homeownership. The Federal Housing Administration and Veterans Administration mortgages made single-family homes accessible to working- and middle-class families. By the 1950s, 60% of Americans owned their homes—a dramatic increase from prewar levels. Mass consumption flourished: automobiles, household appliances, televisions became standard possessions, reflecting widespread material prosperity.\n\nEducational opportunity expanded. The GI Bill enabled veterans to attend college; university enrollment grew from 1.5 million (1940) to over 3 million by 1960. This facilitated genuine upward mobility for many. Manufacturing jobs provided stable, unionised employment with rising real wages, particularly benefiting the white working class. The creation of a large, prosperous middle class seemed to validate the Dream.\n\n**Severe limitations and contradictions:**\n\nHowever, the Dream was starkly conditional on race. Systemic racism excluded African Americans, Latino Americans, and other minorities from many pathways to prosperity. Residential segregation—de facto in the North, de jure in the South—meant black families were excluded from suburban homeownership and the wealth-building it generated. Redlining by banks and mortgage companies systematically denied credit to black neighbourhoods. Employment discrimination confined minorities to lower-paid, less secure positions. Median black family income in 1960 remained approximately 55% of white median income.\n\nThe civil rights upheavals of the 1950s-1960s exposed how the Dream functioned for some while denied to others. Poverty persisted despite prosperity: in 1960, approximately 40 million Americans lived below the poverty line. Michael Harrington's *The Other America* (1962) documented this contradiction, revealing that prosperity coexisted with significant deprivation among the elderly, rural poor, and inner-city residents.\n\nGendered dimensions were also restrictive. The postwar ideology emphasised women's domesticity and dependent status. Although some women worked, occupational segregation, wage discrimination, and the assumption that women's primary role was homemaking limited their economic independence and career prospects. The Dream implicitly assumed a male breadwinner and female homemaker model—reflecting and reinforcing inequality.\n\n**1960s-1970s: Crisis and questioning:**\n\nBy the late 1960s, the Dream faced profound challenge. Inflation, urban decay, and deindustrialisation undermined economic security. Youth rebellion questioned materialism and consumerism as the Dream's foundations. The Vietnam War divided the nation and discredited establishment claims about American progress. Racial uprisings in cities like Watts (1965) and Newark (1967) demonstrated that the Dream remained inaccessible to millions. Economic stagnation in the 1970s—stagflation, declining productivity, rising unemployment—made upward mobility less certain. Real wages stagnated after 1973. Unemployment reached 9% by 1975. Manufacturing employment declined as deindustrialisation accelerated, particularly harming working-class communities, disproportionately affecting minorities.\n\n**1980s: Reinvention and renewal:**\n\nRonald Reagan's presidency (1981-1989) attempted to revive and redefine the Dream through supply-side economics and deregulation. His rhetoric celebrated entrepreneurship, individual enterprise, and the market. For some—particularly wealthy investors, technology entrepreneurs, and the professional class—the 1980s did offer significant wealth creation. The stock market boomed; new industries emerged.\n\nYet inequality widened dramatically. CEO-to-worker pay ratios expanded from approximately 20:1 (1965) to 42:1 (1989). Union membership declined from 20% of the workforce (1980) to 16% (1989), eroding workers' bargaining power. Real wages for non-supervisory workers stagnated or fell. Homelessness increased noticeably. Crack cocaine epidemics devastated inner cities. The Dream remained accessible for the privileged but increasingly elusive for the working class and poor.\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nBetween 1945 and 1989, the American Dream was realised substantially for significant portions of the American population—particularly educated, white, male, unionised workers who benefited from postwar prosperity and achieved homeownership and consumer comfort. The period 1945-1970 showed the Dream functioning most effectively for these groups.\n\nHowever, throughout the entire period, the Dream's realisation was profoundly unequal. Systemic racism, gender discrimination, and economic structures excluded minorities and women from equal participation. By the 1970s-1980s, even as the Dream was theoretically renewed, structural changes in the economy—deindustrialisation, deunionisation, and growing inequality—made it increasingly unattainable for the working and lower-middle classes. The Dream thus reflected important realities of American prosperity for some, but obscured persistent and deepening inequality, making its claim to universal accessibility misleading and ideologically powerful precisely because it explained unequal outcomes through individual rather than structural factors.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
