        "model": "Martin Luther King emerged as the principal leader and spokesperson of the Montgomery bus boycott, which began in December 1955 following Rosa Parks's arrest. Though initially reluctant, King was elected president of the Montgomery Improvement Association (MIA), the organisation coordinating the boycott. He provided crucial moral and strategic leadership, articulating the boycott's aims through speeches and public statements that emphasised nonviolent resistance as the means to challenge segregation. King's role extended beyond rhetoric: he helped organise the car pool system that transported black workers during the boycott and faced arrest himself, demonstrating personal commitment to the cause. His leadership combined practical organisation with inspirational communication—he emphasised dignity, Christian principles, and the moral injustice of segregation, which resonated with the black community and attracted broader support. The boycott lasted 381 days and culminated in a Supreme Court ruling that declared segregation on buses unconstitutional. King's success in Montgomery established him as a national civil rights figure and demonstrated that nonviolent protest could achieve tangible results, providing the template for his subsequent leadership of the broader Civil Rights Movement throughout the 1960s.",
