        "label": "B",
        "question": "Write a short paragraph on one of the following:\n1. The Irish countryside in 1815.\n2. William Carleton.\n3. The Tithe War.\n4. Emigration from Ireland, 1815-1870.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Emigration from Ireland, 1815-1870 represented one of the most significant demographic transformations in Irish history. Prior to 1815, emigration had occurred but remained relatively limited; however, the period 1815-1870 witnessed mass exodus, with approximately 5 million people leaving Ireland. Economic dislocation drove this movement: the decline of domestic industry, consolidation of landholdings, and the shift towards pastoral farming reduced employment opportunities. The Great Famine of 1845-1852 accelerated emigration dramatically, as approximately 1 million died and 1.5 million emigrated during this catastrophic decade. America became the primary destination, with the United States attracting Irish emigrants seeking employment and land ownership opportunities unavailable at home. Ship conditions were notoriously brutal; many emigrants died during the Atlantic crossing aboard 'coffin ships'. Those who arrived established Irish communities in cities like Boston and New York, maintaining cultural and religious identity through parish networks and mutual aid societies. Remittances sent home by successful emigrants provided crucial survival income for families remaining in Ireland. Emigration fundamentally altered Irish society: it reduced population pressure but also stripped the country of younger, more dynamic workers, contributing to economic stagnation. The phenomenon represented both an escape from poverty and persecution and a profound loss of population and human capital that would shape Irish development for generations. Emigration thus became central to the Irish experience during this period, reshaping both Ireland and Irish-American society.",
