        "label": "B",
        "question": "Write a short paragraph on one of the following:\n1. The first Home Rule Bill, 1886.\n2. The Belfast shipyards.\n3. The Gaelic League.\n4. The first Sinn Féin Party.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "The First Home Rule Bill, 1886\n\nThe First Home Rule Bill of 1886 represented the culmination of Charles Stewart Parnell's leadership of the Irish Parliamentary Party and fundamentally reshaped Irish politics. Introduced by Liberal Prime Minister William Ewart Gladstone, the Bill proposed to establish an Irish parliament with control over domestic affairs, whilst Westminster retained control of defence, foreign policy, and taxation. This marked a decisive shift: Gladstone's conversion to Home Rule divided the Liberal Party itself, with moderate Liberals (the Liberal Unionists led by Joseph Chamberlain) splitting away in opposition. The Bill's defeat in the Commons—it was defeated by 343 votes to 313—was a major setback. However, its introduction vindicated Parnell's constitutional strategy. The Parliamentary Party had leveraged its 86 MPs following the 1885 election to force the Home Rule question onto the British political agenda, demonstrating the power of disciplined Irish political organisation. The Bill's failure to pass meant Ireland remained under full Westminster control, but the proposal itself legitimised Irish self-government as a serious political objective. The issue of Home Rule would dominate British and Irish politics for the next three decades, ultimately leading to the Third Home Rule Bill of 1914 (suspended during World War I) and Irish independence. The 1886 Bill thus marked a watershed: it moved Irish nationalism from agitation toward constitutional parliamentary politics and revealed irreversible fractures within British liberalism over the Irish question.",
