        "marks": 30,
        "model": "The 1918 election was a watershed moment in Irish politics that transformed the independence movement. Held in December 1918, the election saw Sinn Féin win a landslide victory, securing 73 of the 105 Irish seats on a platform of establishing an Irish republic. This marked a decisive rejection of the Irish Parliamentary Party, which won only 6 seats, ending its decades of dominance. The election was significant because it reflected the shift in nationalist sentiment following the 1916 Easter Rising and its violent suppression. The rising had radicalised public opinion, and by 1918, support for constitutional nationalism had collapsed in favour of republican separatism. Sinn Féin's victory gave the independence movement a powerful democratic mandate. The newly elected Sinn Féin representatives refused to take their seats in Westminster, instead establishing Dáil Éireann in January 1919, which declared Irish independence. This precipitated the War of Independence (1919–1921), as the British government rejected the claim to sovereignty. The election demonstrated that Irish public opinion had shifted decisively towards complete independence rather than Home Rule. It legitimised the nationalist movement and provided the foundation for subsequent negotiations that eventually led to the Anglo-Irish Treaty of 1921 and the establishment of the Irish Free State, making it one of the most consequential electoral moments in modern Irish history.",
