        "marks": 20,
        "model": "The decision to locate Northern Ireland's second university at Coleraine rather than Derry was driven by economic and political considerations favouring the development of underdeveloped areas. The Macrory Report (1944) and subsequent planning decisions prioritised spreading economic development across Northern Ireland. Coleraine was chosen because it was located in a designated growth area in the northeast, strategically positioned to stimulate industrial development in a region experiencing economic decline. Derry, despite being a significant city, was viewed by the Unionist-dominated Stormont government as peripheral—both geographically (in the far northwest) and politically, given its predominantly nationalist population. The decision reflected the government's economic planning policy of dispersing investment to boost less developed regions, but it also served political interests by directing resources away from a nationalist-majority city. The timing—announced in 1965 by the O'Neill administration—and the controversy it generated revealed underlying tensions between unionist planning priorities and nationalist grievances about resource allocation. The choice ultimately marginalised Derry economically while strengthening Coleraine's position, demonstrating how development policy intersected with sectarian divisions in Northern Ireland.",
