        "label": "C",
        "question": "Answer one of the following:\n1. What was the importance of the elections of 1885 and 1886 for Ireland?\n2. What was the contribution of Michael Davitt to Irish affairs?\n3. What did W.B. Yeats contribute to cultural revival in Ireland?\n4. Why and how did Unionists campaign against Home Rule for Ireland?",
        "marks": 40,
        "model": "The elections of 1885 and 1886 were pivotal in advancing Irish Home Rule and fundamentally reshaping British politics. In 1885, the Third Reform Act enfranchised agricultural labourers, dramatically increasing the Irish electorate. The Irish Parliamentary Party under Charles Stewart Parnell won 86 seats, achieving a decisive majority among Irish MPs and gaining the balance of power in the House of Commons between Conservatives and Liberals. This leverage proved decisive: in 1886, the new Liberal Prime Minister William Ewart Gladstone introduced the First Home Rule Bill, recognising that Irish votes were essential to his political survival. Although the Bill failed due to opposition from Liberal Unionists and Conservatives, its introduction represented a historic achievement—Home Rule was now official Liberal policy and a viable political prospect. The electoral success transformed Parnell and the IPP into kingmakers in Westminster politics, demonstrating that constitutional nationalism could achieve tangible results. The 1886 election itself, called after the Home Rule Bill's defeat, saw Conservatives and Liberal Unionists gain ground, but the crucial point was that Home Rule had entered mainstream political debate. The elections established that Irish self-government was no longer a peripheral issue but central to British electoral politics. They validated Parnell's strategy of parliamentary organisation and party discipline, showing that concentrated voting power could force concessions from major British parties. These elections also intensified unionist opposition in Ulster and reinforced the Land League's achievement in mobilising rural Ireland politically. Ultimately, 1885–1886 marked the transition from agrarian agitation to constitutional dominance, proving that electoral politics could deliver what decades of violence had not—serious consideration of Irish Home Rule at Westminster. The elections established Home Rule as achievable through parliamentary means, shaping Irish politics for the next three decades until 1914.",
