        "label": "B",
        "question": "Write a short paragraph on one of the following:\n1. The Home Rule party, 1912-1918.\n2. Michael Collins.\n3. Belfast during World War II.\n4. Evie Hone.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "**Michael Collins**\n\nMichael Collins emerged as one of Irish republicanism's most significant figures during the independence struggle (1916–1922). A skilled military organiser and politician, Collins served as Director of Intelligence during the War of Independence (1919–1921), where he orchestrated the 'Squad'—a unit that systematically eliminated British intelligence operatives in Dublin, particularly on Bloody Sunday (21 November 1920). His intelligence network was so effective that it severely disrupted British Crown forces' operations. Beyond military strategy, Collins demonstrated pragmatic political judgment, negotiating the Anglo-Irish Treaty (December 1921) as part of the Irish delegation led by Arthur Griffith. Though the Treaty disappointed hardline republicans by partitioning Ireland and accepting Dominion rather than full Republic status, Collins accepted it as \"a stepping stone\" to future independence. As Chairman of the Provisional Government (1922), he oversaw the establishment of the Irish Free State and led government forces during the Civil War (1922–1923) against anti-Treaty republicans. His shooting death in August 1922 during the conflict robbed the young Free State of crucial leadership at a critical moment. Collins's legacy reflects the tensions inherent in the independence movement: his pragmatism and organisational genius advanced Irish sovereignty, yet his acceptance of partition and dominion status provoked the republican split that defined Irish politics for decades. His trajectory—from guerrilla commander to statesman to casualty of civil conflict—encapsulates the complex path to Irish independence.",
