        "question": "Answer one of the following:\n1. What was the contribution of Patrick Pearse to Irish affairs?\n2. What did W.T. Cosgrave achieve as head of government, 1923-1932?\n3. What main events took place during the Eucharistic Congress, 1932?\n4. How did de Valera pursue a policy of neutrality during World War II?",
        "marks": 40,
        "model": "Patrick Pearse was a central figure in Irish nationalist politics and the Easter Rising. As a teacher and intellectual, he shaped nationalist thought through St Enda's School, where he educated young men in Irish language and culture, creating a generation committed to Irish independence. He was a prominent member of the Irish Republican Brotherhood and articulated the ideological case for rebellion, famously stating that blood sacrifice was necessary to awaken the nation. During the 1916 Easter Rising, Pearse served as President of the Provisional Government of the Irish Republic, leading the insurrection from the General Post Office in Dublin. Though militarily defeated, his leadership and the subsequent execution of the Rising's leaders transformed public opinion towards republicanism. His role as spokesperson and military commander made him the public face of the Rising, and his dignified conduct before execution elevated him to martyr status. Pearse's writings and speeches, emphasizing the spiritual and national dimensions of the struggle, profoundly influenced subsequent nationalist politics. His insistence on armed rebellion as a moral necessity became foundational to the republican ideology that guided Irish independence movements through the War of Independence and Civil War. Pearse's legacy extended beyond his death: his concept of national awakening through sacrifice resonated deeply in Irish political culture, and his vision of an Irish-speaking republic shaped constitutional and cultural debates in the new Irish state. Without Pearse's intellectual contribution, military leadership during 1916, and martyrdom, the trajectory of Irish independence would have been significantly different, making him one of the most influential figures in twentieth-century Irish affairs.",
