        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Anti-Semitism became a significant political and social force in late nineteenth-century France, particularly following the Dreyfus Affair of 1894. Captain Alfred Dreyfus, a Jewish officer in the French army, was falsely accused of selling military secrets to Germany. Despite evidence of his innocence, Dreyfus was convicted and sentenced to life imprisonment on Devil's Island, exposing deep-seated anti-Semitic prejudice within French military and judicial institutions. The affair divided French society bitterly between those who supported Dreyfus (the Dreyfusards), including intellectuals like Émile Zola who published 'J'accuse' in 1898 demanding justice, and those who opposed his exoneration. Anti-Semitic sentiment was fuelled by nationalist groups and the Catholic Church, who viewed Jews as foreign elements threatening French identity and Catholic interests. Publications like La Libre Parole, edited by Édouard Drumont, promoted virulent anti-Semitic propaganda, claiming Jews were disloyal to France and controlled finance and media. The Dreyfus Affair demonstrated how anti-Semitism could mobilise mass political movements and infiltrate state institutions. Dreyfus was eventually pardoned in 1899 and fully exonerated in 1906, but the affair highlighted the vulnerability of Jewish citizens to organised discrimination and the fragility of republican principles when confronted by nationalist and religious prejudice in France.",
