    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "C",
        "question": "Answer one of the following:\n1. How did Marilyn Monroe represent Hollywood and the American Dream?\n2. How was the Montgomery bus boycott, 1955-1956, carried out and how successful was it?\n3. What was the importance of the 1969 moon landing?\n4. In what ways did Betty Friedan and/or Norman Mailer criticise aspects of American life?",
        "marks": 40,
        "model": "The Montgomery bus boycott (1955–56) was a pivotal civil rights campaign against racial segregation in public transport. It was carried out through sustained mass action and proved strategically successful, though with qualified long-term impact.\n\nThe boycott was triggered by Rosa Parks's arrest on 1 December 1955 for refusing to give up her seat to a white passenger on a Montgomery, Alabama bus. The local NAACP and church leaders, including the young Dr. Martin Luther King Jr., rapidly mobilised the African American community. A newly formed Montgomery Improvement Association (MIA) coordinated the campaign. Organisers secured 381 days of unified non-participation in bus services by the Black population—approximately 50,000 people who comprised 75% of bus ridership. The boycott was maintained through alternative transport networks: carpools, black taxi operators, and walking. This required extraordinary discipline and financial hardship, sustained through weekly mass meetings at churches and King's inspirational oratory, which framed the protest as a moral crusade.\n\nThe boycott succeeded in achieving its immediate objective. On 20 December 1956, the Supreme Court ruled in Browder v. Gayle that bus segregation was unconstitutional. Montgomery's city council capitulated, and integrated bus service began in December 1956.\n\nHowever, its broader significance must be qualified. While it demonstrated that organised Black resistance could force legal change and inspired subsequent sit-ins and Freedom Rides, it did not end segregation elsewhere. White resistance intensified; the White Citizens' Councils expanded, and violence against Black activists continued. The boycott's success was geographically limited to Montgomery and required an external Supreme Court decision rather than securing local acceptance.\n\nNevertheless, the boycott marked a crucial turning point. It elevated King to national prominence as a leader committed to non-violent direct action—a strategy that would define the civil rights movement through the 1960s. It proved that economic pressure, sustained community solidarity, and moral clarity could challenge institutional racism. The boycott demonstrated Black agency and organisation, challenging the narrative of white benevolence. It validated mass participation and showed that legal victory, though incomplete, was possible. The Montgomery campaign thus became the template for subsequent civil rights victories: desegregation of schools, lunch counters, and voting rights, making it foundational to the movement's long-term trajectory.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
