        "label": "2",
        "question": "How did Anglo-Irish relations develop during the period 1923 – 1949?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Anglo-Irish relations between 1923 and 1949 evolved from formal separation to cautious coexistence, marked by economic conflict, constitutional clarification, and increasing Irish independence, though underlying tensions persisted.\n\nIn 1922–23, the Irish Free State emerged from civil war as a self-governing dominion within the British Commonwealth, yet relations remained fraught. The Irish government, led by W.T. Cosgrave and later Éamon de Valera, prioritized assertion of sovereignty. De Valera's rise to power in 1932 transformed the relationship dramatically. He systematically dismantled the constitutional links binding the Free State to Britain. The Oath of Allegiance, imposed by the Treaty of 1921, was removed in 1933, eliminating a symbol of subordination that had poisoned Irish politics since the civil war. This action, though controversial domestically, signalled Irish determination to escape Treaty constraints. De Valera pursued further constitutional measures: the 1937 Constitution established Éire as a sovereign republic in all but name, with the President as head of state rather than the British monarch, and removed references to Commonwealth membership. These constitutional changes were largely tolerated by Britain, reflecting recognition that rigid control was impossible and that cooperation was preferable to confrontation.\n\nEconomic conflict dominated the mid-1930s. De Valera's government withheld land annuities—payments owed to Britain from the Land War settlement—claiming these were illegitimate obligations. Britain retaliated with punitive tariffs on Irish agricultural exports, particularly cattle, Ireland's principal export. This Economic War (1932–38) severely damaged Irish agriculture and living standards. De Valera's protectionist policies, designed to build self-sufficiency, further strained trade relations. However, the Economic War revealed limits to British power: neither side could force the other into submission. The Anglo-Irish Trade Agreement of 1938 restored normal commercial relations, with Britain accepting the end of annuity payments in exchange for Irish recognition of British naval facilities at Irish ports—a concession that proved temporary and contentious.\n\nWorld War II—or the Emergency, as Ireland termed it—created the most acute tension in Anglo-Irish relations. De Valera's government maintained strict neutrality despite British pressure to join the Allied cause. The Irish government interned both British spies and German agents, demonstrating formal neutrality, yet Ireland's neutrality was de facto more sympathetic to the Allies: supplies flowed to Britain, Irish citizens fought in British forces, and intelligence was shared informally. British frustration was intense: Winston Churchill publicly criticized Irish neutrality and considered military intervention to seize Irish ports, but the political cost and logistical difficulty prevented action. De Valera defended neutrality as essential to Irish sovereignty—the first real test of independence. His position, though unpopular with many Irish and infuriating to Britain, was rooted in the conviction that Irish freedom meant freedom to choose non-participation. The war period demonstrated that despite economic interdependence and military disparity, Britain could not coerce an independent Irish government.\n\nConstitutional clarification came after 1945. De Valera's government formally declared Ireland a republic in 1949, severing the last nominal link to the British Crown and Commonwealth. This crystallized what the 1937 Constitution had already established in substance. Britain acknowledged the Republic of Ireland Act 1948 (passed by the subsequent Fianna Fáil government under Seán Lemass following de Valera's 1948 electoral defeat) with relative equanimity, having long since accepted Irish independence as irreversible. However, Britain did insist that Northern Ireland remained part of the UK, and the Irish government's irredentist claims to the six counties remained a source of friction, though diplomatic rather than acute during this period.\n\nThe partition itself poisoned Anglo-Irish relations throughout. The Irish government never accepted partition as legitimate, viewing it as imposed against Irish will. Britain maintained Northern Ireland as integral to the UK, regarding any Irish claim as interference in internal affairs. This fundamental disagreement, though not producing open conflict during 1923–49, represented an underlying grievance that prevented full normalization. De Valera's 1949 decision to declare a republic was partly motivated by Republican pressure on the partition issue, yet it changed nothing regarding Northern Ireland's status.\n\nDiplomatically, relations gradually improved despite these tensions. By the late 1930s and 1940s, both governments recognized mutual benefit in pragmatic engagement. Trade resumed after 1938. Cultural and sporting contacts continued. The two countries shared democratic values and British cultural influence remained strong in Ireland despite official policies promoting Irish language and culture. By 1949, while constitutional separation was complete and partition remained contested, the acute hostility of the 1920s and economic warfare of the 1930s had given way to distant but functional relations between two neighboring states with deep historical grievances but also practical interdependence.\n\nIn conclusion, Anglo-Irish relations during 1923–1949 transformed from dominion subordination to full republican independence. De Valera's leadership drove constitutional assertion, economic defiance, and wartime neutrality, each step incrementally establishing Irish sovereignty. Britain, unable to reverse Irish independence and recognizing that cooperation served better interests than coercion, gradually accepted Irish assertions, though never without friction. By 1949, the relationship was one of independent states managing differences rather than metropole and colony. Yet partition, unresolved and resented, ensured that fundamental discord persisted beneath surface normalization.",
