        "question": "How extensive was Irish participation in politics in the United States and in Britain during the period, 1850-1966?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Irish political participation in the United States and Britain from 1850–1966 was extensive and structurally significant, though it manifested differently in each country and evolved considerably over this period.\n\nIn the United States, Irish Americans rapidly became a political force. By the mid-nineteenth century, Irish immigrants had concentrated in major cities—Boston, New York, Philadelphia—where their numerical strength translated into electoral power. The Democratic Party became their primary political vehicle; Irish voters consistently supported Democrats, recognising the party's greater receptiveness to immigrant communities. By the 1880s, Irish Americans dominated Democratic organisations in several cities. Boss Tweed's Tammany Hall in New York, though not exclusively Irish-controlled, had substantial Irish membership and demonstrated how Irish Americans leveraged political machinery for community advancement. Irish Americans held mayoralties in Boston (Patrick Collins, Hugh O'Brien) and New York by the early twentieth century, symbolising upward political mobility. At national level, Irish representation grew: James Michael Curley served multiple terms as Boston mayor and governor of Massachusetts, exemplifying Irish political success. However, federal office came more slowly; few Irish Americans reached the Senate or presidency before the twentieth century, reflecting continuing discrimination. The 1960 election of John F. Kennedy represented a watershed moment, demonstrating Irish American arrival at the highest political level after generations of political struggle and accumulation of electoral influence.\n\nIn Britain, Irish political participation was more complex and constrained. Irish immigrants in Britain were often working-class and faced considerable discrimination; they did not achieve the same organisational presence or electoral impact as in America. Working-class areas with Irish concentrations—parts of Manchester, Liverpool, Glasgow—did see some Irish involvement in local politics and trade unionism, but sustained Irish-led political movements were limited. The principal Irish political engagement in Britain concerned Home Rule: during the 1880s–1914 period, Irish Home Rule dominated Westminster politics. The Irish Parliamentary Party, led by Charles Stewart Parnell and later John Redmond, exercised considerable leverage in the House of Commons, holding the balance of power between Liberal and Conservative governments. This gave Irish nationalism a voice at the centre of British political power. However, this was nationalist rather than immigrant political participation; it represented Irish representatives fighting for Irish independence rather than Irish immigrants in Britain achieving integration into British politics. The 1916 Easter Rising and subsequent events transformed Irish political engagement: after independence (1922), Irish immigrants in Britain were no longer representing a colonised nation and lacked a unified political identity. Irish participation in British politics thereafter remained marginal, with Irish immigrants integrating into Labour or Conservative parties without forming distinct political structures.\n\nComparison reveals that Irish participation in American politics was more extensive and sustained because the United States offered structural conditions favouring immigrant political mobilisation: democratic pluralism, the ward-based machine system, and rapid urbanisation created opportunities for Irish American numerical strength to translate into concrete political power. This power accumulated over generations, producing Irish American mayors, governors, and eventually a president. British politics, by contrast, offered fewer openings for immigrant communities. The Irish Parliamentary Party's dominance of Westminster politics in the decades before independence was exceptional—a form of colonial political leverage rather than immigrant integration. After independence, Irish immigrants in Britain lacked equivalent political organisation or distinct electoral purpose.\n\nIn both countries, Irish political participation peaked at different moments: in America during the 1880s–1960s, when Irish Americans built extensive municipal and state political machines; in Britain during the Home Rule period (1880s–1914), when Irish nationalist representatives held parliamentary power. By 1966, Irish American political participation was institutionalised and mainstream; Irish participation in British politics had effectively dissipated, as post-independence Irish immigrants assimilated without forming organised political constituencies. The Irish diaspora's political impact was thus most extensive in the United States, where demographic concentration, democratic structures, and sustained community organisation created lasting political influence.",
