      {
        "label": "2",
        "question": "Account for the fall of Stormont and for the collapse of the power-sharing executive, 1973-1974.",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "The fall of Stormont in 1972 and the subsequent collapse of the power-sharing executive in 1974 resulted from the failure of majoritarian unionist rule to accommodate nationalist aspirations, communal polarisation intensified by violence, and structural weaknesses in constitutional arrangements.\n\nStormont's collapse stemmed fundamentally from the civil rights crisis of the late 1960s. The Stormont parliament, dominated by the Ulster Unionist Party since 1921, had consistently marginalised the nationalist minority through gerrymandering, discriminatory housing allocation, and employment practices. When the Civil Rights Association launched peaceful campaigns from 1968 onwards demanding equal voting rights and fair housing, unionist resistance provoked violent intercommunal clashes. The Apprentice Boys parade in Derry in August 1969 triggered riots that spread to Belfast, forcing the British government to deploy troops. This British military intervention undermined Stormont's authority and marked the beginning of the end for the devolved parliament.\n\nViolence escalated dramatically in the early 1970s, destroying any basis for coexistence at Stormont. The IRA split in 1969 into Official and Provisional wings; the Provisionals launched a sustained bombing and shooting campaign targeting British soldiers and security forces. The Bloody Sunday massacre on 30 January 1972, when British paratroopers killed thirteen civilians in Derry during an anti-internment march, radicalised nationalist opinion and destroyed what remained of Catholic trust in British security forces. Simultaneously, loyalist paramilitaries, including the Ulster Defence Association (UDA) and Ulster Volunteer Force (UVF), intensified sectarian killings targeting Catholics. The death toll reached 467 in 1972 alone. This reciprocal violence polarised communities along sectarian lines, making cross-community cooperation at Stormont politically impossible.\n\nStormont proved incapable of restoring order or governing effectively amid this violence. The Unionist government, led by Brian Faulkner from March 1971, lost control of the security situation. Unionist backbenchers, particularly hardliners like Harry West, opposed any concessions to nationalist demands, while nationalists withdrew cooperation from the parliament. The introduction of internment without trial in August 1971—a measure targeting primarily republicans—further alienated Catholics and demonstrated the Unionist government's reliance on coercive measures rather than political accommodation. By late 1971, Stormont was dysfunctional: it could neither govern effectively nor implement reforms acceptable to both communities.\n\nThe British government, frustrated by Stormont's inability to manage the crisis, intervened decisively. Secretary of State William Whitelaw arrived in March 1972 with a mandate to restore order. When violence continued and Stormont's credibility collapsed, Westminster suspended the Stormont parliament on 30 March 1972, replacing it with Direct Rule from London. This reflected the judgment that devolved government had become unworkable. The fifty-year experiment in separate Home Rule for Northern Ireland effectively ended.\n\nThe power-sharing executive that replaced Stormont was established under the 1973 Northern Ireland Constitution Act, following the Sunningdale Agreement of December 1973. The executive, formed in January 1974, united the Ulster Unionist Party (led by Brian Faulkner), the SDLP (led by Gerry Fitt), and the cross-community Alliance Party. It represented a genuine attempt to create inclusive government through mandatory coalition. However, structural and political weaknesses doomed it within fourteen months.\n\nThe executive faced implacable unionist opposition. Hardline unionists, led by Harry West and Ian Paisley, viewed power-sharing as betrayal and organised the Ulster Unionist Council in January 1974 to reject the executive. These opponents, representing significant unionist opinion, particularly among working-class Protestants, refused to accept Catholic nationalist participation in government. The Sunningdale Agreement's provision for an all-Ireland Council—designed to provide a cross-border dimension—inflamed unionist fears of eventual Irish unity and strengthened anti-executive sentiment among the unionist grassroots.\n\nExternal political pressure proved decisive. The February 1974 general election returned a Labour government committed to maintaining the Northern Ireland settlement, but subsequent local government elections in May 1974 showed strong anti-executive sentiment. More critically, the Ulster Workers' Council (UWC), comprising loyalist workers and paramilitaries, launched a strike on 15 May 1974 demanding the executive's resignation. The UWC paralysed Northern Ireland: electricity supplies were cut, industry shut down, and essential services stopped. The executive, lacking security force backing from the British government, could not break the strike. Brian Faulkner requested military action to restore order, but Secretary of State Merlyn Rees refused. Facing total economic collapse and unable to govern, the executive collapsed on 28 May 1974.\n\nThe executive's failure reflected deeper communal polarisation. Nationalists, though participating in government, remained a minority unable to implement their agenda; unionists felt threatened by Catholic participation and the all-Ireland dimension; and paramilitaries from both communities rejected the political settlement. The SDLP and Alliance Party could not bridge the sectarian divide or convince their respective communities that the executive offered genuine progress. Violence continued throughout the executive's existence, undermining its legitimacy.\n\nThe collapse of both Stormont and the power-sharing executive demonstrates that institutional reform alone could not resolve the fundamental conflict between unionists seeking to maintain the Union and nationalists seeking Irish unity, especially when communal violence had destroyed trust. Westminster's imposition of Direct Rule proved more durable than devolved government, remaining in place for the next 25 years until the 1998 Good Friday Agreement finally established sustainable power-sharing based on parallel consent mechanisms. The 1972-1974 experience revealed that majoritarian unionist rule was untenable, but that the constitutional and political frameworks of the time could not accommodate genuine power-sharing given the intensity of communal division and paramilitary violence.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
