      {
        "label": "1",
        "question": "What were the main features of the First Programme for Economic Expansion, 1958-1963, and how successful was it for the economy and society?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "The First Programme for Economic Expansion (1958–1963) represented a decisive shift in Irish economic policy from protectionism towards free trade and foreign investment, marking the transition from stagnation to growth.\n\n**Main Features**\n\nThe Programme, developed by Seán Lemass's government and economist T.K. Whitaker, abandoned the inward-looking protectionist strategy that had dominated since independence. Its core pillars were: opening the economy to international trade and capital; attracting foreign direct investment through tax incentives and export profit relief; developing export-oriented industries rather than import substitution; expanding agricultural productivity; and investing in infrastructure and education. The government reduced tariffs incrementally, facilitated foreign firms establishing manufacturing bases in Ireland (particularly US companies), and promoted industrial estates. Capital grants and tax concessions attracted multinational corporations seeking European markets. Agricultural modernization received attention through advisory services and mechanization support.\n\n**Economic Success**\n\nThe Programme achieved striking results. GDP growth averaged approximately 4% annually (1958–1963), contrasting sharply with the near-stagnation of the 1950s. Industrial output expanded substantially, with manufacturing employment growing significantly. Exports increased dramatically, particularly manufactured goods, reducing the balance-of-payments deficit that had constrained previous governments. Foreign investment inflows, negligible before 1958, rose substantially as multinational firms (particularly American) established operations: pharmaceutical, chemical, and engineering companies located in Ireland to access European markets under Common Market preferences. The Programme attracted approximately £70 million in foreign investment during this period. Agricultural exports benefited from productivity improvements and market access. Unemployment fell from approximately 7% in 1958 to under 5% by 1963, and emigration rates declined as domestic employment opportunities improved.\n\n**Social Impact**\n\nThe Programme's success enabled rising living standards. Real wages increased as employment expanded and productivity improved. Urbanization accelerated as workers migrated from agriculture to manufacturing towns and Dublin. Consumer spending rose, driven by increased incomes and reduced emigration losses. Educational investment expanded, supporting both industrial needs and broader social development. However, benefits were unevenly distributed geographically: Dublin and industrial towns prospered, while rural areas dependent on agriculture experienced relative decline and continued emigration. The transition from agricultural to industrial employment created social tensions and required significant adjustment.\n\n**Limitations and Qualifications**\n\nWhile successful, the Programme's achievements had structural limits. Industrial growth remained dependent on foreign firms' investment decisions; limited indigenous industrial capacity meant Ireland supplied primarily labour and assembly facilities rather than developing sophisticated manufacturing. Profit repatriation by multinationals limited capital retention for reinvestment. Agricultural productivity improvements, though notable, could not absorb the rural workforce displaced by mechanization, perpetuating migration pressures. Regional imbalance worsened as investment concentrated in accessible areas. Export growth, though impressive, relied heavily on preferential access to Commonwealth and potential Common Market entry; Britain's entry into the EEC (1973) later altered these calculations.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nThe First Programme for Economic Expansion was substantially successful in achieving its primary objectives: reversing economic stagnation, attracting foreign investment, and generating growth and employment. GDP growth, export expansion, and unemployment reduction represent undeniable achievements. However, success was qualified by dependence on multinational investment, geographic concentration of benefits, limited indigenous industrial development, and incomplete agricultural adjustment. Nonetheless, the Programme fundamentally reoriented Irish economic policy towards integration with international markets, establishing the foundation for subsequent development and marking the definitive end of protectionist autarky. Its success encouraged the Second Programme (1963–1970) and established foreign direct investment as central to Irish economic strategy thereafter.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
