        "question": "How successful was nationalism in the Balkans between 1815 and 1871?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Nationalism in the Balkans between 1815 and 1871 achieved significant but incomplete success. While nationalist movements secured independent statehood for several territories and established the principle of national self-determination against Ottoman rule, they remained constrained by great power intervention, internal divisions, and the persistence of Ottoman control over substantial Balkan lands by 1871.\n\nThe Greek War of Independence (1821–1829) represents the most substantial nationalist success of the period. Greek nationalists, inspired by Enlightenment ideals and Byzantine cultural memory, rebelled against Ottoman rule. Despite initial setbacks, including the massacre at Chios (1822), Greek forces under leaders like Theodoros Kolokotronis secured military victories. Crucially, the great powers—Russia, Britain, and France—intervened diplomatically and militarily, recognising Greek nationalist claims as legitimate. The Treaty of London (1829) recognised Greek independence, and the London Protocol (1830) established Greece as a sovereign kingdom. This success demonstrated that nationalism could overcome Ottoman resistance when backed by international support, setting a precedent for subsequent Balkan struggles.\n\nHowever, the Greek victory masked significant limitations on nationalist success. The Greek kingdom established in 1830 encompassed only the southern mainland and some islands—substantially less territory than Greek nationalists claimed. The Ionian Islands remained under British control, and millions of Greek-speakers lived under Ottoman rule in Anatolia and the eastern Mediterranean. This gap between nationalist aspirations and actual territorial gains would persist throughout the century, illustrating how great power interests constrained nationalist achievements.\n\nThe Serbian independence movement achieved qualified success through a different trajectory. Serbian uprisings (1804–1815 under Karađorđe, and 1815–1817 under Miloš Obrenović) against Ottoman rule gradually secured autonomy rather than immediate independence. The Treaty of Bucharest (1812) and subsequent agreements granted Serbia limited self-government within Ottoman sovereignty. By 1830, Serbia had achieved de facto independence under Miloš Obrenović, though Ottoman suzerainty persisted formally until 1878. The 1835 Constitution attempted to create a modern Serbian state, though it remained constrained by Ottoman overlordship and great power involvement, particularly Russian influence. Serbian nationalism succeeded in establishing a functioning state apparatus and national consciousness, but complete sovereignty eluded it within this period.\n\nRomanian nationalism similarly achieved partial success. The 1848 Wallachian and Moldavian revolutions, though suppressed by Russian and Ottoman forces, articulated nationalist demands for union and independence. The Crimean War (1853–1856) altered the balance: the Treaty of Paris (1856) granted the Danubian Principalities (Wallachia and Moldavia) increased autonomy. In 1859, the principalities achieved union under Alexandru Ioan Cuza as 'Romania,' establishing a nationalist state, though Ottoman suzerainty and Russian influence persisted. Romanian nationalism thus secured institutional consolidation and symbolic national identity, but full sovereignty remained incomplete.\n\nBulgarian nationalism, by contrast, achieved only limited success before 1871. The April Uprising (1876) lay outside this period, but throughout 1815–1871, Bulgaria remained under direct Ottoman rule without achieving autonomous status comparable to Serbia or Romania. Bulgarian nationalist sentiment developed through cultural and educational movements, particularly through the work of ecclesiastical figures seeking to establish a Bulgarian Orthodox church independent of the Greek Patriarchate. The Bulgarian Exarchate was eventually established in 1870, representing a significant cultural-nationalist achievement that asserted Bulgarian Orthodox identity against Greek ecclesiastical dominance. However, this remained a cultural rather than political victory; Bulgaria achieved no territorial autonomy.\n\nGreat power competition fundamentally shaped the limits of Balkan nationalism. The Concert of Europe, while occasionally supporting nationalist causes (as in Greece), primarily sought to maintain order and prevent Russian expansion at Ottoman expense. The Eastern Question—how to manage Ottoman decline—became a framework through which European powers managed Balkan nationalism. Russia supported Slavic nationalism partly from Pan-Slavic ideology but primarily to advance imperial interests. Britain and Austria-Hungary opposed nationalist movements that might destabilise Ottoman territory or increase Russian influence. This competition meant that nationalism succeeded only when aligned with great power interests: Greek independence suited Britain and France as well as Russian interests; Serbian autonomy pleased Russia but concerned Austria-Hungary; Romanian consolidation occurred within Franco-Russian diplomatic cooperation. Nationalism that threatened existing great power arrangements—such as comprehensive Balkan unification or radical social revolution—was suppressed.\n\nInternal divisions also constrained nationalist success. Balkan nationalists competed with Ottoman loyalists, regional particularisms, and social conservatism within their own societies. Religious divisions (Orthodox, Catholic, and Muslim populations) complicated nationalist projects. Economic underdevelopment and limited literacy restricted nationalist mobilisation to educated urban elites. Moreover, different Balkan nationalist movements competed with each other: Greek nationalists disputed territory with Slavic nationalists; Serbian and Bulgarian nationalism would later conflict over Macedonia. These internal fractures prevented unified Balkan resistance to Ottoman rule and meant that nationalist states, when established, remained weak and contested.\n\nBy 1871, the overall picture of Balkan nationalism was mixed achievement. Greece, Serbia, and Romania had escaped Ottoman direct rule and established functioning independent or autonomous states with national institutions, constitutions, and international recognition. Yet vast Balkan territories—Bulgaria, Macedonia, Bosnia, Albania—remained under Ottoman control. Ottoman rule persisted over millions of Balkan Christians. The great powers had recognised the principle that Christian nationalism in the Balkans had legitimacy, yet they constrained its realisation through competition and management of the Eastern Question. Nationalism had proven powerful enough to fragment the Ottoman Balkans but insufficient to fully overcome Ottoman resistance or to establish comprehensive Balkan national states.\n\nIn conclusion, Balkan nationalism between 1815 and 1871 achieved significant success in establishing the principle of national self-determination and in creating independent or autonomous states in Greece, Serbia, and Romania. Yet this success remained partial and contested. Millions of Balkan Christians remained under Ottoman rule; great power interests constrained fuller nationalist realisation; and internal divisions within Balkan societies limited nationalist consolidation. The period established the trajectory of Balkan nationalism—eventual triumph—but by 1871, that triumph remained incomplete. The Balkan Wars and Balkan nationalism's fuller realisation lay in the twentieth century, after the Ottoman collapse of 1912–1913.",
