        "question": "In what ways did the movement for Italian unification make progress in the years leading up to 1871?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Italian unification progressed significantly between 1815 and 1871 through a combination of nationalist ideology, strategic diplomacy, military campaigns, and the exploitation of regional weakness. Progress occurred in phases, with each phase building on previous gains until the creation of a unified Italian state in 1871.\n\nIntellectual and ideological foundations emerged early. Mazzini's Young Italy movement, founded in 1831, articulated a vision of a unified, independent, and republican Italy. Though initially unsuccessful in generating popular revolution—insurrections in 1831 and 1848 failed—Mazzini's ideas profoundly shaped nationalist consciousness. His emphasis on popular sovereignty and national self-determination provided ideological legitimacy for the unification project, inspiring conspirators and mobilizing educated elites across Italian territories. This intellectual framework was essential: without Mazzinian nationalism, the practical political and military achievements that followed would have lacked coherent purpose.\n\nCavour's diplomatic strategy represented a decisive shift from failed revolutionary romanticism to pragmatic statecraft. As Prime Minister of Piedmont-Sardinia from 1852, Cavour pursued modernization and expansion through careful diplomacy rather than popular uprising. He transformed Piedmont into a strong, credible alternative centre of Italian leadership. Economic reforms, railway building, and administrative efficiency made Piedmont the natural nucleus for unification. Crucially, Cavour secured French support against Austria, historically the greatest obstacle to Italian unity. The secret alliance at Plombières (1858) committed France to support Piedmont if Austria attacked. This diplomatic coup created the geopolitical conditions for military progress.\n\nMilitary victories translated diplomatic opportunity into territorial gains. The Austro-Piedmontese War of 1859 drove Austria from Lombardy. Though French intervention prevented complete Austrian expulsion and the war ended through the Treaty of Villafranca, Piedmont retained Lombardy—a major Italian territory. Plebiscites in Tuscany, Modena, Parma, and the Papal Legations in 1860 brought these territories into union with Piedmont, expanding the unified state significantly. Cavour's willingness to cede Savoy and Nice to France secured continued French benevolence; this apparent concession actually facilitated larger gains elsewhere.\n\nGaribaldi's popular campaigns extended unification southward. His expedition to Sicily and Naples in 1860, backed by Cavour though undertaken independently, achieved rapid military success against the Bourbon kingdom of the Two Sicilies. Garibaldi's volunteer army (the \"Thousand\") defeated Bourbon forces and mobilized popular support through their nationalist appeal. Crucially, Garibaldi's victories were subsequently incorporated into the constitutional unification process: he handed conquered territory to Victor Emmanuel II, allowing Piedmont to absorb the South while marginalizing republican alternatives. Plebiscites in Sicily and Naples (October 1860) confirmed union with Piedmont. This integration of the South—the most populous region—was impossible without Garibaldi's military achievement, yet its constitutional absorption prevented republican fragmentation.\n\nThe 1861 proclamation of the Kingdom of Italy represented tangible consolidation of these gains. Victor Emmanuel II was declared king of Italy; Cavour became first Prime Minister. Italian territory now encompassed the North (including Lombardy), the Centre, and the South (Naples and Sicily)—though two significant gaps remained: Rome and Venetia. This intermediate success proved critical: it established a functioning state that could pursue final unification from strength rather than weakness.\n\nThe acquisition of Venetia in 1866 closed one gap. After Cavour's death (1861), Italy allied with Prussia against Austria. Though the Italian campaign was poorly conducted, Prussian victory over Austria in the Austro-Prussian War compelled Austrian concession of Venetia to Italy. This demonstrated how broader European great-power politics favored Italian progress: whenever Austria faced stronger rivals, Italy could advance. Venetia's inclusion brought northeastern Italy into the kingdom.\n\nRome's incorporation in 1870 completed territorial unification. The Franco-Prussian War (1870–71) weakened French capacity to defend papal Rome, hitherto protected by French troops. Italian forces entered Rome; Pope Pius IX was confined to the Vatican. While the resulting \"Roman Question\"—papal-Italian antagonism—lasted decades, Rome was now Italian, not a foreign enclave or papal possession.\n\nProgress also occurred through the normalization of Italian diplomatic standing. By 1870, Italy participated in international conferences, secured alliances (with Prussia/Germany), and was recognized as a major European power. The Risorgimento transformed Italy from fragmented territories under foreign dominance to a unified nation-state seated at the table of European great powers.\n\nLimitations and unfinished work remained. Much of the South remained economically underdeveloped and resistant to northern Piedmontese integration. The institutional absorption of diverse regions into a single constitutional framework faced obstacles. Vatican-Italian antagonism posed ongoing difficulties. Yet measured against the 1815 starting point—Austria dominant in Lombardy-Venetia, minor Italian states lacking independence, no unified state structure—the progress by 1871 was remarkable. Through ideological commitment, strategic statesmanship, military success, and opportunistic use of European great-power competition, Italian nationalists transformed their fragmented peninsula into a unified European state.",
