        "question": "Why was Wilfred Owen honoured, both as a soldier and as a poet, following his role in World War I?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Wilfred Owen was honoured both as a soldier and as a poet because of his dual contribution to understanding and representing the First World War. As a soldier, he demonstrated exceptional courage and leadership on the Western Front, while as a poet, he produced work of enduring literary significance that challenged prevailing narratives of the war.\n\nAs a soldier, Owen enlisted in the Artists' Rifles in 1915 and was commissioned as a second lieutenant in the Manchester Regiment in 1916. He served with distinction on the Western Front, participating in major offensives including the Battle of the Somme. Owen's military record earned him the Military Cross in July 1918 for bravery during operations near the Oise River, where he led his men with conspicuous gallantry. This decoration recognized his exceptional conduct under fire and his leadership qualities in one of the war's most gruelling campaigns. He returned to active service after recovering from shell shock, demonstrating remarkable resilience and commitment to duty—qualities that earned him the respect of his contemporaries and military superiors. His willingness to return to the front line despite psychological trauma illustrated the character and fortitude expected of an officer.\n\nHowever, Owen's enduring honour rests primarily on his poetry. Before his death in November 1918, just days before the Armistice, Owen had produced a body of work that fundamentally altered how the First World War was understood and represented. His poems employed innovative techniques—including para-rhyme, internal assonance, and fragmented syntax—to convey the psychological and physical horror of trench warfare. Works such as 'Anthem for Doomed Youth', 'Dulce et Decorum Est', and 'Strange Meeting' rejected the patriotic, jingoistic poetry that had dominated earlier war verse. Instead, Owen depicted the reality of modern mechanized warfare: the gas attacks, the mud, the mutilation, and the moral ambiguity of a conflict that offered no redemptive narrative.\n\n'Dulce et Decorum Est', one of his most celebrated poems, directly confronted the classical Latin tag that patriotic sacrifice was 'sweet and fitting'. Owen's bitter final line—'The old Lie'—exposed how traditional rhetoric had been weaponized to send young men to their deaths. This poetic challenge to official narratives and popular mythology gave voice to the soldier's experience in ways that formal military histories could not. His work validated the experiences of ordinary soldiers and challenged readers to confront uncomfortable truths about industrial warfare.\n\nOwen's status as both combatant and poet amplified his authority. He could not be dismissed as a pacifist theorist or someone remote from the fighting. His poetry carried the weight of direct experience, witnessed suffering, and hard-won moral insight. This combination of battlefield credibility and literary achievement made his voice particularly powerful. He wrote not from safety but from the trenches, and died in action before his full artistic maturity could be realized—a circumstance that deepened the poignancy and impact of his legacy.\n\nFollowing his death, Owen's reputation grew steadily, though initially slowly. His friend Siegfried Sassoon championed his work, and by the mid-twentieth century, Owen was recognized as one of the greatest English-language poets of the century. His dual honour reflects the recognition that he embodied both the sacrifice and the moral reckoning that the First World War demanded. As a soldier, he fulfilled his duty with courage; as a poet, he preserved the authentic memory of the war and ensured that subsequent generations understood its true cost—not in abstractions of national glory, but in the broken bodies and traumatized minds of those who survived it, and in the names of those, like Owen himself, who did not.",
