        "label": "1",
        "question": "Who was the more successful campaigner, Michael Davitt or James Larkin?\nArgue your case, referring to both.",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Michael Davitt was the more successful campaigner. Although both men fought for the dispossessed in Ireland, Davitt achieved lasting institutional and legislative change that reshaped Irish society, whereas Larkin's achievements, though dramatic, proved more ephemeral and were severely undermined within years of his peak activity.\n\nDavitt's success rested on his creation of a mass movement with enduring political power. Having survived transportation for Fenian activities (1870–1877), he emerged to found the Land League in 1879 alongside Charles Stewart Parnell. This organisation mobilised tens of thousands of tenant farmers in a coordinated challenge to landlord power. The League's campaigns were strategically sophisticated: it combined agrarian agitation with parliamentary pressure, public boycotting tactics (notably against Captain Boycott), and international fundraising, particularly from Irish-America. Between 1879 and 1882, the Land War forced the British government to acknowledge that the Irish land system was fundamentally unjust. Davitt's movement directly precipitated the Land Act of 1881, which established the \"three Fs\"—fair rent, fixity of tenure, and free sale—for Irish tenants. This was transformative legislation that addressed the core grievance of the Irish rural poor and fundamentally altered property relations in Ireland. The Act benefited hundreds of thousands of tenant farmers and marked a decisive shift in Irish land ownership from landlord dominance towards peasant proprietorship.\n\nDavitt's influence extended beyond the Land War. His journalism, speeches, and writing (notably *The Fall of Feudalism in Ireland*, 1904) made him an intellectual force in Irish nationalist thought. He articulated a vision of Irish independence rooted in social justice, not merely constitutional nationalism. He remained an active parliamentarian and Land League organiser into the 1890s, sustaining his cause even as the organisation evolved. The institutional legacy of his work—transformed land tenure, weakened landlordism, and peasant proprietorship—persisted and shaped Irish rural society for generations.\n\nJames Larkin's campaign, by contrast, centred on urban labour and was geographically and temporally narrower in scope. Larkin arrived in Ireland in 1907 as a trade union organiser and rapidly mobilised Dublin's poorly paid dock workers and general labourers. His fiery oratory, militancy, and willingness to stage strikes made him a charismatic figure and earned him the title \"Big Jim\". The 1913 Dublin Lockout, precipitated by employer resistance to unionisation, became the defining moment of his Irish career. For several months, Larkin led a massive strike of over 20,000 workers against determined employer opposition led by William Martin Murphy. The lockout drew international attention and Larkin's defiant speeches and mass rallies demonstrated his ability to inspire working-class consciousness.\n\nHowever, the 1913 lockout ended in defeat for the workers. The strike collapsed due to lack of funds, employer solidarity, and the absence of meaningful support from British trade unions or the Irish parliamentary party. Workers were forced to return on employers' terms, with few concessions won. Larkin's attempts to establish a general workers' union and his vision of syndicalist revolution failed to take root institutionally. By 1914, exhausted and isolated, Larkin emigrated to America, leaving Irish labour organising in a weakened state. His departure meant that the momentum he had built dissipated; the Irish labour movement did not recover significant strength until after Irish independence.\n\nWhereas Davitt's legislative victories were concrete and durable, Larkin's campaign resulted in no significant legislative reform and no lasting institutional gain. The employers' victory in 1913 was near-total; wages and conditions for Dublin workers remained appalling. Larkin's radical vision of workers' control and syndicalism had no prospect of immediate realisation in the Irish or British political context of the era. His emigration to America in 1914 marked the practical end of his Irish campaign; he achieved international prominence only after leaving Ireland.\n\nBoth men fought for the powerless against entrenched privilege—Davitt for tenant farmers against landlords, Larkin for urban workers against employers. Both were oratorically gifted, personally courageous, and willing to endure imprisonment and hardship for their causes. Both challenged the prevailing orthodoxy that the existing system was inevitable and just. Yet success must be measured by concrete outcomes: legislative change, institutional development, and sustained improvement in the lives of those campaigned for.\n\nOn these criteria, Davitt achieves a clear superiority. The Land Acts of 1881 and subsequent legislation fundamentally transformed Irish rural property relations and benefited hundreds of thousands of people whose descendants continued to own their land. The institutional mechanisms Davitt helped establish—tenant right, state-facilitated land purchase—persisted into the twentieth century and beyond. The Land League, though it evolved, became a durable framework for rural political organisation.\n\nLarkin's campaign, for all its moral passion and historical drama, failed to achieve comparable structural change. The 1913 lockout was a defeat, not a victory. No trade union legislation favourable to Irish workers followed. Larkin's departure to America in 1914, followed by Irish independence in 1921, meant that his vision of syndicalist labour revolution was never tested in an independent Irish state. Instead, the Irish labour movement's development was shaped by other forces and figures.\n\nIn conclusion, Michael Davitt was the more successful campaigner. His work produced measurable, durable legislative reform that affected millions of Irish people and fundamentally altered Irish society. Larkin's campaign, though historically important and morally compelling, achieved no comparable institutional or legislative outcome and ended in defeat and emigration. Success in social campaigning must be judged by tangible results; by that standard, Davitt's achievement far exceeds Larkin's.",
