        "question": "During the years 1870-1914, how effective was Unionism in resisting Home Rule?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Unionism was highly effective in resisting Home Rule during 1870–1914, successfully blocking three Home Rule bills and maintaining the Union until 1914, though ultimately failing to prevent the Third Home Rule Bill's passage. Unionist effectiveness operated through multiple mechanisms: political organisation, constitutional leverage, mass mobilisation, and strategic alliances.\n\nPolitically, Unionists commanded significant parliamentary strength. After 1886, the split in the Liberal Party—when Joseph Chamberlain and the Liberal Unionists broke with Gladstone over Home Rule—fundamentally weakened Irish Home Rule prospects. The Liberal Unionists merged with the Conservative Party in 1912, consolidating anti-Home Rule forces. Between 1886 and 1906, Conservatives dominated parliament, blocking Home Rule entirely. When Liberals returned to power in 1906, the House of Lords remained Conservative-controlled, providing crucial institutional resistance.\n\nThe House of Lords proved central to Unionist strategy. It rejected the Second Home Rule Bill (1893) decisively, with the Lords viewing Home Rule as a threat to imperial unity and property rights. This veto remained invulnerable until the 1911 Parliament Act reduced the Lords' power to delay legislation by two years rather than reject it outright. This constitutional reform marked a turning point: the Third Home Rule Bill (1914) could now pass, demonstrating that Unionist reliance on the Lords—however effective previously—was ultimately temporary.\n\nIn Ireland itself, Unionism was strongest in Ulster, particularly among Protestant farmers and industrial workers in Belfast. Ulster Unionists, led by James Craig and Edward Carson from 1910, mobilised fiercely against Home Rule. The Ulster Covenant (1912) bound 250,000+ Ulstermen to resist Home Rule by any means, signalling readiness for armed resistance. The Ulster Volunteer Force (1913), numbering 100,000 by 1914, constituted a credible military threat that forced the government to take Ulster opposition seriously. This armed mobilisation made Home Rule administratively difficult—implementing it would require suppressing armed resistance.\n\nBritish Unionism extended beyond Ulster. English and Scottish Unionists, particularly business interests and landowners, feared Home Rule would weaken imperial economic integration and expose British industries to Irish nationalist tariffs. Working-class Unionism also existed in industrial areas, rooted in Protestant identity and imperial pride.\n\nUnionist effectiveness, however, was ultimately limited by structural factors beyond their control. The 1906 Liberal landslide, reducing Conservative strength from 402 to 157 MPs, temporarily undermined Unionist parliamentary dominance. The Parliament Act (1911) decisively weakened the Lords' veto power. By 1914, despite Unionist resistance, the Third Home Rule Bill had passed both Commons and Lords and received royal assent. Asquith's government, dependent on Irish nationalist MPs since losing its overall majority in January 1910, prioritised Home Rule to maintain parliamentary support—80+ Irish nationalist MPs held the balance of power.\n\nUnionism's resistance strategy also contained internal contradictions. The threat of Ulster armed resistance, while politically disruptive, created a justification for postponement rather than permanent prevention of Home Rule. The government's decision to suspend Home Rule's operation until after World War I—rather than reject it—represented a Unionist tactical victory but not final success. The bill remained law, awaiting implementation.\n\nContextually, Unionism operated within genuine constitutional constraints. It could not prevent legislation passing through a democratically elected Commons, only delay it through institutional mechanisms (the Lords, parliamentary arithmetic). The democratic principle that Home Rule had majority Irish and British public support ultimately constrained Unionist capacity to resist indefinitely.\n\nIn conclusion, Unionism was strikingly effective in resisting Home Rule for over two decades (1886–1914), preventing its implementation through parliamentary obstruction, Lords rejection, and Irish armed resistance. However, this effectiveness was not permanent. By 1914, Home Rule became law despite Unionist opposition. Unionism's greatest achievement—delaying Home Rule and securing Ulster's special status in subsequent negotiations—came through disruption rather than absolute prevention. The effectiveness of Unionism in resisting Home Rule was therefore conditional and temporary: maximally successful in blocking three attempts across 28 years, ultimately unsuccessful in preventing the Third Bill's enactment, and moderately successful in extracting concessions regarding Ulster's treatment. Unionism demonstrated that determined, organised, and well-resourced opposition could frustrate reform for extended periods but could not indefinitely resist change when supported by parliamentary majorities.",
        "diagram": "",
