        "question": "How did Isabella Tod and/or Hanna Sheehy-Skeffington contribute to social reform?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Isabella Tod (1836–1896) and Hanna Sheehy-Skeffington (1877–1946) were pivotal figures in social reform in Ireland during the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, addressing women's education, suffrage, and wider social justice through sustained activism and strategic organisation.\n\nIsabella Tod's contributions to social reform were primarily educational and philanthropic. As a Quaker living in Belfast, she founded the Ladies' Institute in 1867, providing women with access to education and intellectual engagement at a time when such opportunities were severely limited. Her work reflected the broader women's education movement of the nineteenth century. Beyond education, Tod was deeply involved in temperance work, seeing alcohol as a root cause of social degradation and poverty. She understood temperance as inseparable from women's welfare, as excessive drinking devastated working-class families and left women economically vulnerable. Tod also championed women's economic independence through her advocacy for women's employment and her work with the Women's Suffrage Society. Her approach was methodical and institutional—she built lasting organisations rather than relying on individual gestures. The Ladies' Institute became a model for women's education in Ulster, establishing a legacy of organised female intellectual life. Tod's activism occurred within the constraints of her era; she worked within existing social structures and emphasised the respectability and moral authority of educated women as agents of social improvement. She believed that women's education would naturally lead to recognition of women's political rights, though she lived before women's suffrage was achieved in Ireland.\n\nHanna Sheehy-Skeffington's contributions to social reform were more directly political and confrontational, reflecting a new generation of activism. Born into a nationalist Catholic family, she studied at the Royal University and became a teacher, but increasingly devoted herself to women's rights and Irish independence. With her husband Francis Sheehy-Skeffington, she co-founded the Irish Women's Franchise League in 1908, campaigning explicitly for women's suffrage. Unlike Tod's gradualist approach, Sheehy-Skeffington embraced militant tactics influenced by the British suffragette movement. She participated in public marches, organised petitions, and used the press to publicise women's political disenfranchisement. A critical distinction in her work was the linking of women's suffrage to Irish independence. Sheehy-Skeffington argued that Irish women were doubly oppressed—as women without votes and as Irish people under British rule—and that women's suffrage must be part of any Irish independence settlement. She also engaged in broader social reform, advocating for women's economic rights, equal pay, and improved conditions for working women. Her journalism and public speaking made her a public figure and spokesperson for feminist causes in Ireland.\n\nBoth women contributed to reform of women's legal status, though through different mechanisms. Tod's work in education created intellectual foundations for women's claims to full citizenship; educated women could argue from knowledge and reasoned argument for their rights. Sheehy-Skeffington's direct political agitation brought women's suffrage into public debate and forced Irish political parties to address it. By 1914, women's suffrage had become a significant political issue in Ireland, largely through the visibility created by activists like Sheehy-Skeffington, though the outbreak of World War I interrupted the campaign.\n\nThe two women also differed in their relationship to nationalism. Tod, a Protestant unionist, operated within an all-Ireland framework but was not primarily motivated by nationalist politics. Sheehy-Skeffington integrated women's rights into nationalist ideology, arguing that Irish independence without women's rights would be incomplete. This distinction reflected broader shifts in Irish politics between 1870 and 1914, as nationalist sentiment intensified and became more central to political identity.\n\nBeyond suffrage and education, both women engaged in social welfare concerns. Tod's temperance work addressed poverty and domestic violence rooted in alcoholism. Sheehy-Skeffington's concern for working women's conditions reflected emerging labour movements and socialist influences on Irish politics. They understood social reform holistically—that women's legal and political equality was connected to economic independence, education, and freedom from exploitation.\n\nThe impact of their work extended beyond their individual lifetimes. Tod's educational initiatives established institutional precedents for women's learning in Ireland. Sheehy-Skeffington's political agitation created organisational models for women's activism and connected women's rights to the broader Irish independence movement, ensuring that women's issues remained part of political discourse during the revolutionary period after 1916. Women's suffrage was eventually achieved in Ireland as part of post-independence settlement, though the process was complicated by civil war and partition.\n\nIn conclusion, Isabella Tod and Hanna Sheehy-Skeffington contributed to social reform through complementary but distinct strategies. Tod pioneered institutional reform in education and moral reform through temperance, establishing women's intellectual authority. Sheehy-Skeffington brought women's political rights into explicit public and political debate through militant organisation and journalism, linking women's suffrage to Irish nationalism. Together, they broadened the scope of 'social reform' to include women's full participation in Irish society—as educated citizens, political agents, and economically independent persons. Their work reflected and shaped the evolution of Irish society from 1870 to 1914, establishing frameworks for women's rights that would influence Irish politics for generations.",
