        "question": "In what ways did the Republic of Ireland expand its involvement in international affairs, 1949-1989?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Between 1949 and 1989, the Republic of Ireland significantly expanded its involvement in international affairs, moving from relative isolation to active participation in major international organisations and a more assertive foreign policy.\n\nThe most substantial development was Ireland's entry into the United Nations in 1955, ending its previous abstention from formal international bodies. This membership provided a platform for Ireland to pursue independent diplomatic objectives. Ireland's UN involvement reflected a shift from de Valera's policy of isolationism. Throughout the 1960s and 1970s, Ireland took an active role in UN debates, particularly on decolonisation issues and peacekeeping. Irish troops participated in UN peacekeeping missions, including in the Congo (1960-1964) and Cyprus, demonstrating a commitment to international stability and service beyond Irish borders. This represented a marked departure from the inward-looking policies of the previous era.\n\nIreland's accession to the European Economic Community (EEC) in 1973 was transformative for international engagement. This decision fundamentally altered Ireland's economic and political orientation toward Europe. Membership in the EEC required Ireland to coordinate foreign policy with European partners and participate in European Political Cooperation (established 1970, formalised in the Single European Act 1987). Through EEC membership, Ireland gained access to larger markets, received structural and agricultural funds, and integrated into European institutions. Irish participation in EEC/EC bodies meant engagement with major international economic and political processes. By the 1980s, Irish ministers sat on European councils, Irish MEPs served in the European Parliament, and Irish officials worked within EC bureaucracy. This integration represented an unprecedented level of involvement in international governance structures.\n\nIreland also expanded bilateral diplomatic relations during this period. The establishment and development of formal diplomatic missions increased Irish presence globally. Irish embassies and consulates expanded in number and function, enabling more direct engagement with individual nations on trade, cultural, and political matters. This created multiple channels for Irish influence rather than reliance on a single international framework.\n\nCultural and educational exchange programmes broadened Ireland's international profile. The promotion of Irish language, literature, and culture abroad through state-sponsored initiatives enhanced Ireland's soft power. Educational scholarships and cultural grants facilitated international connections, positioning Ireland not merely as a participant in international structures but as a distinct cultural presence on the world stage.\n\nIreland's approach to neutrality also evolved during this period, though formally maintained. While Ireland remained non-aligned militarily, its participation in UN peacekeeping and EEC membership represented practical engagement in international affairs that exceeded strict neutrality. Ireland balanced this by refusing NATO membership and maintaining independence on key votes, but the overall trajectory showed gradual integration into Western institutional frameworks.\n\nThe expansion of trade and economic cooperation represented another dimension of international involvement. Irish governments actively promoted exports and foreign investment, negotiating trade agreements and participating in international economic forums. The shift from an agricultural to an increasingly industrial economy required engagement with international markets and regulatory bodies.\n\nBy 1989, Ireland's international position had transformed fundamentally. From a state that sought minimal involvement in international bodies beyond the League of Nations, Ireland had become an active UN member, a member of the European Community with representation in supranational institutions, and a participant in international peacekeeping. The Republic engaged diplomatically across multiple continents and participated in forums addressing global issues from decolonisation to environmental protection. This expansion reflected both pragmatic recognition that Irish interests were served by engagement with the international community and a growing confidence in Ireland's capacity to participate as an equal partner in international affairs. The isolation of the de Valera era had given way to a more outward-looking, internationally engaged state.",
