        "question": "What contrasting views of society were held by Clemens Metternich and Karl Marx?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Metternich and Marx held fundamentally opposed visions of society rooted in their contrasting experiences and ideologies. Metternich, as Austrian Foreign Minister from 1809, sought to preserve the existing hierarchical, dynastic order established by the Congress of Vienna (1815). He viewed society as an organic whole bound by tradition, monarchy, and aristocratic privilege. The state should be centralized under absolute monarchical authority, with clearly defined class structures that reflected divine order and historical precedent. Metternich feared rapid social change and democratic reform, believing they would trigger chaos and revolution. He advocated for international stability through the Concert of Europe and domestic stability through suppression of liberal and nationalist movements—seen in his repression of student radicalism and his support for intervention against revolutions (such as supporting Austria's invasion of Hungary in 1849). For Metternich, society functioned best when traditional elites retained power and the masses remained politically passive.\n\nMarx, writing from the 1840s onward, rejected this entire framework. He argued that society was not naturally hierarchical but historically dynamic, shaped by economic forces and class conflict. Marx contended that capitalism—the dominant economic system of the nineteenth century—created inherent contradictions and inevitably generated class struggle between the bourgeoisie (who owned capital) and the proletariat (workers). Unlike Metternich's static view, Marx saw history as progressive, moving through stages (feudalism, capitalism, socialism). He believed capitalism contained the seeds of its own destruction; as industrialization advanced and wealth concentrated, workers would eventually recognize their exploitation and unite to overthrow the capitalist class. Marx rejected monarchy and aristocracy as feudal remnants incompatible with modern production. Where Metternich saw order and stability as desirable ends, Marx saw revolution and the dictatorship of the proletariat as historically necessary and inevitable.\n\nRegarding nationalism, their views also diverged sharply. Metternich opposed nationalism because it threatened the multi-ethnic Habsburg Empire and the Concert of Europe. He viewed nationalism as a dangerous ideology promoting instability and the breakup of legitimate dynastic states. Marx, initially sympathetic to nationalist movements as anti-feudal forces, later developed a more complex position. He believed that national liberation could advance capitalism and thus hasten its eventual collapse, but he ultimately saw nationalism as a distraction from the deeper reality of class conflict. Working-class solidarity across national boundaries (internationalism) was, for Marx, the true motor of historical change.\n\nOn the role of the state, their disagreement was categorical. Metternich believed a strong centralized state, headed by a monarch and staffed by experienced aristocrats, was essential to govern society effectively and maintain order. The state should intervene to suppress ideologies threatening the established order. Marx argued that the state under capitalism was merely a tool of the ruling class, enforcing capitalist relations of production and protecting bourgeois property. He believed the state would eventually wither away once communism was achieved, as it would no longer be needed to enforce class domination.\n\nMetternich's vision was essentially backward-looking: he sought to preserve and restore the pre-1789 order disrupted by the French Revolution. He represented the interests of monarchy, aristocracy, and the Catholic Church. Marx's vision was future-oriented: he sought to transcend capitalism entirely through revolution. He represented the interests of the emerging industrial working class.\n\nBoth men were responding to the massive social upheaval of the industrial age, but from opposite sides. Metternich represented the ancien régime defending itself against change; Marx represented radical critique of the emerging industrial system and a vision of a classless future. Their competing ideologies would dominate European political conflict throughout the nineteenth and twentieth centuries.",
        "diagram": "",
