        "question": "During the years 1815-1870, what key changes took place in agriculture and/or industry?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "During 1815–1870, agriculture and industry underwent transformative changes that reshaped European economies and societies.\n\nAGRICULTURAL CHANGE\nAgriculture experienced a transition from subsistence to commercial production, driven by population growth and urbanisation. In Britain, the Enclosure Acts consolidated landholdings and encouraged agricultural improvement. The introduction of new crops—particularly the potato in Eastern Europe—increased food supply and supported larger populations. Crop rotation systems improved yields. However, the repeal of the Corn Laws in 1846 exposed European farmers to global competition, particularly from cheaper American and Russian grain. This forced European agriculture towards specialisation: Scandinavian countries focused on dairy and meat exports; Mediterranean regions on wine and olive oil. The Great Famine (1845–1852) in Ireland demonstrated agriculture's vulnerability, killing approximately one million people and forcing mass emigration. Agricultural labour declined as a proportion of the workforce in Western Europe, though remained dominant in Eastern and Southern regions, reflecting uneven development.\n\nINDUSTRIAL CHANGE\nThe period witnessed the spread of industrialisation beyond Britain. The factory system, driven by steam power and mechanisation, transformed textile production and iron manufacturing. Coal mining expanded dramatically to fuel steam engines. The railways became the defining technology: Britain's railway network grew from minimal coverage in 1815 to over 9,000 miles by 1870; continental Europe followed, with the first continental railway (Brussels–Mechelen) opening in 1835. Railways facilitated trade, labour mobility, and resource transport, integrating national markets. Iron and steel production accelerated—Henry Bessemer's 1856 steel-making process reduced costs and enabled mass production. Belgium, France, and the Rhineland emerged as secondary industrial centres by 1870. Factory production increasingly replaced artisanal craftsmanship, causing social dislocation and the growth of an urban working class.\n\nCONNECTED CONSEQUENCES\nThese changes were deeply interconnected. Agricultural improvement freed labour for factories, while industrial growth created demand for agricultural surplus to feed urban populations. Railways transported both agricultural and industrial goods. The shift from rural to urban living reshaped social structures: the bourgeoisie (factory owners, merchants) gained economic and political power; the working class became concentrated in cities, creating conditions for labour organisation and socialist ideology. Nationalist movements drew energy from industrial and agricultural regions seeking economic autonomy—German and Italian unification were partly responses to creating unified markets and industrial blocs. However, the changes created stark regional inequalities: industrial regions in Britain, Belgium, and France prospered, while agricultural Eastern Europe stagnated, contributing to later tensions.\n\nBY 1870, the European economy had fundamentally shifted. Britain remained dominant but faced competition from industrialising rivals. Agriculture remained important but was no longer the primary driver of economic growth in Western Europe. The factory, the railway, and wage labour had become defining features of modern European life.",
