        "question": "What were the main moves towards European unity, 1945-1957?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "European unity between 1945 and 1957 emerged from the ruins of World War II, driven by the imperative to prevent future conflict and counter Soviet expansion. The movement developed through several distinct institutional and ideological phases.\n\nImmediate post-war initiatives established the framework for cooperation. The Brussels Treaty (1948) between Britain, France, and the Benelux countries created a military and economic alliance, explicitly rejecting fascism and promoting democratic values. However, the most transformative early steps came through American assistance. The Marshall Plan (1948-1952) provided economic aid to Western European nations on the condition of coordinated recovery efforts, fostering interdependence and creating the Organisation for European Economic Cooperation (OEEC) to distribute funds. This tied Western Europe economically to the US while demonstrating the benefits of coordinated action.\n\nSecurity concerns accelerated integration. The Soviet blockade of Berlin (1948-1949) and the outbreak of the Korean War (1950) heightened fears of communist expansion. NATO's formation (1949) created a military alliance under American leadership, but European leaders recognized that Western Europe needed its own institutional structures. The Council of Europe (1949) represented an important symbolic step, establishing a forum for political cooperation and promoting shared democratic values, though it lacked binding authority.\n\nThe Schuman Plan (1950) represented the pivotal breakthrough toward genuine integration. French Foreign Minister Robert Schuman proposed placing French and German coal and steel production under a shared supranational authority. This addressed two critical problems: it prevented Franco-German conflict by making war economically irrational, and it provided German industrial revival within a controlled framework acceptable to other European nations. The Plan reflected Jean Monnet's functionalist approach—that cooperation in specific economic sectors would gradually create political unity.\n\nThe European Coal and Steel Community (ECSC), established by the Treaty of Paris (1951) and operational from 1952, represented the first supranational institution. Its six members—France, West Germany, Italy, Belgium, Netherlands, and Luxembourg—surrendered some sovereignty to a High Authority, a Court, and a Common Assembly. The ECSC successfully coordinated coal and steel production, demonstrated the viability of supranational governance, and allowed German industrial participation under international oversight. Its success created momentum for broader integration.\n\nThe immediate post-ECSC period saw both progress and setbacks. The European Defence Community (EDC) proposal (1952) attempted to create integrated European armed forces, but French fears of German rearmament led to its rejection by the French National Assembly (1954). This failure revealed the limits of rapid integration and nationalist sensitivities. However, the Western European Union (WEU), formed in 1955 as a modified version of the Brussels Treaty framework, enabled German rearmament within a European context while reassuring France through British participation.\n\nThe Treaties of Rome (1957) constituted the culmination of this integrative process. Two separate treaties established the European Economic Community (EEC) and the European Atomic Energy Community (Euratom). The EEC, signed by the same six ECSC members, represented an unprecedented commitment to economic integration. It envisaged the gradual elimination of tariffs and quotas between member states, creating a common market; established a Common External Tariff; and aimed at free movement of labor, capital, and services. The treaty created supranational institutions—a Commission (executive), a Council of Ministers, a European Parliament, and a Court of Justice—with real power to enforce integration.\n\nThe EEC treaty reflected evolved thinking on integration. Rather than sector-specific functionalism, it pursued comprehensive economic integration across all sectors, based on the assumption that economic interdependence would generate political solidarity. The concept of \"ever closer union\" embedded in the treaty expressed an explicitly federalist aspiration. The treaty's provisions for a customs union and common external tariff demonstrated commitment to deep integration beyond mere cooperation.\n\nSeveral factors drove these moves toward unity. The shared trauma of World War II created determination to end recurrent European conflict. The Cold War made Western European unity strategically essential for resisting Soviet expansion. American support through Marshall Aid and NATO provided security guarantees and economic incentives for integration. The Franco-German reconciliation, facilitated by Schuman and Monnet, removed the central obstacle to European unity. Economic logic suggested that larger markets and coordinated development would accelerate reconstruction and growth. Ideological commitment to democracy and against fascism created shared values transcending nationalism.\n\nThese developments were not inevitable or unopposed. Britain, under American pressure, declined ECSC membership, preferring Commonwealth and American relationships to continental commitment. Nationalism remained a powerful force—French rejection of EDC demonstrated persisting unease with supranationalism. The integration that occurred involved only six Western European states, leaving Eastern Europe under Soviet domination and Scandinavia and Britain outside.\n\nBy 1957, the movement toward European unity had achieved substantial institutional structures with supranational authority. The EEC represented the most ambitious attempt yet to transcend the nation-state in favor of coordinated governance. Whether termed integration, unification, or unity, the period 1945-1957 established the frameworks and momentum that would define European political development for subsequent decades. The Cold War context was essential—these institutions reflected Western commitment to unity as a defensive and reconstructive strategy, not as an autonomous European choice.",
