        "question": "To what extent did the careers of both Nikita Khrushchev and Mikhail Gorbachev end in failure?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Both Khrushchev and Gorbachev experienced significant career reversals, yet the nature and extent of their 'failure' differ substantially in context and consequence.\n\nKhrushchev's fall from power in October 1964 was a decisive political defeat. After consolidating control following Stalin's death in 1953, Khrushchev pursued de-Stalinization—culminating in his secret speech to the 20th Party Congress in 1956, which denounced Stalin's crimes. This boldly reformed Soviet ideology but destabilized the Communist Bloc. The Hungarian uprising of 1956 and the Suez Crisis exposed Soviet limitations, damaging Khrushchev's authority. Domestically, his agricultural policies—particularly the Virgin Lands scheme and forced collectivization—failed catastrophically, causing food shortages by the early 1960s. His educational reforms and attempted rationalization of party structures alienated both the military-industrial establishment and regional party elites. Crucially, the Cuban Missile Crisis of 1962, though resolving without nuclear war, was perceived as a retreat before American pressure, weakening his standing with hardliners. The Presidium, led by Leonid Brezhnev, ousted him on 14 October 1964 in a coordinated coup. He was stripped of all posts and forced into obscurity, dying in 1971. This was unambiguous failure: total loss of power, public humiliation, and the repudiation of his reformist agenda by his successors.\n\nGorbachev's career ended differently, though equally unsuccessfully in formal terms. His rise to General Secretary in March 1985 was intended to revitalize the Soviet system through controlled reform. Glasnost (openness) and perestroika (restructuring) were radical policies—permitting press criticism, releasing political prisoners, and attempting economic decentralization. These reforms unleashed forces beyond Gorbachev's control. Glasnost exposed the true scale of Stalinist repression and Soviet failures, delegitimizing communist ideology. Nationalism in the Baltic states, Ukraine, and the Caucasus surged; the Soviet system, held together by ideological commitment and coercion, began fragmenting. The failed hardline coup attempt of August 1991 by communist conservatives paradoxically destroyed Gorbachev's position. Boris Yeltsin's emergence as a rival power broker, commanding the Russian Republic, sidelined Gorbachev. By December 1991, the Soviet Union itself dissolved, and Gorbachev resigned on 25 December 1991. He had lost power not through a Kremlin coup but through the collapse of the state he led.\n\nYet assessing 'failure' requires nuance. Khrushchev's reversal was absolute but confined to his personal fate; the Soviet system continued under his successors. His de-Stalinization, though reversed under Brezhnev's 're-Stalinization,' permanently altered Soviet discourse and enabled cultural liberalization in literature and arts. He demonstrated that Stalin's terror was not immutable Soviet practice, a legacy that influenced future reform-minded figures including Gorbachev himself.\n\nGorbachev's loss of formal power was equally complete, yet his reforms—however unintended—transformed global history. He pursued 'New Thinking' in foreign policy, withdrawing Soviet forces from Afghanistan (completed 1989), allowing Eastern European states to leave the Soviet Bloc, and negotiating arms reduction with the West. The fall of communist regimes across Eastern Europe in 1989 and the Cold War's end resulted substantially from Gorbachev's decisions. Though he did not intend to dissolve the Soviet Union, his policies made its continuation impossible. From a communist perspective, this was catastrophic failure. From a Cold War perspective, his reformism ended ideological confrontation and, arguably, resolved the superpower conflict in a way that avoided nuclear apocalypse.\n\nKhrushchev's career ended in complete personal and political failure. His dismissal was ignominious, his reforms were reversed, and his reputation was officially tarnished for decades. Gorbachev's career ended in the loss of power and the dissolution of the state itself—a failure unprecedented in scope. However, Gorbachev's unintended historical contribution—ending Soviet authoritarianism and the Cold War—introduces ambiguity absent from Khrushchev's narrative.\n\nIn conclusion, both careers ended in failure by the criterion of retaining power or achieving their stated objectives. Khrushchev failed entirely: removed from office and rendered powerless. Gorbachev failed equally in formal terms: stripped of office and his state dissolved. However, the historical consequences differ profoundly. Khrushchev's legacy was quickly effaced; Gorbachev's unintended legacy transformed world history. Both failed as Soviet leaders; only Gorbachev succeeded, inadvertently, as an agent of systemic transformation.",
