        "question": "During the years 1945-1990, why did European countries retreat from empire?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "European retreat from empire between 1945 and 1990 resulted from a convergence of military exhaustion, economic weakness, ideological shifts, and sustained anti-colonial resistance that made empire unsustainable and costly.\n\nMILITARY EXHAUSTION AND POWER SHIFT\nWorld War II severely depleted European military capacity. Britain, France, and the Netherlands emerged victorious but economically crippled. The United States and Soviet Union, the true victors, became superpowers while European nations faced relative decline. This power vacuum made colonial control difficult to maintain. The Cold War ideological division further complicated empire: both superpowers opposed traditional colonialism—the US as a former anti-colonial power and the USSR as an ideologically revolutionary state—leaving European powers increasingly isolated in defending imperialism on the world stage.\n\nECONOMIC FACTORS\nEmpire became economically burdensome rather than profitable. Britain's Indian empire cost more to defend and administer than it generated in returns. Post-war reconstruction absorbed European resources; Britain required American loans (1945) and faced bankruptcy by 1947. France poured massive resources into Indochina (1946-1954) and Algeria (1954-1962), spending billions attempting to suppress nationalist movements. These military expenditures drained metropolitan economies when reconstruction was urgent. Colonial territories demanded investment in infrastructure and administration that Europe could ill afford. The cost-benefit analysis shifted decisively: retaining colonies became economically irrational.\n\nANTI-COLONIAL NATIONALISM AND RESISTANCE\nWar itself mobilised colonial populations. Colonial soldiers fought for Britain and France in WWII, then returned home demanding recognition and independence. Wartime rhetoric about democracy and self-determination proved hypocritical when applied selectively. National independence movements, strengthened by WWII, pursued decolonisation with renewed vigour. India's independence movement, led by Gandhi and Nehru, forced Britain to grant independence in 1947, creating a precedent. In Indochina, Ho Chi Minh's Viet Minh defeated France at Dien Bien Phu (1954), ending French colonial rule. Algeria's FLN insurgency (1954-1962) cost France over one million lives and destabilised metropolitan France itself. These prolonged, costly conflicts demonstrated that colonialism required unsustainable military commitment to suppress determined nationalist opposition.\n\nIDEOLOGICAL AND MORAL SHIFTS\nPost-WWII ideology fundamentally challenged imperialism. The United Nations Charter (1945) enshrined self-determination as a principle, creating an international legal framework opposing colonialism. European intellectuals, scarred by fascism's horrors, increasingly questioned whether imperialism—based on racial hierarchy and domination—differed morally from fascism. The Suez Crisis (1956) proved pivotal: when Britain and France invaded Egypt to reclaim the Suez Canal, international condemnation—particularly from the US and USSR—demonstrated that empire was internationally unacceptable. Even former allies rejected European imperial action. Domestically, metropolitan populations increasingly opposed colonial wars. French public opinion split over Algeria; British society questioned the Mau Mau campaign in Kenya. The moral consensus shifted toward decolonisation.\n\nPOLITICAL CALCULATION AND PRAGMATISM\nEuropean governments recognised that controlling unwilling territories required indefinite military occupation, which democratic electorates would not tolerate. Britain's decision to grant Indian independence (1947) reflected pragmatic calculation: maintaining control would require massive force against determined opposition. Belgium's rapid withdrawal from Congo (1960), though chaotic, avoided prolonged conflict. By the 1960s, even conservative governments accepted decolonisation as inevitable. France's Fifth Republic (1958) under de Gaulle negotiated Algerian independence (1962) after recognising the futility of military victory. Granting independence was politically less costly than fighting indefinite colonial wars.\n\nCOLD WAR CONTEXT\nDecolonisation occurred within Cold War competition for influence. The Soviet Union championed anti-colonial movements, supporting movements like Cuba's Castro, Vietnam's Ho Chi Minh, and Angola's MPLA. To counter Soviet influence, the US encouraged European decolonisation, believing independent nations would be more amenable to Western influence than colonial territories. European powers calculated that maintaining formal political control was less important than preserving economic influence and strategic alignment with the West. Neo-colonialism—informal economic and political domination without formal administrative control—became the preferred model.\n\nTIMING AND MOMENTUM\nDecolonisation accelerated through cascade effects. India's 1947 independence emboldened other movements. Indonesia's independence (1949) and Indo-China's (1954) demonstrated European vulnerability. The 1960 \"Year of Africa\" saw seventeen African nations achieve independence. Success bred further success; once the principle of self-determination was conceded, retaining any territory became indefensible. Portugal attempted to reverse this trend, fighting wars in Angola, Mozambique, and Guinea-Bissau (1961-1974), but the 1974 Portuguese revolution ended this resistance, enabling rapid African independence.\n\nEXCEPTIONAL CASES AND RESISTANCE\nSome European powers resisted longer. France's particularly violent decolonisation reflected its definition of empire as integral to its greatness; this produced the Indochina War (1946-1954) and Algerian War (1954-1962). Portugal's authoritarian regime fought until 1974. Southern Rhodesia's white minority delayed decolonisation until 1980. Yet even these exceptions ultimately collapsed under international pressure and internal exhaustion.\n\nCONCLUSION\nEuropean retreat from empire resulted not from a single cause but from converging factors: post-war military weakness made empire impossible to maintain indefinitely; economic costs rendered it irrational; nationalist resistance made it militarily expensive; international ideology and law rendered it illegitimate; and Cold War dynamics made formal empire strategically unnecessary. By 1975, nearly all direct European colonialism had ended. The process was neither inevitable nor predetermined, but once initiated, momentum became irreversible. Empire had become incompatible with post-1945 realities of power, economics, morality, and international law.",
