        "question": "How effectively did Charles de Gaulle tackle problems relating to French colonialism?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Charles de Gaulle tackled problems relating to French colonialism with mixed effectiveness, achieving decisive action on Algeria but pursuing a broader imperial strategy that maintained French influence in Africa.\n\nDe Gaulle's most significant achievement was resolving the Algerian crisis. By 1958, France faced a devastating colonial war draining resources and political stability, with over 400,000 troops deployed. The National Liberation Front (FLN), established in 1954, had sustained a costly insurgency. De Gaulle initially promised to keep Algeria French—\"I have understood you\" (1958)—but recognised that military victory was impossible and that continued conflict threatened metropolitan France. Following the referendum of 1962, de Gaulle granted Algerian independence after the Évian Accords (1962), a pragmatic decision that ended eight years of conflict and restored French political cohesion. This represented effective problem-solving: he acknowledged demographic and military realities and made a decisive break from colonial commitment, though at considerable political cost (the OAS terrorism campaign by French settlers demonstrated the depth of resistance).\n\nHowever, de Gaulle's approach to broader decolonisation was limited and selective. In sub-Saharan Africa, de Gaulle did not pursue full decolonisation but rather managed a transition to nominal independence within a French-dominated framework. The 1960 decolonisations of French African territories were largely peaceful, but this reflected de Gaulle's preference for maintaining French cultural and economic dominance rather than genuine independence. The African colonies became independent republics but remained bound to France through the Franc zone, defence agreements, and French administrative influence. De Gaulle viewed this as a solution to colonial problems—avoiding costly wars—but it substituted direct colonial rule with neo-colonial control. France retained significant military bases and continued economic exploitation, particularly of Moroccan and Tunisian resources. This was effective at preventing insurgency but did not genuinely resolve the underlying problem of colonial domination.\n\nDe Gaulle's handling of Morocco and Tunisia showed similar pragmatism combined with strategic self-interest. France granted Tunisia independence in 1956 and Morocco in 1956, moves de Gaulle supported as preventing protracted conflict. However, France retained military bases and privileged economic access. The Suez Crisis (1956) illustrated de Gaulle's broader approach: he opposed British and French military intervention to recover the canal, recognising that colonial reassertion was obsolete, yet simultaneously moved to strengthen France's cultural and economic ties to former colonies. This dual approach—accepting political decolonisation while entrenching economic and military dependence—was effective in preventing wars but represented an incomplete resolution of colonialism as a structural problem.\n\nDe Gaulle's vision of French grandeur also constrained his decolonisation efforts. He sought to maintain France as a leading world power, and French Africa (l'Afrique française) was central to this ambition. He introduced the concept of the French Community (1958), a looser framework than the Fourth Republic's colonial system, offering African territories a choice between autonomy within the community or independence. This appeared to offer genuine choice, but the economic and military pressures ensured most remained tied to France. De Gaulle was tackling colonial problems—preventing wars, modernising imperial relationships—but not dismantling the underlying structures of dependence. His Fourth Republic predecessors had fought in Indochina (1946–1954), losing at Điện Biên Phủ; de Gaulle avoided repeating this by accepting political independence, but he substituted military defeat with economic hegemony.\n\nThe effectiveness of de Gaulle's approach depended on perspective. From a French standpoint, he successfully avoided costly, prolonged colonial wars after 1962, maintained French influence in Africa, and preserved France's international status. From the standpoint of colonial populations seeking genuine independence and self-determination, de Gaulle's framework entrenched neo-colonial relationships that persisted decades beyond nominal decolonisation. The Algerian independence, while hard-won and costly, was genuine; African independence was formal but substantively limited.\n\nDe Gaulle tackled colonial problems with decisiveness on Algeria and pragmatism on Africa, but his solutions perpetuated French dominance in new forms. He recognised that formal colonialism was untenable in the post-war world and adapted French imperial strategy accordingly, but he did not dismantle the structures of French hegemony. His effectiveness must be measured against his actual objectives—maintaining French power and influence—which he substantially achieved, though at the cost of perpetuating neo-colonial relationships and unresolved questions of genuine decolonisation.",
