        "label": "4",
        "question": "How did Anglo-Irish relations develop during the period 1923-1949?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Anglo-Irish relations between 1923 and 1949 evolved from formal independence within the Commonwealth towards de facto sovereignty, characterized by gradual constitutional separation, economic conflict, and ultimately the establishment of the Irish Republic outside the Commonwealth entirely.\n\nIn 1923, the Irish Free State began as a dominion within the British Commonwealth, nominally subordinate to the Crown. However, from the outset, Irish politicians sought to maximize independence. The Balfour Declaration of 1926 proved significant, establishing that dominions were equals with Britain, not subordinates. This provided constitutional justification for Irish assertions of autonomy. Éamon de Valera, who returned to mainstream politics and became President of the Executive Council in 1932, accelerated the push for genuine independence. He used his interpretation of the Balfour Declaration to progressively strip away symbols and substance of British authority within the Irish Free State.\n\nDe Valera's constitutional amendments of the 1930s fundamentally altered Anglo-Irish relations. In 1933, he removed the oath of allegiance to the Crown that had been mandatory under the 1922 Constitution, a deeply symbolic act that inflamed British opinion. In 1936, following the Abdication Crisis, de Valera exploited British preoccupation with Edward VIII's situation to remove the Governor-General's office, virtually eliminating the Crown's executive presence in Ireland. The same year he removed all reference to the Crown from the Constitution. These changes were constitutional rather than revolutionary, but they systematically dismantled the formal structures of Commonwealth membership.\n\nThe 1937 Constitution represented a decisive break. Éamon de Valera introduced a new Constitution that declared Ireland a sovereign, independent state. While technically de Valera did not declare a republic at this point—maintaining that the President served as formal Head of State in a way that theoretically accommodated Commonwealth membership—in substance Ireland was now a separate state. The Constitution asserted Irish control over the island and was presented as a nationalist assertion. Britain protested but accepted the fait accompli, recognizing that forcible intervention was impossible and that the dominion concept had evolved beyond meaningful British control.\n\nEconomic relations deteriorated sharply in the 1930s. De Valera initiated the Economic War (1933–1938) by refusing to pay land annuities—payments owed to Britain as interest on land reform loans advanced during the Union. Britain responded by imposing punitive tariffs on Irish goods, particularly cattle. Ireland responded with counter-tariffs. This trade war severely damaged Irish agriculture and the economy generally. The conflict reflected Irish determination to achieve economic independence and represented a significant rupture in Anglo-Irish relations. The Anglo-Irish Trade Agreement of 1938 eventually resolved the immediate dispute, with Ireland agreeing to purchase the annuities in a lump sum and Britain removing tariffs, but the antagonism demonstrated deep tensions.\n\nWorld War II further strained relations. Ireland declared itself neutral, a position de Valera maintained throughout the conflict despite British pressure, particularly during the 1940–1941 period when invasion seemed possible. Britain wanted Irish ports and bases; de Valera refused, despite German attacks on Irish shipping. This neutrality, while popular domestically, widened the emotional and political gulf between Britain and Ireland. De Valera famously visited the German embassy to offer condolences on Hitler's death in 1945, a gesture that appalled British opinion and symbolized the depth of Irish-British alienation.\n\nThe final step came in 1949. De Valera had lost office in 1948 to a coalition government led by John A. Costello of Fine Gael. In April 1949, Costello's government formally declared Ireland a Republic, severing the last constitutional link with the Commonwealth. The Republic of Ireland Act ended the dominion status entirely. While ostensibly a Fine Gael initiative, this reflected the consensus of Irish political opinion, including de Valera's Fianna Fáil. Britain, under Clement Attlee's Labour government, accepted the change, though it provoked outcry from unionist opinion in Northern Ireland, whose partition from the Irish Free State in 1922 remained a fundamental point of contention.\n\nThe period 1923–1949 therefore witnessed a complete transformation. Anglo-Irish relations shifted from the formal hierarchy of Commonwealth membership, where Britain retained significant theoretical authority, to a relationship between two sovereign independent states on equal terms. This was achieved through consistent constitutional activism, particularly under de Valera, coupled with economic assertiveness and political will. The Economic War, the 1937 Constitution, wartime neutrality, and finally the 1949 declaration of the Republic all reflected Irish determination to complete the separation begun by independence in 1922. By 1949, Anglo-Irish relations had become international relations between two separate states with distinct interests, rather than relations within a Commonwealth structure. This transformation, though achieved constitutionally rather than violently, represented the culmination of the independence struggle and left unresolved the question of partition, which would continue to shape Anglo-Irish relations in subsequent decades.",
