        "question": "During the period 1900-1920, how was Russia affected by war and revolution?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Between 1900 and 1920, Russia experienced two wars and two revolutions that fundamentally transformed the empire from an autocratic state into a communist republic, reshaping European geopolitics.\n\nThe Russo-Japanese War (1904-1905) initiated this period of upheaval. Russia's defeat by Japan—an Asian power—shattered the myth of Russian invincibility and exposed military inefficiency and outdated tactics. The war's unpopularity and economic strain triggered the 1905 Revolution. Workers, soldiers, and peasants rose in protest; the St Petersburg Soviet coordinated strikes; the Battleship Potemkin mutinied. Tsar Nicholas II was forced to issue the October Manifesto (1905), establishing the Duma (parliament) and promising civil liberties. However, the Tsar retained autocratic powers, dissolving hostile Dumas and limiting reforms. The revolution was suppressed but revealed deep structural weaknesses in the tsarist system.\n\nDuring 1906-1914, Prime Minister Stolypin attempted reform. Agricultural policies aimed to create a class of prosperous peasant farmers to stabilize the countryside. Yet fundamental contradictions persisted: industrialization created a volatile urban working class; the nobility resisted meaningful change; the Duma remained powerless; and nationalist fervor among pan-Slavic movements threatened stability in the Balkans.\n\nWorld War I (1914-1917) proved catastrophic. Russia entered to support Serbia and preserve influence in the Balkans. The war exposed logistical chaos: inadequate supplies, poor transport, incompetent generals, and massive casualties—over 1.8 million killed by 1917. Soldiers deserted; the economy collapsed under military demands; inflation ravaged civilians; food shortages emerged, particularly in cities. The Tsar took personal command at the front in 1915, removing himself from Petrograd and associating the monarchy directly with military failure. His wife, Alexandra, and the mystic Rasputin wielded influence in his absence, further discrediting the regime. By 1917, the monarchy had lost all credibility.\n\nThe February Revolution (March 1917, by the modern calendar) erupted in Petrograd. Food riots and strikes escalated; soldiers refused to fire on protesters and joined them; the Duma established a Provisional Government. The Tsar abdicated. However, the Provisional Government continued the unpopular war and failed to distribute land to peasants or address urban hardship. Dual power emerged: the Provisional Government held formal authority while the Petrograd Soviet (dominated by workers and soldiers) held real power.\n\nThis instability enabled the Bolsheviks under Lenin. The Bolsheviks, having opposed the war from the start, promised \"peace, land, and bread.\" Lenin's April Theses explicitly rejected Provisional Government authority. The Bolsheviks organized armed insurrection. On October 25-26, 1917 (November 7-8, modern calendar), they stormed the Winter Palace in Petrograd and seized power. The October Revolution transformed Russia from a liberal-democratic aspiration into a communist state.\n\nThe Civil War (1918-1920) consolidated Bolshevik control through brutality and organization. The Red Army, led by Trotsky, defeated counter-revolutionary White forces. Lenin's government signed the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk (March 1918), ceding vast territories to Germany to secure peace, and implemented War Communism—requisitioning grain, nationalizing industry, and conscripting labor—to sustain the revolution. The Cheka (secret police) executed suspected enemies systematically. By 1920, the Bolsheviks held power, though the economy was devastated and millions had died from fighting, famine, and disease.\n\nBy 1920, Russia had undergone radical transformation. The Romanov dynasty was extinct; the autocratic system was replaced by communist dictatorship under Lenin; vast territories had been lost; the economy lay in ruins; and ideological commitment to worldwide revolution made Russia a pariah state, isolated by Western powers. Yet the Bolsheviks had survived and would consolidate Soviet power in the following decade. War and revolution had destroyed the old order entirely, creating a new state that would shape twentieth-century history profoundly.",
