        "question": "What did you learn about World War I from your study of the conduct of war and/or women in the workforce during World War I?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "World War I represented a fundamental transformation in modern warfare and society. Study of the conduct of war reveals how industrial capacity and technological innovation became decisive factors. The Western Front exemplified this: trench warfare from 1914–1918 created a static, attritional conflict fundamentally different from pre-war military expectations. Generals like Haig persisted with frontal assaults despite massive casualties—the Somme (1916) saw 60,000 British casualties on day one—reflecting the deadlock inherent in defending entrenched positions armed with machine guns and artillery. Poison gas, introduced by Germany in 1915, represented chemical innovation that shocked contemporaries, though gas masks eventually limited its effectiveness. Naval warfare shifted decisively toward submarine warfare: Germany's unrestricted U-boat campaign (1917) sank merchant shipping at unprecedented rates, threatening Britain's survival and ultimately drawing America into the war in April 1917. The Eastern Front proved more mobile but more devastating in scale, with casualties in the millions partly explaining Russian collapse and revolution by 1917. Air power emerged as a new dimension, evolving from reconnaissance to fighter combat and bombing. This conduct of war demonstrated that 1914–1918 was industrialised slaughter: victory required not military genius but superior resources, manpower reserves, and home front production.\n\nWomen's mobilisation in the workforce was equally transformative. Before 1914, women occupied limited industrial roles; the war demanded total mobilisation. In Britain, women replaced men in munitions factories, textile mills, transport, and agriculture. By 1918, approximately 2.2 million women worked in industrial production, many in dangerous munitions work. The Munitionettes became iconic—young women manufacturing shells and explosives under hazardous conditions, suffering from TNT poisoning that turned skin yellow. Women also served as nurses close to combat zones, establishing themselves as essential to military operations: organisations like the Voluntary Aid Detachment provided over 100,000 female nurses. In Germany, female industrial participation increased substantially, though economic collapse limited resources compared to Britain. In France and Belgium, occupied territories experienced disruption rather than mobilisation. The Soviet Union, after 1917, mobilised women both industrially and militarily.\n\nThis female participation challenged pre-war gender assumptions. Women demonstrated capability in skilled and dangerous work previously reserved for men. However, the experience was contradictory: women received lower wages than men for equivalent work, and trade unions sometimes resisted female employment as a threat to male wages post-war. Yet the visibility of women's contribution became politically significant. In Britain, the Representation of the People Act (1918) granted voting rights to women over 30 who met property qualifications—a direct acknowledgement of wartime service, though not full equality with men. The war suspended gender boundaries temporarily but did not permanently revolutionise women's economic position.\n\nBoth conduct of war and women's mobilisation reveal that World War I was fundamentally total war. Industrial capacity determined outcomes more than tactical brilliance. The Western Front's stalemate could only be broken by exhausting the enemy's resources—manpower, materials, and will. Germany's defeat resulted partly from economic collapse and domestic discontent, not from military defeat in the field. Women's mobilisation illustrates this totality: modern industrial war required every available resource, including female labour. The home front became inseparable from the military front. Civilians died from blockade and bombing; workers' output directly translated into artillery shells and poison gas. The war's conduct thus demanded unprecedented social mobilisation, including women's entry into the industrial workforce on a mass scale. The result was that 1914–1918 established the template for twentieth-century total war: industrial capacity, technological innovation, and complete social mobilisation, including women, determined victory. The conduct of war and women's participation were not separate phenomena but interconnected aspects of industrial-scale conflict that fundamentally altered modern society.",
