        "question": "What was the contribution to European affairs of one or more of the following: Pope Leo XIII; Rosa Luxemburg; Woodrow Wilson?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Pope Leo XIII's contribution to European affairs centred on reasserting papal influence in the post-unification period and addressing the social question through moral teaching. Having inherited a Church weakened by Italian unification and the loss of the Papal States, Leo XIII (1878–1903) pursued the policy of *ralliement*, encouraging French Catholics to accept the Third Republic rather than work for monarchist restoration. This pragmatic approach recognised political realities while preserving Church interests. More significantly, Leo XIII issued *Rerum Novarum* (1891), the landmark encyclical addressing industrialisation and workers' rights. It acknowledged legitimate worker grievances, condemned socialist materialism, and advocated for Catholic social action—a moral alternative to socialism. The encyclical influenced Catholic movements across Europe and established the Church's engagement with modern social questions, countering the image of reactionary ultramontanism.\n\nRosa Luxemburg's contribution was as a major socialist theorist and activist in the revolutionary left. A prominent figure in both Polish and German socialist movements, Luxemburg developed theories of mass strikes as revolutionary instruments and critiqued Lenin's model of centralised party control. Her writings on imperialism and capitalism's contradictions influenced Marxist analysis across Europe. As co-founder of the Spartacus League during World War I, she led opposition to the war and advocated international socialist revolution. During the German Revolution of 1918–1919, Luxemburg and Karl Liebknecht organised the Spartacist uprising against the Social Democratic government. Though the uprising was suppressed and Luxemburg murdered in January 1919, her ideas shaped radical left discourse and represented the revolutionary socialist alternative to both moderate social democracy and Leninist communism. Her legacy symbolised the intense ideological battles dividing European socialism.\n\nWoodrow Wilson's contribution was as architect of liberal internationalism and the peace settlement ending World War I. Wilson's Fourteen Points (January 1918) articulated a vision of post-war order based on national self-determination, open diplomacy, and collective security through a League of Nations. These principles directly challenged both European imperial systems and authoritarian power politics. At the Paris Peace Conference (1919), Wilson championed the League of Nations, which embodied his belief in international law and cooperation as alternatives to militarism and secret alliances. Though the League ultimately proved ineffective and the Treaty of Versailles contained punitive elements Wilson opposed, his vision fundamentally shaped interwar international relations.\n\nWilson's emphasis on national self-determination encouraged independence movements across Europe and the colonial world, destabilising existing empires. However, the gap between Wilsonian ideals and the harsh Versailles settlement—which imposed severe reparations on Germany, created ethnically mixed successor states in Eastern Europe, and left unresolved territorial disputes—fuelled European instability and resentment, particularly in Germany. The Senate's rejection of the League of Nations further weakened the international framework Wilson had envisioned.\n\nTogether, these figures shaped European affairs through distinct channels: Leo XIII through religious moral authority and reconciliation with modernity; Luxemburg through revolutionary socialist theory and practice challenging capitalist and imperialist systems; and Wilson through liberal internationalism attempting to reorganise global relations. Their contributions reveal the ideological contests defining early twentieth-century Europe—between Catholic social thought and secular ideologies, between reformist and revolutionary socialism, and between liberal internationalism and nationalist power politics. Wilson's vision ultimately proved more influential on formal structures, while Luxemburg's ideas inspired lasting radical movements and Leo XIII's social teaching influenced Catholic movements. However, all three confronted the fundamental tensions of an era where traditional empires, nationalist awakening, revolutionary ideologies, and new international frameworks competed for Europe's future.",
