      {
        "label": "1",
        "question": "What were the main challenges facing France, 1920-1945?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "France between 1920 and 1945 faced interconnected political, economic, and security challenges that ultimately undermined the Third Republic and left the nation vulnerable to Nazi occupation.\n\nPolitical instability was chronic. The Third Republic's proportional representation system fragmented parliament into numerous parties, making coalition governments short-lived and decision-making difficult. Between 1920 and 1945, France had 43 governments, averaging less than seven months each. The Radical Party, Socialists, and Communists competed for influence while right-wing factions grew increasingly vocal. The Dreyfus Affair's legacy persisted as ideological divisions ran deep. The Communist Party's growth after 1920 alarmed centrists and conservatives, who feared Soviet revolution spreading westward. By the 1930s, extremism flourished: right-wing leagues like the Action Française and Croix de Feu threatened republican institutions, while the left mobilised in response. The political centre could not hold.\n\nEconomic difficulties compounded instability. France emerged from World War I economically exhausted. Though it demanded German reparations under the Treaty of Versailles (1919), Germany's inability and refusal to pay forced France to occupy the Ruhr in 1923—an expensive military commitment that deepened French debt rather than solved it. The franc depreciated significantly through the 1920s, causing inflation and uncertainty. The Wall Street Crash of 1929 brought global depression to French shores later than other nations, but the impact was severe. Industrial production fell, unemployment rose, and agricultural prices collapsed, devastating rural France. Governments attempted deflationary policies (cutting spending) which worsened the depression rather than solving it. The Popular Front government (1936-1938) under Léon Blum introduced progressive reforms—the 40-hour week, paid holidays, collective bargaining—but these raised business costs during recession and prompted capital flight, weakening the franc further. Economic crisis fuelled political extremism.\n\nSecurity threats loomed increasingly large. France had fought Germany to exhaustion in 1914-1918 and feared German resurgence. The Treaty of Versailles imposed harsh terms on Germany—reparations, territorial losses, military restrictions—but many French doubted these guarantees would hold. Hitler's rise in 1933 vindicated these fears. Nazi rearmament violated the Versailles Treaty; the remilitarisation of the Rhineland in 1936 directly challenged the Locarno Treaties of 1925, which France had guaranteed. Yet France did not act alone: Britain, France's supposed ally, signed the Anglo-German Naval Agreement in 1935, appearing to accommodate German rearmament. France felt abandoned. Military strategists, having learned costly lessons from 1914-1918, invested heavily in the Maginot Line—a fortified defensive system along the German border. Yet this strategy was fundamentally defensive and psychologically demoralising, suggesting France accepted it could not defeat Germany offensively. The line's extension northward was incomplete, leaving the Belgian border vulnerable. When Germany invaded in May 1940, the Maginot Line proved irrelevant to mobile Blitzkrieg warfare.\n\nColonial and diplomatic complications weakened France further. France maintained a vast colonial empire but faced growing nationalist movements, notably in Indochina and North Africa. These commitments diverted resources and attention. Diplomatically, France faced isolation in the late 1930s. The Spanish Civil War (1936-1939) exposed French weakness: the Spanish Republican government, backed by French sympathisers, was crushed by Franco's fascist forces supported by Hitler and Mussolini, while France watched helplessly. The Munich Agreement (September 1938), where Britain and France allowed Germany to annex the Sudetenland from Czechoslovakia without Czech consent, humiliated France and shattered its credibility as a great power and ally to smaller nations. French acceptance of this settlement showed a traumatised nation prioritising appeasement over confrontation.\n\nMilitary vulnerability became undeniable. The French army was experienced but ageing, led by commanders like General Weygand who fought in World War I and thought in 1914-1918 terms. The German army, by contrast, was rebuilt, modernised, and ideologically motivated. Germany's air force (Luftwaffe) was superior; France's aircraft industry was fragmented and inefficient. When war came in September 1939, France and Britain declared war on Germany but conducted only a phoney war, taking no serious offensive action during the winter of 1939-1940. When German forces attacked in May 1940, bypassing the Maginot Line through Belgium, French defences crumbled within weeks. France surrendered on 22 June 1940. The speed of collapse shocked the world but reflected France's underlying weaknesses: demoralised society, divided politics, economic strain, and military disadvantage.\n\nThe armistice of 1940 created new challenges. Northern France and Paris fell under direct German military occupation. The southern zone fell under the Vichy regime—an authoritarian government led by Marshal Pétain which collaborated with Nazi Germany and dismantled republican institutions, introducing an authoritarian \"National Revolution\" emphasising traditionalism, hierarchy, and antisemitism. This division split France and raised questions of legitimacy and resistance that persisted through occupation until liberation in 1944-1945.\n\nIn conclusion, France's challenges between 1920 and 1945 were not discrete problems but interconnected crises: political divisions made coherent policy impossible; economic weakness reduced military investment and national confidence; security fears about Germany proved justified but prompted defensive rather than confident strategies; diplomatic isolation left France without strong allies; and military capacity, though initially formidable, proved inadequate to modern warfare. The Third Republic, already fragile in the 1920s, could not survive the convergence of these pressures in the 1930s. France's collapse in 1940 was not inevitable, but the accumulation of unresolved challenges made it increasingly probable.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
