        "label": "1",
        "question": "How well did President Harry Truman or President Lyndon Johnson handle the challenges he faced?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "President Truman faced unprecedented challenges in managing the transition from wartime to peacetime whilst navigating emerging Cold War tensions. His handling of these pressures was mixed, displaying both significant achievements and notable limitations.\n\nTruman's most substantial success lay in foreign policy during 1945-1948. He inherited a devastated Europe and Japan, and responded with the Marshall Plan (1948), a $12.7 billion aid programme that facilitated European economic recovery and prevented communist expansion into Western Europe. This demonstrated strategic vision and commitment to collective security. His decision to support NATO (1949) represented a decisive break with American isolationism and created a defensive alliance against Soviet expansion. In Germany, Truman's Berlin Airlift (1948-1949), supplying West Berlin during the Soviet blockade, showed determination to contain communism without military escalation—a pragmatic response that strengthened Western resolve.\n\nHowever, Truman's record in Asia revealed serious misjudgements. He initially withdrew American forces from Korea in 1948, then was caught unprepared when North Korea invaded the South in June 1950. His military response—committing American troops without congressional declaration—established a dangerous precedent, but the Korean War itself became a stalemate costing 33,600 American lives. His dismissal of General Douglas MacArthur in April 1951, while necessary to preserve civilian control, was poorly managed politically and damaged his popularity. Most problematically, Truman's policies contributed to the \"loss\" of China to communism in 1949—though this reflected Chinese civil war realities beyond American control, Truman faced intense domestic criticism, particularly from Republicans who weaponised the issue.\n\nDomestically, Truman faced inflationary pressures and labour unrest as the economy demobilised. He handled the 1946 coal miners' strike and railroad strike poorly, ultimately accepting settlements that fuelled inflation to 18.2 per cent by 1948. His Fair Deal domestic agenda, promising expansion of New Deal programmes, stalled in a Republican-controlled Congress after 1946, limiting his domestic achievements. Yet he secured the National Security Act (1947), creating the National Security Council and CIA, and modernised the military structure—this showed administrative competence despite legislative obstacles.\n\nTruman's handling of atomic weapons set concerning precedent. He authorised the bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki without fully exploring Japanese surrender possibilities, though this decision did end the Pacific War without a costly invasion. His subsequent decision to pursue nuclear weapons development, including the hydrogen bomb programme initiated in 1950, accelerated the arms race but reflected Cold War security concerns shared by the military and Congress.\n\nIn civil rights, Truman took tentative steps. His Executive Order 9981 (1948) desegregated the armed forces—a genuine achievement—but his handling of desegregation remained cautious and incomplete. He faced severe political constraints from Southern Democrats whose votes he needed.\n\nPresidents are ultimately judged against available alternatives and contemporary context. Truman inherited enormous challenges: a bipolar world emerging from total war, a Soviet Union expanding into Eastern Europe, economic dislocation, and domestic demands for demobilisation. Against these pressures, his strategic responses to Western Europe's recovery and the containment framework proved historically durable. Marshall Plan aid and NATO became cornerstones of post-war Western security. Yet his Asian policies—particularly Korea's ambiguous status post-1948 and the failure to anticipate Chinese intervention in late 1950—demonstrated limited regional understanding. His dismissal of MacArthur, while correct in principle, was handled poorly.\n\nIn conclusion, Truman handled the challenges he faced with considerable competence in Europe, establishing containment doctrine and alliances that endured throughout the Cold War. His atomic diplomacy and civil rights initiatives showed some progressive instincts despite constraints. However, his Korean War management proved costly and his Asian policy misreading significant. He was neither outstanding nor incompetent, but a capable Cold War manager whose strategic vision for Europe exceeded his grasp of Asian complexities. His presidency established the framework within which Cold War American foreign policy would operate for four decades.",
