        "label": "4",
        "question": "What was the significance of one or more of the following in US history: Hollywood, 1945-1968; the Montgomery bus boycott; religion in modern American culture?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "The Montgomery bus boycott (1955–1956) was profoundly significant in US history as a catalyst for the civil rights movement and a demonstration of effective non-violent resistance that fundamentally altered American racial politics.\n\nThe boycott emerged from decades of systematic racial segregation in Montgomery, Alabama, where Jim Crow laws mandated segregated seating on public buses. On 1 December 1955, Rosa Parks, a seamstress and NAACP member, refused to surrender her seat to a white passenger and was arrested. This act of civil disobedience ignited the Montgomery Improvement Association (MIA), led by Dr Martin Luther King Jr, a 26-year-old minister new to the city. The boycott lasted 381 days, with approximately 90% of Montgomery's African American bus riders—the system's primary users—withdrawing their patronage. This economic pressure proved decisive: the bus company lost revenue while white businesses dependent on black customers suffered losses. The boycott demonstrated that African Americans possessed collective power through non-violent action.\n\nKing's leadership proved transformative. His articulation of non-violent resistance, rooted in Christian theology and influenced by Mahatma Gandhi's philosophy, provided the ideological foundation for the movement. At mass meetings, King inspired sustained participation despite white intimidation, economic retaliation, and the bombing of his home. His eloquence and moral authority elevated the boycott beyond a local protest into a national symbol of black dignity and justice. The boycott generated international media attention, embarrassing the United States during the Cold War when Soviet propaganda highlighted American racism, making civil rights a foreign policy concern.\n\nThe Supreme Court's decision in Browder v. Gayle (November 1956) invalidated Montgomery's bus segregation ordinance, delivering a legal victory that extended beyond the city. This represented one of the first major judicial defeats for Jim Crow segregation and emboldened the civil rights movement. However, white resistance remained fierce: the White Citizens' Council organised economic boycotts of black businesses, and violence persisted. Yet the boycott proved that federal intervention could enforce constitutional rights, establishing a pattern repeated in subsequent struggles.\n\nThe boycott's significance lay in its broader impact on American racial consciousness and political mobilisation. It galvanised the black church as an institutional centre of resistance, transformed King into a national leader whose influence would shape the movement for the next thirteen years, and demonstrated that sustained mass action could challenge entrenched segregation. The MIA's sophisticated organisation—carpooling networks, church coordination, financial support systems—became a model for future campaigns. The boycott inspired similar protests: the Sit-in movement (1960), the Freedom Rides (1961), and the March on Washington (1963) followed its non-violent template and drew on the organisational experience gained in Montgomery.\n\nFurthermore, the boycott revealed fractures within white Southern society. Some white moderates questioned the sustainability of segregation; business leaders recognised economic costs; and religious leaders faced moral challenges to racial injustice. While massive white resistance ultimately prolonged segregation, the boycott demonstrated that the racial status quo was vulnerable to organised black protest, shifting the terrain of American politics toward civil rights reform.\n\nThe Montgomery bus boycott thus stands as historically significant because it marked the emergence of African American mass protest as a transformative force in US history, established non-violent resistance as the dominant strategy of the civil rights movement, elevated King to leadership, achieved a concrete legal victory, and fundamentally altered American racial politics by proving that segregation could be successfully challenged. It bridged the gap between early civil rights activities and the mass movement of the 1960s, making it a watershed moment in the long struggle for racial equality.",
