        "model": "Lambert Simnel and Perkin Warbeck were pretenders to the English throne during Henry VII's reign (1485–1509), both claiming to be heirs to the Yorkist dynasty. Simnel emerged first (1487), claiming to be Edward, Earl of Warwick, a potential rival to Henry VII. He gained support from Yorkist sympathizers and foreign powers, particularly Margaret of Burgundy, who saw an opportunity to destabilize Henry's rule. Simnel's claim culminated in the Battle of Stoke Field (1487), where Henry's forces decisively defeated the pretender's army. Despite this defeat, Henry treated Simnel relatively leniently, eventually employing him at court.\n\nPerkin Warbeck emerged later (1491), claiming to be Richard, Duke of York, the younger of the two 'Princes in the Tower.' Warbeck proved more threatening, gaining support from foreign powers including Scotland, France, and the Holy Roman Empire, as well as from some English Yorkist nobles. He launched several invasion attempts, including in Scotland (1496) and Cornwall (1497). Unlike Simnel, Warbeck was eventually captured and executed (1499), viewed as a more genuine threat to Henry's dynasty.\n\nBoth pretenders reflected the lingering Yorkist threat to Tudor legitimacy and Henry's need to consolidate power after the Wars of the Roses.",
