        "question": "The Composition of Connaught, 1585.",
        "marks": 20,
        "model": "In 1585, the Composition of Connaught was an English attempt to impose order and control over Connaught after decades of resistance and upheaval. Sir Richard Bingham, the Lord President of Connaught, implemented this scheme which aimed to establish English law and secure the province for the Crown. The composition fixed land boundaries and imposed a fixed tax (composition rent) payable to the English crown instead of the traditional clan-based tributes and irregular exactions. This standardised system replaced the extortionate levies previously extracted by English officials and Irish lords alike, promising greater predictability for local landholders. The scheme sought to integrate Irish lords into the English feudal system by having them hold lands under English title in return for the fixed rent. Importantly, it attempted to undermine the traditional Gaelic social structure by weakening clan loyalties and introducing English legal concepts of land ownership. The composition was designed as a revenue-raising measure for the Crown while presenting itself as a reform that would benefit the native population by eliminating arbitrary exactions. However, it represented a significant assertion of English sovereignty over the province and marked a turning point in the conquest and colonisation of Connaught, laying groundwork for later plantation schemes.",
