        "marks": 20,
        "model": "The Treaty of Mellifont (1603) ended the Nine Years War and marked the submission of Irish Gaelic lords to English Crown authority. Hugh O'Neill and Red Hugh O'Donnell led the Ulster rebellion (1594–1603) against English colonial expansion. The war caused severe devastation in Ulster, including crop failure and famine that weakened Irish resistance. O'Neill's defeat at Kinsale (1601), where Spanish reinforcements failed to link with his forces, proved decisive. At Mellifont, O'Neill accepted Crown sovereignty and agreed to disband his forces in exchange for pardons and land retention. The treaty effectively ended independent Gaelic power in Ulster and across Ireland. It established English administrative control over previously autonomous Irish territories and ended the last major armed resistance to Tudor rule. However, the generous terms—O'Neill retained substantial lands—reflected English recognition of his military strength and the Crown's preference for negotiated settlement over continued warfare. Mellifont consolidated English dominion but did not immediately impose plantation or religious change, representing a transitional moment between conquest and colonization in early seventeenth-century Ireland.",
