        "question": "The Conquistadores.",
        "marks": 20,
        "model": "The Conquistadores were Spanish military leaders who conquered vast territories in the Americas for the Spanish Crown during the sixteenth century. Hernán Cortés conquered the Aztec Empire in Mexico (1519-1521), exploiting internal divisions and indigenous alliances against Montezuma II. His force of around 600 men, combined with superior weaponry and tactical cunning, overwhelmed the Aztec state. Francisco Pizarro conquered the Inca Empire in Peru (1531-1539), similarly using divisions among indigenous groups and capturing the Inca ruler Atahualpa. These conquests established Spanish colonial dominion over vast territories and millions of indigenous people. The Conquistadores sought wealth, particularly gold and silver, and aimed to spread Christianity. Their campaigns resulted in catastrophic demographic collapse among indigenous populations through warfare, enslavement, and European diseases. The conquest laid the foundation for Spanish colonial rule in Latin America, creating a vast empire that generated enormous wealth through the exploitation of indigenous labour and resources, particularly the silver mines of Potosí. The legacy of the Conquistadores fundamentally shaped colonial Latin America and contributed to Spain's position as a major European power in the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries.",
