        "marks": 20,
        "model": "Ignatius Loyola (1491-1556) was a Spanish priest and founder of the Society of Jesus (Jesuits), instrumental in the Catholic Counter-Reformation. Born in Guipúzcoa, he initially pursued a military career but after a leg injury during the Siege of Pamplona (1521), he underwent a spiritual conversion. He studied theology and was ordained in 1537.\n\nIn 1540, Pope Paul III approved Loyola's Society of Jesus, which became the Church's most effective weapon against Protestantism. The Jesuits were uniquely disciplined and mobile, with absolute obedience to the Pope and their General Superior. They emphasized education, establishing schools and colleges throughout Europe that became centres of Catholic learning and indoctrination. Loyola's Spiritual Exercises provided systematic methods of prayer and meditation that strengthened Catholic faith and commitment.\n\nThe Jesuits operated as missionaries, educators, and confessors to princes, gaining considerable political influence. They established missions globally, extending Catholic authority to the New World and Asia. Their intellectual rigour and adaptability made them formidable opponents of Protestant expansion. By Loyola's death in 1556, the Jesuits numbered over 1,000 members and represented the organized, militant face of Catholic renewal during the Counter-Reformation period.",
