        "question": "The rise of Moscow in the 16th century.",
        "marks": 20,
        "model": "Moscow's rise as a major power in the 16th century resulted from the decline of the Mongol Golden Horde and the consolidation of Russian principalities under Muscovite leadership. Ivan III (1462–1505) was instrumental in this process, throwing off Mongol suzerainty by refusing to pay tribute after 1480 and expanding Muscovite territory through conquest and marriage alliances. He annexed key cities including Novgorod (1478) and Tver (1485), centralizing authority and establishing Moscow as the dominant Russian state. Ivan IV (1533–1584), known as Ivan the Terrible, continued this expansion, conquering the Kazan Khanate (1552) and Astrakhan (1556), which extended Muscovite control over the Volga River and eliminated rival Tatar powers. Ivan IV adopted the title Tsar, claiming succession to Byzantine imperial tradition and legitimizing Moscow's authority domestically and internationally. The consolidation of autocratic rule, development of a standing army, and centralized administration transformed Moscow from a regional principality into a major European power by century's end. These achievements established Moscow as the nucleus of the Russian Empire, fundamentally reshaping Eastern European politics.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
