        "question": "The \"Shannon Scheme\" at Ardnacrusha.",
        "marks": 20,
        "model": "The Shannon Scheme at Ardnacrusha was a major hydroelectric project completed in 1929 that generated electricity from the River Shannon. It was designed to industrialise Ireland and reduce dependence on British coal imports.\n\nThe scheme involved damming the Shannon at Ardnacrusha to create a power station capable of producing 28 megawatts. Engineers led by Siemens constructed the facility using advanced German technology. The project provided Ireland with its first major source of electrical power, enabling electrification of homes and factories across the country.\n\nThe electricity generated supplied the Electricity Supply Board (ESB), established in 1927 to manage and distribute power nationally. This represented state investment in modern infrastructure and industrial development. The scheme symbolised the Free State's ambition to modernise the economy and reduce reliance on imported fuel.\n\nHowever, the project faced practical limitations. The River Shannon's seasonal flow variations meant unreliable power generation during dry periods. By the 1950s, additional power stations were needed. Despite these constraints, Ardnacrusha demonstrated the government's commitment to infrastructure investment and remained significant to Ireland's electricity supply throughout the period 1949–1989.",
