        "question": "Séan Lemass as Taoiseach.",
        "marks": 20,
        "model": "Séan Lemass served as Taoiseach from 1959 to 1966, representing a pivotal shift in Irish economic policy. He abandoned protectionism and embraced free trade, recognizing that Ireland's isolationist approach had stifled industrial growth. His First Programme for Economic Expansion (1958) promoted foreign industrial investment and export-led development, dramatically transforming the economy from agricultural dependence.\n\nLemass established the Industrial Development Authority to attract multinational corporations, which created manufacturing employment and modernized the industrial base. He negotiated Ireland's entry into the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade (GATT) and positioned the country toward European Economic Community membership, secured under his successor.\n\nDomestically, he introduced free secondary education in 1966, expanding educational opportunities and enabling social mobility. His government increased social spending and housing provision, improving living standards for working-class families.\n\nHowever, rapid industrialization created regional imbalances, with development concentrated in Dublin and the east. Agricultural employment declined sharply, causing rural emigration. Lemass's pragmatic modernization strategy succeeded in halting emigration temporarily and generating economic growth, but uneven development and subsequent economic difficulties revealed the limitations of his approach. Nevertheless, his tenure fundamentally reoriented the Irish state toward economic engagement and social progress.",
