        "label": "C1(i)",
        "question": "The land question - Michael Davitt and the Land League.",
        "marks": 60,
        "model": "Michael Davitt and the Land League fundamentally transformed the Irish land question between 1870 and 1914, making agrarian reform a central political force.\n\nDavitt's background shaped his vision. Imprisoned in 1870 for Fenian activities, he witnessed agrarian suffering firsthand. Released in 1877, he identified land ownership as the root of rural poverty and evictions. Unlike earlier Fenians, Davitt believed constitutional agitation could achieve land reform. He rejected simple violence, advocating instead for organised peasant resistance through legal means.\n\nDavitt founded the Land League in October 1879 with Charles Stewart Parnell as president. The League's strategy was revolutionary: it mobilised tenant farmers to resist unjust rents and evictions through collective action. The organisation spread rapidly, claiming over 200,000 members by 1880. Its methods—boycotts, public shaming of landlords, and rent strikes—proved devastatingly effective without resorting to terrorism. The boycott of Captain Boycott himself in County Mayo became synonymous with peaceful protest.\n\nThe Land League operated during severe agricultural depression. Potato crops failed; grain prices collapsed; tenants could not pay rents. The League articulated grievances previously unorganised: insecurity of tenure, excessive rents, and arbitrary evictions. It demanded the \"three Fs\"—fair rent, fixity of tenure, and freedom to sell—long sought by tenant-right advocates.\n\nBritish response proved harsh. Prime Minister Gladstone suppressed the League under the Protection of Person and Property Act (1881), imprisoning Davitt and other leaders. Yet the timing paradoxically strengthened Davitt's hand: Gladstone simultaneously introduced the First Land Act (1881), granting the three Fs. Davitt thus achieved through agitation what decades of constitutional politics had failed to secure. His imprisonment actually validated his methods.\n\nDavitt's relationship with Parnell was complex. Both dominated the League, but tensions emerged. Davitt, influenced by American radical thinking, increasingly advocated land nationalisation—complete state ownership benefiting peasant proprietors. Parnell favoured peasant proprietorship through purchase. This ideological divide reflected Davitt's socialism versus Parnell's pragmatism. When Parnell signed the \"Kilmainham Treaty\" (May 1882) with Gladstone, accepting the Land Act in exchange for League moderation, Davitt felt betrayed. The League was disbanded shortly after.\n\nDavitt's subsequent influence, though diminished, remained significant. He toured America raising funds for Irish causes. He maintained his conviction that Irish independence required land reform. When the Land League re-emerged as the National League (1882), Davitt participated but never dominated as he had initially.\n\nThe Land League's achievement was tangible. It mobilised rural Ireland into a political force that neither Westminster nor Dublin Castle could ignore. Successive Land Acts (1881, 1896, 1909) progressively shifted ownership from landlords to tenant-farmers through government-aided purchase schemes—direct results of League agitation. By 1914, peasant proprietorship was becoming the norm, reversing centuries of landlord dominance.\n\nDavitt's legacy transcended land reform. He demonstrated that organised, disciplined, non-violent protest could compel imperial concessions. The Land League proved that Irish rural grievances, articulated through peasant solidarity rather than elite constitutional channels, possessed political force. Davitt showed that agrarian discontent could reshape British policy. Though his radicalism ultimately proved too far ahead of Irish political consensus, his methods influenced later nationalist movements. The land question, which had festered unresolved since the Famine, found partial resolution through the agitation Davitt pioneered, making him central to Irish reform between 1870 and 1914.",
