        "label": "E1",
        "question": "Bismarck and the Catholic Church.",
        "marks": 20,
        "model": "Bismarck's conflict with the Catholic Church, known as the Kulturkampf ('cultural struggle'), lasted from 1872 to 1887 and reflected his determination to subordinate all institutions to the Prussian state. Bismarck viewed the Catholic Church as a potential rival to state authority, particularly after papal claims to temporal power in the Vatican Decrees (1870), and feared that German Catholics' loyalty to the Pope might override their allegiance to the Reich.\n\nBismarck introduced the May Laws (1873), which placed Catholic education, clergy appointments, and discipline under state control. Bishops and priests who resisted faced imprisonment or exile. Monasteries were dissolved, and Catholic schools were brought under state supervision. These measures aimed to weaken institutional Catholic power and enforce state supremacy.\n\nHowever, the Kulturkampf ultimately failed. Catholic resistance strengthened rather than weakened, and the Catholic Centre Party emerged as a significant political force in the Reichstag. By the 1880s, Bismarck recognised the futility of confrontation and began reconciliation. The conflict demonstrated that even Bismarck's state apparatus could not crush deeply rooted religious conviction, and it weakened his political coalition by alienating millions of Catholic Germans. The struggle illustrated broader tensions between nationalism, state power, and traditional religious authority in nineteenth-century Europe.",
