        "question": "The Dreyfus Affair in France.",
        "marks": 20,
        "model": "The Dreyfus Affair (1894–1906) was a political and social crisis in France centring on the wrongful conviction of Captain Alfred Dreyfus, a Jewish army officer, for allegedly selling military secrets to Germany. In 1894, Dreyfus was court-martialled and imprisoned on Devil's Island based on forged evidence. The affair became a national scandal when, in 1897, Dreyfus's brother uncovered that a Major Esterhazy was the actual spy, yet the military establishment suppressed this evidence to protect itself.\n\nThe case divided French society deeply. Dreyfusards—including intellectuals, republicans, and socialists—demanded justice and reform, arguing it exposed military corruption and antisemitism. Anti-Dreyfusards—comprising conservatives, Catholic Church leaders, and army supporters—resisted revision, claiming criticism threatened national security and military honour.\n\nThe affair had major consequences. In 1899, Dreyfus received a presidential pardon; in 1906, full exoneration and reinstatement followed. The crisis weakened the military's political influence, strengthened republican and secular forces, and led to separation of Church and State in 1905. It revealed deep divisions within French society between progressive and reactionary elements, and demonstrated how judicial injustice and institutional prejudice could provoke prolonged national conflict.",
