        "label": "E3",
        "question": "Nazi propaganda in Germany.",
        "marks": 20,
        "model": "Nazi propaganda was central to consolidating Hitler's power and maintaining control over the German people between 1933 and 1945. Josef Goebbels, Minister of Propaganda, orchestrated a comprehensive propaganda machine that pervaded every aspect of German life.\n\nThe regime controlled all mass media. Radio broadcasts reached millions of Germans; the cheap Volksempfänger radio set ensured widespread access to Nazi messaging. Newspapers were strictly censored, with editors following daily directives. Film was weaponized—documentaries like Leni Riefenstahl's Triumph of the Will glorified Hitler and the Nazi movement, while entertainment films subtly reinforced Nazi ideology.\n\nEducation was thoroughly Nazified. Textbooks presented racial ideology and Hitler as Germany's saviour. Youth organizations like the Hitler Youth indoctrinated children with Nazi values from age ten onwards.\n\nPublic spectacle reinforced propaganda. Massive rallies at Nuremberg, torchlit processions, and public ceremonies created emotional connections to the regime. Posters and pamphlets displayed antisemitic and racial messages in streets and schools.\n\nPropaganda targeted specific anxieties: it blamed Jews and communists for Germany's problems, promised national revival, and celebrated Aryan superiority. By controlling information and emotion, the Nazis created a climate where dissent became increasingly difficult. This systematic propaganda was essential to both gaining support for Nazi policies, including war and genocide, and suppressing opposition.",
