        "question": "General Francisco Franco.",
        "marks": 20,
        "model": "General Francisco Franco led the Spanish Civil War (1936–1939) and established a fascist dictatorship lasting until 1975. Franco emerged as a military officer and nationalist who opposed the left-wing Spanish Republic. In 1936, he led a military rebellion supported by Nazi Germany and Fascist Italy, who supplied aircraft, troops, and equipment. The Nationalists fought the Republican government, international brigades, and anarchist militias. Franco's forces employed brutal tactics, including aerial bombing of civilians at Guernica (1937). The Nationalist victory gave Franco absolute power, which he used to suppress regional identities, particularly Catalan and Basque nationalism. He established a one-party state modeled on fascism, controlled by the military and the Falange party. Franco banned regional languages, dissolved political parties, and used secret police (Gestapo-style repression) to eliminate opposition. Unlike Hitler or Mussolini, Franco maintained official neutrality during World War II, though he sympathized with the Axis. His regime remained isolated internationally and economically backward until the 1950s. Franco consolidated authoritarian rule through church support, military dominance, and nationalist ideology centered on Spanish unity and Catholic tradition. His dictatorship killed hundreds of thousands and created a society of fear lasting four decades.",
