        "label": "2",
        "question": "How did the Irish government contribute to the consolidation of democracy, 1922-1932?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "The Irish government consolidated democracy between 1922 and 1932 through institutional development, peaceful power transfer, and the establishment of rule of law, despite significant challenges posed by civil war legacy and partition.\n\nInstitutional consolidation formed the foundation. The 1922 Constitution established a bicameral legislature (Dáil and Seanad) and separated executive from legislative power through a ceremonial President and responsible Executive Council. This framework embedded democratic principles in law. The Oireachtas became the supreme legislature, with the Executive Council accountable to the Dáil, creating checks on executive power. The courts gained independence, vital for upholding constitutional rights. These structures provided the legal and institutional scaffolding for democratic practice, distinguishing the new state from autocracy.\n\nThe peaceful transfer of power between governments demonstrated democratic maturity. In 1932, Éamon de Valera and Fianna Fáil won election and assumed office without military intervention or constitutional crisis. This peaceful transition from W.T. Cosgrave's Cumann na nGaedheal government (1922-1932) to Fianna Fáil showed that the state's institutions functioned according to democratic rules: elections determined government, losers accepted defeat. De Valera, who had opposed the Treaty and led anti-Treaty forces during the Civil War (1922-1923), accepted electoral verdicts rather than resort to force. This normalisation was crucial—it proved democracy was not merely a constitutional façade but an operative system.\n\nThe government suppressed anti-democratic threats. The Civil War (1922-1923) posed existential danger to the new state. The pro-Treaty government, led by Michael Collins and Arthur Griffith, defeated the anti-Treaty IRA through military means. Though violent, this consolidation of state authority was necessary to prevent democratic collapse: a functioning state requires monopoly on legitimate force. After 1923, the government progressively disarmed irregular organisations and asserted state control, enabling democratic institutions to operate without militia challenge. The government also criminalised IRA activity (Army Mutiny crisis 1924 notwithstanding), establishing law and order as prerequisites for democracy.\n\nExpansion of civil and political rights strengthened democratic foundations. Universal male suffrage existed from 1922, and women aged 30 with property qualifications could vote (lowered to 21 with full suffrage in 1933). Local government expanded opportunities for political participation beyond the national level. The Seanad, though unelected initially, provided a second chamber for deliberation. These mechanisms widened the franchise and created multiple entry points for citizen participation, characteristic of consolidated democracies.\n\nThe government respected electoral outcomes and constitutional constraints. Cumann na nGaedheal, dominant 1922-1932, lost office without attempting to rig elections or abolish democratic procedures. No government suspended the Dáil, banned opposition parties, or cancelled elections. Cosgrave's administration faced hostile public opinion (particularly over Civil War casualties and economic hardship) yet remained bound by constitutional procedures. This respect for democratic rules, even when politically disadvantageous, proved the system's legitimacy was accepted by elites.\n\nHowever, consolidation remained incomplete. Fianna Fáil's early actions—the oath crisis (1933) and removal of the Governor-General's powers—demonstrated ongoing tensions between constitutional orthodoxy and republican ideology. The Seanad's abolition (1936) and the 1937 Constitution, while still democratic, reshaped institutions substantially. These changes, though constitutionally enacted, reflected unresolved questions about sovereignty and state identity inherited from partition and the Civil War.\n\nThe government's economic and social policies, though conservative, supported stable governance. Cumann na nGaedheal pursued fiscal orthodoxy and protectionism, creating predictable conditions for business and avoiding populist extremism that destabilised other European democracies in the 1920s-1930s. Economic stability, while limiting, did not provoke revolutionary or fascist movements that threatened democracies elsewhere.\n\nIn conclusion, the Irish government consolidated democracy between 1922-1932 through creating functioning institutions, establishing rule of law, defeating armed challenges, respecting electoral verdicts, and maintaining constitutional governance. The peaceful 1932 transition exemplified successful consolidation. Yet partition's legacy and Civil War trauma ensured full democratic normalisation remained aspirational; unionist and anti-Treaty grievances persisted. Nevertheless, by 1932, democratic procedures were institutionalised, contested peacefully, and accepted by political elites as legitimate mechanisms for power allocation—the defining characteristic of consolidated democracy.",
