    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "1",
        "question": "During the period 1840-1914, why did so many Irish people emigrate to the US and to what extent did they become assimilated?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Irish emigration to the United States between 1840 and 1914 resulted from intersecting push and pull factors that fundamentally shaped the Irish diaspora. Approximately 4.5 million Irish emigrated during this period, predominantly to America, driven by conditions at home and opportunities abroad. Understanding this exodus requires examination of why Irish left and how successfully they integrated into American society.\n\nThe Great Famine (1845-1852) catalysed mass emigration. Successive potato crop failures devastated the Irish rural economy, which depended overwhelmingly on subsistence potato farming. Approximately one million died from starvation and disease; a further million emigrated as survival strategy. Those who could afford passage—often assisted by landlords keen to reduce tenancy numbers—fled to America, particularly to Boston, New York, and Philadelphia where established Irish communities provided networks of support and employment. Post-Famine emigration continued at elevated rates because structural economic problems persisted: land consolidation concentrated holdings among wealthy farmers, displacing smaller tenants; agricultural mechanisation reduced rural employment; industrial development remained limited outside Dublin, creating few urban alternatives.\n\nReligious and political grievances reinforced economic motivation. Catholics faced systematic discrimination: the Penal Laws, though relaxed, left Catholics excluded from professions, underrepresented in land ownership, and subordinate in a Protestant-dominated state apparatus. For many, emigration represented escape from sectarian marginalisation as much as poverty. Political repression following nationalist movements—particularly the 1798 Rebellion and Young Ireland movement of 1848—prompted politically active Catholics to seek refuge in democratic America.\n\nAmerica exerted powerful pull factors. Industrial expansion in northeastern cities created labour demand in construction, dock work, mining, and factory employment. Unlike Ireland, America offered wage labour opportunities where Irish could accumulate capital. Chain migration operated systematically: established immigrants sponsored relatives and townspeople, creating self-perpetuating migration streams from specific Irish parishes to specific American neighbourhoods. Letters home describing economic opportunity and religious freedom circulated widely, shaping expectations of American life.\n\nAssimilation proved partial and contested. Irish immigrants faced severe discrimination initially. They competed for low-wage work, provoking nativist hostility and stereotyping. \"No Irish Need Apply\" signs symbolised employment discrimination; Irish were caricatured as drunken, violent, and intellectually inferior in American media and popular culture. They inhabited segregated, impoverished urban neighbourhoods—Five Points in New York, South Boston—reflecting both economic constraint and ethnic preference. These ghettos functioned as transitional communities rather than permanent settlements.\n\nHowever, Irish achieved substantial integration by 1914. Occupational mobility increased across generations. First-generation Irish concentrated in unskilled labour; by the 1900s, increasing numbers entered skilled trades, commercial occupations, and professional classes. Education became the primary assimilative mechanism: Irish parents prioritised children's schooling, enabling upward mobility. The Catholic Church facilitated integration, providing institutional framework and social legitimacy. Irish priests gained prominence in American Catholicism, anchoring ethnic identity within American religious structures. The church operated schools, hospitals, and charitable organisations that advanced Irish-American welfare while mediating between ethnic identity and American citizenship.\n\nPolitical integration accelerated rapidly. Irish achieved voting rights after brief residency periods and mobilised politically as unified bloc. By the 1880s-1900s, Irish-Americans dominated Democratic Party machines in major cities—Tammany Hall epitomised this power. Irish became mayors, police chiefs, and congressmen, translating demographic weight into political influence. This political integration signalled acceptance into American civic structures despite persistent social discrimination.\n\nIntermarriage rates increased substantially among second and third-generation Irish, indicating cultural integration. Language assimilation was rapid: Irish children adopted English-American dialect and abandoned Irish Gaelic. Participation in American popular culture—baseball, vaudeville theatre—demonstrated cultural adoption. By 1914, Irish-Americans identified primarily as Americans, though maintaining selective ethnic identity.\n\nHowever, assimilation remained incomplete and selective. Irish retained distinct religious identity in predominantly Protestant America. Anti-Catholic prejudice persisted; Irish Catholicism became marker of ethnic distinctiveness rather than dissolving into American mainstream. Irish-Americans maintained separate social and fraternal organisations, though these increasingly catered to American-born generations. Continued immigration until 1914 refreshed ethnic consciousness and slowed complete assimilation.\n\nBy 1914, Irish-Americans had achieved remarkable economic and political integration relative to their precarious 1840-1850 position. Yet they retained recognisable ethnic identity through Catholicism, cultural traditions, and community institutions. Assimilation was therefore substantial rather than total: Irish became economically productive, politically integrated American citizens whilst maintaining collective identity and internal communal structures. This pattern—economic integration coupled with selective cultural retention—defined Irish-American experience and established template for subsequent ethnic groups navigating American integration.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
