        "question": "What were the main international tensions in Europe, 1871-1914?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "The main international tensions in Europe between 1871 and 1914 stemmed from a combination of imperial rivalry, nationalist aspirations, alliance systems, and the militarization of European powers.\n\nThe Franco-German rift was fundamental. The Treaty of Frankfurt (1871) imposed harsh reparations and ceded Alsace-Lorraine to Germany, creating lasting French resentment. This made France and Germany natural enemies and shaped European diplomacy for four decades. Bismarck, recognizing French revanchism, constructed the Three Emperors' League (1873) and later the Triple Alliance (1882) with Austria-Hungary and Italy to isolate France diplomatically and militarily.\n\nBalkan tensions emerged as Ottoman power declined. The Russo-Turkish War (1877-78) and the Treaty of Berlin (1878) revealed competing interests: Russian expansion southward toward Constantinople, Austrian expansion into the Balkans, and Serbian nationalism threatening Austro-Hungarian interests. The First Balkan War (1912) and Second Balkan War (1913) destabilized the region further, with multiple powers supporting rival Balkan states. Austria-Hungary's annexation of Bosnia-Herzegovina (1908) provoked Russian and Serbian outrage, nearly triggering conflict and demonstrating the peninsula's volatility.\n\nImperial competition for colonies intensified tensions. The Scramble for Africa pitted European powers against each other for territorial acquisition and resources. The Moroccan Crises (1905, 1911) brought France and Germany into direct confrontation over colonial spheres, with Britain supporting France. These incidents hardened Anglo-German antagonism and demonstrated how colonial disputes could escalate into European conflicts.\n\nAlliance systems rigidified after Bismarck's dismissal (1890). The Franco-Russian Alliance (1894) broke his isolation strategy against France. The Anglo-French Entente (1904) and Anglo-Russian Entente (1907) created the Triple Entente, opposing the Triple Alliance. Rather than maintaining flexibility and peace, these rigid blocs transformed regional disputes into potential continental wars. Powers felt locked into supporting allies regardless of the merits of individual conflicts.\n\nNaval rivalry, particularly Anglo-German antagonism, reflected industrial competition and strategic anxiety. Germany's naval expansion under Admiral Tirpitz challenged British maritime supremacy. The Dreadnought battleship (1906) sparked an arms race. Britain, dependent on naval power for imperial security and trade, viewed German naval growth as existential threat. This competition poisoned political relations and made war seem increasingly inevitable to military planners on both sides.\n\nNationalism destabilized multinational empires. Pan-Slavism inspired Serbian and Russian nationalism, threatening Austro-Hungarian control of diverse Slavic populations. German nationalism fueled imperial ambitions and Alsace-Lorraine revanchism. Italian nationalism drove competition with Austria-Hungary in the Mediterranean and Balkans. These nationalist movements, often encouraged by rival powers, made territorial boundaries contentious and compromise difficult.\n\nMilitarism reinforced tensions through strategic planning and arms races. The Schlieffen Plan made German war plans dependent on rapid victory against France and Russia simultaneously, creating pressure for preventive war if diplomatic windows closed. Military expenditure spiraled: between 1870 and 1914, European military spending tripled. Arms manufacturers lobbied for conflict, and military leaders gained disproportionate influence in government decision-making, particularly in Germany and Russia. Military planning became self-perpetuating: each power's defensive preparations appeared threatening to neighbours, prompting reciprocal military expansion.\n\nEconomic competition and tariff barriers created friction. German industrial growth challenged British economic dominance. Economic nationalism and protectionism (Bülow Tariff 1902) generated commercial tensions alongside political rivalries. Control of resources in the Balkans and colonial territories became linked to industrial survival in national calculations.\n\nThese tensions were not independent but interconnected. The Franco-German enmity made Balkan issues explosive: if Russia supported Serbia against Austria-Hungary, France felt obliged to support Russia against Germany. Imperial rivalries fed arms races and alliance rigidity. Nationalist movements exploited great power rivalries while great powers exploited nationalist movements. By 1914, no issue could be isolated; any conflict risked triggering alliance obligations and continental war. The assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand in June 1914 ignited these accumulated tensions into the First World War.",
