    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "2",
        "question": "What were the social and economic effects of the growth in European unity, 1945-1992?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "The growth of European unity from 1945 to 1992 produced profound social and economic effects across Western Europe, reshaping patterns of production, trade, employment, and living standards while creating new transnational communities and tensions.\n\nECONOMIC INTEGRATION AND TRADE LIBERALISATION\n\nThe European Coal and Steel Community (1951), established by Belgium, France, West Germany, Italy, Luxembourg and the Netherlands, removed tariffs on coal and steel, creating the first supranational economic organisation. This facilitated massive trade growth; intra-European trade increased from approximately 35% of total European trade in 1950 to over 60% by 1992. The removal of barriers enabled French coal to reach German industry, German steel to supply French manufacturing, creating economic interdependence that made future conflict politically costly. The Treaty of Rome (1957) established the European Economic Community with a customs union eliminating all internal tariffs by 1968. This accelerated trade dramatically: intra-EEC trade grew at 8% annually in the 1960s, far exceeding growth rates with non-member states. The Common Agricultural Policy (CAP), implemented from 1962, guaranteed European farmers prices well above world market rates through subsidies and import levies. While protecting rural communities from American competition, it created agricultural surpluses, inflated food prices for consumers, and consumed 70% of the EEC budget by the 1980s, reflecting the political power of farming interests despite economic inefficiency.\n\nINDUSTRIAL RESTRUCTURING AND EMPLOYMENT\n\nIntegration drove industrial restructuring as firms relocated to exploit economies of scale and access larger markets. German industry expanded dramatically, with West German GDP growing at average annual rates of 5-6% in the 1950s-1960s, the \"economic miracle.\" Integration forced inefficient producers out of business; Belgian coal mines closed as competition from German Ruhr coal intensified, displacing thousands of workers. Conversely, German manufacturing expanded, attracting foreign investment. By the 1970s-1980s, traditional heavy industries declined across Western Europe as competition intensified and globalisation affected textiles, shipbuilding and steel production. Belgium and Britain, with older industrial bases, experienced sharper decline than West Germany with newer infrastructure. Industrial employment fell from 42% of the West German workforce in 1950 to 28% by 1992; in Belgium, coal mining employment collapsed from 200,000 in 1950 to 10,000 by 1988. Service sector employment expanded to absorb some displaced workers; by 1992, services comprised 65% of EEC employment.\n\nLIVING STANDARDS AND CONSUMER PROSPERITY\n\nEuropean unity contributed substantially to rising living standards. Real wages in the EEC increased approximately 3.5% annually in the 1950s-1960s, creating mass consumer culture. Car ownership exemplified this: in West Germany, car ownership rose from 3 per 1,000 people in 1950 to 300 per 1,000 by 1980. Housing improved markedly as construction boomed to accommodate urban migration attracted by industrial employment. Welfare states expanded in all member states; social spending as percentage of GDP rose from approximately 8-10% in 1950 to 25-30% by 1980. Integration facilitated this expansion by creating conditions of full employment and economic growth that governments could redistribute. Educational provision expanded significantly; secondary school enrollment across the EEC rose from 25% of age cohort in 1950 to over 80% by 1990, reflecting both prosperity and labour market demands for skilled workers.\n\nREGIONAL INEQUALITY\n\nUnity produced uneven regional effects. Italy's Mezzogiorno (southern region) remained considerably poorer than the industrial north despite EEC membership from 1957; per capita income in Sicily was approximately 50% of northern Italy by 1992. Southern regions benefited less from industrial investment, which concentrated in existing industrial centres like the Ruhr, Burgundy and northern Italy. However, the European Regional Development Fund (established 1975) attempted redistribution, channelling resources to poorer regions; Ireland and Greece, relatively poor at accession (1973 and 1981), received substantial transfers that funded infrastructure development. Ireland's GDP per capita rose from 60% of the EEC average at accession to 95% by 1992, partly reflecting EU investment.\n\nSECTOR-SPECIFIC EFFECTS\n\nAgriculture experienced paradoxical effects. CAP protections allowed small farmers to survive economically when market forces would have eliminated them, preserving rural communities and traditional agricultural landscapes. Yet it encouraged overproduction, stored in notorious \"wine lakes\" and \"butter mountains,\" economically wasteful and environmentally damaging through intensive farming practices. Manufacturing faced intense competition; firms either modernised or failed. The motor industry consolidated dramatically; by 1992, four companies (Volkswagen, Fiat, Peugeot-Citroën, Ford) dominated European production where dozens had competed in 1950. Banking and finance expanded as capital moved freely across borders following progressive liberalisation, particularly after the Single European Act (1987) committed members to complete market integration by 1993.\n\nSCIAL COHESION AND MIGRATION\n\nEconomic integration facilitated labour mobility. Southern Europeans and North Africans migrated northward seeking industrial employment; West Germany employed approximately 3 million foreign workers by 1973, comprising 11% of the workforce. This created social tensions, xenophobia and ultimately restrictive immigration policies after the 1973 oil crisis ended labour demand. Within the EEC, citizens gained increasing rights to work across borders; the Treaty of Maastricht (1992) granted citizens the right to live and work anywhere in the EU, though this didn't apply to migrants from outside the EU. Social policies gradually harmonised as competitive pressures and agreements required similar working conditions and welfare standards, though significant variations persisted.\n\nECONOMIC CRISIS AND LIMITS\n\nThe 1973 oil crisis exposed vulnerabilities; stagflation (simultaneous inflation and stagnation) affected all members, though Germany managed better than Britain or Italy. Rising unemployment and inflation polarised politics, enabling far-right movements to gain support. The 1980s recession struck differently; deindustrialisation devastated manufacturing regions in Britain and Belgium while West Germany maintained competitive advantage. These crises revealed that unity didn't eliminate national economic problems or create automatic prosperity; success still depended on national policy choices, industrial competitiveness and external economic conditions.\n\nCONCLUSION\n\nEuropean unity from 1945 to 1992 produced substantial net economic benefits through expanded trade, industrial growth, rising living standards and technological advancement. However, benefits distributed unevenly by region, sector and time period. Traditional industries and regions declined sharply while service and knowledge-intensive sectors expanded. Agricultural protection preserved rural communities at high cost. Overall, unity accelerated economic development, integration and modernisation across Western Europe, raising average living standards substantially, though creating winners and losers, regional disparities, and social tensions alongside prosperity.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
