        "question": "Why did controversy arise from the activities of the Apprentice Boys of Derry or the siting of a new university at Coleraine?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "The siting of a new university at Coleraine in 1965 generated substantial controversy within Northern Ireland because it was perceived as a deliberate attempt to reinforce unionist dominance and marginalise the nationalist Catholic community in the north-west.\n\nThe decision to locate the second university at Coleraine rather than at Derry (Londonderry) became a focal point for nationalist grievances. Derry, with a substantial Catholic majority, had lobbied intensively for the university, viewing it as an economic and educational opportunity that could reverse the town's economic decline. The region had suffered from industrial decline, particularly the loss of shirt-making and other manufacturing industries, leaving unemployment high among the Catholic population. A university would have provided employment for graduates, attracted investment, and enhanced the town's status. The government's rejection of Derry in favour of Coleraine, a predominantly Protestant town in County Antrim with a smaller population, appeared to nationalists as a transparent rejection of Catholic aspirations and an assertion of unionist political priorities.\n\nThe controversy deepened when it became clear that Coleraine's selection aligned with the broader pattern of unionist economic and social policy in Northern Ireland. The allocation of industry, housing, and investment consistently favoured Protestant and unionist areas, a practice commonly termed discrimination by nationalists. Derry's loss of the university fitted seamlessly into this pattern: the predominantly Catholic west and north-west of the province systematically lagged behind more prosperous Protestant-dominated areas in the east. The establishment of the university at Coleraine, rather than addressing regional imbalance, appeared to entrench it further by directing resources and development opportunities to a unionist stronghold.\n\nFurthermore, the Apprentice Boys of Derry, a predominantly Protestant organisation with historical roots in the defence of Derry during the siege of 1688–89, conducted annual parades and demonstrations in the city. These marches were deeply provocative to the Catholic majority in Derry. The parades, particularly the large August parade, were perceived as triumphalist assertions of Protestant and unionist dominance, commemorating historical victories over Catholics and celebrating the maintenance of the union with Britain. The route of the marches frequently took them through or near Catholic neighbourhoods, and the bands played contentious Orange music, amplifying the sense of intimidation and exclusion felt by Catholics. The parades were seen not merely as historical commemoration but as annual reaffirmations of unionist political supremacy in a city with a Catholic majority who had no wish to be subjected to such displays.\n\nThe Apprentice Boys' activities became increasingly controversial from the mid-1960s onwards as civil rights consciousness grew among Northern Irish Catholics. The organisation's parades symbolised the structural inequality and lack of respect for Catholic identity embedded in unionist governance. Catholics asked why they should tolerate triumphalist marches in their own city, particularly when the same unionists denied them equality in employment, housing, and political representation. The 1964 Apprentice Boys march, for instance, was accompanied by severe rioting when police banned the flying of the Irish tricolour flag, an action that inflamed Catholic resentment and demonstrated the government's willingness to suppress Catholic expression whilst permitting Protestant demonstrations.\n\nBoth the university decision and the Apprentice Boys' activities reflected and reinforced the fundamental imbalance of power in Northern Ireland. The university choice symbolised unionist control of resource allocation and economic development; the Apprentice Boys marches symbolised unionist refusal to acknowledge or respect Catholic identity and presence in a city where Catholics formed the majority. Together, these issues crystallised growing Catholic frustration with systematic subordination and sparked broader demands for civil rights. The controversies illustrated that beneath the surface stability of Northern Ireland lay deep communal divisions and unionist determination to maintain hegemony regardless of the preferences or needs of the Catholic population. These disputes were not aberrations but reflections of the foundational inequality of the unionist state, and they contributed significantly to the emergence of the civil rights movement and the escalation of communal tension in the late 1960s.",
