        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Irish governments between 1949 and 1959 faced interconnected crises of economic stagnation, emigration, and low living standards. Their effectiveness was decidedly limited, though efforts were made across multiple fronts.\n\nEconomically, the First Inter-party Government (1948-1951) inherited a severe balance-of-payments crisis and attempted modest interventions. The 1948 devaluation of the pound created immediate hardship without generating sustained growth. Import restrictions and protectionism, inherited from earlier policy, remained in place but failed to diversify the economy or modernise industry. Fianna Fáil's return under Éamon de Valera (1951-1954) promised little beyond continuity. Neither government mobilised significant investment in productive capacity or strategic industries. The absence of a coherent development strategy left Ireland dependent on agriculture—a sector vulnerable to international price fluctuations and offering insufficient employment.\n\nEmigration accelerated dramatically throughout the decade, reaching crisis proportions by the mid-1950s. The First Inter-party Government did not address root causes: lack of industrial jobs, low agricultural productivity, or inadequate infrastructure. Fianna Fáil's second return (1957-1959) under de Valera initially showed similar passivity. However, the appointment of Seán Lemass as Minister for Industry and Commerce signalled a shift. Though still within this decade, his influence began to redirect policy toward export-driven industrialisation and foreign investment incentives—measures that would bear fruit only later.\n\nSocial provision remained rudimentary. Housing shortages persisted, particularly in urban areas. Education, though widely available, remained underfunded and ill-equipped to serve emerging industrial needs. Healthcare and welfare services reflected limited state capacity and conservative fiscal orthodoxy. Governments prioritised balanced budgets over redistributive investment, constraining their ability to tackle poverty and inequality.\n\nCultural and religious consensus enabled governments to avoid divisive domestic reform but also insulated them from modernising pressures. The 1950 Health Act controversy—where Fianna Fáil opposed provisions for state-provided healthcare as encroaching on Catholic medical authority—exemplified governmental deference to institutional interests over citizen welfare.\n\nIn summary, Irish governments of 1949-1959 proved ineffective against the structural challenges they faced. They inherited a fundamentally uncompetitive economy and lacked the ideological flexibility, financial resources, or strategic vision to transform it. The decade witnessed deepening emigration, economic stagnation, and social stasis. Only as this period ended did policy begin to shift, with tentative moves toward planning and industrial attraction that foreshadowed more decisive intervention in the 1960s. The governments' failures were rooted not in incompetence but in the absence of a break with protectionist orthodoxy and in political caution when radical restructuring was required.",
