        "label": "",
        "question": "What did Seán Lemass achieve in internal and external affairs, and what did he leave undone?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Seán Lemass served as Taoiseach from 1959 to 1966, succeeding Éamon de Valera. His tenure marked a decisive shift from protectionism and inward-looking economic policy toward modernisation, export-led growth, and international engagement.\n\nInternally, Lemass's most significant achievement was the introduction of the First and Second Programmes for Economic Expansion (1958–1963 and 1963–1970). Drafted by T.K. Whitaker, Secretary of the Department of Finance, these programmes abandoned the autarky of the de Valera era in favour of foreign investment, industrial development, and integration into international trade. Foreign firms such as Pfizer, Bausch and Lomb, and others established plants in Ireland, bringing employment and foreign exchange. Industrial output grew substantially, unemployment fell, and emigration began to decline by the early 1960s. The establishment of the Irish Development Authority and expansion of state investment in infrastructure reflected this reorientation. Lemass also introduced tariff reductions and began negotiations for Ireland's entry into the European Economic Community (EEC), recognising that Ireland's future prosperity lay in continental integration rather than isolation.\n\nIn agriculture, Lemass sponsored schemes to modernise farming, though progress was uneven and rural emigration continued. His government maintained welfare provision and pursued modest social reform, including improvements in housing and education, though these remained constrained by limited resources and conservative social attitudes. He relaxed some of the cultural restrictiveness of the de Valera period—for instance, allowing greater openness to foreign culture and ideas—but did not fundamentally challenge Catholic social conservatism or censorship.\n\nIn external affairs, Lemass secured Ireland's membership of the United Nations (1955, though formally in December 1955, his election occurred earlier). More significantly, he pursued active diplomatic engagement, positioning Ireland as a neutral nation participating constructively in international affairs. He sought to enhance Ireland's international standing and economic ties. His efforts toward European integration, culminating in the formal application for EEC membership in 1961 (though blocked initially by de Gaulle's veto on Britain's application), demonstrated his forward-looking vision of Ireland's role in Western Europe.\n\nLemass also took the historic step of meeting with Northern Ireland's Prime Minister, Terence O'Neill, in January 1965—the first such meeting between Irish and Northern Irish leaders in decades. This gesture signalled a willingness to improve relations across the border and move beyond the rigid nationalist-unionist antagonism that had characterised the entire post-1921 period. The meeting was symbolic but limited in concrete outcome.\n\nHowever, Lemass's achievements were incomplete in several respects. His economic programmes, while successful in attracting foreign investment, created a distorted industrial structure heavily dependent on multinational corporations, which offered limited linkage to indigenous Irish industry and could relocate if incentives declined. The benefits of growth were unevenly distributed geographically and socially; the west and rural areas continued to decline. Agricultural modernisation lagged, and the farming community remained economically vulnerable. By 1966, economic growth began to slow, and the long-term sustainability of the model remained uncertain.\n\nSocially, Lemass did little to address the deep conservatism of Irish society. Women's participation in the labour force remained restricted, contraception remained prohibited, divorce was impossible, and censorship of literature and film remained severe. These issues would become focal points of social conflict in the 1970s and 1980s. Unlike some of his contemporaries in Western Europe, Lemass did not pursue sustained cultural or social liberalisation.\n\nRegarding partition, while Lemass acknowledged the reality of partition and adopted a more pragmatic approach than his predecessor, he made no substantial progress toward reunification. His meeting with O'Neill was goodwill but produced no institutional framework for cooperation or political progress on the constitutional question. When communal tensions erupted in Northern Ireland in 1968–1969, after Lemass had left office, the fragility of his diplomatic overture became apparent.\n\nLemass also did not resolve the ongoing tension between nationalist aspiration and pragmatic economic necessity. His embrace of foreign investment and EEC integration represented a de facto acceptance that Ireland's future lay in Western capitalism and European integration rather than in the pursuit of nationalist self-sufficiency; this shift, though necessary, was never fully reconciled with the nationalist ideology that remained dominant in Irish political culture.\n\nIn summary, Lemass achieved a fundamental reorientation of Irish economic policy toward growth, modernisation, and international integration, with measurable success in employment, industrial output, and living standards. Externally, he enhanced Ireland's international presence and took the first tentative steps toward improved north-south relations. However, he left undone the transformation of Ireland's social and cultural life, made limited progress on partition, and established an economic model whose long-term viability and equity remained questionable. His legacy is one of necessary modernisation initiated but not completed, and of pragmatism constrained by the limits of his political will and the structural conservatism of Irish society.",
