        "question": "Who was the more successful reformer, Robert Owen or Robert Peel? Argue your case, referring to both.",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Robert Peel stands as the more successful reformer, though both men advanced significant change within early 19th-century Britain, albeit through different means and with unequal institutional impact.\n\n(Owen's vision and limitations. Owen articulated a radical critique of industrial capitalism and promoted cooperative, communal solutions to social suffering. At New Lanark, he implemented shorter working hours, improved housing, education for children, and established conditions that demonstrated workers could be productive without brutal exploitation. His ideas influenced the cooperative movement and utopian socialist thought. However, Owen's influence remained primarily intellectual and experimental rather than legislative. His attempts to establish cooperative communities—including New Harmony in America—largely failed. He achieved no significant parliamentary legislation and his schemes depended on philanthropic patronage rather than systemic change. While he exposed industrial abuses and offered alternative models, he lacked the political machinery and parliamentary authority to institutionalise reform at national scale.\n\n(Peel's legislative achievement. Peel, by contrast, operated within the political establishment and leveraged prime ministerial power to enact concrete, enforceable reforms. His Factory Acts (particularly the 1833 Act) established statutory limits on child labour in textile mills—the first major protective legislation applying across industry. The legislation prescribed maximum hours for children and compulsory schooling, backed by inspection. This created legal precedent and enforceable standards. His repeal of the Corn Laws in 1846, though economically controversial and caused his party's rupture, demonstrated his willingness to override sectional interest for what he judged rational policy; it reduced food costs for workers and signalled state willingness to prioritise consumer welfare over landed interests. Peel's establishment of the Metropolitan Police in 1829 created a disciplined, professional police force that reduced crime and brought rational administration to public order. These were permanent, institutional changes embedded in law and administration.\n\n(Scope and sustainability. Peel's reforms, though more limited in ambition than Owen's holistic vision, proved durable. The Factory Acts spawned further legislation in 1844 and 1847, progressively tightening protections. The Police model spread across Britain. The Corn Law repeal, though traumatic, became irreversible. Owen's communities dissolved; his cooperative vision took generations to gain traction through voluntary association, and never achieved his envisaged scale. Owen influenced later socialist thought but created no living institutional legacy during his lifetime comparable to Peel's legislative frameworks.\n\n(Constraints and context. Owen operated outside parliament, dependent on persuasion and private enterprise. Peel wielded executive authority and could command parliamentary majorities on specific issues. Both faced formidable opposition—Owen from manufacturers and landowners; Peel from hardline protectionists and laissez-faire ideologues. Peel's advantage lay in access to state power; Owen's constraint was its absence. Yet this distinction underlines rather than diminishes Peel's greater success: effective reform in this period required parliamentary leverage.\n\n(Measured assessment. Owen deserves recognition for moral clarity and imaginative solutions; his demonstration that alternative industrial relations were possible influenced subsequent reform sentiment. Peel merits recognition for translating reformist impulse into enforceable law. The Factory Acts improved conditions for millions; the Police created institutional order; Corn Law repeal shifted economic philosophy. Owen's contribution was inspirational and ideological; Peel's was structural and legislative. Success in reform should be measured not only by moral consistency or radical vision, but by achieved institutional change that persists and extends. On this criterion, Peel's legislative achievements and their subsequent expansion constitute a more successful reformist record than Owen's visionary but ultimately unanchored experiments.",
