        "label": "",
        "question": "During the period 1945-1992, how did one or more of the following develop: affluence, leisure time and the consumer society; nuclear power; the computer?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "The period 1945-1992 witnessed transformative developments in affluence, leisure, consumer society, nuclear power, and computing technology, each fundamentally reshaping European and global society.\n\nAfluence and Consumer Society\nEurope experienced unprecedented economic growth after 1945, particularly in Western Europe. The Marshall Plan (1948-1952) injected $13 billion into European reconstruction, enabling rapid industrialisation and rising living standards. Real wages in Western Europe increased substantially through the 1950s and 1960s. This economic expansion created mass affluence unknown in previous generations. By the 1960s, car ownership became commonplace in Western Europe; France, Germany, and Britain saw automobile sales surge. Consumer goods—refrigerators, washing machines, televisions—became standard household items rather than luxuries. Advertising expanded dramatically to promote consumption. Department stores and supermarkets proliferated, replacing traditional corner shops. The concept of \"keeping up with the Joneses\" reflected the competitive consumption culture emerging in prosperous societies.\n\nLeisure Time\nRising affluence directly enabled expanded leisure. The working week shortened: by 1960, many Western European workers had achieved a five-day week (unthinkable in 1945). Paid holidays became standard; workers accumulated annual leave entitlements. Tourism transformed from an elite activity to a mass phenomenon. Package holidays to Mediterranean resorts became affordable for ordinary families in the 1960s and 1970s. Airlines expanded capacity; budget travel democratised international movement. Domestic leisure also expanded: home entertainment improved with television ownership (reaching 90% of British households by the 1970s), radio ownership remained high, and cinemas—though declining—remained social venues. Sports became mass spectacles, with football attracting unprecedented attendance and viewership. The commercialisation of leisure created new industries and employment.\n\nNuclear Power\nNuclear energy emerged as a utopian solution to post-war energy demands. The Atoms for Peace programme (1953) promoted civilian nuclear development globally. European nations invested heavily: Britain commissioned Calder Hall (1956), the world's first commercial nuclear reactor. France developed an ambitious nuclear programme under de Gaulle, viewing it as essential to national independence and modernisation. By 1992, France derived approximately 75% of its electricity from nuclear power—the highest proportion globally. Germany, Belgium, and the Netherlands also embraced nuclear energy. The technology promised limitless clean energy, supporting industrial growth without pollution. However, safety concerns emerged. The Windscale incident (1957) in Britain, though contained, raised public anxiety. Nuclear waste disposal remained unresolved. The Three Mile Island accident (1979) in the USA and Chernobyl disaster (1986) in the Soviet Union intensified European concerns about reactor safety. Environmental movements increasingly opposed nuclear expansion from the 1970s onwards. By 1992, nuclear's utopian promise faced serious opposition, though it remained a major European energy source.\n\nComputer Development\nComputing evolved from military and scientific specialisation toward consumer and commercial ubiquity between 1945-1992. Early computers (ENIAC, 1946) were room-sized calculating machines for defence and research. Through the 1950s-1960s, mainframe computers served governments, large corporations, and universities. IBM dominated this era, providing computers for administrative and scientific computation. Miniaturisation accelerated in the 1970s. The microprocessor (Intel 4004, 1971) enabled compact machines. Personal computers emerged: the Apple II (1977) and Commodore 64 (1982) brought computing into homes and small businesses. The BBC Micro (1981) promoted computing literacy in Britain and education sectors across Europe. IBM's Personal Computer (1981) established a dominant standard. By the late 1980s, personal computers proliferated in offices and homes. Software became crucial: spreadsheets (Lotus 1-2-3), word processors (WordPerfect), and database programmes transformed office work. The internet remained nascent during this period (ARPANET existed from 1969, but public access came post-1992), so computing in 1945-1992 remained largely isolated from networked communication. Word processing eliminated typewriters in progressive offices; email systems emerged in large organisations by 1992. Computing revolutionised manufacturing through automation and inventory control, banking through digital record-keeping, and scientific research through enhanced calculation capacity. The computer industry itself became economically significant, creating new employment and driving technological competition between nations.\n\nInterconnections\nThese three developments interconnected meaningfully. Nuclear power enabled electrification supporting growing consumer demand and computer infrastructure. Consumer affluence funded both leisure travel (requiring energy) and computer purchases. Computers increasingly managed nuclear reactor operations and monitored safety systems. The three elements collectively represented modernity: scientific advancement, technological optimism, and mass participation in prosperity.\n\nConclusion\nBetween 1945-1992, Western Europe transformed from post-war austerity toward mass affluence, expanded leisure, nuclear-powered energy systems, and computerised information processing. These developments raised living standards dramatically but also generated new anxieties: environmental degradation, nuclear safety, technological unemployment, and cultural homogenisation. By 1992, Europe stood at a threshold where Cold War divisions would collapse, yet prosperity and technology promised continued transformation ahead.",
