        "question": "Who was the greater president, Truman or Johnson? Argue your case, referring to both in your answer.",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "To assess whether Truman or Johnson was the greater president requires evaluation of their legacies across domestic and foreign policy, measured against their respective contexts and consequences.\n\n(Truman inherited the presidency upon Roosevelt's death in April 1945 during the final stages of World War II. His immediate decisions—to use atomic bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki in August 1945—demonstrated decisive action in unprecedented circumstances. While controversial, the bombings ended the Pacific War without a costly invasion of Japan. Truman faced the critical challenge of transitioning America from total war to peace and managing relations with the Soviet Union as wartime alliance dissolved into Cold War rivalry.)\n\n(Truman's foreign policy established foundational Cold War strategy. The Truman Doctrine (1947) committed the US to containing Soviet expansion, articulated explicitly during the Greek civil war and Turkish pressure. This doctrine became the intellectual framework for Cold War engagement for decades. The Marshall Plan (1948) committed $12.7 billion to rebuilding Western Europe, creating economic stability and preventing communist expansion through material deprivation. This represented constructive Cold War strategy beyond mere military containment. The Berlin Airlift (1948-49), when Stalin blockaded West Berlin, demonstrated Truman's resolve: rather than withdraw, he sustained the city through aerial supply for nearly a year, forcing Soviet retreat without direct military confrontation. NATO's formation in 1949 institutionalized Western alliance against Soviet power.)\n\n(Domestically, Truman's record proved mixed. The Fair Deal agenda proposed expanded New Deal programs—national health insurance, civil rights protection, and federal housing—but Congress blocked most legislation. His attempts at price controls and labor management created economic friction. However, Truman integrated the armed forces by executive order in 1948, advancing civil rights despite political cost. His administration prosecuted the Korean War (1950-53) when North Korea invaded the South, preserving South Korean independence, though the war ended in stalemate rather than victory. He dismissed General MacArthur for insubordination, affirming civilian control over the military—constitutionally correct but politically damaging.)\n\n(Johnson assumed the presidency following Kennedy's assassination in November 1963, inheriting both a legislative agenda and escalating involvement in Vietnam. His Great Society represented the most expansive domestic program since the New Deal. The Civil Rights Act (1964) prohibited racial discrimination in public accommodations and employment—transforming legal framework for racial equality. The Voting Rights Act (1965) eliminated literacy tests and authorized federal oversight of Southern elections, directly addressing disenfranchisement. Medicare and Medicaid (1965) provided health insurance for the elderly and poor, establishing permanent social safety nets. Federal Aid to Education Act (1965) invested substantially in schools. These achievements fundamentally altered American society's relationship to social welfare and civil rights.)\n\n(However, Johnson's presidency was consumed by Vietnam War escalation. Following the Tonkin Gulf incident (August 1964)—itself disputed in authenticity—Johnson secured the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution, granting congressional authority for sustained bombing and troop deployment. By 1965, Operation Rolling Thunder, an intensive bombing campaign, began. Troop levels escalated from 900 in 1964 to over 540,000 by 1968. The war killed over 58,000 Americans and an estimated 2 million Vietnamese. Despite massive resources deployed, victory remained elusive. The 1968 Tet Offensive, though a military defeat for North Vietnam, shattered American public confidence in official claims of progress. Johnson announced he would not seek reelection, and the war continued under his successors until 1973 without achieving stated objectives. Vietnam divided the nation, sparked massive protest movements, and discredited the presidency.)\n\n(Comparing the two requires contextual judgment. Truman operated during clearer geopolitical circumstances: containing Soviet expansion was arguably the legitimate strategic imperative. His Cold War architecture—containment doctrine, Marshall Plan, NATO, defensive wars in Korea—created structures that endured productively. His domestic limitations reflected real congressional constraints and postwar economic complexity, not executive failure. His decisiveness in Berlin and Korea demonstrated effective crisis management.\n\nJohnson achieved transformative domestic legislation that permanently expanded federal responsibility for civil rights and social welfare. These achievements represented genuine moral and social progress with lasting institutional impact. Medicare alone affected tens of millions. Civil rights legislation was constitutionally and morally necessary.\n\nHowever, Johnson's Vietnam escalation represents catastrophic strategic failure. The war was fought on premises later revealed as false, achieved no stated objective, and inflicted massive casualties. It consumed resources that could have sustained Great Society programs, ultimately forcing retrenchment. The war damaged American credibility internationally and fractured domestic consensus in ways that persisted for decades. Johnson's credibility gap—the gap between official claims and battlefield reality—fundamentally damaged public trust in government institutions.)\n\n(Truman emerges as the greater president when measured against lasting constructive consequences. His Cold War strategy, while imperfect, successfully contained Soviet expansion without nuclear war for four decades. The Marshall Plan rebuilt Europe and prevented economic collapse. His institutional innovations—NATO, containment doctrine—provided framework for sustained Western security. His domestic failures, while real, reflected genuine constraints rather than strategic miscalculation. Johnson's domestic achievements were profound and necessary, but his foreign policy catastrophe—Vietnam—consumed his presidency, undermined his legitimacy, damaged American power, and caused immense suffering. A president's greatness must account for avoiding disastrous failure as well as achieving positive reform. Truman's restraint in nuclear weapons despite having them, his successful navigation of multiple crises, and his institutional legacy outweigh Johnson's legislative achievements when set against Johnson's Vietnam failure. Johnson might have ranked among America's greatest presidents had Vietnam not occurred; Truman's presidency would likely be assessed similarly regardless of any single additional initiative.)",
