        "question": "Write a short paragraph on one of the following:\n1. O'Connell and Catholic Emancipation, 1829.\n2. Mother Mary Aikenhead.\n3. Impact of the railways.\n4. Education in Ireland, 1815-1870.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "O'Connell and Catholic Emancipation, 1829\n\nDaniel O'Connell's achievement of Catholic Emancipation in 1829 represented a watershed moment in Irish history and demonstrated the power of mass mobilization and constitutional agitation. O'Connell, a lawyer and politician, recognized that the penal laws excluding Catholics from political participation, the professions, and land ownership were unsustainable impediments to Irish progress. He founded the Catholic Association in 1823, which rapidly became a vehicle for galvanizing Catholic opinion across Ireland through parish networks and the \"Catholic rent\"—small contributions that funded the organization and demonstrated mass commitment. The Association's electoral activities, particularly the dramatic by-election victory in Clare in 1828 when O'Connell himself, though unable legally to take his seat, was elected with overwhelming support, created a political crisis that forced the British government's hand. Wellington and Peel recognized that Catholic exclusion could no longer be maintained without provoking serious unrest in Ireland. The Catholic Emancipation Act of 1829 removed most restrictions on Catholic participation in public life, allowing Catholics to sit in Parliament, enter the professions, and hold senior administrative positions. However, the Act also raised the property qualification for voting, effectively disenfranchising many of the poorer Catholics whose votes had proved decisive. O'Connell's victory was therefore incomplete, yet it established him as the undisputed leader of Irish Catholic opinion and validated his constitutional methods of agitation. It demonstrated that organized popular pressure could extract concessions from Westminster, a lesson that would influence Irish nationalist strategy throughout the nineteenth century and beyond.",
