        "marks": 30,
        "model": "The 1916 Rising was a decisive moment in Irish nationalism that transformed public opinion and accelerated the path to independence. On Easter Monday, 24 April 1916, members of the Irish Volunteers and Irish Citizen Army seized key positions in Dublin, including the General Post Office, where Pádraig Pearse read the Proclamation of the Republic. The Rising lasted six days before the rebels surrendered to overwhelming British military force. Although initially unpopular with Dubliners due to the destruction and casualties, the execution of fifteen leaders between 3 and 12 May 1916 proved pivotal. The executions, particularly of Pearse, James Connolly, and others, transformed the rebels into martyrs and swung public sympathy decisively in their favour. Newspapers that had criticised the Rising now condemned the executions as excessive. The Rising demonstrated that Irish republicans were prepared to fight for independence and willing to die for their cause. It revived the concept of armed rebellion as a legitimate path to sovereignty and gave ideological weight to the republican movement. The aftermath created a generation of activists committed to achieving the republic for which the 1916 leaders had died. The Rising's legacy directly enabled Sinn Féin's electoral sweep in 1918 and provided the political momentum for the War of Independence. Thus the Rising, though militarily defeated, proved strategically victorious in mobilising the Irish people behind the independence movement and making partition and dominion status unacceptable to nationalist opinion.",
