    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "B",
        "question": "Write a short paragraph on one of the following:\n1. Emigration in the Famine decade, 1845-1855.\n2. The role of the GAA among emigrants.\n3. Mother Mary Martin.\n4. The life and writings of Domhnall Mac Amhlaigh.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Between 1845 and 1855, approximately one million Irish people emigrated, fleeing the catastrophic Great Famine triggered by potato blight. This mass exodus represented an unprecedented demographic crisis; the Irish population fell from 8.2 million to 6 million by 1851. The vast majority sailed to America, particularly to Boston, New York, and Philadelphia, seeking survival and economic opportunity. Ships carrying emigrants, often called 'coffin ships' due to disease and overcrowding, resulted in mortality rates of up to 10 per cent during the voyage. Those who survived arrived destitute and faced severe discrimination in their host countries, forced into the lowest-paid, most dangerous occupations—dock work, canal-building, and domestic service. Despite these hardships, emigration became the primary survival strategy for Irish families; those with any means scraped together money for passage, while assisted emigration schemes and remittances from already-settled relatives facilitated further departures. This decade established the pattern of Irish emigration as both economic necessity and cultural trauma, permanently altering Ireland's demographic character and creating scattered Irish communities across the Atlantic world that would maintain cultural and familial ties to the homeland for generations.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
