    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "B",
        "question": "Write a short paragraph on one of the following:\n1. Vladimir Ilyich Lenin.\n2. Benito Mussolini's relations with the Catholic Church.\n3. Vichy France, 1940-1945.\n4. Anglo-American popular culture.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Vladimir Ilyich Lenin was the founder of the Soviet communist state and leader of the Bolshevik Revolution of 1917. Lenin believed in Marxist ideology and the inevitability of a working-class revolution to overthrow capitalism. He led the Bolsheviks to seize power in October 1917, establishing a one-party communist dictatorship. Lenin implemented the policy of War Communism (1918-1921) to consolidate control during the Russian Civil War, nationalizing industry and requisitioning grain, which caused severe famine. In 1921, he introduced the New Economic Policy (NEP), which allowed limited private enterprise and small-scale capitalism in agriculture and trade while maintaining state control of major industries. This pragmatic shift helped stabilize the economy and rebuild production after years of conflict. Lenin established the Cheka secret police to eliminate political opposition and suppress dissent, creating the totalitarian apparatus that would characterize Soviet rule. He also founded the Communist International (Comintern) to spread revolution worldwide. Lenin's ideology emphasized centralized planning, the dictatorship of the proletariat, and the leading role of the Communist Party as the vanguard of the working class. His stroke in 1922 and death in 1924 led to a power struggle; Stalin eventually emerged victorious and transformed Lenin's state into a more rigid totalitarian system. Nevertheless, Lenin's vision of a communist society based on collective ownership and the abolition of class hierarchy remained the foundational ideology of the Soviet Union throughout the twentieth century.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
