        "question": "Why were there many social and economic problems in Britain during the 1930s?",
        "marks": 20,
        "model": "Britain faced severe social and economic problems in the 1930s stemming from structural weaknesses exposed by the Great Depression. The economy had never fully recovered from World War I; traditional industries—coal, textiles, shipbuilding, iron and steel—were in decline as overseas competition intensified and international demand collapsed. Unemployment reached approximately 2.5 million by 1932, particularly concentrated in industrial regions like South Wales, Lancashire, and the North East, creating deprivation and social hardship. The return to the gold standard in 1925 at pre-war parity made exports uncompetitive, damaging manufacturing. Agricultural distress compounded these problems, as farming communities suffered from low prices and falling incomes. The 1931 financial crisis forced Britain off gold and triggered banking instability; the pound's value fell sharply. Government response—including severe public expenditure cuts and the means test for unemployment benefit—deepened hardship rather than relieved it, reducing purchasing power and demand. Regional inequality worsened markedly: while London and the South East developed new light industries and experienced relative prosperity, northern industrial zones experienced persistent mass unemployment and poverty. These interconnected problems—industrial obsolescence, international economic collapse, deflationary government policy, and regional imbalance—created a decade of hardship for millions of British workers and their families.",
