    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "B",
        "question": "Write a short paragraph on one of the following:\n1. Serfdom in Russia.\n2. Giuseppe Mazzini.\n3. Haussmann's Paris.\n4. Karl Marx.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Giuseppe Mazzini (1805–1872) was a central figure in Italian nationalism and a theorist of democratic nationalism. Born in Genoa, he founded Young Italy (La Giovine Italia) in 1831, a revolutionary society dedicated to unifying Italy through popular uprising and establishing a democratic republic. Mazzini believed that nationalist movements must be democratic and popular, rejecting both foreign domination and monarchical conservatism. He emphasised the spiritual dimension of nationalism, arguing that each nation had a providential mission to fulfil in European civilization. His writings, particularly in the journal *Italica*, articulated a vision of Italy as a united nation-state, not merely a geographical expression. Though his direct military campaigns—including the defence of the Roman Republic in 1849—ultimately failed against combined Austrian and papal forces, Mazzini's ideological influence was profound. He inspired a generation of Italian patriots and democrats, including Garibaldi, and shaped the intellectual foundation of the Risorgimento. However, his insistence on republicanism and popular sovereignty contrasted sharply with the monarchical path Italy actually took under Cavour and Victor Emmanuel II. Mazzini died in exile in 1872, having witnessed Italian unification accomplished through methods he philosophically opposed—diplomatic negotiation and royal leadership rather than democratic revolution. Nevertheless, his contribution to nationalist thought and his role in mobilising Italian consciousness for unity remains historically significant, establishing him as one of the nineteenth century's most influential nationalist theorists and activists.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
