    "year": 2006,
    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "Q2",
        "question": "What were the social and economic effects of the Famine up to 1870?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "The Great Famine (1845–1852) and its aftermath inflicted profound social and economic devastation on Ireland, with effects persisting well beyond 1870. The catastrophe fundamentally transformed Irish society and economy in ways that shaped the nation's development for generations.\n\nDemographically, the Famine triggered the most severe population crisis in modern Irish history. Death and mass emigration reduced Ireland's population from approximately 8.2 million in 1841 to 5.8 million by 1851—a loss of nearly 30 percent in a single decade. The death toll reached an estimated 1 million from starvation and disease, particularly typhus and dysentery. Emigration removed a further million Irish, predominantly to the United States, Canada, and Australia. This haemorrhaging continued: by 1870, Ireland's population had fallen to approximately 5.4 million. The loss was concentrated among the poorest rural classes—labourers, smallholders, and tenant farmers—who lacked resources to purchase scarce food or afford passage abroad. Survivors were traumatized; mass graves and communal burial pits became permanent features of Irish consciousness, embedding the Famine in collective memory and generating deep resentment towards English government indifference.\n\nAgriculturally, the Famine accelerated pre-existing consolidation of landholding. The crisis exposed the catastrophic vulnerability of the Irish rural economy, which depended overwhelmingly on the potato as subsistence. Approximately 1 million people held plots of less than 5 acres, many as tenants at will paying rack rents to absentee landlords. When blight destroyed successive harvests from 1845 onwards, these subsistence farmers faced destitution. Many could not pay rent and were evicted; landlords, themselves squeezed financially, ejected tenants to consolidate holdings into larger, more profitable grazing farms. The famine years witnessed approximately 500,000 evictions. Survivors and their descendants increasingly worked as agricultural labourers rather than independent farmers. By 1870, consolidation had advanced significantly: the proportion of holdings under 5 acres had fallen sharply, and larger farms dominated the landscape. This transformation destroyed the peasant proprietorship that had characterized pre-Famine rural Ireland and entrenched landlord dominance. Crucially, the trauma fostered lasting peasant antagonism to landlordism, creating psychological and political foundations for later Land League agitation.\n\nEconomically, the Famine revealed and deepened Ireland's structural subordination within the Union. Pre-Famine Ireland possessed limited industrial development; the economy rested primarily on agriculture and subsistence farming. The Famine shattered rural purchasing power and investment capacity, preventing capital accumulation and industrial development. Agricultural consolidation benefited landlords and larger farmers but enriched comparatively few. Most survivors remained impoverished wage-labourers or smallholders. Landlord rents extracted wealth to absentee English estates rather than circulating within Irish communities. Meanwhile, Irish commercial life contracted: rural markets collapsed as purchasing power evaporated. By 1870, Ireland's economy remained fundamentally dependent on agriculture; industrial development lagged catastrophically behind Britain and Ulster. The North-East, with its linen, shipbuilding, and engineering sectors, prospered; the rest of Ireland stagnated. This economic disparity, rooted in pre-Famine patterns but exacerbated by Famine devastation, would define Irish regional inequality thereafter.\n\nSocially and culturally, the Famine fractured Irish community structures. Extended family networks were decimated; neighbourhoods lost entire households. Traditional rural craft industries collapsed as impoverishment eliminated consumer demand. Irish language decline accelerated: survivors, particularly the young, pragmatically adopted English as the language of opportunity in emigration destinations and labour markets. By 1870, the linguistic landscape had shifted decisively towards English. The Church, particularly the Catholic hierarchy, strengthened its institutional authority during and after the crisis—priests distributed relief, buried the dead, and offered spiritual solace. This enhancement of clerical power persisted, shaping Irish religious culture decisively. Simultaneously, emigration created a transnational Irish diaspora, particularly in American cities, which retained emotional connections to Ireland, periodically sent remittances, and nurtured grievances against British imperial policy. This diaspora would become a significant political force, financing nationalist movements and maintaining pressure on British-Irish relations.\n\nPolitically, Famine devastation delegitimized Union and British governance. The British government's response—predicated on laissez-faire ideology and the belief that state intervention would reward idleness—appeared callous and inadequate. Relief measures were belated, inadequate, and means-tested rigorously, forcing the desperate into degrading workhouses. Food exported from Ireland during the Famine (grain, cattle, dairy products) symbolized the colonial extraction that persisted while Irish starved. This contradiction fuelled nationalist anger and fractured Irish faith in parliamentary politics. Young Ireland's revolutionary romanticism in the 1840s and the Fenian movement's emergence in the 1860s both drew energy from Famine-generated grievance. Nationalist ideology increasingly framed the Famine not as a natural disaster but as a consequence of British misgovernance and colonialism—a narrative that shaped Irish political consciousness and demands for sovereignty.\n\nBy 1870, the Famine's social and economic effects remained transformative. The population had halved; the agrarian economy had been restructured around larger holdings and landlord consolidation; industrial development had stalled; the Irish diaspora had assumed strategic significance; and political consciousness had fundamentally shifted towards nationalist assertion. The Famine represented not a discrete catastrophe but a historical rupture that redirected Irish development, impoverized the majority, enriched landlords marginally, and poisoned Anglo-Irish relations fatally.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
