    "year": 2006,
    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "Q2",
        "question": "Why did anti-Irish sentiment emerge in the United States and/or in Britain during the nineteenth century?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Anti-Irish sentiment emerged in the United States and Britain during the nineteenth century as a result of economic competition, religious prejudice, and cultural stereotyping that intensified with large-scale Irish emigration.\n\nIn Britain, anti-Irish feeling was rooted in centuries of political and religious conflict. The Act of Union (1800) brought Ireland formally under British rule, but deep Protestant-Catholic divisions persisted. Irish Catholics were associated with disloyalty to the Crown and sympathy for Irish independence. The Fenian Brotherhood's activities in the 1860s, including their bombing campaign in England, crystallised British fears of Irish terrorism and conspiracy. These incidents hardened British public opinion against Irish people as a whole, portraying them as dangerous agitators and rebels. Additionally, the persistence of agrarian violence in Ireland—such as incidents during the Land War of the 1880s—reinforced English stereotypes of Irish lawlessness and savagery. Anti-Irish propaganda in British newspapers depicted Irish people as inherently violent and unfit for self-government, arguments used to justify continued British control.\n\nIn the United States, anti-Irish sentiment developed primarily after the Great Famine (1845-1852), which triggered mass emigration. Over one million Irish arrived in America seeking refuge from starvation. This sudden influx created intense competition for jobs and housing, particularly in industrial cities like Boston, New York, and Philadelphia. Native-born American workers, already economically insecure, viewed Irish immigrants as a threat willing to work for lower wages, undercutting employment standards. Employers often exploited this prejudice, preferring Irish labour precisely because it was cheaper, which deepened resentment among established workers and fuelled nativist movements.\n\nReligion was a critical factor in American anti-Irish sentiment. The vast majority of Irish immigrants were Roman Catholics, arriving in a predominantly Protestant nation founded on anti-Catholic principles. American Protestantism viewed Catholicism with deep suspicion, associating it with foreign allegiance—particularly to the Pope in Rome—rather than to the American nation. This religious intolerance manifested in discrimination in employment, housing, and education. The slogan \"No Irish Need Apply\" became notorious on job advertisements. Catholic churches and convents were occasionally attacked by Protestant mobs, most notoriously during the Philadelphia riots of 1844. Anti-Catholic organisations like the Know Nothing Party (1850s) explicitly campaigned against Irish Catholic immigration and influence, winning significant political support. This nativist movement reflected broader American anxieties about preserving the nation's Protestant character against what they perceived as a Catholic invasion.\n\nCultural stereotyping reinforced economic and religious prejudices. Both British and American societies developed degrading caricatures of Irish people. They were portrayed as inherently lazy, drunken, violent, and intellectually inferior—stereotypes widely disseminated through newspapers, political cartoons, and popular literature. These caricatures served to justify discrimination by suggesting Irish people were naturally unsuited to positions of responsibility or skilled work. In America, Irish immigrants crowded into urban slums, creating visible poverty that fed stereotypes about Irish improvidence, rather than reflecting structural economic barriers. The fact that many Irish families experienced extreme hardship in their new homes was attributed to supposed Irish character flaws rather than to systemic exploitation and limited opportunities.\n\nIn Britain, anti-Irish sentiment was additionally connected to debates over Irish nationalism and Home Rule. As the Irish independence movement grew stronger in the late nineteenth century, particularly under Charles Stewart Parnell's leadership of the Irish Parliamentary Party, British anxiety about Irish disloyalty intensified. Many British people viewed Irish demands for self-government as threatening to imperial unity and stability. Crimes associated with Irish nationalist organisations—particularly the Phoenix Park murders (1882) and Fenian bombings—provided concrete examples used to portray all Irish people as terrorists or potential rebels. This politicisation of anti-Irish feeling in Britain was inseparable from resistance to Irish Home Rule, which dominated British politics from 1886 onwards.\n\nSocial class also shaped anti-Irish sentiment. In both countries, most Irish immigrants arrived as impoverished, unskilled labourers with little formal education. They occupied the lowest social positions, doing dangerous, undesirable work—construction, mining, domestic service. Established populations used this poverty and occupational status as evidence of Irish inferiority, not recognising that immigration itself created these conditions. The Irish were denied access to better employment and housing precisely because of prejudice, yet their resulting poverty was attributed to inherent deficiency. This circular logic allowed existing hierarchies to appear natural and justified.\n\nIn conclusion, anti-Irish sentiment in the nineteenth century resulted from interconnected factors: economic competition intensified by mass emigration and labour exploitation; religious sectarianism reflecting Protestant-Catholic divisions; cultural stereotyping through media and propaganda; and, in Britain, political fears about Irish nationalism and disloyalty to the Union. These prejudices served crucial functions for dominant groups—justifying economic exploitation of Irish workers, maintaining Protestant religious hegemony, and defending political control over Ireland. Understanding anti-Irish sentiment requires recognising that it was not based on objective characteristics of Irish people, but rather reflected the anxieties, prejudices, and material interests of the societies receiving Irish emigrants or governing Ireland itself.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
