    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "Q1",
        "question": "During the period, 1949 – 1969, what was the impact of the welfare state on one or more of the following: education; health; housing?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "The welfare state profoundly reshaped education, health, and housing in Northern Ireland between 1949 and 1969, though its impact was uneven and contested along religious and political lines.\n\nEDUCATION\nThe Education Act (Northern Ireland) 1947, implemented from 1949, extended free secondary education to all children regardless of class. The Ministry of Education assumed responsibility for funding and administration, ending the previous reliance on voluntary schools to provide education. By 1950, the state provided free schooling across primary and secondary levels, increasing enrolment substantially. However, sectarian divisions intensified rather than diminished. Catholic schools remained under Church control with state funding, while state schools were effectively Protestant. By 1960, approximately 65 per cent of Protestant children attended state schools, while 95 per cent of Catholic children attended denominational institutions. Grammar schools, established under the 1947 Act, were largely accessed by Protestant students; the eleven-plus examination system favoured those with access to better preparatory schooling, predominantly Protestant pupils. Technical education expanded, with technical colleges opening in Belfast and other major towns by the mid-1950s, providing vocational training. However, the welfare state's educational promise of equal opportunity was substantially undermined by residential segregation and the denominational character of schooling. By 1969, educational inequality persisted: Catholic schools received lower per-capita grants, and Catholic pupils faced greater barriers to higher education. The state education system, rather than acting as a unifying force, reinforced communal separation.\n\nHEALTH\nThe National Health Service (Northern Ireland) 1948, implemented from 1949, provided free healthcare at the point of use, eliminating the previous system of poor relief and voluntary hospitals. This represented a revolutionary change for working-class access to medical care. Hospital provision was unified under the state; previously, denominational and voluntary hospitals operated independently. By the mid-1950s, the NHS had achieved significant improvements in maternal and infant mortality rates. In 1949, the infant mortality rate was approximately 42 per 1,000 live births; by 1969, this had fallen to approximately 20 per 1,000, reflecting improved obstetric services and vaccination programmes. Life expectancy increased noticeably. The welfare state funded a massive expansion of general practitioners, with health centres established in urban and rural areas. Dental and optical services became accessible to all social classes. However, sectarian tensions affected implementation. Catholic hospitals initially resisted integration into the NHS structure, and disputes arose over the appointment of consultants and management of Catholic institutions. By 1960, most hospitals had been integrated, though separate Catholic nursing homes and maternity units persisted in some areas. The welfare state's health provision was broadly successful in improving public health outcomes across the community, though administrative friction reflected underlying sectarian divisions.\n\nHOUSING\nThe Housing Trust, established in 1945 but accelerating from 1949, constructed significant numbers of state-built houses. Between 1949 and 1969, approximately 70,000 houses were built by the Housing Trust and local authorities. This addressed severe pre-war housing shortages; in 1949, overcrowding and substandard conditions affected up to 30 per cent of the urban working-class population. New estates were constructed in Belfast, Derry, Armagh, and other towns, providing modern housing with indoor sanitation, electricity, and running water—amenities unavailable in many pre-war dwellings. Rents were subsidised and affordable for working-class families. However, housing allocation became a focal point of sectarian grievance. The Housing Trust was supposed to allocate houses on need; in practice, local authorities (frequently controlled by unionist majorities) allocated houses disproportionately to Protestant applicants. In Derry, the predominantly Catholic population faced acute discrimination; between 1949 and 1969, the unionist-controlled corporation allocated the vast majority of new houses to Protestants despite Catholics forming the majority population. In rural areas, similar patterns emerged. By 1960, housing allocation had become a symbol of Catholic grievance and unionist privilege. The welfare state's housing provision, while materially improving living standards, institutionalised communal inequality. By 1969, housing discrimination was a primary cause of civil rights agitation.\n\nCONCLUSION\nThe welfare state between 1949 and 1969 delivered material improvements in living standards: expanded education, better health outcomes, and improved housing. However, its impact was fundamentally compromised by sectarian divisions embedded in Northern Ireland's political structure. The unionist-controlled state apparatus directed welfare provision to reinforce Protestant-unionist advantage in education, and housing allocation became scandalously discriminatory. Healthcare improvements were least contentious and most universal. By 1969, the welfare state's promise of equality had failed; instead, it had become a mechanism through which communal inequality was perpetuated, contributing directly to the civil rights movement and the crisis of 1969. The welfare state's technical achievements masked structural injustice.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
