        "question": "What was the nature of the regime changes which came about in France during the period, 1815 – 1870?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Between 1815 and 1870, France experienced four distinct regime changes reflecting fundamental conflicts over legitimacy, representation, and national identity that defined post-Napoleonic Europe.\n\nThe Bourbon Restoration (1815–1830) attempted to restore absolute monarchy under Louis XVIII and Charles X, reversing revolutionary gains. Louis XVIII initially adopted the Charter, a limited constitutional settlement recognising parliamentary institutions and civil rights. However, Charles X (1824–1830) pursued ultra-royalist policies, strengthening the Church and nobility at the expense of liberal gains. The 1830 July Revolution, triggered by his Four Ordinances dissolving parliament and restricting voting rights, demonstrated that a return to pre-1789 absolutism was impossible. The regime collapsed after three days of fierce Parisian street fighting, symbolising middle-class and intellectual rejection of reaction.\n\nThe July Monarchy (1830–1848) under Louis-Philippe represented a compromise between revolution and restoration. It established constitutional monarchy with a parliament, yet restricted voting to a narrow wealthy elite (roughly 200,000 men in a nation of 30 million). Louis-Philippe styled himself as a 'citizen-king' rather than absolute monarch, but the regime became increasingly corrupt and stagnant. Economic depression, industrial unrest, and exclusion of workers and many middle-class citizens from political participation created mounting discontent. Banquets organised by opposition figures in 1847–1848 became flashpoints for criticism. When the government banned a major reform banquet in February 1848, Parisian workers and students erected barricades. Louis-Philippe's attempt to negotiate failed; he abdicated within days, ending the July Monarchy after eighteen years.\n\nThe Second Republic (1848–1852) introduced universal male suffrage and abolished slavery in French colonies, representing a significant democratic advance. However, it was internally fractured. Radical republicans, moderate republicans, socialists, and monarchists competed for influence. The June Uprising of 1848, when the conservative majority dissolved the National Workshops supporting urban workers, triggered pitched street battles and a conservative backlash. The election of Prince Louis-Napoléon Bonaparte as president in December 1848 appeared to offer stability, but he represented a third force—neither traditional monarchy nor radical republic. His growing popularity with peasants and workers, combined with his famous name, made him a magnet for those exhausted by factional conflict. When parliament blocked his re-election in 1851, Louis-Napoléon orchestrated a coup d'état on 2 December 1851, arresting opposition leaders and dissolving the Assembly. A plebiscite overwhelmingly endorsed his seizure of power, confirming that many French people prioritised order over republicanism.\n\nThe Second Empire (1852–1870) under Napoléon III transformed the regime's character. Initially authoritarian, it combined dictatorial executive power with universal male suffrage and a legislature, creating what historians call 'Bonapartism'—populist authoritarianism justified by appeals to the people rather than tradition or constitutional law. The regime controlled elections and the press while offering economic development, military prestige, and national greatness. Urban modernisation under Baron Haussmann reshaped Paris with wide boulevards partly designed to facilitate military control. The Empire pursued nationalist foreign policy: intervention in Italy (1859) supported Italian unification while seeming to diminish French influence; the Mexican adventure (1861–1867) proved costly and humiliating; and the rise of Prussian power went ultimately unchecked.\n\nFrom 1860 onwards, Napoléon III liberalised the regime, allowing greater press freedom and parliamentary debate in response to opposition growth. The 1869 elections saw significant gains for Republicans despite official pressure. By 1870, the regime appeared potentially evolving toward constitutional monarchy. However, the Franco-Prussian War (1870) reversed this trajectory. Napoléon III's military gamble to prevent German unification resulted in catastrophic defeat at Sedan in September 1870. His capture, combined with the news from the front, triggered spontaneous revolution in Paris. Republicans proclaimed the Third Republic on 4 September 1870, ending the Empire without formal abdication. The regime collapse was less violent than in 1830 and 1848 but equally decisive.\n\nThese four regime changes reveal consistent underlying patterns. Each regime failed to reconcile irreconcilable forces: the claims of hereditary monarchy versus popular sovereignty; narrow oligarchic rule versus democratic representation; Catholic traditionalism versus secular nationalism. The Restoration and July Monarchy represented elite attempts to contain revolutionary change within frameworks protecting property and social hierarchy. Both were destroyed when excluded groups—workers, radical republicans, and portions of the middle class—mobilised through insurrection. The Second Republic briefly attempted to bridge these divides through universal suffrage but fragmented under pressure from class conflict. The Second Empire offered a novel solution: authoritarian rule legitimised by plebiscites and justified by nationalist achievement, combining universal male suffrage (which most excluded from real power) with executive dictatorship. Its fall came through military defeat rather than internal revolution, yet the speed of collapse showed its foundations were shallow.\n\nThe period 1815–1870 demonstrates that post-revolutionary France could neither restore absolute monarchy nor achieve stable liberal democracy. Each regime change reflected the unresolved tension between competing visions of French society and governance. The legacy of 1789 made pure reaction impossible; yet competing interests—monarchists, republicans, socialists, and Bonapartists—prevented consensus. Only after 1870, through the trials and crises of the Third Republic, would France eventually establish a durable parliamentary regime, though this too required decades of consolidation and remained contested.",
