        "label": "Q2",
        "question": "Why was the EEC established and how did it evolve during the period, 1957 – 1973?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "The European Economic Community was established in 1957 to promote economic integration and political stability in Western Europe following the devastation of the Second World War. Its creation reflected the determination of member states to prevent future conflict through economic interdependence and shared prosperity.\n\nThe primary motivation for the EEC's establishment was economic recovery and integration. The Marshall Plan (1948) had demonstrated the benefits of coordinated European reconstruction, creating momentum for deeper economic cooperation. The Schuman Declaration (1950) proposed pooling French and German coal and steel resources, leading to the European Coal and Steel Community (ECSC) in 1951. This success inspired the ambition to expand integration beyond coal and steel to all economic sectors. The six founding members—France, West Germany, Belgium, Netherlands, Luxembourg, and Italy—recognised that economic integration would bind their economies so tightly that war between them would become economically irrational.\n\nThe Treaty of Rome (1957) established the EEC with three main pillars: a customs union eliminating internal tariffs, a common agricultural policy, and the free movement of labour and capital between member states. The treaty aimed to create a common market of 180 million people, enhancing the economic power of Western Europe and providing collective security against Soviet expansion during the Cold War. The EEC represented a deliberate choice to tie West Germany firmly into the Western alliance, reassuring France through economic integration rather than traditional military containment.\n\nDuring the 1960s, the EEC underwent significant institutional development and policy refinement. The customs union was successfully established ahead of schedule by 1968, eliminating all internal tariffs between member states and establishing a common external tariff. This tariff wall protected member industries from external competition while creating a large internal market encouraging economies of scale. Trade between member states expanded substantially, demonstrating the economic benefits of integration.\n\nThe Common Agricultural Policy (CAP), adopted in 1962, became central to EEC operations though controversial. It guaranteed farmers fixed prices and subsidised exports, providing security for agricultural sectors across member states. However, the CAP proved expensive and protectionist, consuming increasing percentages of the EEC budget and creating tensions with external trading partners. France, under President Charles de Gaulle, insisted on the CAP as a condition for accepting industrial liberalisation favourable to Germany and the Benelux countries, using agriculture as leverage to protect French rural interests.\n\nDe Gaulle's presidency fundamentally shaped EEC evolution, often through confrontation. His 1963 veto of British membership reflected his vision of the EEC as an instrument of French power rather than supranationalism. He opposed what he termed the \"supranational\" pretensions of the European Commission, preferring to strengthen the Council of Ministers where national governments maintained control. The empty chair crisis (1965–1966) exemplified this tension: France boycotted EEC institutions in protest against proposals to strengthen the Commission's authority and reduce French voting power. The resulting Luxembourg Compromise (1966) established that vital national interests could be protected through the veto, effectively limiting supranational integration and preserving national sovereignty as paramount.\n\nThe 1960s also saw tensions over financing and institutional reform. Germany and other industrial states resented the financial burden of agricultural support. Disputes over the EEC budget and revenue sources reflected deeper conflicts between agricultural and industrial interests, and between national and supranational conceptions of European integration. De Gaulle's challenge to supranationalism proved enduring: the principle that national governments could exercise veto power over decisions affecting vital interests became embedded in the EEC's decision-making structure.\n\nThe period 1969–1973 marked renewed dynamism following de Gaulle's resignation in 1969. The Hague Summit (1969) reestablished momentum for enlargement and deepening integration. West Germany's Ostpolitik (opening to Eastern Europe) and France's more accommodating stance toward Britain created conditions for British, Irish, and Danish entry. The enlargement of 1973, admitting the United Kingdom, Ireland, and Denmark, expanded the EEC to nine members and reflected the community's attractiveness as a framework for Western European integration and security.\n\nThe Treaty of Brussels (1971) formalised the enlargement terms. Britain's entry after two previous rejections represented a significant development, though many British remained sceptical of European integration. The 1975 renegotiation of British membership terms and the subsequent referendum reflected the political difficulties of integrating an economically vulnerable nation with different imperial traditions and Atlantic allegiances into a continental community.\n\nBy 1973, the EEC had evolved from an experiment in pooling coal and steel into a comprehensive economic union encompassing nine diverse nations. The institutions—the European Commission (executive), Council of Ministers (representing member states), European Parliament (advisory), and European Court of Justice (interpreting law)—created a novel supranational structure, though national governments maintained ultimate authority through the Council and the veto principle. The EEC had successfully eliminated internal barriers to trade and begun ambitious policies in agriculture and regional development, demonstrating that sustained economic integration was achievable despite national differences. However, unresolved tensions over the extent of supranational authority, agricultural costs, and the pace of integration would shape debates in subsequent decades. The 1973 enlargement signified that the Western European integration process had become institutionally embedded and politically irreversible, though achieving consensus among diverse member states would prove increasingly complex as the community expanded.",
