        "marks": 30,
        "model": "The Gaelic League, founded in 1893 by Douglas Hyde and Eoin MacNeill, was a cultural nationalist organisation dedicated to the revival of Irish language, literature, and traditions. Unlike explicitly political movements, the League pursued cultural independence as a pathway to national consciousness. It established branches throughout Ireland, organising classes in Irish language instruction, promoting Gaelic games and music, and publishing scholarly works on Irish history and folklore. By 1904, membership had grown to over 600 branches, making it a mass movement by early twentieth-century standards. The League's emphasis on de-Anglicisation—the deliberate rejection of English cultural dominance—resonated with middle-class intellectuals, clergy, and teachers. However, the organisation maintained official political neutrality, which limited its direct influence on constitutional nationalism. Nevertheless, the League's cultural work laid intellectual foundations for later political movements; many who joined for cultural reasons became involved in political nationalism. The revival of Irish as a unifying national symbol strengthened collective identity independent of political party allegiance. By 1914, the League's emphasis on cultural distinctiveness had helped establish Irish language and traditions as central to national identity, contributing significantly to the broader context of nationalist mobilisation in the pre-independence period.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
