        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Sir Basil Brooke (Lord Brookeborough) dominated Northern Irish unionist politics as Prime Minister from 1943 to 1963. His tenure exemplified hard-line unionism and Protestant supremacy. Brooke explicitly warned against employing Catholics, famously stating that he had not one Catholic in his employment, reflecting and reinforcing sectarian discrimination that became structural in Northern Ireland. His government systematised gerrymandering, particularly in Londonderry and Armagh, ensuring Protestant-unionist majorities despite Catholic population growth. Brooke resisted any reform of the discriminatory Local Government Act 1898, which excluded most Catholics from local government franchise based on property qualifications. He maintained the Special Constabulary (B-Specials), an almost exclusively Protestant force that Catholics viewed as sectarian militia. Under Brooke, welfare benefits were distributed unequally, with Catholic-majority areas receiving proportionally less investment in housing, education, and employment. His government's refusal to investigate police brutality during rioting consolidated Catholic alienation. Brooke's political philosophy—that unionism and Protestantism were inseparable, and that Catholic rights threatened the state—institutionalised the two-community divide. By the 1960s, his resistance to any accommodation had created deep grievances that erupted in civil rights agitation after 1968. Brooke's legacy was a state structured around Protestant dominance, which generated the conditions for the Troubles.",
