    "year": 2008,
    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "a",
        "question": "Write a short paragraph on one of the following:\n1. Industrialisation in Germany.\n2. Serbia, 1871-1914.\n3. Developments in science, technology and medicine, 1871-1920.\n4. Woodrow Wilson.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Germany's industrialisation between 1871 and 1920 transformed it into Europe's dominant economic and military power. Following unification in 1871, Germany possessed rich coal and iron deposits in the Ruhr and Silesia, which fuelled rapid industrial expansion. Under Bismarck's protective tariff policies and state investment in railways and infrastructure, German industry grew dramatically. By 1900, Germany had overtaken Britain in steel production and chemical manufacturing, becoming Europe's leading industrial nation. The state actively supported industrial development through subsidies and technical education, creating a highly efficient industrial base. This economic strength directly underpinned German military power: the General Staff could mobilise vast resources for weapons production and naval expansion under Admiral Tirpitz's shipbuilding programme. However, Germany's dependence on imported raw materials and food created economic vulnerabilities, driving aggressive foreign policy to secure colonies and markets. Industrialisation also created a large urban working class, generating socialist movements that challenged traditional authority. By 1914, Germany's industrial capacity made it a formidable military power, but also created tensions with Britain and France over trade and colonial competition. The First World War demonstrated how industrial capacity translated into military output: Germany produced enormous quantities of artillery, ammunition, and warships. However, the British naval blockade exposed Germany's strategic weakness—dependence on overseas supplies. Industrialisation thus both enabled and complicated Germany's rise as a great power.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
