        "question": "To what extent was O'Connell's leadership responsible both for the success of the Emancipation campaign and the failure of the Repeal campaign?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "O'Connell's leadership was central to Catholic Emancipation's success but his methods ultimately undermined the Repeal campaign, though structural factors also determined both outcomes.\n\nO'Connell's exceptional organizational genius delivered Emancipation (1829). He mobilized the Catholic masses through the Catholic Association, transforming Irish politics from elite faction to popular movement. The Association's penny subscription scheme created unprecedented organizational reach, converting peasant grievance into political power. O'Connell's personal charisma—his oratory, legal expertise, and constitutional respectability—made Catholic claims credible to British moderates and persuadable MPs. His strategic use of the Clare election (1828), where he stood as a Catholic candidate knowing his election would force the Emancipation issue, demonstrated masterful political timing. The Wellington government conceded rather than face civil unrest, validating O'Connell's mass mobilization strategy.\n\nHowever, the very methods that won Emancipation contained seeds of Repeal's failure. O'Connell's commitment to constitutional, lawful agitation meant he rejected revolutionary force. This won British Liberal support for Emancipation but left him dependent on English parliamentary majorities for Repeal—a dependency that proved fatal. After 1829, O'Connell moderated his rhetoric, accepted a seat in parliament as MP, and became integrated into the British political establishment. His 1834 alliance with the Whigs and acceptance of the Lichfield House Compact meant he prioritized incremental reforms over Repeal. By the 1840s, his dominance faced challenge from younger, more radical nationalists like Thomas Davis and the Young Ireland movement, who viewed O'Connell's constitutionalism as insufficient.\n\nMoreover, structural factors limited Repeal's prospects regardless of leadership. The Act of Union (1800) had created integrated economic and political structures; repeal would have required English consent, which was never forthcoming. The Protestant Ascendancy, Irish landlords, and British commercial interests all benefited from Union. O'Connell's mass movement, which worked brilliantly for a single, achievable legal reform, struggled to sustain momentum for the indefinite struggle Repeal required. The Great Famine (1845-1849) devastated O'Connell's movement: he died in 1847, and mass emigration depleted his organizational base. The Young Ireland rebellion of 1848 demonstrated that radicalization, not O'Connell's constitutionalism, now drove nationalist sentiment.\n\nO'Connell's leadership was thus decisively responsible for Emancipation's success: his organizational innovation, strategic brilliance, and ability to command mass support made the difference. For Repeal's failure, his leadership bore significant responsibility through his constitutional moderation and post-1829 political compromises. Yet British political unwillingness and structural integration into the Union also made Repeal nearly impossible. O'Connell's greatest strength—his ability to mobilize peaceful, constitutional mass politics—proved insufficient for a goal that required either revolutionary action (which he rejected) or English consent (which was never available). His leadership thus succeeded brilliantly within the bounds of the possible but could not transcend those bounds.",
