        "question": "During the period 1870–1914, what was the contribution to Irish affairs of one or more of the following: the Suffrage movement; the first Sinn Féin party; the Irish Volunteers?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "The Suffrage movement, the first Sinn Féin party, and the Irish Volunteers each made significant contributions to Irish affairs between 1870 and 1914, reshaping political consciousness and challenging British authority.\n\nThe Suffrage movement emerged as a powerful force for women's rights and broader social reform. Irish suffragists, particularly those organised under the Irish Women's Franchise League (founded 1908) and other groups, campaigned vigorously for women's voting rights. Leaders such as Hanna Sheehy-Skeffington and Frances O'Flaherty brought the issue to public attention through petitions, public meetings, and deputations to politicians. The movement challenged both British and Irish patriarchal assumptions about women's political participation. While immediate success was denied by the outbreak of World War I in 1914, the Suffrage movement had succeeded in making women's political rights a serious component of Irish public debate and had demonstrated women's capacity for political organisation and activism. Significantly, many suffragists later played prominent roles in the wider independence movement, linking gender equality with national self-determination.\n\nSinn Féin, founded by Arthur Griffith in 1905, offered a radically new vision of Irish independence based on cultural and economic self-reliance rather than constitutional agitation at Westminster. Griffith's policy of abstentionism—rejecting participation in the British Parliament—represented a fundamental departure from the Home Rule strategy that had dominated Irish nationalism since Parnell. Sinn Féin advocated the establishment of an Irish Parliament in Dublin, the withdrawal of Irish MPs from Westminster, and the development of Irish industry and culture independently of Britain. The party promoted the Irish language and Irish manufactures as practical tools of national assertion. Although Sinn Féin achieved limited electoral success before 1914, winning only a handful of seats, its intellectual influence was substantial. It attracted intellectuals, journalists, and activists frustrated with the slow progress of constitutional Home Rule and offered them a concrete alternative programme. Sinn Féin's emphasis on self-reliance and separatism planted ideological seeds that would bear fruit after 1916, making it a crucial bridge between constitutional nationalism and revolutionary republicanism.\n\nThe Irish Volunteers, established in November 1913, represented a dramatic militarisation of Irish nationalist sentiment. Founded in response to the formation of the Ulster Volunteer Force (created to resist Home Rule), the Irish Volunteers rapidly enrolled over 180,000 members by 1914 under the nominal leadership of Eoin MacNeill. The movement appealed to young Irishmen seeking active participation in the nationalist cause and provided military organisation and training. The Volunteers embodied nationalist determination to defend Home Rule through force if necessary and asserted the principle that Irish nationalists, not English politicians, should control Ireland's destiny. The arming and drilling of large numbers of Irishmen fundamentally altered the political atmosphere, demonstrating that substantial sections of the Irish population were willing to resort to armed resistance. Although the Volunteers remained largely passive during the 1913–1914 period, their existence created a latent military capacity that would be mobilised by radical republicans after the outbreak of World War I. The Gunrunning at Howth (July 1914), when Volunteers obtained rifles, symbolised their determination to maintain an armed force.\n\nThese three movements, while distinct in character and immediate objectives, shared a common impact: they broadened the base of Irish political engagement, introduced new ideas and methods, and demonstrated the limitations of existing constitutional approaches. The Suffrage movement expanded political participation beyond traditional male nationalist elites and raised fundamental questions about democratic representation. Sinn Féin offered a coherent intellectual alternative to Home Rule constitutionalism and injected the concept of separatism into mainstream political discourse. The Irish Volunteers provided the organised military capacity that would define Irish politics after 1916.\n\nBetween 1870 and 1914, these movements collectively transformed Irish political culture from one dominated by parliamentary constitutionalism and the pursuit of Home Rule through Westminster towards one incorporating suffragist activism, separatist ideology, and armed nationalism. While none achieved its primary objective before 1914—women did not gain the vote, Sinn Féin did not establish an Irish Parliament, and the Volunteers did not defend Home Rule through armed conflict—each established frameworks and mobilised constituencies that would prove decisive in the revolutionary transformation of Irish politics after 1916. They represented the emergence of new political voices and methods that challenged British authority and the established Home Rule consensus, making the period 1870–1914 one of fundamental realignment in Irish nationalist thinking and action.",
