        "question": "To what extent did Terence O'Neill differ from Lord Brookeborough as leader of Northern Ireland?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Terence O'Neill and Lord Brookeborough differed significantly as leaders of Northern Ireland, though both remained fundamentally committed to the Union and unionist supremacy. Their differences lay primarily in style, economic policy, and approach to community relations.\n\nBrookeborough (1943-1963) embodied traditional Ulster unionism. He maintained strict sectarian control through gerrymandering, discriminatory housing allocation, and employment practices that systematically disadvantaged Catholics. His government pursued protectionist economic policies and resisted modernisation. Brookeborough showed little interest in Catholic grievances and operated on the principle of permanent unionist dominance. His approach was defensive and inward-looking, exemplified by his 1934 statement discouraging Catholics from working in the civil service.\n\nO'Neill (1963-1969) presented himself as a moderniser. He advocated economic development and attempted to attract foreign investment through the Lockwood Plan and Matthew Plan, seeking to transform Northern Ireland's industrial base. More significantly, O'Neill pursued what he termed \"bridge-building\" with the Catholic community. He met Seán Lemass, the Irish Taoiseach, in 1965—a symbolic gesture unthinkable under Brookeborough. O'Neill made conciliatory speeches, visited Catholic schools, and attempted to project an image of a progressive, forward-looking leader concerned with economic rather than sectarian issues.\n\nHowever, these differences must be contextualised carefully. O'Neill's modernisation was fundamentally limited. While he invested in infrastructure and industry, these developments disproportionately benefited Protestant areas and businesses. His \"bridge-building\" was carefully controlled rhetoric rather than substantive reform. Crucially, O'Neill did not dismantle the discriminatory structures inherited from Brookeborough. Gerrymandering, unequal housing allocation, and employment discrimination persisted. O'Neill's meeting with Lemass, though symbolically important, produced no concrete agreements on cross-border cooperation or minority rights.\n\nBoth leaders operated within the same constitutional framework: defence of the Union and unionist political supremacy. Neither questioned the legitimacy of partition or contemplated power-sharing. Both maintained that unionist interests must be protected against what they perceived as nationalist threats. The fundamental difference was one of method rather than ultimate objective. Brookeborough believed unionism could be sustained through explicit sectarian control; O'Neill believed it could be secured through economic modernisation and carefully managed Catholic accommodation.\n\nO'Neill's limitations became apparent during the civil rights crisis (1968-1969). His reforms were insufficient to satisfy Catholic demands for equality, yet alienated hardline unionists who saw even modest concessions as betrayal. When O'Neill announced limited reforms—including a points system for housing allocation and one-man-one-vote in local elections—hardline unionists, including his own backbenchers, opposed them. O'Neill's position became untenable. By 1969, he resigned, having failed to bridge the fundamental divide in Northern Irish society or to reform the structural discrimination that defined the state since partition.\n\nIn conclusion, O'Neill differed from Brookeborough primarily in recognising that unionism's long-term viability required adaptation to modern economic conditions and selective accommodation of Catholic concerns. However, these differences were matters of degree rather than principle. Both leaders ultimately prioritised unionist political dominance. O'Neill's modernisation was superficial; his bridge-building lacked substance. He failed to address the root causes of Catholic alienation—systematic discrimination embedded in the state's institutions. Consequently, his tenure represented not a fundamental shift in unionist politics but rather a failed attempt at modernisation within parameters that remained essentially unchanged from Brookeborough's era. The civil rights crisis demonstrated that cosmetic reforms and rhetorical gestures could not resolve the deep structural inequalities that defined Northern Ireland under both leaders.",
