    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "a",
        "question": "How did the Cold War develop in Europe and how did it end?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "The Cold War in Europe developed from the breakdown of the wartime alliance between the Soviet Union and the Western powers following World War II. At the Yalta Conference (February 1945) and Potsdam Conference (July-August 1945), tensions emerged over the future of Eastern Europe and Germany. Stalin sought to establish Soviet dominance in Eastern Europe as a security buffer; the Western Allies, particularly the USA, increasingly viewed this as aggressive expansion of communism.\n\nThe immediate post-war period witnessed Soviet consolidation of control over Eastern Europe. In Poland, the USSR installed a communist government despite promises of free elections. By 1946-1947, communist parties took power in Hungary, Romania, Bulgaria, and Czechoslovakia, often through rigged elections or Soviet military pressure. Britain's wartime leader Churchill warned of an 'Iron Curtain' descending across Europe in his March 1946 Fulton speech, capturing Western alarm at Soviet actions.\n\nUS policy hardened in response. The Truman Doctrine (March 1947) committed America to containing communist expansion globally. The Marshall Plan (1948) offered economic aid to rebuild Western Europe, explicitly to prevent communist parties from gaining support among economically desperate populations. This was rejected by Soviet-dominated Eastern Europe, deepening the division. By 1948, Europe was clearly divided into two competing blocs.\n\nGermany became the focal point of confrontation. The USSR blockaded West Berlin (1948-1949), attempting to starve the Western sectors into submission. The Western Allies responded with the Berlin Airlift, supplying two million people by air for nearly a year, demonstrating Western resolve. The blockade's failure marked a Soviet setback and hardened Cold War divisions. Germany was formally partitioned: the Federal Republic of Germany (West Germany) was established in 1949, aligned with the West; the German Democratic Republic (East Germany) followed, firmly in the Soviet sphere.\n\nThe consolidation of opposing alliances institutionalized Cold War division. NATO was established in April 1949, binding Western European states and North America in a military alliance against Soviet expansion. The USSR responded by forming the Warsaw Pact in 1955, creating a parallel military structure controlling Eastern European satellites. These alliances embodied the bipolar structure of Cold War Europe: two mutually hostile systems, separated by ideological, political, and military divides.\n\nThe 1950s and early 1960s saw periodic crises reinforcing Cold War rigidity. The Hungarian Uprising (1956) demonstrated Soviet determination to maintain control: when Hungarians attempted reform and potential withdrawal from the Warsaw Pact, Soviet tanks rolled in, crushing the revolt and killing thousands. This brutal suppression showed that the Soviet sphere was not negotiable. The Berlin Wall (August 1961) symbolized Cold War division—East Germany built it to prevent its population fleeing to the West, creating a concrete barrier dividing the city and Europe.\n\nFrom the mid-1960s, a period of détente began, though Cold War structures persisted. Both superpowers, exhausted by arms racing and concerned about nuclear war, sought accommodation. The Partial Test Ban Treaty (1963) and Strategic Arms Limitation Talks (SALT) represented attempts to manage rather than end the Cold War. However, underlying tensions remained: the Soviet invasion of Czechoslovakia (1968) to crush reformist communism demonstrated continuing Soviet dominance of Eastern Europe, and the arms race continued despite rhetoric of relaxation.\n\nThe Cold War's end came suddenly in 1989-1991, following the Soviet Union's internal collapse. Mikhail Gorbachev's policies of glasnost (openness) and perestroika (restructuring) from 1985 onward weakened Soviet control. Crucially, Gorbachev signalled that the USSR would not use force to maintain communist regimes in Eastern Europe, reversing the Brezhnev Doctrine which had justified intervention in Hungary and Czechoslovakia.\n\nThis signal unleashed a cascade of change. In Poland, the Solidarity movement, which had challenged communist rule since 1980, gained momentum; by 1989, Poland held semi-free elections and a non-communist government was formed. Hungary opened its border with Austria in September 1989, allowing East Germans to flee West. The Berlin Wall fell in November 1989 as East German authorities, facing popular pressure and lacking Soviet support, ceased enforcing border controls. Across Eastern Europe—Czechoslovakia, Romania, Bulgaria—communist regimes collapsed between late 1989 and 1990, often peacefully (the Velvet Revolution in Czechoslovakia), sometimes violently (Romania's execution of Ceaușescu).\n\nGermany reunified in October 1990, with the former East Germany absorbed into the Federal Republic. This would have been unthinkable during the Cold War; its achievement symbolized the conflict's end. The Soviet Union itself dissolved in December 1991, replaced by the Commonwealth of Independent States. Russia, now under Boris Yeltsin and weakened economically and militarily, ceased to be a superpower capable of sustaining Cold War competition.\n\nNATO expanded eastward after 1991, incorporating former Warsaw Pact members and Soviet republics—Poland, Hungary, Czech Republic, and others joined by the early 2000s. This expansion reflected the West's victory and Russia's continued weakness, though it generated lasting resentment in Moscow.\n\nThe Cold War's development in Europe stemmed from incompatible Soviet and Western interests in post-1945 Europe: Soviet security concerns and communist ideology clashed with Western capitalism and democracy. It institutionalized in competing alliances, military buildups, and ideological confrontation. Its end resulted not from military defeat but from the internal collapse of the Soviet system, which could no longer sustain either its economy or its control over Eastern European satellites. The sudden, dramatic collapse of communist regimes in 1989-1991, following Gorbachev's decision not to intervene militarily, ended the division that had defined European politics for forty years. Europe's subsequent reunification and integration into Western institutions reflected the conclusive Western victory.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
