        "question": "Why did Katanga secede from the Congo and why did the secession bring about international involvement?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Katanga's secession from the Congo in July 1960 resulted from deep economic, political, and ethnic divisions that intensified following Belgian withdrawal. The province, representing about half of Congo's wealth and containing vast mineral deposits—particularly copper, cobalt, and uranium—possessed distinct economic interests from the impoverished rest of the country. Under Belgian rule, Katanga had been developed as a separate entity with privileged access to resources and better infrastructure. The Association Kasai (CONAKAT) party, led by Moïse Tshombe, mobilized Katangan regional consciousness and opposed domination by the Lumumbist central government, which threatened foreign investor interests and mining company operations, particularly the Belgian-controlled Union Minière. Katanga's white settler and Belgian business communities saw secession as essential to protect their economic interests against nationalist expropriation.\n\nTshombe declared Katanga independent on 11 August 1960, just weeks after Congo's independence (30 June 1960). This occurred amidst the Congo Crisis—a broader collapse of state authority following the immediate mutiny of the Force Publique, the flight of Belgian settlers, and Prime Minister Patrice Lumumba's radical pan-African nationalism. Katanga's secession directly challenged UN-supported moves toward a unified, centralized Congo under Congolese control. The wealthy mining province's departure threatened the viability of the entire Congolese state and deprived it of crucial revenue.\n\nInternational involvement stemmed from Cold War competition and economic interests. The UN Security Council authorized an unprecedented peacekeeping force (ONUC) to restore order and preserve Congo's territorial integrity. However, Cold War divisions complicated the response. The Soviet Union supported Lumumba's central government and opposed secession as a neo-colonial plot. The United States and Western powers initially tacitly supported Tshombe's Katanga as a bulwark against communism and to protect Western mining interests; they feared that a unified Congo under Lumumba would shift toward the Soviet bloc. Belgium, the former colonial power, maintained significant military and economic presence and supported Tshombe's government, sending officers and military advisers to Katanga and allowing Belgian mercenaries to operate there.\n\nUN Secretary-General Dag Hammarskjöld deployed ONUC forces to restore central authority, but Western powers resisted military action against Katanga. Following Lumumba's deposition and murder (January 1961), Soviet support for the Congo diminished, but the Katanga issue persisted. UN military operations against Katanga (Operation Morthor, 1961) failed initially due to lack of clear mandates and Belgian-mercenary resistance. Only after Hammarskjöld's death and renewed UN determination did intensive military operations in late 1962-early 1963 finally force Katanga's reintegration under UN military pressure, demonstrations of UN resolve, and the threat of complete economic isolation. Katanga's reabsorption into Congo by January 1963 was secured by international force, reflecting the UN's commitment to preserving post-colonial territorial integrity against secessionist challenges, even as Cold War interests and economic motivations shaped the pace and intensity of that commitment.",
