        "marks": 30,
        "model": "Robert Peel (1788–1850) was a dominant figure in British politics whose career reflected evolving attitudes to conservatism and reform during 1815–1871. Initially a strict conservative, Peel served as Home Secretary (1822–1827) under Lord Liverpool, where he modernised the police force and rationalised the criminal code—establishing the Metropolitan Police in 1829. His premiership (1834–1835, 1841–1846) marked a pragmatic shift toward moderate reform. Most significantly, Peel repealed the Corn Laws in 1846, a protectionist measure that had kept grain prices artificially high. Though this decision split the Conservative Party and ended his political career, it reflected his belief that free trade and reducing food prices served the national interest. His conversion to Catholic emancipation in 1829, though initially resisted by ultra-conservatives, demonstrated his willingness to accept political reality over rigid ideology. Peel's legacy lay in redefining conservatism as compatible with cautious reform and free-market economics, establishing a template for later moderate conservatism. His fall from power in 1846 occurred because hardline protectionists viewed his pragmatism as betrayal, yet his policies ultimately proved influential in shaping mid-Victorian Britain's industrial and commercial development.",
