        "label": "B",
        "question": "Write a short paragraph on one of the following:\n1. The Third French Republic.\n2. The technology of warfare.\n3. The \"Home Front\" in Britain during World War II.\n4. Bing Crosby's contribution to radio and cinema.",
        "marks": 30,
        "model": "The 'Home Front' in Britain during World War II\n\nBritain's Home Front transformed civilian life fundamentally between 1939 and 1945. The government implemented comprehensive controls: rationing of food, fuel, and clothing; evacuation of children from cities to safer rural areas; blackout regulations and air-raid precautions; and conscription of women into auxiliary services and munitions factories. The Blitz (1940–1941) saw sustained German bombing campaigns targeting London, Coventry, and industrial centres, killing over 40,000 civilians and destroying homes across the country. In response, the government mobilized the entire population: Anderson shelters were distributed, the Home Guard was formed for civil defence, and gas masks were issued. Women played unprecedented roles—working in factories, serving in the Women's Auxiliary Air Force, and managing households under severe rationing. The rationing system, while austere, was designed equitably to ensure fair distribution. Morale fluctuated but remained resilient, particularly under Churchill's leadership. The government maintained social cohesion through propaganda, appeals to duty, and measures like the Education Act (1944), which promised post-war social reform. Food production was intensified through 'Dig for Victory' campaigns. By war's end, the Home Front had demonstrated the capacity of the British people to endure prolonged hardship and contributed materially to eventual victory, establishing expectations for a welfare state that manifested in the post-war Labour government's reforms.",
