    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "a",
        "question": "Would you agree that Ireland experienced a decade of revolutionary change, 1912-1922?\nGive reasons for your answer.",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Ireland did experience a decade of revolutionary change between 1912 and 1922, though the nature and extent of that change was uneven and contested.\n\nThe case for revolutionary change is substantial. In 1912, Home Rule was a constitutional aspiration within the union. By 1922, an independent Irish Free State existed, sovereign in domestic affairs. This represents fundamental transformation. The Easter Rising of 1916, though militarily defeated, shifted the political landscape decisively. It delegitimised the Irish Parliamentary Party, which had pursued Home Rule constitutionally for 30 years, and elevated Sinn Féin—which won 73 of 105 seats in the 1918 general election—as the authentic voice of Irish nationalism. The Rising's ideological impact exceeded its military scale: it reframed independence as a moral imperative rooted in Irish republicanism, not merely a practical arrangement. The subsequent War of Independence (1919-1921) was genuinely revolutionary in character. The IRA, under Michael Collins's leadership, pioneered guerrilla warfare, undermining British authority systematically. By 1921, British intelligence networks in Ireland had been devastated; Crown forces operated in a hostile environment. This forced Britain to negotiate the Anglo-Irish Treaty, which fundamentally altered the constitutional relationship. Ireland moved from subordinate union to dominion status with real autonomy.\n\nThe social and political upheaval was profound. The old Home Rule consensus, built by Parnell and Redmond, collapsed. Sinn Féin's ideology of self-determination and republicanism replaced the moderate constitutionalism that had dominated Irish nationalism since 1886. The Catholic Church, which had supported Home Rule cautiously, adjusted to the new reality. Women's activism—visible in Cumann na mBan and broader republican mobilisation—challenged Victorian political conventions, though independence did not immediately translate to suffrage equality. The property and land question, which had driven Irish politics for decades, was superseded by the sovereignty question. British landlords' power in Ireland was effectively ended. Control passed to an Irish state, eventually implementing land reform.\n\nHowever, the character of change requires qualification. The revolution was incomplete and internally divided. The Treaty, signed in December 1921, did not deliver a fully independent republic. It created a dominion within the Commonwealth, retaining the monarch as head of state and requiring an oath of allegiance. This compromise split the independence movement: anti-Treaty republicans rejected it as betrayal, leading to the Civil War (1922-1923). The revolution thus turned inward, with Irish fighting Irish. Collins, who had orchestrated the guerrilla campaign, was killed in August 1922 by anti-Treaty forces.\n\nMoreover, the revolution's social radicalism was limited. The 1916 Rising's proclamation promised democratic participation, social justice, and equal rights. The Free State that emerged was conservative: dominated by former guerrillas who became a new establishment, aligned with the Catholic hierarchy, hostile to labour radicalism and women's equality. The 1922 Constitution reflected this conservatism. Land redistribution, though extensive, did not fundamentally restructure rural society. Industrial workers' aspirations—evident in the 1913 Lockout and wartime strikes—received little satisfaction. Women lost ground: excluded from juries, limited in divorce rights, facing employment discrimination. The revolution prioritised national independence over social transformation.\n\nGeographically, the revolution was incomplete. Partition, enacted in 1921 despite nationalist opposition, divided the island. Northern Ireland remained within the union, controlled by a Protestant-dominated Unionist majority. This was not revolutionary change in that territory; it was counter-revolutionary, preserving the union for six counties. Nationalists in the north experienced it as imposed subordination. The Free State's jurisdiction covered 26 counties, not the whole island. De Valera's return to power in 1932 and his Constitution of 1937—which declared the Irish Free State a sovereign republic in fact, though it retained Commonwealth association until 1949—represented a second phase of revolutionary assertion, but this lies outside the 1912-1922 frame.\n\nThe continuities alongside change merit emphasis. The civil service, judiciary, and police inherited from British administration largely remained in structure and personnel. The Irish Defence Forces evolved from the IRA but adopted conventional military organisation. The Catholic Church's influence actually strengthened in the new state. Banking, commerce, and professional classes adapted to the new political order without fundamental restructuring. The Anglo-Irish aristocracy lost political power but retained considerable land and social status initially. Revolutionary rhetoric often masked reformed continuity.\n\nYet the period 1912-1922 was unquestionably transformative in its historical significance. In 1912, Irish independence was a minority vision; by 1922, it was realised institutionally, however imperfectly. The political culture shifted from constitutionalism and compromise to republicanism and direct action. Violence became embedded in the nationalist repertoire in ways it had not been since the 1860s. The relationship between Ireland and Britain moved from legislative union to treaty relationship. British military withdrawal was real and permanent. The legitimacy of the Westminster parliament in Ireland was shattered. These are revolutionary changes.\n\nIn conclusion, Ireland experienced revolutionary change in this decade, but with significant reservations. The change was structural and political rather than profoundly social or economic. It was incomplete geographically, contested internally, and compromised by partition and civil war. Yet measured against 1912—when Home Rule still seemed the nationalist horizon and constitutional politics dominated—the shift to independent statehood, however flawed, constituted revolutionary transformation. The revolution achieved its primary objective: Irish separation from British rule. That it did not achieve all its secondary objectives reflects the complexity of revolutionary change in practice.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
