    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "a",
        "question": "During the years 1920-1945, how well did the government of Northern Ireland deal with social and economic problems and/or threats to security?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Between 1920 and 1945, the Northern Irish government faced substantial social, economic, and security challenges, but responded inconsistently—achieving relative stability and modernisation in certain areas while neglecting fundamental inequalities and sectarian division that would generate long-term instability.\n\nEconomically, Northern Ireland inherited serious structural problems. The pre-partition Irish economy relied heavily on agriculture and traditional industries; the North's industrial base, concentrated in shipbuilding (Harland and Wolff in Belfast) and linen manufacture, was vulnerable to post-war decline. The 1920s and 1930s brought severe depression. Unemployment reached catastrophic levels—over 27% by 1930—far exceeding the UK average. The government's response was limited. While it benefited from the British welfare state extension (unemployment insurance, old-age pensions), these measures were inadequate given local industrial collapse. The linen industry, employing 100,000 at its peak, contracted sharply. Shipbuilding fluctuated with international demand. The government did little to diversify the economy or attract new manufacturing; industrial policy remained passive. By 1939, economic conditions had improved marginally but Northern Ireland remained significantly poorer than Britain, with persistent structural unemployment. The government's approach—relying on British subvention and welfare payments—stabilised living standards but failed to address underlying economic weakness.\n\nSocially, the government inherited a deeply divided community. Catholics comprised approximately one-third of the population; Protestants, the majority. From 1920, the Stormont government (established under the Government of Ireland Act 1920) was dominated by the Ulster Unionist Party, which governed continuously until 1972. Housing was severely inadequate, particularly in Belfast's working-class areas where both communities lived in overcrowded, insanitary conditions. The government's response was limited. While some housing was constructed, particularly post-1945, programmes remained underfunded and slow. Education revealed stark sectarian division: the government supported segregated schooling through state funding of Protestant schools while Catholics relied on church-funded schools, receiving less support. The 1930 Education Act failed to address this inequality. Public employment systematically discriminated against Catholics; the civil service, police, and local government positions overwhelmingly favoured Protestants. Employment in private industry, particularly shipbuilding, was also sectarian. The government did not intervene to prevent such discrimination. Consequently, Catholic unemployment rates significantly exceeded Protestant rates throughout the period. The government's inaction on social inequality—housing, education, employment—meant that sectarian grievances deepened rather than diminished.\n\nOn security, the government faced challenges from Irish republican activity and communal tension. The Anglo-Irish Treaty (1921) partitioned Ireland; the Irish Free State (later Republic) rejected partition, and republicans viewed Northern Ireland as illegitimate. The IRA conducted sporadic attacks in the North during the 1920s, most seriously during 1921-1922. The government's response was severe: it recruited the Special Constabulary (part-time police), predominantly Protestant, which used harsh methods against suspected republicans and Catholic civilians. Sectarian violence escalated, particularly in Belfast (1920-1922), where communal rioting killed over 500 people. The government's reliance on a sectarian security force exacerbated rather than resolved communal tensions. By the 1930s, IRA activity declined, but communal suspicion remained. During World War II, the government maintained tight security; the IRA's campaign (1939-1940s) was largely ineffective in the North. The government's security policy—using force, internment, and a partisan police force—achieved short-term suppression of republicanism but failed to build cross-community consensus or legitimacy.\n\nThe government did achieve some stability. Stormont provided institutional continuity; elections (though restricted before 1928 by property qualifications) allowed democratic participation. The extension of the British welfare state from the 1930s provided material security for many. By 1945, compared to the chaos of 1920-1922, Northern Ireland was more orderly. However, this stability rested on Unionist political dominance and sectarian division rather than genuine social integration or economic prosperity.\n\nConclusion: The Northern Irish government between 1920 and 1945 dealt inadequately with the fundamental challenges it faced. Economically, it failed to prevent severe depression or modernise the industrial base; reliance on British welfare masked structural weakness. Socially, it did nothing to address sectarian discrimination in housing, education, and employment, thereby cementing Catholic alienation and grievance. On security, it suppressed republican activity but used methods (the Special Constabulary) that deepened sectarian division. The government's record was primarily one of managing Unionist interests and maintaining Protestant political supremacy rather than addressing the underlying social, economic, and communal problems that confronted Northern Ireland. This failure to tackle inequality and build inclusive institutions created instability that would erupt in subsequent decades.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
