    "parts": [
      {
        "label": "a",
        "question": "What was the importance of Grosse Isle in the history of Irish emigration?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Grosse Isle's importance in Irish emigration history lies in its role as both a quarantine station and a symbol of the catastrophic human cost of the Great Famine emigration (1845–1851). Located in the St Lawrence River near Quebec, the island processed over 100,000 Irish emigrants, but became a death trap where thousands perished from typhus, dysentery and cholera, earning it the grim epithet 'island of the dead'.\n\nThe island's strategic position made it the primary quarantine facility for ships arriving in North America. Irish emigrants, fleeing starvation during the Famine, embarked on overcrowded vessels—the so-called 'coffin ships'—in which disease spread rapidly. Upon arrival at Grosse Isle between 1846 and 1849, sick passengers were forcibly quarantined. However, overcrowding, inadequate medical facilities, insufficient food and unsanitary conditions meant the island itself became a breeding ground for disease. In 1847 alone, known as 'Black '47', approximately 5,424 Irish died on the island, with estimates suggesting the actual death toll may have reached 6,000. This single year accounted for roughly one-third of the total Irish deaths at Grosse Isle across the entire Famine period. The island's burial pits, where victims were interred in mass graves, became stark physical reminders of this tragedy.\n\nGrosse Isle's importance extends beyond mortality statistics to how it exposed the vulnerability of Irish emigrants and the inadequacy of state provision. Irish emigrants were often destitute, having sold everything to afford passage. Once on the island, they had no resources to secure proper food or shelter beyond what authorities—themselves overwhelmed—could provide. The Canadian authorities, unprepared for the scale of Irish arrival, struggled to manage sanitation, medical care and burial. The island thus exemplified how Irish emigration, though presenting an opportunity for escape, came at devastating human cost, particularly for the poorest Famine refugees.\n\nThe island also became symbolically significant in Irish and Irish-American memory. For Irish emigrants who survived Grosse Isle, it represented a threshold between death and survival, between the desperation of Famine Ireland and the promise—however uncertain—of America. Many who landed at Grosse Isle went on to establish Irish-American communities, carrying with them traumatic memories of the quarantine station. This collective memory shaped Irish-American identity and remained part of Irish diaspora consciousness long after the Famine ended. Monuments erected on the island in the late 20th century, including the Irish Famine Memorial, formally acknowledged the historical significance of Grosse Isle as a site of mass suffering and death.\n\nFurthermore, Grosse Isle is significant for illuminating the social conditions that drove Irish emigration. The fact that so many Irish were willing to endure such horrific conditions—disease, death, and unknown futures—demonstrates the absolute desperation induced by the Famine. It also highlights how class intersected with emigration: wealthier Irish could afford better ships and medical care, whilst the poorest were crowded into the worst vessels, making them far more likely to contract disease during the Atlantic crossing or at quarantine.\n\nThe island also represents a turning point in North American immigration policy. The catastrophic scenes at Grosse Isle, combined with similar chaos at other ports, eventually prompted governments to introduce stricter regulations on ship conditions and passenger density, though such measures came too late to protect the bulk of Famine emigrants. Thus Grosse Isle serves as evidence of how emigration policy was shaped by the historical experience of Irish arrival.\n\nIn conclusion, Grosse Isle's importance in Irish emigration history is multifaceted: it was the literal gateway through which over 100,000 Irish passed; it was the scene of mass death on a scale that defined the Famine emigration experience; it exposed structural inadequacies in how states managed emigration; and it became a symbol of Irish suffering that endured in diasporic memory and shaped subsequent Irish-American identity. Without understanding Grosse Isle, the history of Irish emigration cannot be fully grasped, as the island encapsulates both the scale of Irish departure and the human tragedy embedded within it.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
    ]
