        "question": "Why did attempts to unite Italy after 1852 succeed when earlier attempts had failed?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Earlier unification attempts failed due to structural weaknesses, external obstacles, and lack of sustained powerful support. The 1848-49 revolutions demonstrated these fatal gaps: Piedmont's military defeats by Austria in 1848-49 revealed inadequate military capacity; the papacy's refusal to lead a unified Italy (Pius IX's allocution of April 1848) shattered the liberal-nationalist consensus; and the absence of coordinated strategy across fragmented states allowed Austrian intervention to suppress uprisings sequentially. The Papal States' independence blocked geographic unity, and internal divisions between republicans (Mazzini), constitutionalists (moderates), and federalists meant no shared vision.\n\nAfter 1852, unification succeeded because three critical conditions converged: **Piedmont's strategic ascendancy**, **Franco-Piedmontese alliance**, and **astute political leadership**.\n\n**Piedmont's dominance** was established deliberately. Under Cavour (Prime Minister from 1852), Piedmont transformed into a modern centralized state with efficient administration, industrialization, and constitutional government. Its 1848 Statute remained in force, providing legitimacy as Italy's constitutional standard-bearer. Crucially, Piedmont positioned itself as the sole Italian state capable of challenging Austria militarily—Austria being the fundamental obstacle to unification. By 1859, Piedmont possessed superior military organization and resources compared to 1848-49.\n\n**The Franco-Piedmontese alliance of 1858** (Plombières meeting, July) reversed the external constraint that had destroyed earlier movements. Napoleon III agreed to support Piedmont against Austria in exchange for Nice and Savoy, securing France's eastern frontier. This was transformative: France's military might could counter Austrian intervention. The 1859 war saw French and Piedmontese forces defeat Austria at Magenta and Solferino, forcing Austrian withdrawal from Lombardy. Earlier attempts had faced Austrian military superiority with no counterbalance; now Piedmont had one.\n\nCavour's strategy proved superior to predecessors'. Rather than attempting simultaneous uprisings across Italy (which fragmented resistance), Cavour focused on incremental territorial expansion under Piedmontese control. After 1859, Lombardy voted to join Piedmont (1860); central Italian states (Tuscany, Parma, Modena) were persuaded or manipulated into union through plebiscites. Cavour orchestrated these via moderate nationalist movements and exploited papal weakness—Pius IX, isolated and opposed to Italian unity, could not mobilize effective resistance as Catholic opinion remained divided.\n\n**The southern question was solved through Garibaldi's military genius and Cavour's opportunism.** Garibaldi's 1860 Sicilian expedition exploited Bourbon weakness, conquering Sicily and Naples without great-power opposition (Britain tacitly supported it; France was distracted by Mexican intervention). Garibaldi's actions created momentum that Cavour—initially hesitant—then channelled into Piedmontese absorption. Victor Emmanuel II's entry into southern Italy completed unification by preventing a separate Garibaldi-dominated southern state. Earlier movements had lacked this combination: Mazzini's 1857 and earlier initiatives failed partly because no charismatic military figure of Garibaldi's calibre and strategic alignment existed.\n\n**The international context shifted decisively.** Britain, economically and strategically interested in a strong Italy countering Austria and France, offered tacit support. The Concert of Europe's capacity to enforce the 1815 settlement had weakened; Crimean War (1853-56) had fractured conservative solidarity, and Napoleon III's nationalism undermined legitimist restoration principles. Austria, distracted by Hungarian discontent and financial strain after 1859, could not reassemble coalitions to restore Italian rulers or prevent Piedmontese expansion. In 1848-49, these powers had combined against nationalism; by 1859-61, fragmentation enabled it.\n\n**Ideological consensus crystallized around Piedmontese leadership.** The 1848-49 failure of radicalism (republican uprisings crushed; Mazzini's conspiracies ineffective) shifted opinion toward constitutional-monarchical unification under Victor Emmanuel II and the Piedmontese Statute. This reconciled moderate liberals, conservative nationalists, and many Catholics who accepted the king as necessary for order and independence from revolutionary disorder. Earlier movements had been fragmented between competing visions; now a dominant moderate-nationalist coalition formed.\n\n**Timing and sequence mattered crucially.** Cavour's success depended on operating after 1848's failure had eliminated republican competition, after Napoleon III's seizure of power (1848-52) had clarified French strategy, and after Austrian exhaustion from 1848-49 had exposed vulnerabilities. He moved methodically: securing British neutrality, forging the French alliance, then exploiting it. Earlier attempts had lacked this patience and sequencing.\n\nIn synthesis: unification after 1852 succeeded because Piedmont combined administrative modernity, military capacity, and constitutional legitimacy with Cavour's pragmatic strategy, because Franco-Piedmontese alliance removed Austria's invulnerability, because international consensus shifted against restoration, and because the ideological fragmentation of 1848 yielded to unified moderate-nationalist purpose. Earlier attempts failed when these conditions were absent: Piedmont was militarily weaker, Austria enjoyed great-power backing, competing ideological camps paralyzed coordination, and no single state possessed sufficient strength or credibility to lead. The post-1852 success was contingent on learning from failure, altered international alignments, and a leader of supreme political acumen.",
