        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Charles Darwin fundamentally transformed biological science through his theory of evolution by natural selection, developed during the 1830s-1850s and published in *On the Origin of Species* (1859). Darwin's voyage on HMS Beagle (1831-1836) to the Galápagos Islands provided crucial observational evidence. He observed finches with varying beak structures adapted to different food sources and noted how animal breeders artificially selected traits over generations. Darwin synthesised these observations with Malthus's population theory to propose that organisms with advantageous traits survive and reproduce more successfully, gradually changing species over vast timescales. This challenged prevailing creationist views and the concept of immutable species.\n\nDevelopments in electricity during this period established foundations for modern physics and technology. Michael Faraday's experiments (1820s-1830s) demonstrated electromagnetic induction—that changing magnetic fields generate electrical currents—leading to the dynamo and electric motor. Faraday's work showed electricity and magnetism were interconnected forces, unifying understanding of natural phenomena. Hans Christian Ørsted's discovery of electromagnetism (1820) demonstrated that electric current produced magnetic fields. These discoveries enabled practical applications: Samuel Morse developed the electromagnetic telegraph (patented 1840), revolutionising long-distance communication by transmitting coded electrical signals through wires. The telegraph network expanded rapidly across Europe and America, connecting continents and transforming commerce, journalism and warfare.\n\nBiological science advanced significantly beyond Darwin. Microscopy improvements revealed cellular structure: Matthias Schleiden and Theodor Schwann established cell theory (1830s-1840s), demonstrating that all living organisms comprise cells, fundamentally reshaping understanding of life. Louis Pasteur's germ theory (1860s) proved that microorganisms caused fermentation and disease, displacing miasma theory and establishing scientific basis for hygiene, sanitation and medical practice. His work on vaccination against anthrax (1876) demonstrated how understanding microbes enabled disease prevention, extending beyond the 1871 endpoint but representing trajectory of biological understanding.\n\nTechnological innovation accelerated dramatically. The steam engine, perfected by James Watt earlier, powered industrial transformation. George Stephenson's locomotive (1825) and expanding railway networks (1830s-1860s) revolutionised transport, enabling movement of goods and people at unprecedented speed and scale. Railways facilitated industrial expansion, urbanisation and social change. Telegraph and railway developed symbiotically: telegraph communicated train schedules and coordinated operations across networks.\n\nPhotography emerged as transformative technology. Louis Daguerre's daguerreotype (1839) and later developments enabled visual documentation of reality, revolutionising art, science and social record-keeping. Photography provided precise visual evidence for scientific observation, particularly valuable for astronomy and microscopy.\n\nThese developments were interconnected. Electrical discoveries enabled telegraph communication; improved microscopy supported germ theory; railway expansion demanded better engineering and communication technologies. The period witnessed shift from natural philosophy to modern experimental science: systematic observation, mathematical analysis, controlled experimentation and practical application became hallmarks of scientific method.\n\nThe social impact was profound. Darwin's evolution theory challenged religious orthodoxy and traditional worldviews, generating intense debate about humanity's place in nature. Electrical and transportation technologies transformed daily life, commerce and military capability. Scientific understanding of disease prevention and hygiene improved public health. These developments established frameworks—cellular theory, germ theory, evolutionary theory, electromagnetic theory—that dominated subsequent scientific progress.\n\nScientific institutions expanded: the British Association for the Advancement of Science (founded 1831) promoted scientific research and public understanding. Universities increasingly emphasised experimental science alongside traditional classical education. The period established science as central to progress, economic development and national power, motivating government and private investment in research and technological development that characterised the modern era.",
