        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Betty Friedan and Marilyn Monroe, though representing contrasting responses to post-war American gender expectations, both illuminate the contradictions and evolving consciousness about women's roles in mid-twentieth-century US society.\n\nMarilyn Monroe epitomised the 1950s ideal of femininity that emerged after World War II. During the war, women had entered the industrial workforce in unprecedented numbers, proving their capability in manufacturing and production roles. However, post-war ideology sought to restore traditional domesticity, and Monroe became the cultural icon of this restored feminine ideal—glamorous, sexually desirable, yet ultimately domestic. Her image in films like Gentlemen Prefer Blondes (1953) and The Seven Year Itch (1955) celebrated beauty, sexuality, and an apparently passive acceptance of male desire. Yet Monroe's career itself represented a fundamental shift: she was economically independent, controlled her own image to a significant degree, and wielded considerable power in Hollywood. The very existence of her stardom—earning substantial income, commanding respect as a professional—contradicted the domestic confinement to which post-war ideology supposedly confined women. Moreover, Monroe's later attempts to be taken seriously as an actress (studying at the Actors Lab, seeking dramatic roles) revealed her own dissatisfaction with being reduced to a sex symbol, suggesting that even the decade's most celebrated feminine icon chafed against restrictive expectations.\n\nBetty Friedan directly challenged these post-war gender assumptions. Her 1963 book, The Feminine Mystique, articulated what she termed \"the problem that has no name\"—the widespread dissatisfaction of educated, middle-class women confined to domesticity. Friedan argued that post-war American culture had constructed a limiting ideology of femininity that confined women to the roles of wife and mother, despite their educational qualifications and aspirations. She demonstrated that women's wartime workforce participation had proven their capability, yet post-war society had systematically pushed them back into domestic roles through advertising, media, and social pressure. Friedan's analysis revealed that the 1950s celebration of homemaking masked a profound restriction of women's potential. Her work resonated with millions of women and catalysed the revival of feminism, establishing her as an intellectual leader of the women's movement. More significantly, Friedan's own career—as a journalist, author, and activist—embodied the alternative role for women that she advocated: intellectual engagement, professional achievement, and public participation.\n\nTaken together, Friedan and Monroe reflect the changing and contradictory position of women in this period. Monroe's enormous popularity revealed American culture's simultaneous desire to reassert traditional femininity after wartime disruption, yet her stardom proved that women could achieve significant economic and cultural power. Friedan's influence showed that educated women were increasingly conscious of the constraints placed upon them and willing to articulate and organise against them. The 1950s presented as a period of restored domesticity, yet it simultaneously contained the seeds of the women's liberation movement of the 1960s. Both figures, in their different ways, demonstrated that American women in the post-war era were neither passive nor content with narrow definitions of their roles. Monroe's image promised glamorous fulfilment within traditional boundaries, yet her actual life and career exceeded those boundaries. Friedan explicitly rejected those boundaries altogether. The decade that produced Monroe's iconic status also produced the intellectual and social conditions for Friedan's challenge to the \"feminine mystique.\" By the mid-1960s, Friedan's vision of women's liberation was beginning to replace the 1950s' insistence on feminine domesticity. Both women, therefore, mark the transition from post-war restoration of traditional gender roles toward the liberation movements that would reshape American society in subsequent decades. Their contrasting trajectories—Monroe as the embodiment of 1950s femininity, Friedan as its critic and organiser of resistance—map the precise moment when American women's consciousness and expectations began to shift fundamentally.",
