        "question": "Discuss the part played by the Butlers of Ormond in Irish affairs from 1477 up to the death of Henry VIII.",
        "marks": 80,
        "model": "The Butlers of Ormond were the most powerful English noble family in Ireland during the late fifteenth and sixteenth centuries, exercising dominance through landholding, military authority, and political influence over the Pale and surrounding regions. Their role evolved significantly across this period, shaped by shifting royal policies and their own strategic interests.\n\nIn the late fifteenth century, the Butlers consolidated their territorial power. The earldom of Ormond controlled vast estates in Tipperary, Kilkenny, and Carlow, giving them leverage over the Pale's southern boundary. During the Wars of the Roses, the family navigated between York and Lancaster, ultimately supporting the Tudor claim. This loyalty paid dividends: Henry VII rewarded them with confirmation of lands and titles. The Butlers emerged as stabilising forces in an unstable political environment, serving as Lord Lieutenants and Justices of Ireland, roles that enhanced their authority and allowed them to pursue private interests under a crown mandate.\n\nUnder Henry VIII, the Butlers' position became more complex. The King's expansionist policies in Ireland—particularly Surrender and Regrant after 1541—threatened their traditional autonomy. However, the family adapted pragmatically. The 8th Earl of Ormond initially resisted religious change but ultimately accepted the break with Rome, securing his lands and titles. The Butlers benefited from the dissolution of monasteries: they acquired ecclesiastical properties, expanding their wealth and influence. This opportunism exemplified how the family maintained power through flexibility rather than principled resistance.\n\nMilitarily, the Butlers played a crucial defensive role. They fought against Gaelic Irish lords encroaching on the Pale, particularly the O'Tooles and O'Byrnes of Wicklow. These conflicts were chronic and costly, yet the Butlers bore much of the burden of protecting English interests. They also confronted the Fitzgerald family of Kildare, rivals for pre-eminence in the English lordship. The Fitzgerald Rebellion of 1534–1535, led by Silken Thomas Fitzgerald, positioned the Butlers as loyalists: they supported the crown against the rebels, and Henry VIII rewarded them by crushing the Fitzgeralds, eliminating their chief rivals.\n\nPolitically, the Butlers wielded considerable influence over governance. Multiple family members served as Lord Lieutenant or Chief Governor, allowing them to shape policy in their favour. They supported centralisation of royal authority in Ireland, which paradoxically strengthened their own position as the crown's principal agents. They were instrumental in extending English law and administration into the wider lordship, though this brought them into conflict with Gaelic lords who resisted English encroachment.\n\nSocially and culturally, the Butlers remained thoroughly anglicised. Unlike some Anglo-Irish families that adopted Gaelic customs—the \"degenerate English\" condemned by crown officials—the Butlers maintained English language, law, and culture. This distinction was politically valuable: they presented themselves as reliable representatives of English civilization, justifying their authority and securing royal confidence.\n\nBy Henry VIII's death in 1547, the Butlers had secured their position as the dominant English noble family in Ireland. Their lands were extensive and profitable, their titles confirmed, and their role in royal administration assured. Yet their power remained contingent on royal favour and their ability to maintain control over their territory against Gaelic expansion. The family's pragmatic approach—adapting to religious change, cooperating with Tudor centralisation, eliminating rivals with royal support—ensured their survival and prosperity through a transformative period in Irish history, establishing foundations for their continued prominence in the sixteenth century.",
        "diagram": "",
