        "question": "How did it happen that Parnell came to be such a dominant and effective political figure by 1886?",
        "marks": 80,
        "model": "Parnell emerged as the dominant figure in Irish nationalist politics by 1886 through a combination of exceptional leadership skills, strategic political positioning, and his ability to unite fractious Irish parliamentary and popular movements.\n\nParnell's rise was facilitated by the fragmentation of Irish nationalism after 1879. The Land War had created momentum for agrarian reform, but the movement lacked coherent direction. Parnell, elected MP for Meath in 1875, positioned himself at the intersection of constitutional nationalism and agrarian agitation. He became president of the Land League in 1880, giving him authority over the grassroots movement that commanded peasant loyalty. This dual base—combining parliamentary respectability with popular support—was unprecedented and immensely powerful. Where previous nationalist leaders had operated in one sphere or the other, Parnell controlled both.\n\nHis parliamentary tactics proved devastatingly effective. Parnell pioneered the use of systematic obstruction in Westminster, where Irish MPs could delay or disrupt business to force concessions. This strategy, deployed from 1877 onwards, demonstrated that Irish representation could not be ignored. The tactic made Irish issues central to British politics and forced governments to engage seriously with Irish demands. By 1880, Parnell had established himself as a figure British ministers could not dismiss or marginalise.\n\nParnell's personal qualities reinforced his political advantage. Unlike many Irish nationalist leaders, he was a Protestant landlord from Avondale, which gave him social standing and credibility with moderate opinion. Yet he allied decisively with radical agrarian forces. This bridging of classes and traditions was crucial. His aloofness and reserve—often remarked upon—gave him an air of authority and dignity that contrasted with the rhetoric of other nationalist figures. He was not a populist orator but a calculating strategist, which appealed to serious politicians on both sides of the Irish Sea.\n\nThe Land League years (1879–1882) consolidated his dominance. Parnell used the League's machinery to channel agrarian discontent into nationalist politics. The combination of rent strikes, boycotts—particularly the famous boycott of Captain Boycott in 1880—and political agitation created a powerful movement. His arrest in 1881 and imprisonment in Kilmainham only enhanced his status as a martyr to the cause. When he negotiated the Kilmainham Treaty with Chief Secretary Forster in 1882, securing land reform concessions, he demonstrated he could deliver tangible results. This reinforced his claim to effective leadership.\n\nParnell's reconstruction of the Irish Parliamentary Party after 1882 was his greatest organisational achievement. He imposed strict party discipline, making the party a cohesive voting bloc rather than a collection of independent figures. MPs who broke the whip faced removal. This discipline made the Irish party indispensable—a tool that could be deployed strategically. After the 1885 general election, the Irish party held the balance of power at Westminster with 86 seats, and Parnell's disciplined party could determine whether Liberal or Conservative governments commanded a majority. This gave him unprecedented leverage.\n\nThe conversion of Gladstone to Irish Home Rule in 1886 vindicated Parnell's entire strategy. By combining constitutional pressure, parliamentary obstruction, and mass agitation, Parnell had forced the leader of the Liberal Party to commit to Irish self-government. The First Home Rule Bill of 1886, though defeated, represented a historic shift in British politics engineered by Parnell's political skill. He had made Home Rule a serious proposition in Westminster politics, something that seemed impossible a decade earlier.\n\nBy 1886, Parnell stood at the apex of nationalist politics. He controlled the Parliamentary Party absolutely, commanded support from the Land League and popular forces, had proven his negotiating effectiveness, and had secured a commitment to Home Rule from the dominant British political force. No other Irish nationalist figure before him had achieved such comprehensive dominance or such tangible political results. His effectiveness rested on disciplined organisation, strategic intelligence, and the ability to unite constitutional and agrarian nationalism around a single leader and programme.",
