      {
        "label": "Q2",
        "question": "During the period 1922-1939, who achieved more in Anglo-Irish relations, Cosgrave or de Valera?\nArgue your case, referring to both.",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "During 1922–1939, Éamon de Valera achieved significantly more in Anglo-Irish relations than W.T. Cosgrave, though each pursued different strategies with measurable outcomes.\n\nCosgrave's tenure (1922–1932) prioritised stability and gradual advancement within the Commonwealth framework. His administration accepted the Treaty settlement and worked constructively with Britain. The Boundary Commission (1924–1925) produced disappointing results for the Irish Free State—it confirmed the existing border rather than transferring substantial territory—but Cosgrave accepted the outcome diplomatically. He secured the elimination of the Council of Ireland and achieved the Statute of Westminster (1931), which granted dominions effective independence in foreign policy and legislation. However, his approach was cautious. He did not challenge the oath of allegiance to the Crown, maintained Free State membership in the Commonwealth, and avoided confrontational policies. His government ratified the Geneva Convention (1924) and participated in League of Nations affairs, demonstrating international engagement, but these were largely procedural gains within an existing framework rather than transformative shifts in sovereignty.\n\nCosgrave's economic and social policy had indirect relevance to Anglo-Irish relations. His government prioritised agricultural development and maintained fiscal orthodoxy, which limited conflict with Britain but also constrained Ireland's economic independence. The Anglo-Irish Trade Agreement of 1923 was unfavourable to Irish interests. His cautious diplomacy reflected genuine constraints—the Irish Free State was economically vulnerable and militarily weak relative to Britain—but it also meant that substantive sovereignty gains remained limited during his premiership.\n\nDe Valera (1932–1939 and beyond) pursued a more assertive nationalist agenda that produced tangible constitutional and economic achievements. On taking office, he immediately began systematically dismantling the Treaty framework. In 1933, he removed the oath of allegiance to the Crown, a symbolic but constitutionally significant act that had rankled Irish nationalism since 1922. This was achieved without triggering British military or economic retaliation, demonstrating de Valera's shrewd political judgment. He exploited Britain's reluctance to use force and its concern with appeasement in Europe.\n\nMost significantly, de Valera secured the external relations act (1936), which removed the Crown's formal role in Irish foreign affairs, and then enacted the 1937 Constitution. This constitution fundamentally restructured the Irish state: it replaced the Free State with \"Éire\" (or \"Ireland\"), established the President as head of state (displacing the British monarch), and asserted Irish sovereignty over the entire island—though the 26 counties alone accepted it. The constitution was a revolutionary achievement: it created a genuinely independent framework while Britain, preoccupied with Nazi Germany, made only diplomatic protests. De Valera had effectively achieved a republic in substance, if not yet in name, without a war and despite Britain's residual constitutional position.\n\nDe Valera's economic strategy also marked a break with Cosgrave's subordination to British interests. The Anglo-Irish Trade War (1932–1938) resulted from de Valera's refusal to pay land annuities owed under the Treaty. Though economically damaging to Ireland in the short term—agricultural exports fell sharply—de Valera used the conflict to underline Irish independence and eventually negotiated a settlement (1938) that eliminated these payments. The 1938 agreement also returned the Treaty ports (Cobh, Berehaven, Lough Swilly) to Irish control. This was a concrete territorial and strategic gain: it freed Ireland from de facto British naval presence and proved invaluable when Ireland remained neutral in the Second World War (1939–1945). Cosgrave's gradualism had left these ports in British hands; de Valera's assertiveness recovered them.\n\nDe Valera also secured the return of the National Archives and Irish historical documents from British custody, a symbolic reclamation of Irish sovereignty over its own past. His government pursued an independent foreign policy in the League of Nations and asserted Éire's neutrality as an expression of sovereignty—something inconceivable under Cosgrave's Commonwealth membership.\n\nThe contrast is stark: Cosgrave accepted the Treaty's constitutional framework and worked for marginal improvements within it. His gains—the Statute of Westminster, the elimination of the Council of Ireland—were important but were achieved within a Commonwealth context and did not fundamentally alter the power relationship. De Valera, by contrast, systematically dismantled that framework without major conflict, recovered territory and strategic assets, and created a genuinely independent constitutional order. By 1939, Éire was recognized internationally as independent; the Crown had no executive role; the ports were Irish; and neutrality was asserted as an expression of sovereignty.\n\nIt might be objected that de Valera's economic policy damaged Irish prosperity and that his constitutional nationalism was largely symbolic. The trade war caused hardship, and the 1937 Constitution, while asserting independence, could not force British acceptance of Irish sovereignty over Northern Ireland—a fundamental nationalist aspiration that neither leader achieved. However, the question asks who achieved more in Anglo-Irish relations, not who solved partition or maximized prosperity. In reshaping the constitutional relationship, recovering territory and strategic assets, and establishing practical independence, de Valera achieved demonstrably more.\n\nCosgrave's achievement was securing a peaceful transition from the Civil War and building a stable state within the Commonwealth. This was substantial and underestimated. However, de Valera's achievements—constitutional independence, recovery of the ports, elimination of land annuities, and assertion of neutrality—were more transformative of the actual relationship between the Irish state and Britain. De Valera achieved more because he moved Ireland from dominion status toward genuine independence, whereas Cosgrave consolidated dominion status without fundamentally altering it.\n\nIn conclusion, while both leaders advanced Irish interests, de Valera accomplished far more in reshaping Anglo-Irish relations. He converted Treaty constraints into constitutional independence, recovered strategic territory, and established Éire as an autonomous state. Cosgrave's steadier approach built stability but left the fundamental structures of British dominance intact. De Valera's assertive nationalism, deployed with political acumen and timing, achieved measurably greater gains in sovereignty and independence during the 1932–1939 period.",
        "diagram": "",
        "model_source": "ai-h1"
      }
