        "question": "How did one or more of the following challenge widely-held views in the US: Norman Mailer; Betty Friedan; Muhammad Ali?",
        "marks": 100,
        "model": "Norman Mailer, Betty Friedan, and Muhammad Ali each challenged fundamental assumptions embedded in post-war American society, using different methods and targeting distinct orthodoxies that had calcified during the 1950s and early 1960s.\n\nNorman Mailer challenged the intellectual and cultural consensus about American power, masculinity, and artistic authenticity. His 1957 essay \"The White Negro\" and later works confronted the sanitised, conformist culture of the Eisenhower era by arguing that white American intellectuals should learn from African American existential responses to oppression. Mailer rejected the dominant view that American consumer capitalism represented enlightenment or progress; instead, he depicted it as spiritually deadening. His concept of \"hip\" consciousness—living authentically in opposition to institutional control—directly contradicted the widely-held belief in the 1950s that consensus, suburban domesticity, and corporate organisation represented American success. In \"Advertisements for Myself\" (1959), Mailer positioned the writer as a necessary rebel against mass society, challenging the view that intellectuals should serve national Cold War objectives. His aggressive, visceral prose style itself was an assault on the polite, measured discourse expected of public intellectuals. By the 1960s, when he covered events like the 1967 Pentagon march, Mailer articulated a counter-narrative to official Cold War ideology, arguing that American militarism and imperial overreach—not Soviet communism—posed the real threat to American democracy.\n\nBetty Friedan's \"The Feminine Mystique\" (1963) systematically demolished the post-war consensus about women's nature and role. The dominant ideology, amplified by advertisers, psychologists, and popular culture, held that women found complete fulfilment in domesticity, motherhood, and consumer activities within the home. This \"feminine mystique\" was presented as natural, psychological fact. Friedan challenged this by demonstrating that it was a constructed mythology imposed on women to serve economic and social interests. She documented the \"problem that has no name\"—the widespread unhappiness and intellectual stunting experienced by educated women confined to domestic roles. Friedan's evidence, drawn from interviews, psychological research, and cultural analysis, showed that the post-war glorification of the housewife masked exploitation and the waste of human potential. Her book challenged the widely-held view that women's liberation was unnecessary, that legal equality had been achieved, and that women themselves did not want careers or public roles. Friedan provided empirical grounding for the claim that the American consensus on gender roles rested on falsehood. She argued that women's full participation in public life—intellectual, professional, and political—was both desirable and necessary for American society, directly contradicting the ideology that had dominated American culture since the 1940s.\n\nMuhammad Ali (formerly Cassius Clay) challenged the deepest orthodoxies of American nationalism, patriotism, and racial hierarchy. His 1964 conversion to Islam and adoption of a Muslim name repudiated the assumption that African Americans should assimilate into dominant white American Christian culture. This was experienced by many white Americans as a shocking rejection of the nation itself. More dramatically, his 1966 refusal to be drafted into the Vietnam War directly challenged the widely-held view—especially among Cold War consensus-builders—that military service represented a patriotic duty that transcended individual conscience. Ali's statement, \"I ain't got no quarrel with them Viet Cong,\" articulated a radically different vision of American responsibility and national interest. He rejected the official narrative that the Vietnam War was a necessary defence of freedom and democracy, positioning it instead as an imperial war against a people of colour. By refusing induction, Ali challenged the assumption that patriotism meant unquestioning obedience to state military decisions. He faced prosecution, imprisonment threat, and loss of his boxing career—the cost of his challenge. Yet his stance also reflected a broader questioning within American society of Cold War certainties. Ali's example demonstrated that African American athletes, traditionally expected to be politically deferential and grateful for access to mainstream success, could claim moral authority and speak truth to power. His challenge to racial hierarchy was inseparable from his challenge to American militarism.\n\nThese three figures operated in overlapping but distinct registers. Mailer challenged the cultural and spiritual emptiness he saw at the heart of American consumer capitalism and Cold War ideology. Friedan challenged the pseudo-scientific and psychological mythology that confined women to domestic roles and masked their systematic exclusion from public power. Ali challenged the intersection of American militarism, imperial foreign policy, and racial hierarchy, asserting that patriotism could mean resistance to state power. Collectively, they exposed the gap between American self-image as a free, democratic, and rational society and the actual constraints, mythologies, and violence that underpinned American institutions. Each found large audiences—Friedan's book became the founding text of second-wave feminism; Ali became a global icon of resistance; Mailer influenced literary and cultural criticism. Their challenges were not marginal: they helped shape the social movements and intellectual ferment that transformed American society in the 1960s and 1970s, proving that the post-war consensus was neither natural nor inevitable, but rather contingent and vulnerable to sustained intellectual and moral critique.",
